window.DEEP_STORIES["airplane"] = {"slug":"airplane","title":"The Airplane","emoji":"✈️","tagline":"How humans finally learned to fly, and never looked back","intro":"For thousands of years, people watched birds and wondered: why can't we do that? Some told stories about wings made of feathers and wax. Some flew kites. A few brave (or foolish) people jumped off towers flapping homemade wings. Then, slowly, inventors began to treat flying as a puzzle that could be solved with careful thinking, testing and teamwork. In these 20 parts you will travel from ancient legends and Chinese kites to hot-air balloons, from Sir George Cayley's gliders and Otto Lilienthal's daring hillside jumps to two bicycle makers from Ohio who flew for 12 seconds and changed the world. You will meet record-breaking pilots, ride in the first airliners, hear the roar of the jet engine, chase the sound barrier, and learn exactly how a wing holds up a 400-tonne jumbo jet. Finally, you will meet today's drones, Mars helicopters and the electric planes of tomorrow. Fasten your seatbelt!","outline":[{"n":1,"title":"Dreaming of Wings","when":"Ancient times – about 1100 CE"},{"n":2,"title":"Sketches and Balloons","when":"About 1480 – 1800"},{"n":3,"title":"Sir George Cayley Cracks the Code","when":"1799 – 1857"},{"n":4,"title":"Steam Engines and Hopping Machines","when":"1842 – 1903"},{"n":5,"title":"Otto Lilienthal, the Glider King","when":"1889 – 1899"},{"n":6,"title":"Two Brothers from Dayton","when":"1878 – 1901"},{"n":7,"title":"The Wind Tunnel Secret","when":"1901 – 1902"},{"n":8,"title":"Twelve Seconds That Changed the World","when":"1903"},{"n":9,"title":"How a Wing Really Works","when":"Science explainer"},{"n":10,"title":"Proving It to the World","when":"1904 – 1909"},{"n":11,"title":"Daredevils and Record-Breakers","when":"1909 – 1914"},{"n":12,"title":"Steering the Sky: Controls, Propellers and Engines","when":"Science explainer"},{"n":13,"title":"War, Mail and Crossing Oceans","when":"1914 – 1937"},{"n":14,"title":"The Airliner Is Born","when":"1919 – 1950s"},{"n":15,"title":"The Jet Engine Roars","when":"1930 – 1960"},{"n":16,"title":"Breaking the Sound Barrier","when":"1940s – 2003"},{"n":17,"title":"Jumbo Jets and a Shrinking World","when":"1969 – 2019"},{"n":18,"title":"Safety, Problems and Hard Questions","when":"1950s – today"},{"n":19,"title":"Smart Planes and Drones","when":"1918 – today"},{"n":20,"title":"The Future of Flight and the Long Journey","when":"Today – the future, looking back"}],"people":[["Abbas ibn Firnas (about 810–887)","scholar and inventor in Córdoba, said to have attempted a glide with feathered wings around 875; account written long after"],["Eilmer of Malmesbury (about 980s – after 1066)","English monk said to have glided from an abbey tower around 1010"],["Leonardo da Vinci (1452–1519)","Italian artist and inventor who sketched flying machines and studied bird flight"],["Giovanni Alfonso Borelli (1608–1679)","Italian scientist who showed human muscles are too weak to flap wings"],["Joseph-Michel Montgolfier (1740–1810) and Jacques-Étienne Montgolfier (1745–1799)","French paper-makers who invented the hot-air balloon"],["Jean-François Pilâtre de Rozier (1754–1785)","co-pilot of the first free human balloon flight (1783)"],["François Laurent, Marquis d'Arlandes (1742–1809)","flew with Pilâtre de Rozier in 1783"],["Jacques Charles (1746–1823)","French scientist who flew the first hydrogen balloon with a person aboard"],["Jean-Pierre Blanchard (1753–1809)","French balloonist who crossed the English Channel in 1785 with John Jeffries"],["Sir George Cayley (1773–1857)","English engineer, 'father of aeronautics', defined the four forces and the fixed-wing design"],["William Samuel Henson (1812–1888) and John Stringfellow (1799–1883)","designed the Aerial Steam Carriage"],["Félix du Temple (1823–1890)","French naval officer whose steam plane made a short hop in 1874"],["Alexander Mozhaysky (1825–1890)","Russian naval officer whose steam plane made a short hop in 1884"],["Clément Ader (1841–1925)","French engineer whose Éole made an uncontrolled hop in 1890"],["Hiram Maxim (1840–1916)","inventor who built a huge steam-powered flying test rig in 1894"],["Lawrence Hargrave (1850–1915)","Australian inventor of the box kite"],["Samuel Pierpont Langley (1834–1906)","Smithsonian Secretary who flew unmanned steam models but whose crewed Aerodrome failed in 1903"],["Charles Manly (1876–1927)","engineer and pilot of Langley's Great Aerodrome; built its advanced engine"],["Otto Lilienthal (1848–1896)","German 'Glider King', made over 2,000 glides and died after a crash"],["Gustav Lilienthal (1849–1933)","Otto's brother and research partner"],["Percy Pilcher (1867–1899)","British glider pioneer who died in a crash"],["Octave Chanute (1832–1910)","French-born American engineer who collected and shared flight knowledge and encouraged the Wrights"],["Wilbur Wright (1867–1912)","co-inventor of the first successful powered, controlled airplane"],["Orville Wright (1871–1948)","co-inventor; flew the first powered flight on 17 December 1903"],["Charlie Taylor (1868–1956)","mechanic who built the Wright Flyer's engine"],["John T. Daniels (1873–1948)","lifesaving station member who took the famous first-flight photograph"],["Thomas Selfridge (1882–1908)","US Army officer, first person killed in a powered airplane crash"],["Alberto Santos-Dumont (1873–1932)","Brazilian aviator in Paris; made the first officially witnessed powered flights in Europe (1906)"],["Gustave Whitehead (1874–1927)","German-American whose claimed 1901 flight lacks solid evidence"],["Richard Pearse (1877–1953)","New Zealand farmer-inventor whose possible 1903 flight attempts are uncertain; he never claimed to have beaten the Wrights"],["Glenn Curtiss (1878–1930)","American engine-builder and aviator, flew the June Bug; rival of the Wrights in patent battles"],["Alexander Graham Bell (1847–1922)","telephone inventor who led the Aerial Experiment Association"],["Louis Blériot (1872–1936)","French aviator who first flew an airplane across the English Channel (1909)"],["Raymonde de Laroche (1882–1919)","French aviator, first woman to earn a pilot's licence (1910)"],["Harriet Quimby (1875–1912)","first American woman to earn a pilot's licence; flew across the English Channel in 1912"],["Henri Fabre (1882–1984)","French engineer who flew the first seaplane (1910)"],["Eugene Ely (1886–1911)","American pilot who first took off from (1910) and landed on (1911) a ship"],["Calbraith Perry Rodgers (1879–1912)","made the first flight across the USA in 1911, with many stops and crashes"],["Tony Jannus (1889–1916)","pilot of the first scheduled airline flight (1914)"],["Lawrence Sperry (1892–1923)","demonstrated the gyroscopic autopilot in 1914"],["Anthony Fokker (1890–1939)","Dutch aircraft maker whose company introduced machine-gun synchronization (1915)"],["Hugo Junkers (1859–1935)","German engineer who built the first practical all-metal airplane"],["John Alcock (1892–1919) and Arthur Whitten Brown (1886–1948)","made the first nonstop transatlantic flight (1919)"],["Bessie Coleman (1892–1926)","first African American woman and first woman of Native American descent to earn a pilot's licence"],["Charles Lindbergh (1902–1974)","made the first solo nonstop flight from New York to Paris (1927)"],["Amelia Earhart (1897–disappeared 1937)","first woman to fly solo across the Atlantic (1932)"],["Ellen Church (1904–1965)","nurse who became the first airline stewardess (1930)"],["Jimmy Doolittle (1896–1993)","pilot who made the first full 'blind' instrument-only flight (1929)"],["Frank Whittle (1907–1996)","British engineer who patented the turbojet engine"],["Hans von Ohain (1911–1998)","German engineer whose jet engine powered the first jet flight (1939)"],["Chuck Yeager (1923–2020)","US test pilot who first flew faster than sound in level flight (1947)"],["Jacqueline Cochran (1906–1980)","first woman to fly faster than sound (1953)"],["Richard Whitcomb (1921–2009)","NASA engineer who discovered the area rule"],["Pete Knight (1929–2004)","flew the X-15 to Mach 6.7 in 1967"],["David Warren (1925–2010)","Australian scientist who invented the flight data recorder"],["Chesley 'Sully' Sullenberger (born 1951)","captain who landed US Airways Flight 1549 on the Hudson River in 2009"],["Bertrand Piccard (born 1958) and André Borschberg (born 1952)","pilots of Solar Impulse 2's round-the-world flight"]],"glossary":[["airfoil","the special curved cross-section shape of a wing that helps it make lift"],["lift","the upward force a wing makes when air flows over and under it"],["weight","the downward pull of gravity on the plane"],["thrust","the forward push from propellers or jet engines"],["drag","air resistance that slows the plane down"],["angle of attack","the angle between the wing and the oncoming air"],["stall","a sudden loss of lift when the wing meets the air at too steep an angle (nothing to do with the engine stopping)"],["wing warping","the Wrights' method of twisting the wingtips to roll the plane"],["pitch, roll and yaw","the three ways a plane can rotate: nose up/down, tilting wings, nose left/right"],["aileron","a hinged flap on the wing that makes the plane roll"],["elevator","a hinged surface on the tail that makes the nose go up or down"],["rudder","a hinged surface on the tail fin that swings the nose left or right"],["wind tunnel","a tube or box where air is blown over models to test how they fly"],["jet engine","an engine that sucks in air, squeezes it, burns fuel in it, and blows it out fast to push the plane forward"],["turbofan","a jet engine with a big fan at the front that pushes extra air around the engine's core"],["Mach number","speed compared to the speed of sound; Mach 1 is the speed of sound, Mach 2 is twice as fast"],["sonic boom","the loud bang heard on the ground when a plane's shock waves pass by"],["pressurized cabin","a sealed cabin pumped with air so people can breathe comfortably at high altitude"],["fly-by-wire","a system where computers send the pilot's commands to the control surfaces electronically"],["drone","an aircraft flown without a pilot on board, by remote control or by its own computer"]],"parts":[{"n":1,"title":"Dreaming of Wings","when":"Ancient times – about 1100 CE","summary":"Long ago, people watched birds and wished they could fly too. They told stories about magic wings, flew silk kites in China, and a few brave people even jumped off towers wearing feathers. They got hurt, but they started the greatest adventure in the sky!","sections":[{"heading":"Why Everyone Envied the Birds","paragraphs":["Imagine sitting on a hillside four thousand years ago. You have no car, no bicycle and no map. To get anywhere you walk, ride a donkey or paddle a boat. A river in front of you might take a whole day to cross. A mountain might take a week. Then a hawk lifts off a rock, tilts its wings, and in a few seconds it is gliding over the river and circling above the mountain as if neither of them existed.","No wonder people were jealous. Birds could do things that seemed like pure magic. A swallow can twist and dart after insects faster than you can follow it with your eyes. An eagle can hang almost still in the wind, wings spread, hardly flapping at all. Geese fly in great V shapes across whole continents every autumn and come back every spring. For a human stuck on the ground, birds were the ultimate symbol of freedom.","Flying would also have been incredibly useful. A person who could fly could see enemies coming from far away, find lost animals, carry messages across a valley, or escape from a dangerous place. Kings and generals dreamed about it for war. Poets dreamed about it for the feeling of floating among the clouds. Ordinary farmers probably just dreamed about getting to the market faster.","But wanting to fly and knowing HOW to fly are two very different things. For thousands of years, nobody understood what was really happening when a bird stayed up in the air. People did not know that air is a real substance made of countless tiny particles that push on everything they touch. They did not know about lift, drag or balance. So, for a very long time, the dream of flight lived mostly in stories.","In this first part of our journey we will visit those stories, then travel to ancient China, where people really did put objects into the sky using wind and string. Finally we will meet two brave men, one in Spain and one in England, who are said to have strapped on wings and jumped. Their stories were written down long after they happened, so we have to be careful detectives about what is certain and what is not. That kind of careful thinking, as you will see, is exactly what humans needed before they could truly fly."]},{"heading":"Wings in the Old Stories","paragraphs":["The most famous flying story in the world comes from ancient Greece. It is a myth, which means a traditional story that people told to explain the world or teach a lesson, not a record of something that really happened. It is the tale of Daedalus and his son Icarus.","In the legend, Daedalus was a brilliant craftsman from Athens. He worked for King Minos on the island of Crete and built him a giant maze called the Labyrinth, where a monster called the Minotaur, half man and half bull, was kept. Later the king grew angry with Daedalus and would not let him and his son leave the island. Crete is surrounded by sea, and the king controlled the ships. So Daedalus looked up. If they could not escape by land or by water, they would escape by air.","Daedalus collected feathers and fastened them together with thread and beeswax, making two pairs of wings shaped like a bird's. Before they took off, he gave Icarus a warning. Do not fly too low, or the spray from the sea will soak the feathers and make them heavy. Do not fly too high, or the heat of the Sun will melt the wax. Stay in the middle.","At first all went well. But Icarus loved the feeling of soaring and climbed higher and higher. In the story, the Sun's heat softened the wax, the feathers came loose, and Icarus fell into the sea and drowned. His heartbroken father named the nearby island Icaria, and the waters there are still called the Icarian Sea. The Roman poet Ovid told this story in great detail in his long poem called the Metamorphoses, written about 2,000 years ago, and artists have painted it ever since. In one famous painting, Landscape with the Fall of Icarus, made in the 1500s and usually linked to the Flemish artist Pieter Bruegel the Elder, a farmer keeps ploughing his field while poor Icarus is just a tiny pair of legs splashing into the sea in the corner.","Today people still say someone is 'flying too close to the Sun' when they take a big risk out of too much confidence. Of course, real science tells us the story could never happen the way it is told. The higher you climb in the sky, the COLDER it gets, not warmer. Jet airliners fly through air that is far below freezing. But myths are not meant to be science. This one is about ambition, warnings and listening to good advice.","Greece was not the only place with flying legends. A Persian legend tells of a king named Kay Kavus who tied hungry eagles to his throne and dangled meat above them, so that as they flew up toward the food they carried the throne into the sky. Another old tale says Alexander the Great was carried aloft in a basket pulled by griffins, imaginary creatures that were part eagle and part lion. Ancient Indian writings describe vimanas, magnificent flying palaces or chariots used by gods and heroes. In Greek myths, the messenger god Hermes zipped around in winged sandals. A British legend even claims that an ancient king named Bladud made himself wings and died trying to fly over a city.","There are also stories that sound a little more like engineering. A Greek thinker named Archytas of Taranto, who lived around 400 BCE, is said to have built a wooden pigeon that could move through the air, perhaps pushed by a puff of steam and held on a wire or pivot. Nobody knows what it really looked like, or if it existed at all. It may simply be a legend that grew over the centuries.","All these stories have something in common. They show that people from many cultures, on many continents, shared the same dream. And almost all of them imagined flight the same way: either borrowing the power of real birds, or copying birds by wearing wings. That idea would turn out to be a trap, as we will see at the end of this part."]},{"heading":"Silk, Bamboo and the Wind: China's First Kites","paragraphs":["While stories of feathered wings were being told around the Mediterranean, people in China were doing something remarkable. They were putting real objects into the sky and keeping them there. These objects were kites.","Many historians think the kite may be the very first human-made aircraft that was heavier than air, meaning it stays up even though it weighs more than the air it pushes aside. That makes the kite a distant great-great-grandparent of every airplane and glider. Kites in China are often said to date back more than 2,000 years, and some writers place them as early as the 400s BCE.","Chinese tradition links the first kites to two famous figures. One was Mozi, a philosopher and engineer who taught that people should care for everyone equally. The other was Lu Ban, a legendary master carpenter who is still honoured as the patron of builders and woodworkers in China. One old story says Mozi spent three years making a bird out of wood that flew for a day before breaking. Another says Lu Ban carved a wooden magpie that could stay aloft. These are traditions passed down in ancient books, not proven history. Nobody has dug up a 2,400-year-old kite to check. But the stories tell us something important: in China, building things that rode the wind was thought of as a skill for clever craftsmen.","The earliest kites were said to be made of wood. Later, kite makers used a combination that turned out to be almost perfect. For the frame they used bamboo, which is light, strong and springy, so it bends in a gust instead of snapping. For the sail they used silk, a smooth, thin fabric that is very strong for its weight. For the flying line they used fine silk thread, which could hold a strong pull without breaking. After paper was invented in China during the Han dynasty (which began in 206 BCE), paper kites became popular too, because paper was cheaper than silk. In Chinese, an old word for kite meant 'paper hawk'.","How does a kite actually fly? When the wind blows against the tilted face of a kite, the air is pushed downward and backward. The air pushes back on the kite, upward and backward, just as a wall pushes back on you when you lean on it. The string holds the kite in place, so instead of being blown away, it climbs. The tail helps keep the kite pointing the right way, like the feathers on an arrow. Without realising it, ancient kite makers had discovered several of the key ideas of flight: a tilted surface, moving air, and the need for balance and steadiness.","Over the centuries Chinese kites became works of art. They were painted with dragons, birds, butterflies and heroes from old stories. By the Tang dynasty (618 to 907 CE), people were attaching small bamboo pipes and whistles to kites so that they hummed and sang in the wind. That is why one modern Chinese word for kite, fengzheng, comes from the name of a stringed musical instrument: the kite was literally a harp played by the wind."]},{"heading":"Kites at Work: Signals, Wind and Lifting People","paragraphs":["Kites in ancient China were not only toys. Old Chinese writings describe them being used for serious jobs: measuring distances, testing the wind, sending signals and carrying messages. Picture an army camped on a plain. A kite rising above the camp could be seen for kilometres, and its colour or shape could tell friendly troops something without anyone shouting a word. A kite also showed which way the wind was blowing and roughly how strong it was, which mattered to sailors, soldiers and anyone planning to light a fire.","One story tells how a kite was used in a siege around 549 CE, when a city was surrounded by enemies. A kite carried a message out over the enemy lines, asking for rescue. Whether or not help arrived in time, the idea was clever: the sky was the one road the enemy could not block.","A darker set of stories shows that people in China also understood that kites could lift something as heavy as a human being. According to Chinese histories written centuries later, a cruel ruler named Gao Yang, emperor of a kingdom called Northern Qi, forced prisoners to jump from a high tower attached to large kites or bamboo mats, as a kind of punishment, around the year 559 CE. Most of them did not survive. But one, a prince named Yuan Huangtou, is recorded as gliding all the way to a road called the Purple Way, about 2.5 kilometres away, and landing alive. It is a sad story about how prisoners were treated, and historians cannot check every detail. But if it is even partly true, it would be one of the earliest reports of a person being carried through the air by a wing-like surface.","Much later, the Italian traveller Marco Polo, who visited China in the late 1200s, wrote about sailors who tied a man to a large kite before a voyage. They watched how the kite flew: if it rose straight and high, the voyage would be lucky; if it would not climb, the ship stayed in port. Polo noted how dangerous and unkind this was for the man on the kite. Stories like his helped carry news of kites to Europe, though Europeans would not take kites seriously as a scientific tool for hundreds of years.","China gave the world other early flying objects too. Small paper hot-air lanterns, now called sky lanterns, are believed to go back a very long time in China. A famous general named Zhuge Liang, who lived from 181 to 234 CE, is said to have used them to send signals or to startle enemy troops, which is why they are sometimes called Kongming lanterns after his nickname. These lanterns worked like tiny hot-air balloons: a small flame heated the air inside a paper bag, and the warm, lighter air floated the lantern upward. Chinese children also played with a toy sometimes called the bamboo-copter, a stick with little blades on top that whirled up into the air when you spun it between your palms. Hundreds of years later, a toy very much like it would delight two young brothers in Ohio, as you will read in Part 6.","Notice something about all of these Chinese inventions. A kite, a sky lantern and a bamboo-copter do not flap. They use wind, warm air or spinning blades. In a way, these toys and tools were closer to how real aircraft would one day fly than any pair of feathered wings."]},{"heading":"The Tower Jumpers: Abbas ibn Firnas of Córdoba","paragraphs":["Across the world, people kept trying the most obvious idea of all: strap on wings, find something high, and jump. Historians call these brave and often unlucky people the 'tower jumpers'. Most of them were badly hurt. Very few left reliable records. But a couple of their stories became famous.","One comes from Córdoba, in what is now southern Spain. About 1,200 years ago, Córdoba was one of the largest and most splendid cities in Europe. It was the capital of al-Andalus, the part of Spain ruled by Muslim emirs, and it had libraries, mosques, gardens, paved streets and scholars studying mathematics, medicine, music and the stars.","Among those scholars was Abbas ibn Firnas, who lived from about 810 to 887. He was what we call a polymath, a person who is expert in many different subjects. He was a poet and musician at the emir's court, an astronomer and an inventor. He is said to have designed a water clock, built a device that showed the movements of the planets and stars, found a way to cut rock crystal (a kind of clear quartz) in Spain instead of sending it all the way to Egypt, and worked on making clear glass.","According to the story, Abbas ibn Firnas covered himself in feathers, fixed wings to his arms, and launched himself from a high place. Witnesses said he glided a long way through the air before coming down. But the landing went badly. He crashed hard and hurt his back. Afterwards he is said to have explained what went wrong: he had not given himself a tail. Birds, he realised, use their tails to slow down and land gently, and he had none.","That explanation is actually very smart. A tail helps any flying thing stay steady and control its landing. Modern airplanes all have tail surfaces for exactly that reason.","But here is where we must be good history detectives. The main account of the flight was written by a historian named al-Maqqari, who died in 1632. That means he was writing roughly 750 years after Abbas ibn Firnas lived. Al-Maqqari did not explain where he found all his details, and no full eyewitness description has survived. The flight is often dated to around 875, but nobody knows the exact year. One interesting clue does exist: al-Maqqari quoted a poem by a court poet named Mu'min ibn Said, who lived at the same time as Abbas ibn Firnas, saying that he 'flew faster than the phoenix', a mythical bird. Because the verse sounds like teasing, some historians think it may be a joke about a real event. So something probably did happen, but how far he glided and exactly what his wings looked like are uncertain.","Abbas ibn Firnas is still remembered with pride. In 1973 a statue of him was put up near Baghdad International Airport in Iraq. In 1976, astronomers named a crater on the Moon after him. And in 2011 a bridge in Córdoba was named in his honour. Remember, though: he did not build an airplane, and nothing he made had an engine or could be steered like an aircraft. He was a brave early experimenter, one of the very first people we know by name who tried to ride the air."]},{"heading":"Eilmer, the Flying Monk of Malmesbury","paragraphs":["About a hundred and thirty years later, and over 1,500 kilometres to the north, another man had the same idea. His name was Eilmer, and he was a Benedictine monk at Malmesbury Abbey, a great stone monastery in the south-west of England. His name is sometimes written Elmer, Æthelmær or even Oliver.","Imagine the scene. It is early in the 1000s, a time when England was ruled by Anglo-Saxon kings and Vikings were still raiding the coasts. The abbey is the tallest building for miles. Young Eilmer, who had read the old story of Daedalus, has made himself a pair of wings and fastened them to his hands and feet. He climbs to the top of a tower, waits for a breeze, and leaps.","According to the account, the wind caught his wings and carried him forward. He glided for more than a furlong, an old English measurement equal to about 200 metres. That is about two football pitches! Some modern writers have estimated he might have been in the air for around 15 seconds. Then something went wrong. Perhaps the wind swirled around the buildings, or perhaps he panicked. He lost control, fell, and broke both his legs. He was lame, meaning he limped, for the rest of his life.","And what did Eilmer say had gone wrong? Amazingly, the same thing as Abbas ibn Firnas: he had forgotten to give himself a tail. There is no proof that Eilmer had ever heard of the man in Córdoba, so it seems that two people, far apart, reached the same smart conclusion from their painful crashes.","How do we know any of this? Almost everything comes from one writer: William of Malmesbury, a monk and one of the best historians of medieval England. He wrote a big history of the English kings around the year 1125, about a hundred years after the flight. William lived in the same abbey and probably heard the story from older monks who had known Eilmer when he was an old man. That makes the account better than a random legend, but it is still second-hand, so we cannot be completely sure of the distance or the details. Historians usually say Eilmer flew 'around 1010', because the exact year is not recorded.","Eilmer is remembered for something else too. William wrote that when a bright comet appeared in the sky in 1066, the elderly Eilmer saw it and cried out that it was a sign of disaster for England. Later that year, England was conquered by William of Normandy at the Battle of Hastings. That comet is the one we now call Halley's Comet. It even appears on the famous Bayeux Tapestry.","Today, Malmesbury Abbey has a stained-glass window, put in in 1920, that shows Eilmer holding his wings. A lane in the town, traditionally said to be near where he landed, is called Olivers Lane. Not bad for a monk who broke both his legs!"]},{"heading":"The Big Mistake: Copying the Flapping Bird","paragraphs":["Abbas ibn Firnas and Eilmer were not the only tower jumpers. Old Chinese histories say that around the first century CE, a man was dressed in bird feathers and is reported to have glided about 100 metres during the reign of the ruler Wang Mang. Over the following centuries, more people in Europe and the Middle East tried feathered cloaks, wooden wings and stiff capes. Many crashed. Some died. Almost none of them left behind notes that anyone else could learn from.","Why did they keep failing? The biggest reason is that they were copying the wrong thing. When we watch a bird, the most obvious thing it does is flap. So most dreamers believed that flapping was the secret, and that if a person could just flap big enough wings, they would rise into the sky. But flapping is actually one of the hardest ways to fly, and it is very hard to copy.","A bird's body is a marvel of lightweight engineering. Many bird bones are hollow, with thin struts inside like the beams of a bridge. Their feathers are incredibly light for their size. And their chest muscles, which power the downstroke of the wing, are enormous compared with the rest of the body. In a pigeon, the flight muscles make up a very large share of its whole weight. Our arm and chest muscles are much smaller compared with our heavy bodies. A person flapping homemade wings simply cannot produce enough power to lift themselves. Scientists would not prove this with numbers until the 1600s, as you will see in the next part.","There was a second problem: nobody understood the air itself. Ancient and medieval people knew that wind could push sails and turn windmills, but they did not have a clear idea that a moving wing could create an upward push called lift. They did not know about drag, the air resistance that slows things down. And they did not understand balance and control: how a flying thing keeps from tipping over, and how it can be steered. That is why the 'forgot the tail' explanations of Abbas ibn Firnas and Eilmer are so interesting. Both men had stumbled onto the idea of stability, but they had no way to study it properly.","Look back at the kite. It does not flap at all. It holds a fixed, tilted surface into moving air, and it uses a tail for steadiness. In a strange way, the Chinese kite makers were closer to the answer than the brave men leaping from towers. Many centuries later, an English inventor would look at the problem in exactly this way: stop flapping, keep the wing still, and find another way to push it forward. But that story belongs to Part 3.","Another lesson from the tower jumpers is about sharing knowledge. Each jumper mostly started from scratch. There were no journals, no test reports and no diagrams passed from one inventor to the next. When experimenters finally began writing down careful measurements and sharing them, progress sped up enormously.","By around the year 1100, then, humanity had beautiful myths, clever kites, sky lanterns and a few painful tower jumps. What it did not have was understanding. Dreaming was not enough. Someone needed to study birds and air the way a scientist would: watching, measuring, drawing and asking why. About four hundred years later, in Renaissance Italy, a painter with a notebook full of backwards handwriting began to do exactly that. His name was Leonardo da Vinci."]}],"deepDive":"Historians of early flight face a basic problem: almost every story before about 1500 comes from sources written long after the event. Eilmer's flight is known mainly from William of Malmesbury's Gesta regum Anglorum (about 1125), roughly a century later, though William's local connection to the abbey gives it some weight. Abbas ibn Firnas is worse off: the main account comes from al-Maqqari in the 1600s, about seven centuries later, with only a contemporary poem as supporting evidence. The American historian of technology Lynn White Jr. took both stories seriously and argued that they show genuine gliding experiments, while others warn that later writers liked to tell dramatic tales about clever men. The honest position is that both men probably attempted something like a glide, and both probably crashed, but distances and details cannot be verified. The physics behind their 'forgot the tail' explanations is real. A stable aircraft needs its centre of lift and its centre of gravity arranged so that, if the nose pitches up or down, the air pushes it back toward level flight. A tail placed well behind the wing does this, the way the fletching on an arrow keeps the point forward. Without one, a human with wings tends to pitch and tumble, especially near the ground where gusts and turbulence are strongest. Kites are also scientifically important. A kite is a heavier-than-air craft held at an angle of attack, the angle between its surface and the wind, and kept stable by its bridle and tail. Joseph Needham, the great historian of Chinese science, argued that Chinese kites, sky lanterns and the spinning bamboo-copter toy were among the first technologies to use aerodynamic and buoyant forces. When kites became common in Europe, and later when George Cayley experimented with kite-shaped wings and Lawrence Hargrave built box kites, they formed a direct link from ancient Chinese toys to the first airplanes. Finally, the human-muscle problem is about power-to-weight ratio. A fit person can produce only a few hundred watts for long, while their body is heavy. Human-powered aircraft were finally flown in the 1970s, but only with huge, ultra-light wings built from modern materials and pedals driving a propeller, not flapping arms.","funFact":"Abbas ibn Firnas, the scholar who reportedly glided over Córdoba around 1,150 years ago, has a crater on the Moon named after him.","check":{"q":"According to the stories, what did both Abbas ibn Firnas and Eilmer of Malmesbury say they had forgotten to give themselves?","a":"A tail","wrong":["An engine","A parachute","A second pair of wings"]},"sources":[{"title":"Eilmer of Malmesbury","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Eilmer_of_Malmesbury"},{"title":"Abbas ibn Firnas","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Abbas_ibn_Firnas"},{"title":"Icarus","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Icarus"},{"title":"Kite","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Kite"},{"title":"Man-lifting kite","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Man-lifting_kite"},{"title":"Early flying machines","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Early_flying_machines"},{"title":"History of Flight","publisher":"NASA Glenn Research Center","url":"https://www.grc.nasa.gov/WWW/K-12/UEET/StudentSite/historyofflight.html"}],"photos":[{"src":"img/deep/airplane/01-1.jpg","alt":"Landscape with the Fall of Icarus: a ploughman works on while Icarus's legs splash into the sea (bottom right).","file":"Pieter Bruegel the Elder - Landscape with the Fall of Icarus - WGA03321.jpg","url":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Pieter_Bruegel_the_Elder_-_Landscape_with_the_Fall_of_Icarus_-_WGA03321.jpg","author":"Pieter Brueghel the Elder","license":"Public domain"},{"src":"img/deep/airplane/01-2.jpg","alt":"Stained-glass window in Malmesbury Abbey (1920) showing Eilmer, the 'flying monk', with his wings.","file":"Eilmers.flight.arp.jpg","url":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Eilmers.flight.arp.jpg","author":"Arpingstone","license":"Public domain"},{"src":"img/deep/airplane/01-3.jpg","alt":"Both sides of the monument to Abbas ibn Firnas beside the bridge named after him in Córdoba, Spain.","file":"Ibn-firnas-cordoba.png","url":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Ibn-firnas-cordoba.png","author":"Juanmazur","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0"}],"words":4170},{"n":2,"title":"Sketches and Balloons","when":"About 1480 – 1800","summary":"Leonardo da Vinci filled notebooks with drawings of flying machines, but never flew. Then, in 1783, two French paper-makers filled a giant bag with hot air, and a sheep, a duck and a rooster went for a ride. A few weeks later, two men floated over Paris, the first people ever to fly freely!","sections":[{"heading":"The Man Who Watched Birds","paragraphs":["Imagine walking through the streets of Florence or Milan in the late 1400s and passing a man standing completely still, staring up at the sky, scribbling in a small notebook. He watches a kite-bird circle, then a pigeon flap away, then a swift dart past. He sketches the angle of each wing, writes a note, sketches again. That man is Leonardo da Vinci, and he is doing something that almost nobody before him had done so carefully: studying flight like a scientist.","Leonardo, who lived from 1452 to 1519, is famous today as the painter of the Mona Lisa and The Last Supper. But painting was only one part of his life. He was also an engineer, an architect, an anatomist who studied how bodies are built, and a tireless inventor. He filled thousands of notebook pages with drawings and ideas about water, weapons, machines, plants, the human body and, again and again, flight.","Leonardo wrote most of his notes in mirror writing, from right to left, so that you need a mirror to read them easily. He was left-handed, and writing backwards may simply have been more comfortable and less smudgy for him. Whatever the reason, it makes his notebooks look mysterious and has helped make them famous.","From the 1480s onward he made over a hundred drawings about flying. He studied how birds tilt their wings to turn, how they spread their tail feathers to brake, and how they use rising air to soar without flapping. He also studied bats, whose wings are made of a thin skin stretched over long finger bones. Leonardo liked bat wings as a model because, unlike a bird's wing, air cannot leak through gaps between feathers.","In one of his notebooks he wrote that a man with wings that were large enough and properly attached might learn to overcome the resistance of the air and rise up. He also believed that air pushes back against a moving object just as much as the object pushes against the air. That was a sharp observation. More than 150 years later, Isaac Newton would turn this kind of idea into his famous third law of motion: every action has an equal and opposite reaction. It is still one of the keys to understanding how wings work, as you will learn in Part 9."]},{"heading":"Flapping Machines, Air Screws and a Notebook About Birds","paragraphs":["Leonardo's most famous flying-machine drawings are of ornithopters. The word comes from Greek words meaning 'bird' and 'wing', and it means a machine that flies by flapping its wings. In some of his designs, the pilot lies flat on his stomach on a wooden frame. His feet push pedals and his hands pull levers, and through ropes and pulleys these movements make two huge bat-like wings flap up and down. In other designs the pilot stands upright inside the machine like a person climbing a ladder.","These drawings are clever and beautiful, but they share a fatal flaw: they still depend on human muscle to flap the wings. Leonardo himself seems to have realised over the years that a human being is too heavy, and too weak, to fly with flapping wings. In his later notes he paid more attention to gliding, the way large birds ride on the wind with still wings. He sketched a fixed-wing glider design, and modern builders have found that gliders based on his ideas can fly with today's light materials.","One of his most famous sketches is what is often called the 'aerial screw' or 'helical air screw'. It looks like a giant spiral of linen stretched over a frame, sitting on a round platform. The idea was that if the spiral were spun quickly, it would screw itself up into the air, a bit like a screw twisting into wood. People often call it the ancestor of the helicopter. But it would not have worked. Human muscles could not spin it fast enough, and even if they could, the platform would have spun in the opposite direction. Real helicopters solve that problem with a small tail rotor or two rotors spinning opposite ways.","Leonardo also drew a parachute: a pyramid-shaped tent of sealed linen held open by a wooden frame, with a person hanging below. He wrote that with such a device a man could jump from any great height without injury. Was he right? In the year 2000, a British skydiver named Adrian Nicholas built a version from wood, canvas and rope, the kind of materials Leonardo could have used, and floated down with it from a hot-air balloon over South Africa. It worked, though he released it and finished his descent with a modern parachute because the heavy wooden pyramid would have been dangerous to land with.","Here is the important point for historians: there is no reliable evidence that Leonardo ever built and flew any of his flying machines. A popular legend says that he, or one of his pupils, tried to fly from Monte Ceceri, a hill near Florence, around 1505, and crashed. But no notebook or letter confirms it, and many historians think the story was invented later. Leonardo's flying machines lived on paper.","And for a long time, they stayed hidden on paper. After Leonardo's death in 1519, his notebooks were scattered, sold and lost. Some ended up in private collections or royal libraries. His ideas about flight were not widely known until the late 1700s and 1800s, long after other inventors had moved on. So, sadly, his careful studies did not directly help the people who finally learned to fly.","The most complete record of Leonardo's thinking about flight is a small notebook called the Codex on the Flight of Birds. A codex is simply an old handwritten book with pages bound together. This one dates to about 1505 and is surprisingly small: just 18 sheets of paper, each about 21 by 15 centimetres, about the size of a school exercise book.","Inside, Leonardo explains how birds fly, how they balance and how they deal with wind. He noticed something that later aircraft designers had to learn all over again: the point where a bird's weight is centred is not always the same point where the air pushes up on its wings. Getting those two points in the right relationship is what makes a flying thing stable or wobbly. He described how a bird shifts its wings and tail to keep balanced, rather like a tightrope walker adjusting with a pole.","The codex also includes ideas for flying machines, notes on gravity and weight, and even sketches that have nothing to do with flight. In the margin of one page there is a faint drawing in red chalk that some researchers believe could be a self-portrait of Leonardo as a younger man, though not everyone agrees.","Today the Codex on the Flight of Birds is kept at the Royal Library in Turin, Italy. It is so precious that it rarely travels. In 2013, it was lent for 40 days to the Smithsonian's National Air and Space Museum in Washington, D.C., where it was displayed in the same building as the 1903 Wright Flyer, the first successful powered airplane. Visitors could see, in one museum, both an early dream of flight and its fulfilment four hundred years later."]},{"heading":"Borelli Does the Math","paragraphs":["Even after Leonardo, people kept trying to fly by flapping. If a person only had better wings, the reasoning went, they could surely flap their way into the sky. In the 1600s, an Italian scientist finally put numbers on the problem and showed why this was hopeless.","His name was Giovanni Alfonso Borelli. He was born in Naples in 1608 and died in Rome in 1679. Borelli was a mathematician and physicist who became fascinated by how living bodies move. He is often called the father of biomechanics, the science that studies bodies as if they were machines made of levers, ropes and pulleys. He looked at bones as levers and muscles as the ropes that pull them, and he used geometry and mathematics to work out how much force muscles must produce for walking, running, jumping, swimming and flying.","His great book was called De Motu Animalium, which is Latin for On the Movement of Animals. Borelli died before it was printed, and it was published in two volumes, the first in 1680 and the second in 1681. His patron, Queen Christina of Sweden, who was living in Rome, helped pay for it, and the book was dedicated to her.","In the part about flight, Borelli compared birds with humans. A bird's chest muscles, which pull its wings down, are huge for its size and make up a large share of its body weight. A human's chest and arm muscles are tiny in comparison with our heavy bodies. Borelli concluded that human muscles are far too weak to flap wings fast and hard enough to lift a person, no matter how well the wings are made. It was simply not possible for a person to fly like a bird using his own strength.","This was a disappointing conclusion for dreamers, but it was a big step forward. Instead of just trying and crashing, Borelli used measurement and reasoning to say why something would not work. That is how science saves lives and time. His work suggested that if humans were ever going to fly, they would need a completely different approach. Some people decided the answer was not to fly like a bird at all, but to float like a cloud."]},{"heading":"The Paper-Makers of Annonay","paragraphs":["Our story now jumps to the small French town of Annonay, about 70 kilometres south of Lyon, in the 1780s. Here lived the Montgolfier family, who ran a successful paper-making business. Two of the sixteen Montgolfier children would change history: Joseph-Michel, born in 1740, and Jacques-Étienne, born in 1745.","The brothers were very different. Joseph was a dreamer, full of ideas, but not very good at running a business. Étienne had trained as an architect in Paris and was practical and organised; he came home to manage the family paper mill and brought in new paper-making techniques. Together, one dreaming and one building, they made a great team.","How did they get the idea for a balloon? One story says Joseph noticed that clothes drying over a fire billowed and puffed upward. Another says that in November 1782, while he was in the city of Avignon, Joseph sat watching a fire and thinking about a famous fortress, Gibraltar, which the French and Spanish armies were trying to capture. He wondered whether the same force that carried sparks and smoke upward could carry soldiers over the walls.","Joseph made a small box of very thin wood covered with taffeta, a crisp silk fabric, about the size of a small cupboard. He lit some paper underneath it. The box rose up and bumped against the ceiling. He wrote excitedly to Étienne, and the two began building bigger and bigger models. In December 1782 they tested a much larger one, and it floated nearly 3 kilometres before coming down.","The brothers believed the secret was a special gas in the smoke, which they even called 'Montgolfier gas'. So they preferred smoky fires of wet straw and wool. In fact, they were wrong about the smoke. What really lifted their balloons was simply hot air. When air is heated, its tiny particles move faster and spread further apart, so a bag of hot air weighs less than the same-sized bag of cool air around it. The cooler, heavier air pushes underneath and floats the balloon up, the same way water pushes a cork to the surface. They had the right result for the wrong reason, which happens surprisingly often in the history of science.","On 4 June 1783, the brothers gave their first public demonstration in the market square of Annonay, in front of local officials and townspeople. Their balloon was made of sackcloth lined with paper, held about 790 cubic metres of air, and was put together from separate pieces held by about 1,800 buttons. The crowd watched a fire being lit beneath it. The great bag swelled, strained against the ropes, and then, when it was released, rose high into the sky. It stayed up for about 10 minutes and came down roughly 2 kilometres away. Estimates of how high it climbed vary a lot, from about 1,000 to 2,000 metres. News of the flying bag raced to Paris."]},{"heading":"A Sheep, a Duck and a Rooster at Versailles","paragraphs":["Paris was electrified. The Academy of Sciences invited the Montgolfiers to the capital, and King Louis XVI wanted to see a balloon for himself. Étienne went to Paris and teamed up with Jean-Baptiste Réveillon, a successful wallpaper manufacturer. Together they built a splendid balloon of taffeta, coated with alum to make it less likely to catch fire, and painted sky blue with golden decorations. It was a flying work of art.","Nobody knew whether a living creature could survive high up in the air. Would the air be breathable? Would the cold be deadly? It is said that the king suggested sending up two condemned prisoners. Instead the inventors chose animals for the first test with passengers.","On 19 September 1783, in the courtyard of the royal palace of Versailles, an enormous crowd gathered, including King Louis XVI and Queen Marie Antoinette. Into a wicker basket under the balloon went three passengers: a sheep, a duck and a rooster. The sheep was given the name Montauciel, which means 'climb to the sky'. The choice was clever. A sheep was thought to be similar to humans in size and body. A duck was used to flying high, so if it was harmed, the problem was probably the balloon, not the altitude. A rooster was a bird that does not fly high, so it was somewhere in between.","The balloon rose into the sky, drifted for about 8 minutes, travelled roughly 3 kilometres and landed in a wood. It climbed to about 500 metres. When people rushed to the landing site, they found the animals alive. The rooster seemed to have a slightly hurt wing, probably because the sheep had stepped on it or kicked it, not because of the flight. The experiment showed that living creatures could survive a trip into the sky. The way was open for people."]},{"heading":"Two Men Floating Over Paris","paragraphs":["For the first human flights, the Montgolfiers built an even bigger balloon, about 23 metres tall and 15 metres wide, with a gallery, a kind of round balcony, where passengers could stand. At the bottom, in the middle, hung an iron fire basket. Passengers had to keep feeding it straw to keep the air hot.","Before trying a free flight, they did tethered tests, with the balloon tied to the ground by long ropes. In October 1783, Étienne Montgolfier himself rose a short way into the air on a rope from the Réveillon workshop yard in Paris, and so did a young scientist named Jean-François Pilâtre de Rozier, who rose to the end of his tether, about 25 metres up.","Pilâtre de Rozier was a chemistry and physics teacher who had opened his own museum of science in Paris. He was bold and enthusiastic, and he argued that the honour of being the first person to fly should go to someone eager and educated, not to a prisoner. He was joined by an army officer, François Laurent, the Marquis d'Arlandes.","On 21 November 1783, at the Château de la Muette, on the western edge of Paris near the Bois de Boulogne, the great balloon was filled with hot air. In the early afternoon the ropes were released, and for the first time in history human beings rose freely into the sky with nothing holding them to the ground.","Picture the view. Below them spread the rooftops of Paris, the river Seine, church towers and crowds of tiny people looking up. The balloon drifted across the city at perhaps 900 metres high. But it was not a peaceful ride. The two men had to keep feeding the fire. At one point sparks burned holes in the fabric, and they had to put out small fires with wet sponges, and by some accounts with Pilâtre's coat. D'Arlandes later wrote a lively account describing the noises and alarms of the trip.","After about 25 minutes, having travelled roughly 9 kilometres, they came down safely at the Butte-aux-Cailles, which was then just outside the city. They had made the first free flight by human beings in history. The famous American scientist and diplomat Benjamin Franklin, who was living in France at the time, was among those who followed these experiments closely.","It is worth being clear about what this was. It was a real flight, but it was not an airplane. A balloon is lighter than air: it floats because it is less dense than the air around it, like a bubble rising in water. An airplane is heavier than air and must create lift by moving fast through the air. The first true airplane flight was still 120 years in the future."]},{"heading":"Hydrogen Balloons and the English Channel","paragraphs":["At exactly the same time, another French scientist was working on a different kind of balloon. Jacques Charles, a physicist, knew about a gas discovered by the English scientist Henry Cavendish in 1766: hydrogen, which Cavendish had called 'inflammable air'. Hydrogen is the lightest gas there is, much lighter than hot air, so it could lift far more weight with a smaller balloon.","The challenge was to make enough hydrogen and keep it from leaking. Charles worked with two brothers, Anne-Jean and Nicolas-Louis Robert, who had found a way to coat silk with rubber dissolved in a liquid, making it nearly airtight. To produce the hydrogen, they poured sulfuric acid onto scrap iron. This made huge amounts of gas, but it was slow, messy and expensive.","On 27 August 1783, they launched a small unmanned hydrogen balloon from the Champ de Mars in Paris, where the Eiffel Tower stands today. It flew for about 45 minutes and came down about 21 kilometres away near the village of Gonesse. The villagers there had never seen anything like it. Frightened, they attacked the strange object with pitchforks and tore it to pieces. Benjamin Franklin was in the crowd at the launch. According to a well-known story, when someone asked him what use a balloon could possibly be, he replied: 'What is the use of a newborn baby?' He meant that new inventions start small and grow up into something important.","Then, on 1 December 1783, just ten days after the first hot-air flight with people, Jacques Charles and Nicolas-Louis Robert took off from the Tuileries Garden in central Paris in a hydrogen balloon. Reports say a crowd of around 400,000 people came to watch, perhaps half the population of Paris. Charles's balloon had many features that balloons still use today: a net over the top to spread the weight, a basket hanging below, a valve to let gas out to come down, and bags of sand as ballast that could be thrown out to go up. They flew for about 2 hours and travelled about 36 kilometres before landing at Nesles-la-Vallée. Then Charles took off again alone, without Robert's weight, and shot up to about 3,000 metres. His ears hurt and it was bitterly cold, so he let out some hydrogen and came down. He never flew again.","Soon people wanted to fly farther. Jean-Pierre Blanchard, a French balloonist who had once tried to steer balloons with flapping oars and paddles, decided to cross the English Channel, the stretch of sea between England and France. On 7 January 1785, he took off from Dover Castle in England with an American doctor named John Jeffries, who helped pay for the trip. Over the cold grey sea the balloon kept sinking. According to accounts of the flight, the men threw out almost everything to stay up, including their equipment and even some of their clothes. After about two and a half hours, they reached the French coast and came down in a forest near Guînes. They were the first people to cross the Channel by air. A stone column was later put up near the landing place.","Ballooning had dangers too. In June 1785, Pilâtre de Rozier, the first person to fly, tried to cross the Channel from France to England in a new kind of balloon that combined a hot-air balloon with a hydrogen balloon. Shortly after take-off, the balloon came down near Wimereux on the French coast, and both Pilâtre de Rozier and his companion, Pierre Romain, were killed. They are thought to be the first people to lose their lives in an air accident. It was a sad reminder that flying needed great care.","Blanchard went on to make the first balloon flight in North America, on 9 January 1793, from the yard of a prison in Philadelphia. President George Washington was there to watch, and the balloon landed across the river in New Jersey."]},{"heading":"Why Balloons Were Not the Answer","paragraphs":["Within a few years of 1783, balloons were everywhere. They drew huge crowds, inspired fashions, songs and even balloon-shaped furniture. Scientists carried thermometers and barometers aloft to study the atmosphere. During the French Revolution, the French army set up special balloon units to watch enemy soldiers from above, and decades later balloons were used for observation in the American Civil War.","But balloons had one huge problem. A balloon floats inside the air like a cork in a stream, so it goes wherever the wind takes it. A balloonist can go up and down by heating air, letting out gas or dropping ballast, and sometimes find winds blowing in different directions at different heights. But a balloon cannot really be steered against the wind. Blanchard's oars and flappers made almost no difference. Imagine trying to row a hot-air balloon to your school: if the wind blew the other way, you would end up miles off course.","Balloons were also enormous for the little weight they could carry, slow, fragile and at the mercy of storms. Inventors later added engines and propellers to long, cigar-shaped balloons, creating airships, which could be steered. But airships were huge, expensive and slow.","So the old dream remained. Birds do not drift with the wind; they go where they want, even into the wind. To do that, a flying machine would probably have to be heavier than air and hold itself up by moving through the air, like a bird with fixed wings or a kite on a string. Borelli had shown that human muscles could not flap wings. Leonardo's drawings were mostly hidden away. What was needed was someone who could think about flying in a completely new way.","That person was already alive. In 1783, when the first balloons rose over Paris, he was a boy of about ten in England. Around 1799, this English country gentleman took a small silver disc and scratched onto it a picture of a flying machine. It had a fixed wing, a body, a tail and paddles to push it forward. It looked surprisingly like an airplane, more than a hundred years before the first airplane flew. His name was George Cayley, and in Part 3 you will see how he cracked the code of flight."]}],"deepDive":"Balloons work by buoyancy, described by Archimedes' principle: any object in a fluid is pushed upward by a force equal to the weight of the fluid it displaces. Air at about 20 degrees Celsius near sea level has a density of roughly 1.2 kilograms per cubic metre. Heat it to about 100 degrees Celsius and, at the same pressure, its density drops to about 0.95 kilograms per cubic metre, because density is inversely proportional to absolute temperature (293 K versus 373 K). Each cubic metre of hot air therefore gives only about 0.25 kilograms of lift. That is why the 1783 Montgolfier balloons were so enormous: a balloon of 1,700 cubic metres might give a few hundred kilograms of lift, much of it used up by the heavy envelope, gallery, fire basket and fuel. Hydrogen, with a density of only about 0.09 kilograms per cubic metre, gives roughly 1.1 kilograms of lift per cubic metre, about four times as much as hot air. That explains why Charles's 380 cubic metre balloon could carry two people while the Montgolfier balloon carrying two people needed several times the volume. Charles's interest in gases also led to the law that bears his name: at constant pressure, a gas's volume grows in proportion to its absolute temperature. He never published it; Joseph Louis Gay-Lussac did in 1802 and credited Charles's earlier unpublished work. Historians also debate earlier claims. In 1709 the Portuguese-Brazilian priest Bartolomeu de Gusmão demonstrated a small hot-air device to the king of Portugal, and some Portuguese-speaking writers call him the true inventor of the balloon, but evidence is thin and most aviation historians credit the Montgolfiers with the first practical hot-air balloon. In 1670, the Italian Jesuit Francesco Lana de Terzi proposed an airship lifted by four copper spheres emptied of air, but thin spheres would have been crushed by air pressure. As for Leonardo, scholars stress that his flight studies, while brilliant, had almost no influence on later pioneers because his notebooks were unknown for centuries. Borelli's 1680 analysis of muscle power was more influential: it helped persuade later thinkers, including Cayley, to separate the job of lift from the job of propulsion.","funFact":"The first passengers ever carried by a hot-air balloon, in September 1783 at Versailles, were a sheep named Montauciel, a duck and a rooster, and all three landed alive.","check":{"q":"Who were the first people to make a free flight, in a Montgolfier hot-air balloon over Paris on 21 November 1783?","a":"Pilâtre de Rozier and the Marquis d'Arlandes","wrong":["The Montgolfier brothers themselves","Jacques Charles and Nicolas-Louis Robert","Jean-Pierre Blanchard and John Jeffries"]},"sources":[{"title":"Montgolfier brothers","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Montgolfier_brothers"},{"title":"Codex on the Flight of Birds","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Codex_on_the_Flight_of_Birds"},{"title":"Science and inventions of Leonardo da Vinci","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Science_and_inventions_of_Leonardo_da_Vinci"},{"title":"Giovanni Alfonso Borelli","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Giovanni_Alfonso_Borelli"},{"title":"Jacques Charles","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Jacques_Charles"},{"title":"Jean-Pierre Blanchard","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Jean-Pierre_Blanchard"},{"title":"Jean-François Pilâtre de Rozier","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Jean-Fran%C3%A7ois_Pil%C3%A2tre_de_Rozier"},{"title":"History of Flight","publisher":"NASA Glenn Research Center","url":"https://www.grc.nasa.gov/WWW/K-12/UEET/StudentSite/historyofflight.html"}],"photos":[{"src":"img/deep/airplane/02-1.jpg","alt":"Leonardo da Vinci's drawing of a flapping-wing flying machine, or ornithopter, where a person would lie and pump the wings.","file":"Ornithopter Leonardo da Vinci.png","url":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Ornithopter_Leonardo_da_Vinci.png","author":"Leonardo da Vinci","license":"Public domain"},{"src":"img/deep/airplane/02-2.jpg","alt":"1783 engraving: the Montgolfier balloon carrying a sheep, a duck and a rooster rises over a huge crowd at Versailles.","file":"Expérience aërostatique faite à Versailles le 19 septembre 1783.jpg","url":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Exp%C3%A9rience_a%C3%ABrostatique_faite_%C3%A0_Versailles_le_19_septembre_1783.jpg","author":"This image is available from the United States Library of Congress's Prints…","license":"Public domain"},{"src":"img/deep/airplane/02-3.jpg","alt":"Charles and Robert's striped hydrogen balloon lifts off from the Tuileries Garden, Paris, on 1 December 1783.","file":"Globe Aérostatique. De M.rs Charles et Robert, Au moment de leur départ du Jardin des Tuileries. le 1.er X.bre 1783 - estampe - Dessiné par M.r Duperreux fils ; Gravé par L. Boutelou - btv1b69421925.jpg","url":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Globe_A%C3%A9rostatique._De_M.rs_Charles_et_Robert,_Au_moment_de_leur_d%C3%A9part_du_Jardin_des_Tuileries._le_1.er_X.bre_1783_-_estampe_-_Dessin%C3%A9_par_M.r_Duperreux_fils_;_Grav%C3%A9_par_L._Boutelou_-_btv1b69421925.jpg","author":"Boutelou, Louis-Alexandre (1761-17..?). Graveur Duperreux, M.r (fils).…","license":"Public domain"}],"words":4231},{"n":3,"title":"Sir George Cayley Cracks the Code","when":"1799 – 1857","summary":"In a big house in Yorkshire, a curious gentleman named George Cayley stopped trying to copy flapping birds. Instead he drew a plane with a still wing, a body and a tail, and worked out the four forces that every airplane must balance. He even sent a grown man gliding across a valley!","sections":[{"heading":"A Gentleman Who Could Not Stop Asking Why","paragraphs":["Imagine a cold, windy hillside in North Yorkshire, England, a little over two hundred years ago. Sheep are grazing. Rooks are wheeling over the trees. And a well-dressed young man is standing very still, watching the birds with a notebook in his hand, timing how fast they fly and scribbling down numbers. His neighbours probably thought he was a bit odd. Today he is often called the 'father of aeronautics', the person who turned the dream of flying into a real science.","His name was George Cayley. He was born on 27 December 1773 in Scarborough, a seaside town on the Yorkshire coast, into a wealthy family that had lived at the village of Brompton, near Scarborough, for centuries. When his father died, George became Sir George Cayley, the 6th Baronet, and inherited Brompton Hall and the land around it. That meant he never had to work for a living. Many rich gentlemen of the time spent their days hunting, gambling or going to parties. Cayley spent his days inventing.","And he invented a LOT. He designed a self-righting lifeboat that would flip upright again if a wave rolled it over. He worked out the 'tension wheel', a light wheel held together by thin wires pulled tight, which is the same idea used in bicycle wheels today. He sketched an early version of caterpillar tracks, which he called a 'universal railway', so heavy vehicles could cross soft, muddy ground. He suggested automatic signals for railway crossings. He even designed an artificial hand for a man on his estate who had lost his own. He helped found the Royal Polytechnic Institution in London in 1838, a place where ordinary people could see the newest science and machines, and he served for a few years (1832 to 1835) as a Member of Parliament for Scarborough.","But the puzzle he loved most, his whole life long, was flight. Cayley was not the first person to dream of flying, and he was not even the first to experiment with it. What made him special was the WAY he thought about it. Instead of strapping on feathers and jumping off a tower, he asked careful questions. How much does the air push on a flat surface moving at a certain speed? Does a curved surface push differently from a flat one? What angle works best? How heavy can a flying machine be for a given size of wing? He measured, he tested, he wrote things down, and he changed his mind when the numbers told him to. That is the heart of science, and it was exactly what flight had been missing.","Some of his early experiments were small and playful. As a young man, around the 1790s, he built a tiny 'aerial top', a toy helicopter with two little spinning rotors made of feathers, powered by a bent whalebone bow. Similar toys had been shown in France a few years earlier. When the string unwound, the rotors spun in opposite directions and the little toy whirled up toward the ceiling. It was a toy, but it taught Cayley something important: a surface moving through the air at an angle could create an upward push. That push is what we now call lift."]},{"heading":"The Silver Disc of 1799","paragraphs":["In 1799, when he was 25, Cayley did something that sounds tiny but turned out to be huge. He took a small silver disc, only about 2.5 centimetres (1 inch) across, about the size of a large coin, and had a design engraved on it. On one side was a flying machine. On the other was a little diagram of the forces acting on a wing moving through the air. That disc still exists. It is kept in the collection of the Science Museum in London, and it is one of the most important small objects in the whole history of flight.","Why is a picture on a coin-sized piece of silver so important? Look closely at the flying machine on it. There is a fixed wing, a wing that does not flap, held out flat and slightly tilted. Below the wing hangs a boat-shaped car where the pilot would sit. At the back there is a tail, shaped like a cross, to keep the machine steady. The pilot was supposed to push the machine forward with a pair of paddles, sometimes called flappers, a bit like oars.","The paddles were not a good idea, and nobody ever flew that exact machine. But the big idea behind the drawing was brilliant. For thousands of years, dreamers had tried to copy birds by making wings that did everything at once: hold the flyer up AND push it forward by flapping. Cayley split the job in two. The wing would stay still and do only one thing: make lift to hold the machine up. Something else, a separate system, would push the machine forward. And the tail would keep it stable and help control it.","Every airliner you have ever seen follows this same plan. The wings make lift. The engines (jet engines or propellers) make the forward push. The tail keeps the plane steady and helps steer. When you look at a jumbo jet at the airport, you are really looking at a giant, modern version of the little machine scratched onto Cayley's silver disc in 1799.","The other side of the disc mattered just as much. The force diagram showed that when air pushes on a tilted wing, the push can be split into two parts: one part that pushes UP (lift) and one part that pushes BACKWARD (drag). Splitting a force into parts like this is a standard trick in physics. By drawing it, Cayley showed he understood that a wing is not just something that floats. It is something that turns the push of moving air into an upward force, and it always pays a price in backward drag."]},{"heading":"Meet the Four Forces","paragraphs":["Cayley's most famous gift to the world is a simple way of thinking about any flying machine. He realised that four main forces act on an airplane. You will meet them again in much more detail in Part 9, but here is a quick tour, because once you know them, flight stops being magic and starts being a puzzle you can understand.","Force number one is WEIGHT. This is the pull of gravity, dragging the machine down toward the centre of the Earth. Everything has weight: the frame, the wings, the engine, the fuel, the pilot, the passengers and their luggage. The heavier the machine, the harder the other forces have to work.","Force number two is LIFT. This is the upward push made by the wings as they move through the air. Lift fights against weight. If lift is bigger than weight, the plane climbs. If lift is smaller, the plane sinks. If they are equal, the plane stays at the same height. A wing only makes lift while air is flowing past it, which is why a plane has to keep moving forward to stay up.","Force number three is DRAG. This is air resistance, the backward push of the air against anything moving through it. You feel drag when you cycle fast into the wind or stick your hand out of a moving car (always with an adult, and only if it is safe). Drag tries to slow the plane down. A smooth, streamlined shape has less drag than a bumpy, boxy one.","Force number four is THRUST. This is the forward push that keeps the plane moving. Today it comes from propellers or jet engines. Thrust fights against drag. NASA's beginner's guide to aeronautics puts it neatly: the engine's job is to overcome drag; it is the wings that make the lift.","So you have two pairs of opponents, like two tug-of-war games happening at once. Lift pulls up against weight. Thrust pulls forward against drag. When each pair is perfectly balanced, the plane cruises along at a steady speed and height. When one force wins, the plane speeds up, slows down, climbs or descends. Cayley described these ideas in a long paper called 'On Aerial Navigation', published in three parts in 1809 and 1810, and he summed up the whole challenge of flight in one famous sentence. In his own words, the problem was 'to make a surface support a given weight by the application of power to the resistance of air'. In other words: use an engine to push a wing through the air fast enough that the air holds the weight up.","That one sentence sounds simple, but it was a revolution. It told every future inventor exactly what they had to build: a good wing, a light structure, and a powerful engine. It also explained why flapping arms would never work. As Giovanni Borelli had shown more than a century earlier, human muscles are far too weak to provide that kind of power."]},{"heading":"The Whirling Arm and the First Modern Model Plane","paragraphs":["Knowing the four forces is one thing. Measuring them is another. How do you find out how much lift a wing makes when there are no wind tunnels, no electronic sensors and no airplanes to test on? Cayley's answer was a machine called a whirling arm.","Picture a tall post with a long horizontal arm on top, able to spin around like the hand of a giant clock lying on its side. At the end of the arm, Cayley fixed a small test surface, a flat or slightly curved plate, about the size of a large book. A weight hanging on a cord was allowed to fall, and as it fell it pulled the cord, which spun the arm around. The test surface swept through the air in a big circle, as if it were flying. By changing the weight, Cayley could make the arm spin faster or slower. By tilting the test surface at different angles, he could see how the push of the air changed.","Whirling arms were not Cayley's invention. The English scientists Benjamin Robins and John Smeaton had used them in the 1700s to study air resistance and windmill sails. But Cayley pointed his whirling arm straight at the problem of flight. Using it, he measured how the force on a surface grew with speed and with the angle at which it met the air, what we now call the angle of attack. He found that even a small angle could produce a useful amount of lift, and that the drag grew as the angle got steeper. Whirling arms had one big weakness: after a few turns, the arm stirred the air around into a swirl, so the test surface was no longer meeting still air. Later inventors would solve that problem with wind tunnels, as you will see in Part 7. But for its time, the whirling arm gave Cayley real numbers instead of guesses.","Then, in 1804, Cayley put his ideas together into an actual flying object. It was a model glider, small enough to hold in your hands, and it looked wonderfully homemade. The wing was an ordinary paper kite, fixed to a wooden pole about a metre long at a small upward tilt. At the back of the pole was a tail shaped like a cross, which could be adjusted. A movable weight on the front of the pole let him shift the balance point forward or backward.","When Cayley launched it down a hillside, it glided. Not flapping, not floating like a balloon, but gliding, sliding smoothly forward and gently down through the air, held up by its wing, kept straight by its tail. It is often called the first true model airplane with a modern layout: a main wing in front, a tail at the back, and a body joining them. Many paper airplanes you fold today have the same basic plan.","Cayley was delighted. He wrote that it was very pretty to watch it sail down a steep hill, and he noticed that small changes to the tail or the balance weight changed how it flew. He was learning something that would become central to aviation: a flying machine must be STABLE. If a gust tips the nose up, the machine should tend to return to level flight by itself instead of flipping over. Getting the balance point in the right place, and giving the tail the right size and angle, made the difference between a smooth glide and a crash."]},{"heading":"Curved Wings, Tilted Wings and Fish-Shaped Bodies","paragraphs":["'On Aerial Navigation', the paper Cayley published in 1809 and 1810, was full of ideas that were far ahead of their time. It appeared in a science magazine called Nicholson's Journal of Natural Philosophy, Chemistry and the Arts, and it is one of the founding documents of aeronautics. Here are some of the clever things it contained.","First, Cayley noticed that a curved wing worked better than a flat one. Birds' wings are slightly arched, higher in the middle than at the front and back edges. Cayley saw that this curve, now called camber, helped a wing make more lift. Almost every airplane wing since then has had some kind of curved shape, called an airfoil.","Second, he worked out a trick for keeping a flying machine from rolling over sideways. If you look at many airplanes from the front, you will see that the wings are not perfectly flat. They tilt upward slightly from the body to the tips, making a very shallow V. This is called dihedral. Cayley explained that if a gust tips one wing down, the V shape helps the machine level itself again. He had spotted this by watching birds and by experimenting with his models. Look at a passenger jet head-on the next time you are at an airport and you will see Cayley's V.","Third, Cayley thought hard about drag. He knew that a machine with a blunt, boxy shape would waste lots of power pushing air out of the way. So he looked at nature again, this time at fish. He studied the shape of a trout and suggested that a body shaped like a fish or a bird, rounded at the front and tapering to a point at the back, would slip through the air (or water) with less resistance. This is the idea of streamlining, and it is used today in everything from airplanes to racing cars and bullet trains.","Fourth, he wrote about the size of the problem. He calculated roughly how big a wing would need to be to hold up a person, and how much power would be needed to push it through the air. His numbers showed clearly that a person flapping their arms could never do it, but that a light enough engine might. He also understood the danger of trying to fly too soon. He believed in testing small models first, then larger ones, then carefully letting a person go up: a slow, step-by-step approach that later pioneers like Otto Lilienthal and the Wright brothers would also follow.","Not everything Cayley thought of was correct, and not everything he designed was practical. In 1843 he published a design for an 'Aerial Carriage' with four round rotors, a little like a cross between a helicopter and an airplane. It was never built at full size, and it would not have worked with the engines of the day. But even his wilder ideas show how his mind worked: always trying combinations, always asking 'what if?'"]},{"heading":"Flying Boys, a Nervous Coachman and Brompton Dale","paragraphs":["For many years Cayley was busy with his estate, his family, his other inventions and his work in Parliament. But in the 1840s, when most people would be thinking of slowing down, he returned to flying with fresh energy. He was now in his seventies, and he wanted to see a real person carried through the air by one of his machines.","Before 1849 he built a glider with several wings stacked one above another, a little like a very early biplane or triplane. Multiple wings were a clever trick: they gave more lifting area without making the machine too wide and floppy. According to Cayley's own notes, this glider carried a boy of about ten years old a short distance down a hillside, lifting him off the ground for a few metres. We do not know the boy's name. He was probably the son of one of the servants or workers at Brompton. It must have been both exciting and terrifying for him.","In 1852 Cayley wrote about a larger design that he called a 'governable parachute', meaning a glider that could be steered. The next year, in 1853, he built a full-size glider big enough to carry a grown man. Its exact shape is not fully known, but it had a large main wing, a tail, and a little boat-like seat for the pilot. Cayley was about 79 years old.","The test took place at Brompton Dale, a small valley near his home, in front of a house called Wydale Hall. The glider was launched from one side of the dale. It swooped down and across the valley and came to earth on the other side. It was a short flight, probably a few hundred metres at most, and the pilot had very little real control. But for a brief moment, a grown man had been carried through the air by a fixed-wing flying machine. Many historians count this as the first glider flight to carry an adult.","Who was the pilot? Here history gets fuzzy. The traditional story says it was Cayley's coachman, the servant who drove his horse-drawn carriage. Other accounts say it was a footman or a butler, and one writer has named a man called John Appleby. A 2007 biography suggested it may actually have been Cayley's grandson, George John Cayley. The honest answer is that nobody knows for sure.","There is also a famous story about what happened afterwards, and it should be treated as a story rather than proven history. According to a family tale written down many years later, the shaken coachman climbed out of the wreckage and said something like: 'Please, Sir George, I wish to give notice. I was hired to drive, not to fly.' In other words, he quit on the spot! It is a funny line, and it may even be true, but there is no record made at the time to prove it.","Long after Cayley's death, people wondered whether his glider design could really fly. Modern replicas based on his drawings have been built and flown, including on the same Yorkshire hillside, and they showed that his design really could glide. Today the sign at the edge of Brompton village proudly calls it 'The Birthplace of Aviation'."]},{"heading":"The Missing Piece: An Engine","paragraphs":["So if Cayley understood wings, tails, stability, streamlining and the four forces, why did he not build an airplane that took off by itself and flew under power? The answer is simple and frustrating. He had no engine that was light enough and strong enough.","In Cayley's lifetime, the most powerful engines in the world were steam engines. They were wonderful machines for pumping water out of mines, driving factory looms and pulling railway trains. But they were extremely heavy. A steam engine needs a boiler full of water, a firebox burning coal or wood, thick metal walls to hold the steam pressure, and a supply of fuel and water to keep going. For a train running on rails, all that weight did not matter much. For a flying machine, it was a disaster. Every extra kilogram of engine needed extra wing to hold it up, and extra wing meant extra weight and drag, which needed a bigger engine, which meant more weight... a vicious circle.","Engineers talk about the power-to-weight ratio: how much power an engine gives for each kilogram it weighs. Steam engines of the early 1800s had a very poor power-to-weight ratio. Cayley knew this. He calculated that a flying machine would need an engine far lighter, for its power, than anything that existed.","So he tried to invent one. Around 1807 he built a hot-air engine, which used the expansion of heated air instead of steam to push a piston. The Institution of Mechanical Engineers credits him with making the first successfully working hot-air engine. He also experimented with the idea of an engine powered by small explosions of gunpowder, a dangerous but interesting idea that hinted at the later internal combustion engine, in which fuel burns inside the cylinder. None of these engines was good enough to lift a flying machine.","Cayley died at Brompton on 15 December 1857, at the age of 83, and was buried in the churchyard of All Saints' Church in the village. He never saw a powered airplane fly. But he had done something even more important: he had written the instruction manual. He had shown what an airplane should look like, what forces it must balance and how to test ideas safely. Decades later, Wilbur Wright himself praised Cayley's work, saying that he had carried the science of flight to a point it had never reached before.","Cayley's paper ended with a challenge for the future. He believed that one day people would travel through the air faster and more safely than by land or sea, once a light enough engine was found. Inventors read his work and accepted the challenge. And since steam engines were the most powerful machines anyone had, many of them decided to try the obvious thing: build a flying machine and bolt a steam engine onto it. Their heavy, hissing, puffing contraptions are the story of Part 4."]}],"deepDive":"Why do historians call Cayley the father of aeronautics when he never flew a powered plane? The answer lies in the difference between building a machine and defining a problem. Before Cayley, almost every serious attempt at flight was an ornithopter, a machine meant to flap like a bird, so that one surface had to provide both lift and propulsion. Cayley's 1799 disc and his 1809–1810 paper separated these functions, and that separation is the conceptual foundation of every fixed-wing aircraft since. In modern terms, he resolved the aerodynamic force on a wing into components perpendicular (lift) and parallel (drag) to the airflow, recognised that thrust exists to balance drag rather than to hold the craft up, and grasped that lift depends on speed, area and angle of attack. He also identified camber, dihedral for roll stability, a rear tail for pitch stability and streamlining for low drag. Historians do debate some details. Cayley's whirling-arm numbers were rough, because the arm dragged the surrounding air into a swirl and because his surfaces were small and moved slowly, so his measurements could not be scaled up with confidence; later researchers, including the Wright brothers, found that many published lift figures were unreliable. The 1849 and 1853 flights are known mainly from Cayley's notes and family recollections, so the pilot's identity, the exact distance and even the precise design of the 1853 glider remain uncertain, and the coachman's famous resignation line is best treated as family tradition. Another question is influence: Cayley published in scientific journals, but much of his material sat in notebooks that were only studied in detail in the 20th century, so later inventors sometimes rediscovered his ideas independently. Still, his 1809–1810 paper was widely known, and the Aeronautical Society of Great Britain, founded in 1866, built directly on his approach. The key lesson for teens and adults is that his real breakthrough was method: measure forces, test models, value stability, and recognise power-to-weight ratio as the limiting factor. The airplane had to wait about 50 years after his death for the petrol engine, but the design brief was already written.","funFact":"Cayley's world-changing 1799 airplane design was engraved on a silver disc only about 2.5 centimetres (1 inch) across, which is kept today in the Science Museum in London.","check":{"q":"What big idea did Cayley show on his 1799 silver disc?","a":"Separating lift (a fixed wing) from thrust, with a body and a tail","wrong":["A flapping wing that both lifts and pushes","A hot-air balloon with a steering sail","A jet engine for airplanes"]},"sources":[{"title":"George Cayley","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/George_Cayley"},{"title":"Small silver disc engraved with an early design of Sir George Cayley","publisher":"Science Museum Group Collection","url":"https://collection.sciencemuseumgroup.org.uk/objects/co29509/small-silver-disc-engraved-with-an-early-design-of-sir-george-cayley"},{"title":"Replica of Aerial Top used by Sir George Cayley","publisher":"Science Museum Group Collection","url":"https://collection.sciencemuseumgroup.org.uk/objects/co29483/replica-of-aerial-top-used-by-sir-george-cayley"},{"title":"Four Forces on an Airplane","publisher":"NASA Glenn Research Center","url":"https://www1.grc.nasa.gov/beginners-guide-to-aeronautics/four-forces-on-an-airplane/"},{"title":"History of aviation","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/History_of_aviation"},{"title":"Brompton-by-Sawdon","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Brompton-by-Sawdon"}],"photos":[{"src":"img/deep/airplane/03-1.jpg","alt":"Cayley's 1799 silver disc: a fixed-wing flying machine on one side, a diagram of the forces on a wing on the other.","file":"Cayley's 1799 Silver Disk.jpg","url":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Cayley%27s_1799_Silver_Disk.jpg","author":"Sir George Cayley","license":"Public domain"},{"src":"img/deep/airplane/03-2.jpg","alt":"Portrait of Sir George Cayley, the English 'father of aeronautics', painted by Henry Perronet Briggs.","file":"George Cayley.jpg","url":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:George_Cayley.jpg","author":"Henry Perronet Briggs","license":"Public domain"},{"src":"img/deep/airplane/03-3.jpg","alt":"A replica of Cayley's man-carrying glider, with its boat-shaped seat and wheels, at the Yorkshire Air Museum.","file":"George Cayley Glider (Nigel Coates).jpg","url":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:George_Cayley_Glider_(Nigel_Coates).jpg","author":"Nigel Coates (talk)","license":"CC BY-SA 3.0"}],"words":3939},{"n":4,"title":"Steam Engines and Hopping Machines","when":"1842 – 1903","summary":"After Cayley, inventors tried to make airplanes fly with big, hot, puffing steam engines. Some machines hopped a little way off the ground, one giant machine broke its own rails, and a model plane flew nearly a kilometre over a river. But nobody could steer them yet!","sections":[{"heading":"The Airliner That Was Only a Picture","paragraphs":["Imagine opening a newspaper in London in 1843 and seeing a picture of a huge, graceful flying machine soaring over the pyramids of Egypt. Its long, straight wings stretch out like a giant seagull's. Two propellers spin behind the wing. A tidy cabin for passengers hangs underneath. The caption promises that soon you will be able to fly to faraway lands in comfort. It looks amazingly like a modern airplane. There is only one problem. It does not exist.","This was the 'Aerial Steam Carriage', nicknamed the Ariel. It was the dream of two men from the small town of Chard in Somerset, in the south-west of England. William Samuel Henson, born in 1812, was an engineer who worked in the local lace-making industry. His friend John Stringfellow, born in 1799 in Sheffield, had also come to Chard to work in lace-making, building the delicate bobbins and carriages used in lace machines. Lace machines needed precise, light, cleverly made parts, which turned out to be excellent training for building flying machines.","Henson and Stringfellow had studied Sir George Cayley's ideas, and their design followed his plan closely: a fixed wing for lift, propellers for thrust, and a tail for stability and steering. Henson received a British patent for it, number 9478, in 1842. The design was enormous for its time. It was to have a wingspan of about 46 metres (150 feet), wider than the wings of many modern airliners, and it was meant to weigh about 1,400 kilograms (3,000 pounds). A specially designed lightweight steam engine of about 50 horsepower was supposed to drive the two propellers. It had a three-wheeled undercarriage, like a modern plane, and it was meant to take off by rolling down a sloping ramp. Henson and Stringfellow hoped it would fly at about 80 km/h (50 mph) and carry 10 to 12 passengers as far as 1,600 kilometres (1,000 miles).","In 1843 the two inventors, along with two business partners named Frederick Marriott and D. E. Colombine, set up the Aerial Transit Company. Its plan, according to the patent, was to carry 'letters, goods and passengers from place to place through the air'. To attract money from investors and support from Parliament, the company launched one of the first big advertising campaigns in aviation history. Artists produced beautiful prints of the Ariel flying over London, over the Nile, over India and over China. The pictures were copied in newspapers and magazines and sold as posters. People talked about the Ariel everywhere.","Not everyone was impressed. Some newspapers laughed at the idea or suggested the whole thing was a hoax or a trick to get money. Investors stayed away. And the hard truth was that the Ariel could never have flown. A 50-horsepower steam engine, with its boiler, water and fuel, was nowhere near powerful enough for a machine that large and heavy. The full-size Ariel was never built.","Still, the Ariel was important. It was the first detailed design for a propeller-driven airplane with a fixed wing, and it showed the world what an airliner might one day look like. Its pictures spread the idea of airplanes far and wide, even though the real machine stayed on paper. Many years later, when London's main airport opened its first purpose-built hotel, it was named 'The Ariel' in honour of the dream from Chard."]},{"heading":"Thirty Yards in a Lace Factory","paragraphs":["Henson and Stringfellow did not just draw pictures. Between 1844 and 1847 they built and tested models, including one with a wingspan of about 6 metres (20 feet), driven by small steam engines. The tests were disappointing. The models would not fly on their own; at best one made a tentative hop off the wire that guided it. Henson eventually lost heart, married, and later emigrated to the United States, leaving aviation behind.","Stringfellow kept going. In 1848 he built a smaller model, a monoplane with a wingspan of about 3 metres (10 feet), powered by a tiny steam engine he had made himself. He tested it inside a disused lace factory in Chard, where the long, empty rooms gave him space and kept the wind out. On the first attempt the model ran along a guide wire, then flew about 3 metres before it became unstable and crashed, damaging itself.","Stringfellow repaired it and tried again. This time the model left its guide wire and flew freely. According to the accounts, it flew for about 30 yards (roughly 27 metres) in straight, level, powered flight. Thirty yards is not very far, about the length of three buses. But many historians count it as one of the first times an engine-driven, heavier-than-air model flew under its own power, rather than simply gliding downward.","It is worth thinking about why this tiny model could fly when the giant Ariel could not. There is a simple rule of size: when you make something bigger, its weight grows much faster than the area of its wings. A model that is twice as long, twice as wide and twice as tall weighs about eight times as much, but has only about four times as much wing area. So small models can fly with weak engines, while full-size machines need far more power for every kilogram. This 'square-cube' problem would trip up inventors again and again.","Stringfellow never stopped tinkering. In 1866 a group of enthusiasts founded the Aeronautical Society of Great Britain, one of the first organisations in the world devoted to the science of flight. Two years later, in 1868, the society held the world's first aeronautical exhibition, at the Crystal Palace, a giant glass-and-iron exhibition hall in London. Stringfellow, now almost 70, showed a model with three wings stacked one above another, a triplane, and won a prize of 100 pounds for a small steam engine with the best power for its weight. That prize sums up the whole problem of the time: everyone knew the challenge was to make an engine light enough for its power. John Stringfellow died in Chard in 1883, and the town's museum still remembers him as a pioneer of powered flight."]},{"heading":"Downhill Hops in France and Russia","paragraphs":["Models were one thing, but could a steam-powered machine carry a person? Two naval officers, one in France and one in Russia, decided to find out. Sailors knew a lot about wind, engines and how to build strong, light structures, so perhaps it is not surprising that they joined the race.","Félix du Temple de la Croix was a French naval officer born in 1823. With his brother Louis, he began by building large model airplanes in the 1850s. One model, said to weigh around 700 grams, was first powered by clockwork and later by a tiny steam engine. Both versions took off under their own power, flew a short distance and landed safely. In 1857 du Temple patented a design for a full-size monoplane with a tail and even a retractable undercarriage, wheels that could be folded away in flight, an idea far ahead of its time.","The full-size machine was built at Brest, a naval port in north-west France. It had a wingspan of about 13 metres and was made partly of aluminium, which was then a rare and expensive metal. Without its pilot, it weighed about 80 kilograms. Its engine used a clever steam boiler that du Temple designed himself, made of many thin tubes so it could heat water quickly while staying light. Later, the great American engineer Octave Chanute praised it as a very light steam boiler for its power.","Around 1874, with a person aboard, the machine was sent racing down a sloping ramp like a ski jump. It left the ground, travelled through the air for a very short distance, and came back down safely. It was not a real flight. The machine was not climbing on its own power, and the pilot could not steer it. But it is generally seen as the first time a full-size, engine-powered airplane carrying a person left the ground at all, a brief, powered hop.","Ten years later, in Russia, Alexander Mozhaysky tried something similar. Mozhaysky, born in 1825, was a Russian naval officer who had also built models and studied kites. In 1876 he began work on a full-size steam-powered monoplane. It had two small steam engines, which he had bought in England, driving three propellers. Around 1884, at a military field near St Petersburg, it was launched down a ramp with a mechanic aboard as pilot. It is said to have left the ground briefly before coming down, and one wing was damaged.","Decades later, during the Soviet era, some Russian books claimed that Mozhaysky had made the world's first powered flight. Russia's main aeronautics research institute, TsAGI, studied the claim carefully between 1971 and 1981. Its experts concluded that Mozhaysky's engines were much too weak to keep the plane flying. At best it made a short hop helped by the ramp. Even so, Mozhaysky deserves credit as a serious engineer whose ideas about propellers and controls were impressive for his time."]},{"heading":"Clément Ader and the Bat-Winged Éole","paragraphs":["Clément Ader was one of the most inventive engineers in France. Born in 1841, he first became famous not for flying but for sound. In 1878 he improved on Alexander Graham Bell's new telephone, and in 1880 he helped set up the telephone network in Paris. In 1881 he invented the 'théâtrophone', a system that sent live opera from the Paris Opera house through telephone lines to listeners about 3 kilometres away, with separate microphones for the left and right ear. It was the first stereo sound transmission in history.","But Ader's real passion was flight, and his inspiration came from a surprising animal: the bat. Bats are expert flyers, and their wings are made of thin skin stretched over long finger bones, which makes them light and flexible. Ader studied bats closely, and he designed a flying machine that looked like a giant one. He called it the Éole, after Aeolus, the god of the winds in Greek mythology.","The Éole had curved, bat-shaped wings about 14 metres across, made of silk stretched over a frame of light wood. At the front was a four-bladed propeller that looked a bit like the feathers of a huge bird. To power it, Ader built his own steam engine, and it was a little marvel: four cylinders, about 20 horsepower, and a weight of only about 51 kilograms. That was far lighter for its power than most steam engines of its day. With the pilot aboard, the whole machine weighed about 300 kilograms.","On 9 October 1890, on the grounds of a country estate at Armainvilliers, east of Paris, Ader climbed into the Éole and opened the steam valve. The propeller whirred, the machine rolled forward over flat ground, and then, according to the accepted account, it lifted off and travelled about 50 metres through the air at a height of roughly 20 centimetres, about the height of a shoebox, before settling back down. Only a few people were there to see it.","Historians give Ader real credit: the Éole was probably the first full-size, piloted airplane to leave flat ground under its own engine power, without a ramp or a downhill slope. But it was not a controlled flight. The Éole had no proper way for the pilot to steer it up and down or bank it from side to side. It stayed so close to the ground that it was probably being helped by 'ground effect', a cushion of air squeezed between a wing and the ground that gives extra lift when a wing flies very low. It could not climb higher, turn, or keep going.","Ader went on to build bigger machines for the French Army. His Avion III, with two steam engines of about 30 horsepower each and a wingspan of about 15 metres, was tested at Satory, near Versailles, in October 1897. On 14 October, a gust of wind pushed it off the circular track it was running on and damaged it. It did not fly. The army stopped paying for his work, and when its official reports were finally made public in 1910, they described the trials as unsuccessful. By then, Ader had begun claiming that he had made a long flight in 1897, but the evidence did not support him.","Ader did leave one lasting gift. He called his machines 'avions', from the Latin word 'avis', meaning bird. The name stuck. To this day, the ordinary French word for an airplane is 'avion', and people all over the French-speaking world say it every time they talk about flying."]},{"heading":"Hiram Maxim's Giant on Rails","paragraphs":["If anyone could solve the problem with sheer power, it should have been Hiram Maxim. Born in 1840 in Sangerville, Maine, in the United States, Maxim was a self-taught inventor who later moved to England. He is best known, sadly, for inventing the first fully automatic machine gun, the Maxim gun. He was rich, determined and confident, and in the early 1890s he decided to build a flying machine on a scale nobody had dared before.","At Baldwyn's Park, his home at Bexley in Kent, just south-east of London, he laid down a straight railway track about 550 metres (1,800 feet) long. On it he built an enormous test machine. It was about 12 metres long, with wings spanning over 30 metres, larger than the wings of many modern passenger jets. It is said to have had room for a crew of three. Two huge propellers, each about 5 metres (17 feet) across and made of layers of pine wood, were driven by two lightweight steam engines, often said to give about 360 horsepower together, burning naphtha, a light petroleum fuel, to heat the boilers quickly. With everything aboard, the machine weighed about 3.5 tonnes.","Maxim was a careful man, and he did not want the giant to fly away out of control. So it was not designed to fly freely. Instead, it ran on its rails, and four extra wheels on outrigger arms ran under a second set of wooden guard rails a few metres outside the main track. If the machine rose more than a few centimetres, these wheels would press up against the guard rails and hold it down. It was a test rig, a way to measure whether the wings could produce enough lift to raise such a heavy machine.","In 1894, on a run usually dated to 31 July, Maxim opened up the engines. The great propellers roared, steam hissed, and the machine charged down the track faster and faster. Then the wings did their job almost too well. The lift was so strong that the machine rose off its main rails and strained upward against the guard rails, until it tore part of them away. For a brief moment it was free in the air, perhaps a metre or less above the ground, before Maxim shut off the steam and it crashed back down, badly damaged.","Maxim had proven one important thing: wings really could lift a machine weighing several tonnes if you pushed them fast enough. But he had also shown how far there still was to go. His machine had no real way to be balanced or steered in free flight. And his steam plant, although astonishingly light for a steam engine, was still a heavy, complicated beast. Maxim concluded that a practical flying machine would need a lighter type of engine, such as one burning petrol.","He gave up the project, but he did not give up on flight entirely. He used what he had learned to design fairground rides in which passengers sat in little cars that swung outward as they spun around a tall tower, giving them a taste of flying. One of these 'Captive Flying Machines' opened at Blackpool Pleasure Beach in 1904, and visitors have been riding it for more than a century."]},{"heading":"Lawrence Hargrave's Box Kites","paragraphs":["While engineers in Europe and America were building huge, heavy machines, a quiet inventor on the other side of the world was taking a very different approach. Lawrence Hargrave was born in England in 1850 and moved to Australia as a teenager. He worked as an engineer and astronomer's assistant in Sydney, then gave up his job to spend his time studying flight.","Hargrave experimented with many things, including flapping models, rubber-powered models, and small engines powered by compressed air. He also worked on rotary engines, in which the cylinders spin around a fixed crankshaft; this type of engine later powered many early airplanes, although his own versions never became powerful enough for a full flying machine. But his most famous invention was much simpler. In 1893 he invented the box kite.","A box kite looks like two open-ended boxes joined by sticks, one at each end. The flat top and bottom surfaces of each box act like wings, stacked one above the other, while the side panels help keep the kite steady. Compared with ordinary flat kites, box kites made more lift for their size, produced less drag, and were remarkably stable. They did not need a tail and flew calmly even in strong winds.","On 12 November 1894, on the beach at Stanwell Park, south of Sydney, Hargrave tied four of his box kites together in a line, added a simple sling seat, and anchored the kite string through a spring balance to two heavy sandbags. With the help of a caretaker named James Swain, he let the kites rise in a wind of about 34 km/h (21 mph). Up went the kites, and up went Hargrave with them, about 4.9 metres (16 feet) into the air. He had been lifted off the ground by fixed wings with no gas, no hot air and no engine, just wind.","Hargrave's box-kite design proved that stacked, stable wings could carry a person, and it strongly influenced the next generation of builders. The box kite became one of the main ideas behind the biplane, an airplane with two wings stacked one above the other. Several early European airplanes of the 1900s looked very much like big box kites with engines attached. His work also inspired Alexander Graham Bell, who went on to experiment with giant kites built from many small triangle-shaped cells.","Hargrave also did something unusual: he refused to patent any of his inventions. He believed that ideas should be shared freely so that science could move faster, and he thought patent fees were a waste of money. 'Excellence of design and workmanship will always defy competition,' he wrote. Australia did not forget him. From 1966 to 1994, his portrait and drawings of his flying machines appeared on the back of the Australian 20-dollar note."]},{"heading":"Langley's Aerodromes Over the Potomac","paragraphs":["In the United States, one of the most respected scientists in the country was also working on the flying problem. Samuel Pierpont Langley was born in 1834 in Roxbury, Massachusetts. He became a famous astronomer, the first director of the Allegheny Observatory in Pittsburgh, where he studied the Sun. In 1887 he was made Secretary of the Smithsonian Institution in Washington, D.C., which meant he was in charge of the nation's leading science museum and research organisation.","Langley began his flight research the scientific way. In 1887 he started building small rubber-band-powered models and gliders. At Allegheny he built a large whirling arm, much bigger and faster than Cayley's, to measure the forces on test surfaces at different speeds and angles. In 1891 he published his results in a book called 'Experiments in Aerodynamics'. He was convinced that powered flight was possible.","Then he began building models driven by tiny steam engines. He called them 'aerodromes', a word he made up from Greek roots meaning something like 'air runner'. His models had two pairs of wings, one behind the other (a design called tandem wings), a tail, and propellers driven by a lightweight steam engine of about one horsepower. Several early versions failed or broke, and his team spent years rebuilding and improving them.","Because the models could not take off from the ground, Langley launched them from a catapult on top of a houseboat floating on the Potomac River near Quantico, Virginia, about 50 kilometres south of Washington. Launching over water had an extra advantage: when a model came down, it landed on the river instead of smashing into the ground.","On 6 May 1896, Aerodrome No. 5 was flung off the catapult. It climbed, circled gently, and flew for about 90 seconds at about 40 km/h (25 mph), reaching heights of perhaps 25 to 30 metres before its fuel and water ran out and it settled softly onto the water. It was fished out, dried, and flown again the same day. Its two flights that day covered roughly 1,000 metres and 700 metres. Langley's friend Alexander Graham Bell, the inventor of the telephone, photographed the flight. Later that year, in November 1896, the slightly different Aerodrome No. 6 flew about 1.5 kilometres.","These were the first sustained flights by engine-powered, heavier-than-air machines of a decent size. They caused a sensation. But notice two things. First, there was nobody aboard. Second, nobody was steering. The models were carefully balanced so that they would fly steadily by themselves in calm air, but they simply went wherever the air and their own trim took them. A few years later, in 1898, the US War Department gave Langley 50,000 dollars to build a full-size Aerodrome that could carry a pilot. What happened to that machine in 1903 is a dramatic story that you will read in Part 8."]},{"heading":"Power Was Not Enough","paragraphs":["Let's step back and look at what sixty years of steam-powered experiments had achieved. Henson and Stringfellow designed a beautiful airliner that could never fly, and a small model that flew about 30 yards. Du Temple and Mozhaysky made short hops after racing down ramps. Ader's Éole skimmed about 50 metres just above the ground. Maxim's 3.5-tonne giant tore itself loose from its rails for a moment. Hargrave hung from kites on a beach. Langley's unmanned models flew about a kilometre over a river.","That is a lot of progress. These inventors proved that wings could lift heavy machines, that engines could drive propellers, and that a well-balanced model could fly steadily by itself. They designed lighter engines, better propellers and stronger structures. And they inspired thousands of people to believe that flight was coming.","But there was one thing none of them had solved. Not one of their machines could be properly CONTROLLED by a pilot in the air. Think about riding a bicycle. Having strong legs to push the pedals is not enough; you must also balance, lean into turns and steer, constantly making small corrections without even thinking about it. A flying machine is like a bicycle that can also tip forward and backward and slide sideways, all at once, in moving air full of gusts. Most of the steam inventors had concentrated on lifting and pushing. They hoped that once a machine was in the air, it would simply stay steady, or that they could work out the steering later.","There was also the engine problem. Steam engines, even the cleverest ones made by Ader, Maxim and Langley, were heavy, slow to start and needed water and fuel for their boilers. A new kind of engine, the petrol-burning internal combustion engine, was being developed for the first motor cars in the 1880s and 1890s. It would eventually be the key to powered flight, but it was not yet light and reliable enough.","So the next step did not come from a bigger engine at all. It came from someone who decided to forget about engines for a while and learn to fly like a bird, one careful glide at a time. In Germany, an engineer who had watched storks since he was a boy was building wings of cotton and willow and running down hills into the wind. His name was Otto Lilienthal, and he is the hero of Part 5."]}],"deepDive":"Why did steam fail as an aero engine, and how do historians judge the 'hop' claims? The core issue is specific power, the power an engine delivers per kilogram of total installed weight. A practical airplane of the 1900s needed something in the order of 1 horsepower for every 5 to 7 kilograms of engine, a figure steam struggled to approach once boilers, water and fuel were counted. Ader's 4-cylinder engine was remarkable on paper, about 20 horsepower for 51 kilograms of engine alone, but the full steam system weighed much more, and endurance was limited by water. Maxim got around this with brute force and naphtha burners, often given as about 360 horsepower in total on a 3.5-tonne rig, which showed that lift scales with wing area and the square of speed, yet his machine had essentially no flight control system. Historians also argue about definitions. For an aircraft to count as having truly flown, most aviation historians look for four things: it is piloted, it takes off under its own power (not just a ramp or slope), it sustains flight without losing height simply because of launch momentum, and the pilot can control it. By these tests, du Temple (about 1874) and Mozhaysky (about 1884) achieved ramp-assisted hops; Ader (1890) achieved a powered take-off from level ground but an uncontrolled, ground-effect hop of about 50 metres; Maxim (1894) proved lift on a restrained rig; and Langley (1896) achieved sustained but unpiloted and uncontrolled flight. Nationalist claims complicate the picture: Soviet-era publications promoted Mozhaysky until TsAGI's 1971–1981 study found his engines too weak, and Ader's later claim that Avion III had flown a long distance in 1897 was contradicted by the French Army's own report, published in 1910. Another debate concerns scaling. Stringfellow's 1848 model and Langley's 1896 aerodromes flew because small models have far more wing area per kilogram than full-size craft (the square-cube law). That is why successful models did not lead directly to successful piloted machines. Langley's full-size Great Aerodrome would show this painfully in 1903. The broad historical verdict is that this period solved structure and proved lift, while the decisive problems of control and a light, reliable internal-combustion engine were still waiting.","funFact":"The everyday French word for airplane, 'avion', comes from the name Clément Ader gave his bat-winged steam flying machines in the 1890s.","check":{"q":"What important problem had none of the steam-powered flying machines solved?","a":"How a pilot could control (balance and steer) the machine in the air","wrong":["How to make a wing produce any lift at all","How to build a propeller","How to make a machine leave the ground"]},"sources":[{"title":"Aerial Steam Carriage","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Aerial_Steam_Carriage"},{"title":"John Stringfellow","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/John_Stringfellow"},{"title":"Félix du Temple de la Croix","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/F%C3%A9lix_du_Temple_de_la_Croix"},{"title":"Alexander Mozhaysky","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Alexander_Mozhaysky"},{"title":"Clément Ader","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cl%C3%A9ment_Ader"},{"title":"Hiram Maxim","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hiram_Maxim"},{"title":"Lawrence Hargrave","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Lawrence_Hargrave"},{"title":"Langley Aerodrome","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Langley_Aerodrome"}],"photos":[{"src":"img/deep/airplane/04-1.jpg","alt":"An 1843 print imagining Henson's Aerial Steam Carriage 'Ariel' flying over the pyramids of Egypt. It never really flew.","file":"Ariel, the first carriage of the Aerial Transit Company) - W. Walton lith. ; Day & Haghe lithrs. to the Queen LCCN2003675273.jpg","url":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Ariel,_the_first_carriage_of_the_Aerial_Transit_Company)_-_W._Walton_lith._;_Day_%26_Haghe_lithrs._to_the_Queen_LCCN2003675273.jpg","author":"Miscellaneous Items in High Demand, PPOC, Library of Congress","license":"Public domain"},{"src":"img/deep/airplane/04-2.jpg","alt":"An 1891 magazine engraving of Clément Ader's bat-winged steam flying machine, the Éole, shown in the air.","file":"L'oiseau de M. Ader, La science française, 12 sept 1891, Gallica.tif","url":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:L%27oiseau_de_M._Ader,_La_science_fran%C3%A7aise,_12_sept_1891,_Gallica.tif","author":"Dessin de Balladiez, gravure de Tellier","license":"Public domain"},{"src":"img/deep/airplane/04-3.jpg","alt":"Lawrence Hargrave and James Swain with box kites at Stanwell Park, Australia, 12 November 1894.","file":"Hargrave-demo.jpg","url":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Hargrave-demo.jpg","author":"Unknown author","license":"Public domain"}],"words":4354},{"n":5,"title":"Otto Lilienthal, the Glider King","when":"1889 – 1899","summary":"A German engineer named Otto Lilienthal watched storks, built bat-shaped wings and jumped off hills to glide through the air, more than 2,000 times! Photos of him flying amazed the whole world. Other brave pioneers, like Percy Pilcher in Britain and Octave Chanute in America, joined the race to fly.","sections":[{"heading":"Two Boys Who Watched the Storks","paragraphs":["Picture a small town on the flat, marshy plains of northern Germany in the 1850s. The town is called Anklam, in a region then known as Pomerania, part of the Kingdom of Prussia. Every spring, big white storks with long red legs and black-tipped wings come flapping back from Africa to build huge, messy nests on the rooftops and chimneys. Two brothers stand in a meadow, heads tipped back, watching the storks glide in great slow circles without seeming to move their wings at all. How do they do that? the boys wonder. And could a person do it too?","The older boy was Karl Wilhelm Otto Lilienthal, born in Anklam on 23 May 1848. His younger brother Gustav was born the following year, in 1849. The family was not rich, and their father died when the boys were still young, but their mother encouraged their curiosity. Otto and Gustav studied the birds around them, especially storks, which are large, heavy birds that still manage to float effortlessly on the wind. A heavy bird that can glide, the brothers reasoned, was proof that air could hold up something big.","Like many dreamers before them, the boys first tried the obvious thing. They built wings that strapped onto their arms and ran about trying to flap their way into the sky. It did not work. As you saw in Part 2, the Italian scientist Giovanni Borelli had already explained in 1680 that human muscles are far too weak to flap wings like a bird. But the failure did not stop Otto. It just made him want to understand the problem properly.","At school in Anklam, from 1856 to 1864, Otto had a teacher who mattered: the astronomer Gustav Spörer, who taught him mathematics and also the study of birds. Then Otto trained to become an engineer, first at a regional technical school in Potsdam, then with a practical internship at the Schwartzkopff machine-building company in Berlin, and finally at the Royal Technical Academy in Berlin from 1867 to 1870. He was a strong student with a talent for designing machines.","His studies were interrupted by war. In 1870 and 1871, during the Franco-Prussian War, Otto served for a year as a soldier during the siege of Paris. Afterwards he returned to Berlin and worked as a design engineer for machine factories. In 1877 he received his first patent, for a machine used in mining. Over his life he would earn 25 patents, four of them for flying inventions.","In 1883 Otto started his own company in Berlin, making steam boilers and small steam engines. He designed a small engine with tubular boilers that he believed was much safer than other engines of the time, which sometimes exploded. The factory earned the money that paid for his flying experiments. Otto was also unusually kind as a boss for his era: in 1890 he began sharing 25 percent of the factory's profits with his workers, and he supported a people's theatre in Berlin so that ordinary families could enjoy plays. He and Gustav even invented a set of children's stone building blocks, called Anchor Stone Blocks, which later became famous in Germany.","Otto married Agnes Fischer in 1878, and they had four children. He was a busy factory owner, father and inventor. Yet the question from the stork meadow never left him. Gustav had spent years living in Australia, and when he returned to Germany in the mid-1880s, the two brothers took up their old dream together, this time as grown-up engineers."]},{"heading":"A Book About How Birds Fly","paragraphs":["Before Otto ever left the ground, he spent years measuring. Starting around 1867, the brothers built test equipment to find out exactly how much force the air pushes on wings of different shapes. One of their devices was a whirling arm, similar to the one Sir George Cayley used (you met him in Part 3): a long arm that spun around a central post, carrying a small test wing through the air so its push could be measured. They tested flat plates and curved plates, tilted at different angles, and wrote down the numbers.","Their most important discovery was this: a wing that is gently curved, arching upward like the wing of a stork, makes far more lift than a flat board. This curve is called camber. Birds have it, and Otto realised it was no accident. A curved wing turns the air flowing past it downward more smoothly and efficiently, and in return the air pushes the wing up. Many earlier experimenters had used flat surfaces in their calculations. Otto proved with real measurements that curved was better.","In 1889 he published everything in a book. Its German title was Der Vogelflug als Grundlage der Fliegekunst, which means Birdflight as the Basis of Aviation. (The English translation did not appear until 1911, so English-speaking inventors at first learned about his work through articles and summaries.) The book was packed with drawings of bird wings, careful measurements and tables of numbers showing how much lift and drag a curved wing made at each angle.","Otto also invented a clever way to draw this information as a single curved graph, which engineers today call a polar diagram. On it, you can see at a glance how lift and drag change together as the angle of the wing changes. Aircraft designers still use this kind of diagram more than a century later. Wilbur Wright later wrote that nobody did as much as Lilienthal to convince the world of the advantages of curved wing surfaces.","The book was not perfect. Otto still believed that the best long-term answer might be flapping wings, like a bird's, and he spent some of his later years building flapping-wing machines that never worked well. And, as you will see in Part 6 and Part 7, his famous tables of numbers would later cause trouble for two brothers in America. But in 1889 the book was a giant step: it treated flight as a science, built on measurements instead of guesses.","There was one more lesson in the book, and it was perhaps the most important. Otto believed you could not learn to fly just by doing sums at a desk. You had to go out into the real wind and practise, over and over, the way a young bird practises. Thinking about flying, he argued, was not the same as flying. So, at around the age of 43, the factory owner from Berlin decided to become a pilot."]},{"heading":"Leaping From the Hills","paragraphs":["In the spring of 1891, Otto took his first proper glider to a sloping sand pit near the villages of Derwitz and Krielow, west of Potsdam. His glider looked like a pair of giant bat wings. Its frame was made of light willow rods, covered with tightly stretched cotton cloth, with a curved shape copied from the birds he had studied for so long. There was no seat, no cockpit and no engine. Otto simply stood inside an opening in the middle of the wings, gripped the frame and rested his forearms on padded supports, so that he hung beneath the glider with his legs dangling.","To launch, he ran down the slope into the wind until the air under the wings lifted him. That first year his hops were short, about 25 metres. But they were real glides: the air, not his legs, was holding him up. Every flight taught him something, and each winter he improved his designs.","How did he steer? This is the key to Lilienthal's method. His gliders had a fixed tail to keep them pointing into the wind, but no moving controls at all. Instead, Otto steered by swinging his body. If the nose started to rise too high, he threw his legs forward to move his weight toward the front, which tipped the nose down. If one wing dropped, he swung his hips and legs toward the other side to level it. Modern hang-glider pilots still use this idea of weight-shifting, though today's gliders are much bigger and easier to handle.","Otto needed safe places to practise near home. In 1892 he built a shed with a tower on a hill called the Maihöhe in Steglitz, Berlin, giving him a jump-off point about 10 metres high. Then, around 1893 to 1894, he did something remarkable: he had an artificial hill built near his home in Lichterfelde, a suburb of Berlin. It was a cone of earth about 15 metres high, with a shed for his gliders tucked inside the top. Because it was round, he could always launch straight into the wind, whichever direction it blew. Berliners called it the Fliegeberg, the Flying Hill. It still stands today, now a memorial in a park.","For longer flights, Otto travelled northwest of Berlin to a range of bare, grassy hills near the town of Rhinow. From 1893 he flew from a hill called the Hauptmannsberg there, and from 1896 also from the Gollenberg near the village of Stölln. On these higher slopes, with steady winds, his glides grew much longer. Some stretched to about 250 metres, a distance nobody else had come close to matching while he was alive.","Imagine being one of the farm children of Rhinow on a summer Sunday. Up on the hilltop stands a bearded man in a jacket and knickerbocker trousers, holding a huge pale wing above his head. He runs a few steps, leaps, and suddenly he is floating out over the slope, legs swinging, the cloth humming in the wind. A few seconds later, he lands softly in the grass far below, and a helper starts carrying the glider back up the hill so he can do it all again."]},{"heading":"Two Thousand Glides and the Photos That Amazed the World","paragraphs":["Between 1891 and 1896, Otto Lilienthal made more than 2,000 glides. Most lasted only a few seconds, and if you added up all his time in the air it came to only about five hours. That might not sound like much. But no human being before him had ever flown so often, so deliberately, and come back to try again. He was not a lucky tower jumper. He was a test pilot, gathering experience flight by flight.","He also kept inventing. He built about a dozen different single-wing (monoplane) gliders, two biplane gliders with one wing stacked above another, and several flapping-wing machines. In 1894 he began producing one design, which he called the Normalsegelapparat, meaning the standard sailing machine, and selling copies to other people. Its wings spanned about 6.7 metres and the whole glider weighed only about 20 kilograms, light enough for one person to carry. At least nine were sold. Because of this, the Otto Lilienthal Museum in Anklam calls it the world's first production aircraft: the first flying machine made in a series and sold to customers. Original Lilienthal gliders or pieces of them survive today in museums in London, Moscow, Munich and Washington.","What really made Lilienthal famous, though, was photography. Cameras in the 1890s were getting faster, and photographers came to the hills to try to capture him in the air. One of them was Ottomar Anschütz, a German pioneer of high-speed photography. Another was the American physicist Robert Williams Wood. At least 145 photographs of Lilienthal's flights are known. Sadly, the original glass negatives were destroyed during World War II, but prints had already travelled the world.","Think about how startling those pictures were. For thousands of years people had drawn and painted imaginary flying humans. Now magazines and newspapers in Germany, Britain, France, Russia and the United States printed real photographs of a real man soaring through the sky on wings he had built himself. You could see his legs hanging down, the shape of the wings, the hill below. Here at last was proof that a human could fly, at least in a glider.","Visitors came to see him for themselves. Among them were Samuel Langley, the American scientist whose steam-powered models you met in Part 4, and the Russian scientist Nikolai Zhukovsky, who later became one of the founders of Russian aeronautics and took a Lilienthal glider back to Moscow. A young British engineer named Percy Pilcher came too. Otto also wrote letters to experimenters around the world, including Octave Chanute in Chicago. He wrote articles for German science magazines, explaining what he had learned so that others could build on it.","Lilienthal knew that gliding was only the first step. His plan was to master control in the air first, and only then add an engine. He even began experimenting with a small engine using carbonic acid gas to flap the tips of a wing. But first, he believed, a pilot had to become truly at home in the wind."]},{"heading":"The Last Flight","paragraphs":["Sunday, 9 August 1896, was a bright summer day at the Gollenberg near Stölln. Otto was flying his standard glider. He had already made three flights that day. On the fourth, something went wrong. Partway through the glide, the glider pitched its nose up sharply. The Lilienthal Museum describes him being caught by a sudden pocket of rising warm air. The wing tilted too steeply against the airflow and stalled.","A stall, remember, has nothing to do with an engine (gliders do not even have one). It means the wing is meeting the air at too steep an angle, so the smooth airflow over the top breaks away and the lift suddenly collapses. The glider dropped. Otto threw his weight forward to bring the nose down, but swinging his body could only move the glider's balance a small amount, and it was not enough. From a height of about 15 metres, he fell to the ground.","He was badly hurt. A bone in his neck, the third cervical vertebra, was broken. He was carried from the hill and then taken to Berlin, to the clinic of the famous surgeon Ernst von Bergmann. But doctors could not save him. Otto Lilienthal died on 10 August 1896, the day after his fall. He was 48 years old.","People often repeat his supposed last words, Opfer müssen gebracht werden, meaning Sacrifices must be made. Those words are even carved on his gravestone. But historians are not sure he ever said them; the director of the Otto Lilienthal Museum has doubted it. So it is best to treat the famous phrase as a story rather than a proven fact.","What his death did prove was something important about flying. Weight-shifting worked well enough for an expert in gentle conditions, but in a sudden gust it was too weak. A pilot needed a better way to control a flying machine, one that did not depend on how far he could swing his legs. Gustav Lilienthal carried on with aviation work for decades, but Otto's death shocked experimenters everywhere. The question of control would become the central puzzle of the next few years."]},{"heading":"Percy Pilcher, Britain's Flying Hawk","paragraphs":["One of the people most inspired by Lilienthal was Percy Sinclair Pilcher. He was born in Bath, England, on 16 January 1867. As a boy of 13 he joined the Royal Navy as a cadet and served for seven years, then trained as an engineer at a shipbuilding company in Govan, Glasgow. From 1891 he worked as an assistant lecturer in engineering at the University of Glasgow.","In 1895 Pilcher built his first hang glider, which he called the Bat. Later that year he travelled to Germany to meet Otto Lilienthal himself and talk about gliding. What he learned helped him design two more gliders, the Beetle and the Gull. Then he built his best machine, the Hawk, based closely on Lilienthal's ideas but with a clever addition: wheels underneath, so it could roll across the ground.","Pilcher also found a new way to get into the air. Britain has fewer big sandy hills than the Rhinow area, so he often had his gliders towed into the wind by a line pulled by men or horses, a bit like launching a kite. In 1897, on the grounds of Stanford Hall, a country house in Leicestershire, the Hawk glided about 250 metres, a record for that time. His sister Ella helped him, sewing the cloth for the wings and assisting with test flights.","Pilcher wanted to go further than Lilienthal had. He designed a powered triplane, with three wings stacked one above another, meant to carry a small engine of about 4 horsepower. To build an engine he went into partnership with a motor engineer, Walter Gordon Wilson. The project cost a great deal of money, and Pilcher fell into debt. He needed wealthy sponsors.","On 30 September 1899 he invited important guests to Stanford Hall to watch a demonstration. But a few days earlier, the triplane's engine crankshaft had broken. Not wanting to disappoint his visitors, Pilcher decided to fly the Hawk glider instead. It had been raining, and the wet canvas wings had shrunk as they soaked up water, putting extra strain on the bamboo frame. While he was being towed into the air, the tail snapped. The Hawk fell from about 10 metres. Percy Pilcher died on 2 October 1899 without waking up. He was 32.","His triplane was never flown in public. More than a century later, in 2003, engineers at Cranfield University in England built a replica for a BBC television programme and found the design was more or less workable: their copy made a steady flight of 1 minute 25 seconds in calm air. Nobody can say for certain what would have happened if Pilcher had lived. His restored Hawk glider is displayed today at the National Museum of Scotland in Edinburgh."]},{"heading":"Octave Chanute, the Great Sharer","paragraphs":["Across the Atlantic, a very different flying pioneer was at work. Octave Chanute was born in Paris on 18 February 1832, and his family moved to the United States when he was a small boy. He became one of America's leading civil engineers, the kind of person who designs bridges, railways and big structures. He helped design the Hannibal Bridge, opened in 1869, the first bridge to cross the Missouri River at Kansas City. He planned the vast stockyards of Chicago and Kansas City, and he developed a way of soaking railway sleepers (the wooden beams under the tracks) in chemicals so they would not rot. From 1873 to 1883 he was chief engineer of the Erie Railway.","When Chanute grew older and wealthier, he turned his practical mind to flight. He did something that sounds simple but was enormously valuable: he collected everything. He read reports, papers and letters from experimenters all over the world, from Cayley to Lilienthal, and wrote clear summaries of what each person had tried and what had happened. These appeared first as a series of articles from 1891 to 1893, and then, in 1894, as a book called Progress in Flying Machines. It was the most complete survey of attempts to build fixed-wing flying machines that had ever been published. In 1893 he also helped organise an international conference on aerial navigation at the World's Fair in Chicago.","Chanute was too old to be a daring pilot himself, but he wanted to test ideas in the real wind. In the summer of 1896, at the age of 64, he took a team to the sand dunes along the southern shore of Lake Michigan, near Miller Beach, Indiana. His helpers included the engineer Augustus Herring and a carpenter named William Avery. The dunes were perfect: soft sand to land on, steady winds blowing in off the lake, and no trees in the way.","The team tested several hang gliders. One had many small wings arranged in layers that could swing back slightly in a gust, and an early version had twelve wings. But their most successful glider was a two-surface machine: a biplane, with one long wing above another. Chanute braced the two wings together with upright posts and crossed wires, using a pattern called the Pratt truss that he knew from building railway bridges. This made the wings very strong and stiff without being heavy. Herring and Avery made many glides with it, the longest reported at roughly 100 metres, and steered it by shifting their weight, as Lilienthal had.","That strong, wire-braced biplane design turned out to be one of the most important ideas in early aviation. The Wright brothers later based the structure of their own kites, gliders and first airplane on Chanute's double-decker plan.","Above all, Chanute believed in sharing. He wrote hundreds of letters to experimenters in many countries, answered their questions, introduced them to one another, and passed on the latest results. In 1897 he helped bring Lilienthal's table of measurements for curved wings to English-speaking readers. In a time without the internet, Chanute was like a one-man information network for everyone trying to fly."]},{"heading":"News Travels to Dayton","paragraphs":["By the end of the 1890s, the picture looked both exciting and frightening. Lilienthal had shown that a person could glide again and again, and he had given the world measurements, photographs and a proven wing shape. Chanute had shown that a strong biplane glider was possible and had gathered all the world's flying knowledge in one book. Pilcher had been preparing to add an engine. Yet two of the boldest pilots, Lilienthal in 1896 and Pilcher in 1899, had died because their gliders could not be controlled well enough when something went wrong.","Meanwhile, in August 1896, newspapers around the world carried the sad news that the German Glider King had fallen to his death. In the city of Dayton, Ohio, the story reached two brothers who ran a small bicycle shop. They had been fascinated by flying since they were boys. Wilbur Wright later called Lilienthal 'without question the greatest of the precursors', meaning the greatest of the people who came before them. Lilienthal's death did not frighten the brothers away. It made them think hard about the one problem that had killed him: control. Their names were Wilbur and Orville Wright, and Part 6 tells their story."]}],"deepDive":"Lilienthal's lasting scientific gift was data. With his brother Gustav he measured the forces on curved and flat test surfaces at many angles, and in 1889 published the results as tables and as a 'polar diagram', a single curve plotting lift against drag as the angle of attack changes. Engineers still use polars today to choose wing shapes. His key finding, that a gently cambered surface produces much more lift for its drag than a flat plate, overturned the flat-plate assumptions that many earlier calculators had used. But his numbers came with hidden conditions. They described one particular wing curve, measured on a whirling arm and in natural wind, and they were usually applied together with an older multiplier for air pressure, the 'Smeaton coefficient', which turned out to be too high. When Chanute published Lilienthal's table in English in 1897, many experimenters, including the Wrights, used it as their starting point. The Smithsonian's National Air and Space Museum explains that the Wrights' early gliders underperformed predictions based partly on a misreading of that table, which pushed them to gather their own data. Historians also debate the limits of Lilienthal's control method. A hang-glider pilot steers by moving the combined centre of gravity of pilot and glider, but on a glider spanning about 6.7 metres the pilot can shift that centre only a few centimetres. In calm air this is enough; in a sudden gust or thermal it may not be. His fatal stall in 1896 showed this weakness, and it is one reason the Wrights later insisted on movable control surfaces instead. Another debate is about what might have happened had he lived. Lilienthal was working on engines, and Pilcher's triplane was nearly ready in 1899; a 2003 Cranfield University replica of Pilcher's design flew for 85 seconds in calm air. Yet most historians caution that a workable machine is not the same as a controllable one, and that neither man had solved roll control in a way that would scale up. Finally, Lilienthal's 'over 2,000 flights' deserve context: each was short, the total airtime was only about five hours, and yet that was far more real flying experience than anyone else on Earth had at the time.","funFact":"Otto Lilienthal had a cone-shaped hill about 15 metres high built near his home in Berlin just so he could launch his gliders into the wind from any direction, and it still stands today.","check":{"q":"How did Otto Lilienthal steer his gliders?","a":"By swinging his body to shift his weight","wrong":["By pulling on ropes attached to a rudder","By using a steering wheel connected to ailerons","By changing the speed of a small engine"]},"sources":[{"title":"Otto Lilienthal","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Otto_Lilienthal"},{"title":"Otto Lilienthal: Biography","publisher":"Otto Lilienthal Museum, Anklam","url":"http://www.lilienthal-museum.de/olma/ebiog.htm"},{"title":"Lilienthal and the Wright Brothers","publisher":"Otto Lilienthal Museum, Anklam","url":"http://www.lilienthal-museum.de/olma/ewright.htm"},{"title":"Percy Pilcher","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Percy_Pilcher"},{"title":"Octave Chanute","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Octave_Chanute"},{"title":"Researching the Wright Way","publisher":"Smithsonian National Air and Space Museum","url":"https://airandspace.si.edu/researching-wright-way"}],"photos":[{"src":"img/deep/airplane/05-1.jpg","alt":"Otto Lilienthal hangs beneath his glider, soaring away from a hillside during one of his 1890s flights.","file":"Otto Lilienthal gliding experiment ppmsca.02546.jpg","url":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Otto_Lilienthal_gliding_experiment_ppmsca.02546.jpg","author":"Unknown author","license":"Public domain"},{"src":"img/deep/airplane/05-2.jpg","alt":"The Fliegeberg in Berlin-Lichterfelde today: Lilienthal's flying hill, now with steps and a memorial on top.","file":"Berlin-lichterfelde-fliegeberg-seitlich.JPG","url":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Berlin-lichterfelde-fliegeberg-seitlich.JPG","author":"Roland.h.bueb","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0"},{"src":"img/deep/airplane/05-3.jpg","alt":"Percy Pilcher's Hawk glider hanging in the National Museum of Scotland, Edinburgh, seen from below.","file":"National Museum of Scotland (42719086411).jpg","url":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:National_Museum_of_Scotland_(42719086411).jpg","author":"Paul Hudson from United Kingdom","license":"CC BY 2.0"}],"words":4023},{"n":6,"title":"Two Brothers from Dayton","when":"1878 – 1901","summary":"Wilbur and Orville Wright got hooked on flying when their dad gave them a little rubber-band flying toy. They grew up to run a bicycle shop, figured out how to balance a flying machine by twisting its wings, and tested big gliders on the windy sand dunes of Kitty Hawk. But their 1901 glider flew so badly that Wilbur almost gave up!","sections":[{"heading":"A Flying Toy in Cedar Rapids","paragraphs":["It is 1878, in the town of Cedar Rapids, Iowa. Bishop Milton Wright comes home from one of his many church journeys with something hidden in his hands. His two youngest sons crowd around. He tosses the object into the air, and instead of falling, it whirs up toward the ceiling, hovers for a moment, and flutters back down. The boys are amazed.","The toy was a small flying model made of paper, bamboo and cork, with two little propellers spun by a twisted rubber band. It was based on a design by the French inventor Alphonse Pénaud, a clever experimenter with model aircraft. Today we might call it a toy helicopter. The brothers called it the bat. They played with it until it broke, and then they built copies of their own. Years later, they said this toy was where their interest in flying began.","The two boys were Wilbur Wright, then 11, and Orville Wright, then 7. Wilbur was born on 16 April 1867 near Millville, Indiana. Orville was born on 19 August 1871 in Dayton, Ohio. They were two of seven children born to Milton Wright and his wife, Susan Catherine Koerner. Their older brothers were Reuchlin and Lorin, and their younger sister was Katharine, who would become one of their closest supporters. Twins named Otis and Ida died as babies.","Their father was a bishop in a church called the Church of the United Brethren in Christ, and his work meant the family moved a lot: twelve times in all, before finally settling in Dayton in 1884. The house had a large library, and the children were encouraged to read widely and to think for themselves. Their mother, Susan, was very good with tools and machines; she could build and fix household things and even toys for her children. Both brothers later said they got their mechanical skill from her.","The Wright family was also unusually close. The Smithsonian's National Air and Space Museum notes that the parents taught their children that family bonds were their most dependable support. That closeness gave Wilbur and Orville a quiet confidence. Later, when famous experts said one thing and the brothers' own tests said another, they were willing to trust their own work.","Neither brother ever married. They lived, worked, argued and invented side by side for most of their lives. Wilbur once wrote that from the time they were little children, he and Orville had lived together, played together and worked together, and thought together. That partnership would become one of the most famous in the history of invention."]},{"heading":"Hard Years and a Printing Press","paragraphs":["Wilbur was a brilliant student with an extraordinary memory, a good athlete and a fine writer and speaker. People who knew him described him as calm and steady. He planned to go to Yale, one of America's top universities. But in the winter of 1885 to 1886, when Wilbur was 18, he was struck in the face by a stick during a game of ice hockey and lost his front teeth. The injury left him with health problems and he became low and withdrawn. He gave up his plans for Yale.","For the next few years, Wilbur stayed mostly at home. His mother was very ill with tuberculosis, a serious lung disease, and Wilbur cared for her until she died in 1889. During these quiet years, which historians sometimes call his lost years, he read almost everything in his father's library: history, science, literature and religion. All that reading turned out not to be lost at all. It trained him to study a problem carefully and to write about it clearly.","Orville was different: more lively, more impulsive and more optimistic, always taking things apart to see how they worked. He became fascinated by printing. As a teenager he built his own printing press, using parts from an old buggy and other scrap, and in 1889 he left high school after his junior year to start a printing business. Wilbur had also missed out on his diploma when the family moved, so neither brother ever received a high school diploma, though both kept studying hard on their own for the rest of their lives.","Wilbur soon joined Orville's business. Together they published a weekly newspaper for their neighbourhood called the West Side News, and later a daily paper. They also printed work for one of Orville's school friends, Paul Laurence Dunbar, who became one of the first African American poets to win national fame. It was a small business, but it taught the brothers how to run a company, how to build and repair machines, and how to work as a team.","The family home at 7 Hawthorn Street in Dayton would become the place where the brothers talked and argued through their flying ideas, often late into the night. According to family stories, they enjoyed arguing so much that they would sometimes switch sides halfway through, each now defending the other's original point."]},{"heading":"Bicycle Men","paragraphs":["In the early 1890s, America went bicycle crazy. A new design called the safety bicycle, with two wheels of the same size and a chain driving the back wheel, was much easier and safer to ride than the old high-wheeled penny-farthing. Suddenly everyone wanted one. In December 1892, Wilbur and Orville opened a shop in Dayton to sell and repair bicycles. It started as the Wright Cycle Exchange and was soon renamed the Wright Cycle Company.","The shop moved several times along West Third Street and nearby streets as the business grew. Early bicycles sold for about 40 to 100 dollars each, which was a lot of money then. In 1896 the brothers began building their own bicycles by hand. Their more expensive model was called the Van Cleve, after an ancestor of theirs who was one of Dayton's first settlers, and their cheaper model was the St. Clair. From 1897 to 1909 their shop was at 1127 West Third Street. Many years later, in 1937, the car maker Henry Ford had that building taken apart and rebuilt as a museum exhibit at Greenfield Village in Dearborn, Michigan, where you can still visit it.","The bicycle business mattered for flight in three big ways. First, it earned the money that paid for all their experiments; the Wrights never had a government grant or a rich sponsor. Second, it gave them hands-on skill with light, strong structures: thin metal tubes, wire spokes, chains, sprockets and bearings. Third, and perhaps most important, it taught them about balance.","Think about riding a bike. A bicycle standing still falls over. Yet a rider in motion stays upright by making constant tiny corrections, leaning and steering without even thinking about it. The Wrights came to believe a flying machine would be the same. It did not need to be perfectly stable by itself, any more than a bicycle does. What it needed was a skilled rider with good controls who could keep it balanced moment by moment. That idea would shape everything they did.","In the summer of 1896, Orville fell seriously ill with typhoid fever, a dangerous infection. While he lay in bed recovering, the newspapers reported that Otto Lilienthal had died after his glider crashed. Wilbur read about it, and the brothers talked about it. Lilienthal's flights, and his death, sparked their serious interest in the problem of flight. They began to read whatever they could find, though at first there was not much in Dayton."]},{"heading":"A Letter to the Smithsonian","paragraphs":["By 1899, Wilbur had decided to study flight properly. On 30 May 1899 he sat down and wrote a letter to the Smithsonian Institution in Washington, D.C., the great national museum and research centre that was then led by Samuel Langley. The letter still survives, two handwritten pages now kept by the National Air and Space Museum.","In it, Wilbur explained that he had been interested in mechanical and human flight since he was a boy, and that he believed flight was possible. He wrote that he wanted to read everything already published on the subject, and that he hoped to add his own small contribution to help whoever would eventually succeed. He was polite, modest and serious. He was not claiming to be a genius; he simply wanted to learn.","The Smithsonian replied quickly. It sent several pamphlets, including writings by Langley and by Lilienthal, and a list of recommended books. The museum's own history says the most important recommendations were Octave Chanute's Progress in Flying Machines and a yearly collection of articles called The Aeronautical Annual. The brothers read everything. In Chanute's book they found a summary of almost every serious attempt to fly up to 1894, including Cayley's ideas, the steam machines of Part 4, and the gliders of Lilienthal and Chanute himself.","Reading all this history, the brothers noticed something important. Many experimenters had worked hard on two of the problems of flight: building wings that could lift a machine, and finding engines powerful enough to drive it. But hardly anyone had solved the third problem: how to balance and steer a machine once it was in the air. Lilienthal and Pilcher had both died because their gliders could not be controlled well enough. Wilbur wrote later that the problem of balance was the real difficulty, and that once a machine could be controlled, the rest would follow."]},{"heading":"The Secret in a Twisted Box","paragraphs":["So how do you keep a flying machine balanced? Wilbur watched birds for clues. He noticed that when a soaring bird such as a buzzard began to tip to one side, it seemed to correct itself by changing the angle of its wingtips: one tip met the air at a slightly steeper angle, the other at a shallower one. The wing with the steeper angle got more lift and rose, and the bird rolled level again. A bird, Wilbur realised, does not balance by swinging its body like Lilienthal. It balances by changing the shape of its wings.","Could a machine do the same? The brothers first thought about building wings that could pivot on shafts and gears, but that would be heavy and complicated. Then, one day in July 1899, Wilbur was in the bicycle shop chatting with a customer and absent-mindedly fiddling with an empty cardboard box, a long, thin box that had held a bicycle inner tube. He squeezed the ends in opposite directions. The top and bottom of the box twisted, one end tilting up and the other tilting down, but the box stayed strong and in one piece.","Wilbur suddenly saw it. Imagine the top and bottom of the box as the two wings of a biplane. If you could twist the whole wing structure like that, the tips on one side would meet the air at a steeper angle while the tips on the other side met it at a shallower angle. One side would rise and the other would sink, and the machine would roll, just like the bird. There was no need for heavy pivots or gears. You could just twist the wings themselves. The brothers called this idea wing warping.","To test it, they built a biplane kite with a wingspan of about 5 feet (1.5 metres). Its structure was based on the strong, wire-braced double-decker that Chanute and Herring had used for their 1896 glider, but with one important change: the brothers left out the diagonal braces between the front and back posts, so the wings could twist. Four strings ran from the wingtips to two sticks held by the person on the ground. Tilting the sticks in opposite directions twisted the wings.","Around the end of July 1899, Wilbur took the kite to a field near Dayton and flew it. The only people watching were a few schoolboys. When he tilted the sticks, the kite rolled to one side or the other, quickly and exactly as he wanted. It was less than three months after his letter to the Smithsonian, and the brothers already had their first big idea working. No photographs of that kite survive, but a reproduction can be seen today. The next step was to build something big enough to carry a person."]},{"heading":"Why Kitty Hawk?","paragraphs":["A full-size glider needed a special testing ground. The brothers needed strong, steady winds, so that a glider could stay up while moving slowly over the ground. They needed wide open spaces without trees, and soft ground for crash landings. And they wanted privacy, away from crowds and newspaper reporters. Dayton, with its gentle breezes and busy streets, had none of these things.","So in 1900 Wilbur wrote to the United States Weather Bureau in Washington, D.C., which collected wind records from weather stations across the country. He also asked Octave Chanute for advice. Chanute suggested a sandy seacoast, where sea breezes are strong and sand dunes give soft landings. Studying the wind tables, Wilbur noticed a tiny place called Kitty Hawk, North Carolina, a fishing village on the Outer Banks, a long, narrow chain of sandy islands separated from the mainland by wide sounds of water.","He wrote to the Weather Bureau station at Kitty Hawk. The man in charge there, Joseph Dosher, replied that the beach was wide and free of trees, and that the winds were steady. A local man named William Tate, the village postmaster and its best-educated resident, also wrote back with a warm welcome. 'I will take pleasure in doing all I can for your convenience and success and pleasure,' Tate promised. That settled it.","Getting there was an adventure in itself. In early September 1900, Wilbur set off alone by train from Dayton, carrying pieces of the glider and equipment. In Elizabeth City, North Carolina, he found that hardly anyone even knew how to reach Kitty Hawk. He finally hired a local boatman to sail him across Albemarle Sound in a small, leaky fishing boat, which was battered by a storm on the way. The trip took days. Wilbur stayed with the Tate family until Orville arrived a couple of weeks later with more supplies, and the brothers set up a tent camp in the sand.","Life in camp was rough. The wind blew sand into everything, including their food and their beds. Fresh supplies had to come by boat. In later years, swarms of mosquitoes made some nights almost unbearable. But the brothers loved it too. They wrote cheerful letters home to their sister Katharine about the fish, the wild birds, and the sound of the sea. And the wind, which is what they had come for, blew strong and steady off the ocean."]},{"heading":"The 1900 Glider: Flying It Like a Kite","paragraphs":["The brothers' first full-size glider, built starting in August 1900, was a biplane with wings spanning about 17.5 feet (about 5.3 metres). It weighed about 52 pounds (about 24 kilograms). It used the same wire-braced structure and wing-warping system they had tested with their kite. But they also made some bold choices that were different from Lilienthal's.","First, the pilot did not hang underneath. He lay flat on his stomach in a gap in the middle of the lower wing. This cut down drag, the air resistance that slows a flying machine, because a person lying down pushes against much less air than one dangling upright. Second, instead of a tail, the glider had a small horizontal surface sticking out in front, called a forward elevator, which the pilot moved with a lever to tilt the nose up or down. The brothers believed this front elevator would help protect them from the kind of sudden nosedive that had killed Lilienthal. Third, the pilot controlled the wing warping with his feet, using a crossbar that twisted the wings.","Most of the time, they flew the 1900 glider as a kite, with ropes held by people on the ground, sometimes with chains or sandbags on board instead of a pilot. This was a clever and safe way to test it. By measuring the pull on the ropes and the angle of the kite, they could work out how much lift and drag the wings made. William Tate's young nephew, Tom Tate, sometimes rode on the glider while it was flown as a tethered kite.","The results were mixed. The glider made less lift than the brothers had calculated, using the standard numbers of the day, so it needed stronger winds than expected to carry a person. But the controls, the museum says, worked beautifully. Near the end of their stay, they took the glider to the big sand dunes called Kill Devil Hills, about 4 miles (6.4 kilometres) south of the village. On 20 October 1900, Wilbur made about a dozen real free glides, sliding down the slope with nobody holding the ropes. In all that first season, their free gliding added up to only about two minutes. But it was enough to show that their ideas about control were sound. They left the glider behind and went home to Dayton, already planning a bigger one."]},{"heading":"1901: The Disappointing Giant","paragraphs":["In July 1901 the brothers returned, this time setting up camp right at Kill Devil Hills, closer to the big dunes. They built a wooden shed to hold their new glider and lived in a tent beside it. They had made the 1901 glider much bigger to get more lift: its wings spanned about 22 feet (about 6.7 metres) and it weighed about 98 pounds (about 44 kilograms). It was the largest glider anyone had ever tried to fly. They also made the wings more strongly curved, because the old tables suggested a deeper curve would give more lift.","They had visitors that summer. Octave Chanute came to watch, as he would each year from 1901 to 1903, along with two other experimenters he had introduced to the brothers. The Wrights made many more glides than in 1900, and some were much longer. The giant glider really could carry a person through the air. But almost everything else was a disappointment.","The biggest problem was lift. When the brothers measured it carefully, the 1901 glider was making only about one-third of the lift their calculations had predicted. It is as if you built a bridge expecting it to hold 30 tonnes and found it could hold only 10. Something in the numbers had to be wrong.","There were control problems too. The front elevator became oversensitive and twitchy, so the glider swooped up and down. The brothers flattened the wing curve a little by adjusting the wires, which brought back good pitch control, but lift stayed poor. Stranger still, when the pilot warped the wings to roll into a turn, the glider would sometimes start to turn the right way and then suddenly swing around in the opposite direction. The wing that was lifted more was also being dragged backward more. Engineers now call this adverse yaw. On one hard landing, Wilbur was thrown forward and bruised, though not badly hurt.","The brothers packed up and headed home in August, earlier than they had planned. On the train back to Dayton, Wilbur was gloomier than he had ever been. He reportedly told Orville that men would not fly in their lifetime, and one later version of the story says he said not for a thousand years. These words were remembered and repeated long afterwards, so nobody knows exactly what he said. Wilbur himself, in a speech in 1908, recalled that he had told Orville in 1901 that man would not fly for fifty years. Either way, he was close to giving up."]},{"heading":"Trust Nothing, Test Everything","paragraphs":["Wilbur did not stay gloomy for long. Octave Chanute invited him to give a talk in Chicago to the Western Society of Engineers, an important club of professional engineers. Wilbur was nervous, but his sister Katharine urged him to accept. On 18 September 1901 he stood before the audience and gave a careful report on the gliders of 1900 and 1901, illustrated with lantern slides, the projected photographs of the day. It was the first public account of the brothers' experiments. Preparing the talk forced Wilbur to go back over all their measurements, one by one.","As he did, a startling thought grew stronger. Perhaps the problem was not their glider at all. Perhaps the problem was the numbers everybody used. To predict the lift of a wing, experimenters of the day used a formula that included a value for air pressure called the Smeaton coefficient, worked out back in the 1700s, together with tables of measurements such as Lilienthal's famous table for curved wings. The Wrights had trusted these numbers, just as everyone else did. But their 1901 glider was making only a third of the lift those numbers promised. Either the brothers were doing something very wrong, or the most respected data in the field was wrong.","That was a bold thought for two bicycle makers with no college degrees. Lilienthal was the hero they admired most. Chanute was their friend and adviser. Questioning the great experts felt almost rude. But the brothers had grown up in a family that taught them to think for themselves, and they had spent years arguing ideas back and forth until the truth came out.","So, in the autumn of 1901, Wilbur and Orville made a decision that would change the history of flight. They would stop trusting other people's numbers. They would build their own testing equipment, right there in the back of the bicycle shop, and measure the lift of wings themselves, carefully and patiently, one shape at a time. As Part 7 will show, their homemade wind tunnel was about to uncover the secret everyone else had missed."]}],"deepDive":"Why did the 1901 glider make only about a third of its predicted lift? The Wrights, like everyone else, estimated lift with an equation of the form lift = k x S x V squared x CL, where S is wing area, V is airspeed, CL is a lift coefficient read from a table (often Lilienthal's), and k is the Smeaton coefficient, a number for the pressure of air, originally from 18th-century work associated with John Smeaton and long given as about 0.005. Part 7 tells how their own experiments showed k was nearer 0.0033, roughly a third lower, which explains much of the shortfall. Historians also point to another issue, noted by the Smithsonian: Lilienthal's table described one specific wing curve and measured angles in a particular way, and applying it to the Wrights' differently shaped, more deeply curved 1901 wings was a mismatch. The brothers measured their own lift by flying the gliders as kites and recording the pull on the lines and the angle of the tether, a clever field method that let them compare reality with prediction. The 1901 season also revealed adverse yaw. When wing warping increases the angle of one wing to raise it, that wing gets more lift but also more drag, so it is pulled backward and the nose swings the wrong way. The Wrights did not fully solve this until 1902, when they added a movable rudder linked to the warping (Part 7). Their choice of a forward elevator, or canard, is debated too. It gave them a visible, responsive pitch control and they believed it softened stalls, but it made the craft less naturally stable, which suited their bicycle-inspired view that a pilot should actively balance the machine. Later designers mostly moved the elevator to the tail. Finally, the famous gloomy quotation deserves care. Wilbur's remark on the train was recalled years later, and versions differ: 'not in our lifetime', 'not within a thousand years', or, in Wilbur's own 1908 recollection, 'not for fifty years'. What is certain is the result: instead of quitting, the brothers decided to test the data themselves.","funFact":"The Wright brothers' idea for steering an airplane, wing warping, came to Wilbur while he was idly twisting an empty bicycle inner-tube box in their bicycle shop.","check":{"q":"Why did the Wright brothers choose Kitty Hawk, North Carolina, for their glider tests?","a":"It had strong steady winds, soft sand and privacy","wrong":["It was close to their home in Dayton","It had a big airport with long runways","The Smithsonian owned a test field there"]},"sources":[{"title":"Wright brothers","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Wright_brothers"},{"title":"Who Were the Wright Brothers?","publisher":"Smithsonian National Air and Space Museum","url":"https://airandspace.si.edu/stories/editorial/who-were-wright-brothers"},{"title":"Researching the Wright Way","publisher":"Smithsonian National Air and Space Museum","url":"https://airandspace.si.edu/researching-wright-way"},{"title":"The Wright Brothers at Kitty Hawk","publisher":"Smithsonian National Air and Space Museum","url":"https://airandspace.si.edu/stories/editorial/wright-brothers-kitty-hawk"},{"title":"Wright Cycle Company","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Wright_Cycle_Company"},{"title":"Kitty Hawk Proved the Ideal Location for the Brothers' Experiments","publisher":"US National Park Service, Wright Brothers National Memorial","url":"https://www.nps.gov/wrbr/learn/historyculture/places.htm"},{"title":"Kitty Hawk, North Carolina","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Kitty_Hawk,_North_Carolina"}],"photos":[{"src":"img/deep/airplane/06-1.jpg","alt":"Orville (left) and Wilbur Wright standing side by side in bowler hats.","file":"Orville and Wilbur Wright, full length portrait, standing, wearing derbies LCCN2016649438.jpg","url":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Orville_and_Wilbur_Wright,_full_length_portrait,_standing,_wearing_derbies_LCCN2016649438.jpg","author":"Miscellaneous Items in High Demand, PPOC, Library of Congress","license":"Public domain"},{"src":"img/deep/airplane/06-2.jpg","alt":"The Wright Cycle Company building from Dayton, Ohio, moved to Greenfield Village in Dearborn, Michigan.","file":"Wright Cycle Shop (Charles Webbert Block), Greenfield Village, Oakwood Boulevard, Fairlane, Dearborn, MI.jpg","url":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Wright_Cycle_Shop_(Charles_Webbert_Block),_Greenfield_Village,_Oakwood_Boulevard,_Fairlane,_Dearborn,_MI.jpg","author":"w_lemay","license":"CC BY-SA 2.0"},{"src":"img/deep/airplane/06-3.jpg","alt":"1901: helpers launch the Wrights' glider at Kill Devil Hills, with Wilbur lying on the lower wing.","file":"WrightBrothers 1901 2 WilburInTheAir.jpg","url":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:WrightBrothers_1901_2_WilburInTheAir.jpg","author":"Unknown author","license":"Public domain"}],"words":3936},{"n":7,"title":"The Wind Tunnel Secret","when":"1901 – 1902","summary":"Wilbur and Orville built a little wooden wind tunnel in their bicycle shop and tested about 200 tiny wings. They found out that the old flying numbers were wrong, so they worked out better ones. In 1902 their new glider flew beautifully, and they became the best pilots in the world.","sections":[{"heading":"Back Home with a Big Problem","paragraphs":["In late August 1901, Wilbur and Orville Wright rode the train home from the sand dunes of North Carolina feeling beaten. Their new glider had been the largest anyone had ever flown, with wings 22 feet (6.7 metres) across, and it had been a disappointment. It lifted only about a third as much as their calculations promised. It bucked and dipped in strange ways. Once or twice it had turned the wrong way when Wilbur tried to tilt it into a turn. The brothers had come home with sunburn, mosquito bites and a heap of puzzling notes.","Back in Dayton, Ohio, life carried on around them. Their bicycle shop on West Third Street still had to make money. Customers came in for tyres, chains and new spring models. But in the evenings, and in every spare moment, the two brothers kept turning the problem over. Why had the glider failed to lift as much as it should?","There were only two possibilities. Either the brothers had built their glider badly, or the numbers they had used to design it were wrong. Those numbers came from the most respected experts of the day, including the German glider pioneer Otto Lilienthal, who had made more than 2,000 glides before his death in 1896. Saying that Lilienthal's tables might be wrong felt a bit like a pair of school students announcing that the textbook had a mistake in it.","Then came an invitation that forced them to think even harder. Octave Chanute, the Chicago engineer who had become their friend and adviser, asked Wilbur to give a talk to the Western Society of Engineers in Chicago. On 18 September 1901 Wilbur stood up in front of a room of professional engineers and described the brothers' gliding experiments. The talk, later printed as 'Some Aeronautical Experiments', was honest about what had gone wrong. Preparing it made Wilbur look very carefully at the old figures, and he grew more and more suspicious of them.","Imagine being Wilbur at that moment. You are 34 years old, you never went to university, and you run a bicycle shop. Yet you are starting to believe that some of the most famous data in the young science of flight cannot be trusted. You could give up, as many people before you had. Or you could do something almost nobody in America had done: build your own laboratory and measure the air yourself."]},{"heading":"The Bicycle That Tested Wings","paragraphs":["The Wrights' first test machine was wonderfully odd. They took a bicycle and fixed a third wheel flat, lying sideways like a little table, above the front of the frame. On the rim of this wheel they mounted two small test surfaces. One was a small curved wing, the kind of shape they wanted to study. The other was a flat plate used as a reference. The two were arranged so that the wind would push them in opposite directions and try to turn the wheel.","Then one of the brothers pedalled hard down the streets of Dayton, at up to about 15 miles per hour (24 km/h). As the bicycle rushed forward, air flowed over both surfaces. If the curved wing pushed harder than the flat plate, the wheel would turn one way. If the plate won, it would turn the other way. By changing the angle of the curved wing until the wheel stopped turning, they could compare the two forces. It was a kind of tug-of-war, played by the wind.","The neighbours must have wondered what on earth the bicycle men were up to. But the strange machine did its job. The results were rough, because a bicycle on a street meets gusts, bumps and swirling air from passing carts. Still, they were good enough to show something important: the curved wing was not producing the lift that Lilienthal's table said it should at that angle. The old data really did look wrong.","The brothers knew they needed a steadier, more controlled stream of air. They needed a place where the wind would always blow at the same speed, in the same direction, and where they could repeat a test as many times as they liked. In other words, they needed a wind tunnel.","A wind tunnel was not a new idea. In 1871 a British engineer named Francis Wenham had built what is usually called the first enclosed wind tunnel, a long box with a fan driven by a steam engine. Wenham and his colleague John Browning had even noticed that long, narrow wings seemed to work better than short, stubby ones. A few other experimenters had built tunnels since. But in America they were still very rare, and nobody had used one to collect the kind of complete, practical design data the Wrights now wanted."]},{"heading":"A Wooden Box and Two Hundred Tiny Wings","paragraphs":["In the autumn of 1901 the brothers built their main wind tunnel in the back room of the bicycle shop. It was a plain wooden box about 6 feet (1.8 metres) long, with a square opening about 16 inches (roughly 40 centimetres) across inside. A glass window on top let them peer down at the model being tested. At one end a metal fan blew air through the box. The fan was not electric: it was turned by a belt from the small gasoline engine that already powered the lathe, drill press and other tools in the workshop.","The air rushed through the tunnel at roughly 25 to 30 miles per hour (about 40 to 48 km/h), similar to a strong breeze on the dunes at Kitty Hawk. Early tests showed that the air came out of the fan swirling and uneven, which would spoil the measurements. So the brothers added screens and a grid shaped like a honeycomb to straighten the flow before it reached the models. Even something as simple as where they stood mattered: if one of them moved around the room, the air in the shop shifted and the readings could change. So they learned to stay in exactly the same place during every test.","The cleverest part of the tunnel was not the box at all. It was the measuring device inside it, called a balance. A balance is a tool that measures the pushes of the air on a model. The Wrights made theirs from whatever was lying around a bicycle shop: lengths of bicycle spoke wire and bits of scrap metal (many accounts say old hacksaw blades too), bent and soldered together. NASA engineers who studied the Wrights' work much later called the balance the heart of any successful wind tunnel.","The brothers built two balances. One was for lift, the upward push that holds a wing up. The other was for drag, the backward push of the air that slows a wing down. The lift balance worked much like the bicycle test, only far more precisely. A small test wing was hung on one side of the balance and a set of flat plates on the other. When the wind blew, the wing and the plates pushed against each other, and a pointer moved across a scale marked in degrees. Reading the pointer told the brothers how strong the lift of the test wing was compared with the push on a flat plate facing straight into the wind.","This trick was very smart. The balance did not measure forces in pounds or grams directly. Instead, it measured ratios: how many times stronger or weaker one force was than another. That meant the brothers did not have to know the exact wind speed perfectly, because it cancelled out. It is a good lesson for any young scientist: sometimes the smartest experiment is one designed so that the things you cannot control do not matter.","From October to December 1901 the back room of the bicycle shop became one of the most important science laboratories in the world, although almost nobody knew it. The brothers cut small model wings out of sheet steel with tin snips and hammered them into shape. Some were flat. Some were gently curved. Some were deeply curved, with the highest point of the curve near the front edge, and others with it further back. Some were square, some long and thin, some had rounded tips, some had pointed ones. A few were even stacked one above another, like a tiny biplane.","Altogether they tried about 200 different wing shapes. Many got a quick first test to see if they were worth studying. Then they picked several dozen (around 38 to 50, depending on how they are counted) of the most interesting and tested each one carefully at many angles, from flat to steeply tilted, writing every pointer reading into notebooks in neat columns. Each wing might need dozens of readings. It was slow, patient, repetitive work, the kind that does not look exciting in a film but wins in real science.","Their sister Katharine, a schoolteacher who lived with them in the family home on Hawthorn Street, watched her brothers disappear into the shop night after night. Charlie Taylor, the mechanic they had hired to help run the bicycle business, kept customers happy so the brothers could keep experimenting. Without that help, the testing could never have been squeezed into just a few months.","By Christmas 1901 they had what no one else in the world possessed: tables of carefully measured numbers showing how dozens of wing shapes behaved at many different angles. In effect, they had written their own design handbook for wings. And then they stopped. The bicycle season was coming, money mattered, and Wilbur felt they had learned what they needed. Their wind tunnel work had taken only about three months."]},{"heading":"The Mistake Hidden in an Old Number","paragraphs":["To understand the brothers' biggest discovery, you need to know about a number with an old-fashioned name: the Smeaton coefficient. In the 1750s an English engineer named John Smeaton, who built windmills and lighthouses, had studied how hard moving air pushes on a flat surface. Engineers used his findings to create a number for the push of the wind. For more than 100 years, nearly everyone working on flight plugged this number into their lift formula. The value commonly used was about 0.005 (often written as 0.0054).","Here is the idea, without the hard maths. To predict how much lift a wing would make, designers of the 1890s multiplied together several things: the area of the wing, the speed of the air squared, a number for the wing's shape and angle, and the Smeaton coefficient. If any one of those numbers was wrong, the whole answer would be wrong, a bit like a recipe where the amount of flour is misprinted.","The Wrights' tests, together with their careful measurements from the dunes, pointed to a surprising conclusion. Lilienthal's numbers for how a curved wing behaved were actually fairly good for the kind of wing he had tested. The real culprit was the old Smeaton number that everybody, including the Wrights, had been using. Their results showed that a value of about 0.0033 was much closer to the truth. The old figure was too high by more than half again, which meant designers had been expecting far more lift than wings could really give. Octave Chanute had collected as many as 50 different values for this coefficient from various experimenters, which shows how confused the subject was.","There was another lesson too. Lilienthal's wings had a particular shape, and the Wrights' 1901 glider had a different one, with a different curve and a different outline. Copying numbers measured for one wing and using them for another, quite different wing simply did not work. Every wing shape needed its own measurements. That is exactly what the brothers' tables now gave them.","Today, engineers no longer use the Smeaton coefficient at all. They use the density of air and something called a lift coefficient, measured for each wing shape in modern wind tunnels and with computer models. But the method of testing many shapes carefully and trusting measurements over old assumptions is the same method the Wrights used in their back room."]},{"heading":"Long, Thin and Gently Curved","paragraphs":["The tiny steel wings taught the brothers two big design rules, and both are still followed today.","The first rule was about the shape of the wing seen from above. Engineers call this the aspect ratio. It compares a wing's span (how far it stretches from tip to tip) with its chord (how deep it is from the front edge to the back edge). A long, narrow wing has a high aspect ratio. A short, stubby wing has a low one. The Wrights found that long, narrow wings gave much more lift for the amount of drag they made. Their 1901 glider had wings 22 feet long and 7 feet deep, an aspect ratio of about 3. For 1902 they planned wings about 32 feet (9.8 metres) long but only about 5 feet (1.5 metres) deep, an aspect ratio of about 6.","Why does this work? At the tips of a wing, air from the higher-pressure region underneath curls around into the lower-pressure region on top, making whirlpools of air called wingtip vortices. These vortices waste energy and add drag. A long, narrow wing has less of its area close to the tips, so less is lost. Look at an albatross, which can glide over the ocean for hours: its wings are extremely long and narrow. Modern gliders and long-distance airliners have long, slender wings for the same reason.","The second rule was about camber, the gentle upward curve of a wing seen from the side. In 1901 the brothers had used a deep curve, about 1 in 12, meaning the hump of the wing was one-twelfth as high as the wing was deep. It had made the glider hard to control, because the push of the air shifted around unexpectedly as the angle changed. The wind tunnel showed that a much flatter curve worked better. For 1902 they chose a camber of about 1 in 24 or 25, with the highest point about a third of the way back from the front edge. That is very close to the shape of many wings used on light aircraft a century later.","They also learned that the front edge of a wing should be fairly thin and smoothly shaped, and that biplane wings could be placed one above the other with enough gap between them so they did not spoil each other's airflow. Each of these lessons went straight into the design of their next glider."]},{"heading":"The 1902 Glider and the Mystery Spin","paragraphs":["In late August 1902 the brothers were back at Kill Devil Hills, about 4 miles (6 km) south of the village of Kitty Hawk on the Outer Banks of North Carolina. Their old camp shed had been battered by storms, so they repaired it and added a little kitchen and living area. They slept on bunks rigged up near the roof and cooked their meals in the shed. Then they assembled their new glider, the third they had built.","It was a beauty. The wings spanned 32 feet 1 inch (about 9.8 metres), with an area of about 305 square feet (28 square metres), yet the whole machine weighed only around 112 to 117 pounds (about 51 to 53 kg). It was made of spruce and ash wood, covered with tightly stretched cotton cloth. As in 1900 and 1901, the elevator, the surface that tilts the nose up or down, stuck out in front of the wings. The pilot lay flat on the lower wing, in a padded cradle around his hips. Sliding his hips to one side pulled wires that twisted (warped) the wingtips to tilt the glider left or right. And at the back, new for 1902, stood a tail: a fixed double rudder made of two tall, narrow upright fins.","From the very first glides in September, the new machine lifted beautifully, just as the wind tunnel had promised. But a frightening problem soon appeared. Sometimes, when the pilot warped the wings to level the glider after a gust, it did not straighten out. Instead it slid sideways, swung around, and spun down into the sand with one wingtip digging in. The brothers called this 'well digging', because the wingtip seemed to be drilling a hole in the ground. Luckily the glides were low and the sand was soft, but it was clearly dangerous. The brothers came to realise that, in some situations, the fixed tail was actually making things worse.","The cause turned out to be a subtle effect of the wing warping itself. When one wingtip was twisted to give it more lift, it also got more drag. That extra drag held the wing back, swinging the nose in the wrong direction. Engineers today call this adverse yaw. With fixed fins at the back, the problem could get worse: as the glider slipped sideways, the air pushed on the fins in a way that helped spin the glider round even more."]},{"heading":"A Sleepless Night and a Movable Rudder","paragraphs":["According to a story the family told, Orville drank too much coffee one evening in early October 1902 and lay awake that night thinking about the spinning problem. By morning he had an answer: the fins at the back should not be fixed. They should move, so the pilot could swing the tail to stop the nose from turning the wrong way. His diary entry for 2 October 1902 notes that he had 'studied out a new vertical rudder'.","Wilbur liked the idea and added a brilliant improvement. Instead of giving the pilot yet another control to work, he suggested connecting the rudder to the same hip cradle that warped the wings. Then, whenever the pilot slid his hips to warp the wings, the rudder would automatically swing the right amount to cancel out the unwanted turning. The brothers took off one of the two fins and made the other movable, about 5 feet (1.5 metres) tall and 14 inches (36 centimetres) wide, able to swing about 30 degrees to either side. By 6 October it was ready.","The difference was dramatic. Now the glider could bank smoothly, turn and come out of the turn under control. On 9 October 1902 the brothers made what historians count as their first truly controlled turns. The 'well digging' problem was solved.","With this change, the Wrights had invented something that every airplane since has used: control around all three axes. Picture a plane with three imaginary lines through it. Turning around one line makes the nose go up or down. That is pitch, and the Wrights controlled it with their front elevator. Turning around the second line tilts the wings left or right. That is roll, which they controlled with wing warping. Turning around the third line swings the nose left or right. That is yaw, controlled by the new rudder. Later planes would use different parts, such as hinged ailerons instead of wing warping, but the idea of the pilot controlling all three at once is still the heart of how airplanes fly. You will explore the cockpit controls in detail in Part 12.","The brothers knew their idea was valuable. In March 1903 they applied for a patent on a 'Flying Machine' based on the 1902 glider. What it really protected was not a particular glider shape but their system of control, the idea of warping the wings together with a movable rudder."]},{"heading":"The Best Pilots in the World","paragraphs":["Once the glider could be controlled, the brothers flew it again and again. Between 19 September and 24 October 1902 they made somewhere between about 700 and more than 1,000 glides, depending on which records and counts you use. Many came in the last few weeks of October, when the wind blew steadily over the dunes. Some glides were more than 600 feet (180 metres) long, and the longest lasted up to about 26 seconds, in winds of over 30 miles per hour (48 km/h).","Picture a typical glide. Two people grip the wingtips and run downhill into the wind on the side of Big Kill Devil Hill, a sand dune about 90 feet (27 metres) high. The pilot lies flat on the lower wing, chin forward, hands on the elevator lever. The wings bite into the wind and lift him off the sand. Then for many seconds he glides down the slope just a few feet above the ground, making small corrections with his hips and hands, until the skids hiss into the sand. Then everyone walks the glider back up the dune to do it all again.","The camp became busy that October. Octave Chanute visited, along with Augustus Herring, an experienced glider builder, and George Spratt, a young doctor fascinated by flight who had visited in 1901 too. The brothers' older brother Lorin also came along. Chanute had a glider of his own design brought along for testing, but it did not fly well. Watching the Wrights glide so easily, the visitors could see that the bicycle makers from Dayton had moved far ahead of everyone else.","This is one of the most important points in the whole story. Other inventors had built engines and machines and then hoped to learn flying afterwards, often in a single risky attempt. The Wrights did the opposite. They learned to fly first, safely, in hundreds of short, low glides on soft sand. By the end of 1902 they had more experience controlling a winged machine in the air than anyone else alive. They were not only the best designers of flying machines. They were also the most skilled pilots in the world.","Wilbur and Orville packed up camp at the end of October 1902 feeling very different from the year before. The wings worked. The controls worked. They knew how to fly. There was just one thing missing: power. They needed an engine to drive the glider forward, and propellers to turn that engine's power into thrust. They assumed they could simply buy a suitable engine. They wrote to engine makers asking for one that weighed no more than about 180 pounds (82 kg) yet produced at least 8 horsepower. The replies were disappointing. Nobody could sell them the right engine. And when they looked for a book that explained how to design airplane propellers, they discovered there was no such book at all."]}],"deepDive":"Historians of technology often say the Wrights' greatest invention was not the airplane but their method. Before 1901, most designers worked with a lift equation of the form L = k S V squared CL, where L is lift, S is wing area, V is airspeed, CL is a coefficient for the wing shape and angle, and k is the 'Smeaton coefficient', a number meant to capture the pressure of moving air. The traditional value of k, about 0.005 in the units of the time, came from 18th-century work on windmills, and it was never checked very carefully for small, slow wings. Combining their 1901 kite measurements with their tunnel data, Wilbur concluded k was nearer 0.0033. Modern values work out at about 0.00326 in the same units, so their estimate was remarkably good. Because the old k was more than 50 percent too high, designers had been overestimating lift by the same amount. The tunnel itself was modest: a roughly 6-foot box with a 16-inch square test section and air at around 25 to 35 mph. Its genius was the balances. Rather than measuring forces directly, which was very hard with 1901 instruments, the lift balance compared the test wing with flat reference plates, and the drag balance measured the ratio of drag to lift. Ratios cancel out errors in wind speed and air density, which is exactly why the Wrights could trust their numbers. They tested about 200 models in quick screening runs and took detailed measurements of about 38. The results confirmed what Wenham and Browning had suggested decades earlier: high aspect ratio wings give a better lift-to-drag ratio, which NASA estimates rose to about 8 for the 1902 glider, roughly a third better than their previous gliders. Historians still debate how much the Wrights' data departed from Lilienthal's. The modern view is that Lilienthal's tables were fairly accurate for his own wing shape and test method, but the Wrights had applied them to a different wing with a different aspect ratio, and combined them with a wrong value of k. The deeper lesson, one that engineers still teach, is that data measured under one set of conditions cannot be blindly reused under another.","funFact":"The Wrights' wind tunnel balances, which helped produce the data for the world's first successful airplane, were built from bicycle spoke wire and scrap metal, including (according to many accounts) old hacksaw blades.","check":{"q":"After their wind tunnel tests, what value did the Wrights decide was closer to the correct Smeaton coefficient?","a":"About 0.0033","wrong":["About 0.05","Exactly 1","About 0.0054"]},"sources":[{"title":"Wright brothers","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Wright_brothers"},{"title":"History of Wind Tunnels","publisher":"NASA Glenn Research Center","url":"https://www.grc.nasa.gov/WWW/K-12/WindTunnel/history.html"},{"title":"Wright glider (1900, 1901, 1902 and 1911 gliders)","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Wright_Glider_of_1902"},{"title":"Wind tunnel","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Wind_tunnel"},{"title":"Wright Brothers National Memorial","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Wright_Brothers_National_Memorial"},{"title":"The First Flight","publisher":"National Park Service, Wright Brothers National Memorial","url":"https://www.nps.gov/wrbr/learn/historyculture/thefirstflight.htm"},{"title":"Researching the Wright Way","publisher":"Smithsonian National Air and Space Museum","url":"https://airandspace.si.edu/researching-wright-way"}],"photos":[{"src":"img/deep/airplane/07-1.jpg","alt":"A replica of the Wright brothers' wooden wind tunnel (1901) at the Deutsches Museum in Munich.","file":"Wright Brothers Wind Tunnel Replica.jpg","url":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Wright_Brothers_Wind_Tunnel_Replica.jpg","author":"MLWatts","license":"CC BY-SA 3.0"},{"src":"img/deep/airplane/07-2.jpg","alt":"October 1902: the Wrights' new glider is launched over the sand at Kill Devil Hills, with Wilbur lying at the controls.","file":"1902 Wright Brothers' Glider Tests - GPN-2002-000125.jpg","url":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:1902_Wright_Brothers%27_Glider_Tests_-_GPN-2002-000125.jpg","author":"Unknown author","license":"Public domain"},{"src":"img/deep/airplane/07-3.jpg","alt":"Drawing of the Wright brothers' wind-tunnel balance, made of thin metal strips, with a dial to read the angle.","file":"Balance des frères Wright, schéma.png","url":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Balance_des_fr%C3%A8res_Wright,_sch%C3%A9ma.png","author":"Unknown, Smithsonian 1935","license":"CC0"}],"words":4072},{"n":8,"title":"Twelve Seconds That Changed the World","when":"1903","summary":"The Wright brothers' mechanic Charlie Taylor built a light engine, and the brothers invented their own propellers. On a freezing morning, 17 December 1903, Orville flew their Flyer for 12 seconds. It was the first time a person had flown a powered airplane under control, and by lunchtime Wilbur had flown for almost a whole minute.","sections":[{"heading":"Nobody Would Sell Them an Engine, So Charlie Built One","paragraphs":["At the end of 1902 the Wright brothers had a glider that worked and the skill to fly it. To turn it into a powered airplane they needed an engine. In December 1902 they wrote to about ten companies that made engines, mostly for the new motor cars that were beginning to rattle along American roads. Their request was simple, but very demanding: an engine weighing no more than about 180 pounds (82 kg) that could produce at least 8 horsepower, and that would run smoothly without shaking the delicate wooden airplane apart.","The answers were disappointing. Most engine makers did not have anything that light and that powerful, and they were too busy with car customers to build a special engine for two unknown bicycle makers. The one engine that came close was far too heavy. If the Wrights wanted an engine, they would have to build it themselves.","This is where a quiet hero steps into the story. Charlie Taylor was a skilled machinist born in 1868 in a log cabin in Cerro Gordo, Illinois. He had come to work at the Wright Cycle Company in 1901, fixing bicycles so that Wilbur and Orville could spend more time on flying. By 1902 they trusted him to run the shop on his own. Taylor had never built an airplane engine. Nobody had built many of them. But he was a superb mechanic, and he was not afraid of a challenge.","Working from rough sketches the brothers drew, and with them constantly checking and discussing each step, Taylor built the engine in about six weeks in the bicycle shop in early 1903. He later recalled that they hardly made any proper drawings: the three of them would sketch a part on a scrap of paper, and he would pin the sketch up over his workbench and get to work. The tools were the ordinary machines of the workshop: a lathe for shaping metal, a drill press, files and hand tools, all driven by belts from the shop's small engine.","The finished engine was a four-cylinder, water-cooled gasoline engine, a bit like a simple car engine of the time but stripped down to save every possible ounce. Its most unusual feature was its crankcase, the main body that holds the moving parts. Instead of heavy cast iron, it was cast from aluminium mixed with a little copper. Today aluminium is everywhere, from drink cans to airplanes, but in 1903 it was still an expensive, unusual metal, and very few engines used it. The casting was made at a foundry near Dayton, and Taylor then bored out the holes for the cylinders by hand.","The pistons and their rings were made of cast iron. There was no fuel pump: gasoline flowed down by gravity from a tank holding about one US gallon (3.8 litres), hung from a strut on the wing. There was no proper carburettor either, only a simple chamber where the fuel mixed with air. There was no throttle to make the engine go faster or slower, and there were no spark plugs in the modern sense. It was about as simple as an engine could be while still working.","When the engine was first tested in the shop in February 1903, the aluminium crankcase cracked and had to be recast. The second version worked. It weighed about 180 pounds (82 kg) with everything attached, and it produced about 12 horsepower when it was running at about 1,025 revolutions per minute (turns of the crankshaft each minute). That was 50 percent more power than the brothers had asked for. They were delighted, because extra power meant they could afford a slightly heavier, sturdier airplane.","Twelve horsepower does not sound like much. A modern family car might have 100 to 200 horsepower, and a big airliner's engines are thousands of times more powerful. But for 1903 it was a brilliant achievement in lightness. And it was enough, because the Wrights had already done the hardest work: their wings made lift efficiently, and they knew exactly how much power they needed.","Charlie Taylor's name is less famous than the Wrights', but people who work on airplanes have not forgotten him. Today the US Federal Aviation Administration gives an award called the Charles Taylor Master Mechanic Award to mechanics who have spent 50 years or more keeping aircraft safe."]},{"heading":"Propellers Are Spinning Wings","paragraphs":["With the engine under way, the brothers turned to the propellers, expecting this to be the easy part. Ships had used propellers for decades. Surely, they thought, there would be some engineering book explaining how to design one. There was not. Ship propeller makers mostly worked by trial and error, building a propeller, testing it, and changing it until it worked well enough. Nobody had a real theory, and air is about 800 times less dense than water, so ship designs could not simply be copied.","The brothers' breakthrough was an idea that sounds obvious today but was new then: a propeller is really a wing that spins. Each blade is shaped like a wing, curved and tilted, and as it whirls round it meets the air at an angle, just like a wing moving forward. The 'lift' of a spinning blade points forwards instead of upwards, and that forward push is thrust. That meant they could use the wing data from their own wind tunnel to design the blades.","But it was tricky. A wing moves through the air at one speed, while a propeller blade moves slowly near its centre and very fast at its tip. On top of that, the whole airplane is moving forward while the blades turn, and the blades also push the air backwards, which changes the angle at which the air meets them. Everything depended on everything else. Orville later remembered how he and Wilbur argued about it for weeks, sometimes so hotly that they would end up swapping sides, each convinced by the other's arguments, and then argue all over again.","In the end they worked out their own method for calculating the best blade shape and twist. Their propellers were just over 8 feet (about 2.6 metres) across, carved from three layers of spruce wood glued together. The tips were covered with thin canvas to stop the wood from splitting, and the propellers were painted with aluminium paint. The brothers' own calculations are said to have predicted that they would turn about two-thirds of the engine's power into useful thrust. Modern tests of accurate reproductions found the real figure was even better, reaching around 75 to 82 percent at best, which is impressive for a first attempt and not far from wooden propellers designed many years later."]},{"heading":"Building the Flyer","paragraphs":["The airplane that would carry the engine, which the brothers simply called the Flyer, was bigger than any of their gliders. Its two wings stretched 40 feet 4 inches (about 12.3 metres) from tip to tip, with a total area of about 510 square feet (47 square metres), roughly the floor space of a small classroom. The frame was made of spruce, a light, strong wood, for the straight pieces, and ash, which bends without breaking, for the curved wing ribs. Over the frame the brothers stretched a fine cotton cloth called muslin, sold under the cheerful brand name 'Pride of the West'. They sewed it on at a slant so the threads ran diagonally, which helped stiffen the wings.","The engine sat on the lower wing, a little to the right of centre. The pilot lay flat on his stomach just to the left of it, in a hip cradle like the one on the 1902 glider. Because the engine was heavier than the pilot, the brothers made the right wing about 4 inches (10 centimetres) longer than the left, so it would lift a little more and keep the machine balanced. In front stood a double elevator to control pitch. At the back was a double rudder, linked to the wing-warping cradle exactly as in 1902.","Bicycle-style chains carried power from the engine to the two propellers, which were mounted behind the wings and pushed the airplane forward. One chain was crossed in a figure of eight so that the two propellers spun in opposite directions. That way the twisting effect of one propeller cancelled out the twist of the other, and the plane would not tend to roll to one side. The chain gears also slowed the propellers down: the engine turned about 23 times for every 8 turns of the propellers.","The Flyer had no wheels. Instead it rested on skids like a sled. To take off, it would sit on a small wheeled trolley on a wooden launching rail about 60 feet (18 metres) long, made of two-by-four planks covered with a thin strip of metal. The brothers jokingly called it the 'Junction Railroad'. The plane would run along the rail into the wind and lift off, leaving the trolley behind. The whole rail could be taken apart and carried anywhere, and it cost almost nothing. Empty, the Flyer weighed about 605 pounds (274 kg); with a pilot aboard it was around 745 pounds (338 kg). Altogether, the brothers built it for less than 1,000 dollars, paid from their own bicycle business."]},{"heading":"Meanwhile, on the Potomac River","paragraphs":["The Wrights were not the only people trying to fly in 1903. In Washington, D.C., one of the most famous scientists in America, Samuel Pierpont Langley, was preparing his own machine. Langley was the Secretary (the head) of the Smithsonian Institution, and in 1896 his unpiloted steam-powered models had flown about a kilometre over the Potomac River. In 1898 the US War Department gave him 50,000 dollars, and the Smithsonian added more, to build a full-sized airplane that could carry a person. Langley called it the Great Aerodrome.","Its engine was remarkable. Langley's assistant Charles Manly, a young engineer, had taken over an engine that a New York builder named Stephen Balzer could not finish to the required standard, and redesigned it. The result was a five-cylinder engine producing about 50 horsepower, roughly four times the power of the Wrights' engine, and it was light for its time. Manly was also chosen to be the pilot.","But Langley's approach was very different from the Wrights'. His machine was to be launched by a catapult from the roof of a houseboat anchored in the river, and it had no wheels because it was meant to land on the water. Nobody had practised flying it, and nobody had studied how to control it in the air the way the Wrights had with their hundreds of glides.","On 7 October 1903 the Great Aerodrome was catapulted off its houseboat near Quantico, Virginia. It dropped straight into the river. One reporter wrote that it went down 'like a handful of mortar'. Langley blamed the launching machinery. On 8 December 1903 they tried again near Washington. This time the frame broke apart as it left the catapult, and the Aerodrome flipped over and plunged into the icy water. Both times Manly was pulled out wet and shaken but unhurt.","Newspapers mocked the failures, and some members of Congress complained about wasting public money. Langley gave up his experiments. He died in 1906. Today historians believe the main problems were a frame too weak for its job and a lack of real control, the very problems the Wrights had spent years solving. Just nine days after Langley's last attempt, the bicycle makers from Dayton would show what careful testing could do."]},{"heading":"Broken Shafts and a Coin Toss","paragraphs":["The Wrights arrived back at Kill Devil Hills on 25 September 1903. They built a new wooden shed to hangar the Flyer next to their old living quarters and spent the autumn putting the airplane together. While they worked they also took out their 1902 glider whenever the wind was good, practising again and again so their flying skills would be sharp.","Autumn on the Outer Banks was harsh. Storms tore at the camp, rain dripped through the roof, and as winter approached the nights became bitterly cold. The brothers wrote home about wearing all their clothes and wrapping up in blankets to keep warm.","Then came mechanical trouble. When they ran the engine, it sometimes misfired, sending jolts through the propeller shafts. The shafts, which were hollow steel tubes, worked loose and then cracked. In late November Orville took the train all the way back to Dayton to make new shafts out of solid spring steel. He returned to camp on 11 December. Every day of delay brought winter closer, and the brothers wanted to be home for Christmas.","On 14 December 1903 everything was ready. The wind was light, so they laid the launching rail down the slope of Big Kill Devil Hill to help the Flyer pick up speed. They flipped a coin to decide who would fly first. Wilbur won. Several men from the nearby Kill Devil Hills Life-Saving Station, whose job was to rescue sailors from shipwrecks along the coast, came to help carry the machine up the hill.","The engine roared, Wilbur released the restraining wire, and the Flyer raced down the rail. Orville ran alongside, holding the wingtip to keep it level, but the machine soon outpaced him. It lifted off, but Wilbur pointed the nose up too steeply. The Flyer climbed a few feet, slowed down, and settled heavily into the sand after about three and a half seconds and about 105 feet (32 metres). A skid and part of the elevator were damaged. Wilbur took the blame, calling it an error due to lack of experience with this machine. The brothers did not count it as a real flight, because the plane had been launched downhill and had not kept flying. Repairs took a couple of days. The next try would be Orville's turn."]},{"heading":"Thursday, 17 December 1903","paragraphs":["The morning of Thursday, 17 December 1903 was freezing. Puddles near the camp were covered with ice, and a strong, cold wind blew from the north at about 20 to 27 miles per hour (32 to 43 km/h). It was risky to fly in such a wind, but the brothers decided to try. Strong wind actually helped in one way: a plane flying into a headwind needs less ground speed to get airborne.","They put a flag up as a signal to the lifesaving station, and men came over to help. Five local people saw what happened next: John T. Daniels, Adam Etheridge and Will Dough from the lifesaving station, W. C. Brinkley, a businessman from a nearby town, and Johnny Moore, a teenage boy from Nags Head. This time the rail was laid on flat ground, facing straight into the wind.","Orville set up the brothers' camera on its tripod, pointed at the end of the rail, and asked John Daniels to squeeze the rubber bulb that would open the shutter if the Flyer took to the air. Daniels had never taken a photograph before; one account says he had never even seen a camera. The camera used glass plates 5 by 7 inches in size instead of film.","At about 10:35 in the morning, Orville lay down in the cradle and released the wire. The Flyer moved forward along the rail, with Wilbur running alongside steadying the wingtip. After about 40 feet (12 metres) it lifted into the air. It bobbed up and down because the front elevator was very sensitive and Orville was still learning to handle it, rising to about 10 feet (3 metres) and then darting toward the ground. After 12 seconds the skids hit the sand about 120 feet (37 metres) from where it had taken off. Against that strong headwind its ground speed was only about 6.8 miles per hour (11 km/h), slower than a person jogging, but its speed through the air was about 34 miles per hour (55 km/h).","It was a short hop by today's standards. You could fit the whole flight inside the wingspan of a large modern airliner. But it was something completely new. For the first time, a machine carrying a person had raised itself into the air by its own engine power, moved forward without losing speed, and landed at a point as high as where it started, all under the pilot's control. Daniels, so excited he was not sure he had squeezed the bulb at all, had in fact taken one of the most famous photographs ever made. It shows the Flyer just off the rail, with Wilbur standing beside the wingtip, watching."]},{"heading":"Four Flights and a Gust of Wind","paragraphs":["The brothers did not stop there. They warmed their hands in the shed, carried the Flyer back and laid out the rail again. At around 11 o'clock Wilbur made the second flight, about 175 feet (53 metres) in roughly 12 seconds. About 20 minutes later Orville made the third, about 200 feet (61 metres) in 15 seconds. Each time the pilots were getting better at handling the twitchy elevator.","At about noon Wilbur set off on the fourth flight. The Flyer pitched up and down for the first few hundred feet, but then he got it under control and it flew steadily onward, low over the sand. It kept going and going. At around 800 feet a sudden dip brought the nose down and the machine struck the ground, breaking the frame that held the front elevator. Wilbur had been in the air for 59 seconds and had covered 852 feet (260 metres) over the ground. Because of the headwind, he had actually travelled through about half a mile (around 800 metres) of moving air.","The brothers carried the Flyer back to camp, planning to repair the elevator and maybe try to fly all the way to the weather station in Kitty Hawk, about 4 miles away. But as the men stood talking, a powerful gust caught the airplane and began to tumble it over. John Daniels grabbed hold of it and was carried along inside the frame as the Flyer flipped end over end. He came out bruised but not seriously hurt, and he liked to say for the rest of his life that he had survived the first airplane crash.","The Flyer was not so lucky. Ribs were broken, the engine was damaged and the chain guides were bent. It would never fly again. The brothers' flying for 1903 was over. But it did not matter. In four flights on a single cold morning, they had done what people had dreamed of for thousands of years. Balloons had carried people since 1783, and gliders had flown before, but these were the first sustained, powered, controlled flights in an airplane carrying a pilot.","It is worth remembering how many people made those 12 seconds possible. Cayley had worked out the basic shape of an airplane. Lilienthal had shown that humans could glide and had shared his data. Chanute had shared knowledge and encouragement. Charlie Taylor had built the engine. And five men from the Outer Banks had hauled the machine across the sand in the freezing wind."]},{"heading":"Inform Press: The World Barely Notices","paragraphs":["That afternoon the brothers walked about 4 miles (6 km) to the weather station in the village of Kitty Hawk, which had a telegraph line, and sent a message to their father, Bishop Milton Wright, in Dayton. The telegraph operators had to relay it along the line, and it arrived with a couple of mistakes. It read: 'Success four flights thursday morning all against twenty one mile wind started from Level with engine power alone average speed through air thirty one miles longest 57 seconds inform Press home Christmas.' It was signed 'Orevelle Wright', with Orville's name misspelled, and the longest flight had been written down as 57 seconds instead of 59.","Their brother Lorin took the news to the Dayton Journal newspaper. The editor was not impressed. If the flights had lasted 57 minutes, he reportedly said, that might be news, but 57 seconds was too short to matter. The story did not run.","Meanwhile, a telegraph operator along the line had leaked the message to a newspaper in Norfolk, Virginia, the Virginian-Pilot. On 18 December it published an exciting but largely made-up story, claiming among other things that the Flyer had flown three miles. A few other newspapers copied it. Many readers who saw the wildly wrong report simply did not believe it, especially after Langley's famous machine had crashed into the Potomac just days earlier.","In early January 1904 the brothers sent their own short, factual statement to the press, describing what had really happened. But public excitement did not follow. Most people, including many scientists, still did not believe a pair of bicycle makers from Ohio had solved a problem that famous inventors had failed at. The news faded quickly.","The Wrights did not mind as much as you might expect. They were cautious, private people, and they knew their real work was only beginning. Twelve seconds and 120 feet was a start, not a finish. They needed to fly longer, turn in circles and land safely again and again, and they needed to protect their invention with a patent. They also understood something deeper than most people of their time: exactly why their wings held them up. They had measured it, tested it and built it into every part of the Flyer. So how does a wing really hold a heavy machine up in the air? That is the question Part 9 will answer."]}],"deepDive":"Why do historians call the 17 December flights the first 'sustained, powered, controlled' airplane flights, when others had left the ground before? Each word matters. 'Powered' rules out gliders such as Lilienthal's. 'Sustained' means the machine kept flying under its own power without losing speed, and landed on ground as high as its takeoff point; hops by Ader's Eole or Maxim's test rig were brief and uncontrolled. 'Controlled' means the pilot could steer around all three axes. Orville's first flight barely met these tests, which is why many historians see Wilbur's 59-second flight as the most convincing. Some numbers show how close to the edge the Wrights were working. Charlie Taylor's engine produced about 12 horsepower from a displacement of about 3.3 litres and weighed about 180 pounds, giving roughly 15 pounds per horsepower. Langley and Manly's engine was far better, around 52 horsepower from an engine that was actually lighter, yet Langley's machine failed, because power without control and a sound structure was useless. The Wrights designed for a thrust of about 90 pounds and measured about 132 pounds, enough for a flying speed of around 30 to 35 mph. Their propellers, designed with their own theory of the spinning blade as a rotating wing, are one of their least famous but most impressive achievements; modern tests of replicas have measured peak efficiencies of about 82 percent. The fate of the Flyer itself became a long story. It was stored in Dayton and soaked in mud in the 1913 flood. A bitter feud began after the Smithsonian displayed a heavily modified Langley Aerodrome, which Glenn Curtiss had briefly hopped off a lake in 1914, with a label calling it the first machine 'capable' of piloted flight. In protest, Orville sent the Flyer to the Science Museum in London in 1928. Only after the Smithsonian published the truth about the 1914 modifications in 1942 did he agree it could come home. It went on display in Washington on 17 December 1948, 45 years to the day after it flew, and it now hangs in the National Air and Space Museum.","funFact":"The man who took the famous photo of the first airplane flight, John T. Daniels, had never used a camera before that morning.","check":{"q":"How long did Orville Wright's first powered flight last on 17 December 1903?","a":"12 seconds","wrong":["59 seconds","12 minutes","3 seconds"]},"sources":[{"title":"Wright Flyer","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Wright_Flyer"},{"title":"Wright brothers","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Wright_brothers"},{"title":"Charlie Taylor (mechanic)","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Charlie_Taylor_(mechanic)"},{"title":"The First Flight","publisher":"National Park Service, Wright Brothers National Memorial","url":"https://www.nps.gov/wrbr/learn/historyculture/thefirstflight.htm"},{"title":"Langley Aerodrome","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Langley_Aerodrome"},{"title":"Samuel Pierpont Langley","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Samuel_Pierpont_Langley"},{"title":"John T. Daniels","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/John_T._Daniels"},{"title":"Wright Brothers National Memorial","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Wright_Brothers_National_Memorial"}],"photos":[{"src":"img/deep/airplane/08-1.jpg","alt":"17 December 1903: Orville flies the Wright Flyer for the first time while Wilbur watches beside it.","file":"Wright First Flight 1903Dec17 (full restore 115).jpg","url":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Wright_First_Flight_1903Dec17_(full_restore_115).jpg","author":"John T. Daniels","license":"Public domain"},{"src":"img/deep/airplane/08-2.jpg","alt":"The 1903 Flyer's four-cylinder engine, chains and pilot's hip cradle, photographed in 1928.","file":"1903 Wright Flyer pilot seat and engine.jpg","url":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:1903_Wright_Flyer_pilot_seat_and_engine.jpg","author":"Attributed to Orville Wright (1871–1948).","license":"Public domain"},{"src":"img/deep/airplane/08-3.jpg","alt":"7 October 1903: Langley's Great Aerodrome on the catapult of its houseboat, ready to launch.","file":"Attempted Flight of Langley's Full Sized Plane, being readied for Launching, October 7th 1903. (50255040742).jpg","url":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Attempted_Flight_of_Langley%27s_Full_Sized_Plane,_being_readied_for_Launching,_October_7th_1903._(50255040742).jpg","author":"tormentor4555","license":"Public domain"}],"words":3912},{"n":9,"title":"How a Wing Really Works","when":"Science explainer","summary":"A wing is a clever tool for shoving air downward, and when it pushes air down, the air pushes the plane up. In this part you will find out why fast planes need long runways, what a stall really is, and how to test wings yourself with paper and a little breath.","sections":[{"heading":"A Tug-of-War in Four Directions","paragraphs":["Picture a jumbo jet sitting at the end of a runway. Fully loaded with passengers, bags and fuel, a big Boeing 747 can weigh around 400 tonnes. That is about as heavy as 60 or 70 African elephants. In a minute or so, that whole enormous machine will be climbing into the sky, held up by nothing you can see. How?","Back in Part 3 you met Sir George Cayley, who first sorted out the four forces that act on every airplane. Now it is time to look at them properly, because once you understand these four, almost everything else about flying makes sense.","The first force is weight. Weight is the pull of gravity, dragging the plane down toward the centre of the Earth. It includes everything on board: the metal of the plane itself, the people, the suitcases, the snacks and, very importantly, the fuel. A long-haul airliner can burn more than 100 tonnes of fuel on a single trip, so it gets lighter and lighter as the flight goes on.","The second force is lift. Lift is the upward push that the wings make as air flows around them. Lift is the force that fights weight. Scientists define lift as the part of the air's push that acts at right angles (perpendicular) to the direction the plane is moving through the air. In normal level flight that means straight up, but it is worth remembering that lift is not magic 'up-force'. If a plane tilts its wings, the lift tilts too.","The third force is thrust. Thrust is the forward push from the propellers or jet engines. The pilot controls it with the throttle: more throttle, more thrust.","The fourth force is drag. Drag is air resistance, the force that tries to slow the plane down. You feel drag when you stick your arm out while riding a bike fast, or when you try to run into a strong wind. Drag depends on the plane's shape, on how big it is, and very strongly on how fast it is going.","Here is the part that surprises many people. When a plane is cruising along steadily, at the same height and the same speed, the forces are balanced: lift exactly equals weight, and thrust exactly equals drag. A cruising airliner is not 'winning' against gravity. It is in a perfect tie. Isaac Newton's first law says that an object with balanced forces keeps doing what it was already doing. So a plane already moving at 900 kilometres per hour just keeps moving at 900 kilometres per hour, as long as the engines keep replacing the energy that drag steals.","Things only change when the forces become unbalanced. If thrust is bigger than drag, the plane speeds up. If drag is bigger, it slows down. If lift is bigger than weight, the plane curves upward. If weight wins, the plane sinks.","And what about a glider, which has no engine at all? A glider is always gently sliding downhill through the air, a bit like a bicycle coasting down a long slope. Part of its weight acts like a forward push, taking the place of thrust. To stay up for hours, glider pilots look for rising air, such as warm bubbles of air called thermals, or wind that is pushed upward by a ridge. Otto Lilienthal, whom you met in Part 5, used exactly this idea on his hills near Berlin."]},{"heading":"The Wing Is a Machine for Pushing Air Down","paragraphs":["If you could slice an airplane wing in half from front to back and look at the cut end, you would see a special shape called an airfoil. It is rounded and thick at the front, called the leading edge, and thin and sharp at the back, called the trailing edge. On most wings the top is more curved than the bottom. That curve is called camber, and the Wright brothers spent weeks in their wind tunnel (Part 7) finding the best amount of it.","Now picture the wing moving through the air. The wing usually meets the oncoming air at a slight tilt, with its front edge a little higher than its back edge. The angle between the wing and the oncoming air is called the angle of attack. For an airliner cruising high up, the angle of attack might be just a few degrees, so small you would hardly notice it.","Here is the key idea. As air flows past this tilted, curved wing, the wing bends the flow. Air that passes over the top follows the curved surface and leaves the trailing edge heading slightly downward. Air underneath is deflected downward too. Behind every flying wing, a huge amount of air is moving down. Scientists call this downward-moving air the downwash.","Remember Isaac Newton's third law: for every action there is an equal and opposite reaction. When you push off from the side of a swimming pool, the wall pushes you away. When a rocket blasts gas downward, the gas pushes the rocket up. In the same way, when a wing pushes a great mass of air downward, the air pushes the wing upward. That upward push is lift.","It helps to think about how much air we are talking about. A wing does not just nudge a thin layer of air near its surface. It affects a big region of air above and below it, stretching out for a distance about as large as the wing itself. A jumbo jet in flight is constantly throwing many tonnes of air downward every single second. If you have ever watched a helicopter land on grass, you have seen the same idea very clearly: the grass flattens in a circle because the spinning blades, which are really long thin wings, are hurling air down.","Angle of attack is one of the most important ideas in all of flying. Tilt the wing up a little more and it turns the air down more steeply, so it makes more lift. Tilt it down and it makes less. At small angles, the lift grows roughly in step with the angle: double a small angle of attack and you get about double the lift. Pilots change the angle of attack all the time by raising or lowering the nose with the elevator, which you will meet properly in Part 12.","This also explains a puzzle. If wings need a special curved shape to work, how do paper airplanes fly with completely flat wings? The answer is that a flat wing can still make lift if it meets the air at an angle. A tilted flat board turns the air downward too. It just does the job less efficiently than a well-shaped airfoil, making more drag for the same lift. A curved airfoil is like a better-designed tool, not a totally different trick."]},{"heading":"Pressure: The Other Half of the Story","paragraphs":["Ask a second scientist how a wing works and you might hear a different explanation. 'It's all about pressure,' they might say. 'The air pressure above the wing is lower than the air pressure below it, so the wing is pushed upward.' Are they disagreeing with Newton? Not at all. They are describing exactly the same flow of air from a different point of view.","First, what is air pressure? Air is made of trillions upon trillions of tiny molecules zooming around and bumping into everything. Every bump is a tiny push. Added together, all those pushes on a surface make pressure. At sea level, the air presses on every square metre of you with a force equal to the weight of about 10 tonnes. You do not get squashed because the air inside your body pushes back just as hard, and because the pressure comes from every direction at once.","Around a flying wing, the pressure is not the same everywhere. Over the curved top surface, the pressure drops below normal. Under the bottom surface, it is usually a little above normal. The air is pushing up on the bottom harder than it is pushing down on the top. Add up all those pushes over the whole wing, and the result is lift.","In the year 1738, a Swiss mathematician and physicist named Daniel Bernoulli published a book called Hydrodynamica about how fluids such as water and air move. In it he showed that where a fluid flows faster, its pressure is lower, and where it flows slower, its pressure is higher. This idea is called Bernoulli's principle. Around a wing, the air over the top really does speed up a lot, and its pressure really does drop. So Bernoulli's principle correctly describes what is going on.","How big is the pressure difference? Surprisingly small, compared with the air's normal pressure. Here is a rough calculation. A big 747 has a wing area of roughly 525 square metres. If it weighs about 400 tonnes, each square metre of wing has to hold up roughly 750 kilograms. That works out to a pressure difference of only about 7 per cent of normal sea-level air pressure. A tiny difference, spread over a huge area, can hold up something as heavy as a building.","So which explanation is right: Newton's 'the wing pushes air down' or Bernoulli's 'lower pressure on top'? The honest answer is both. They are not rival theories. The wing can only push air downward by creating these pressure differences, and the pressure differences exist because the air is being curved and turned by the wing. One cannot happen without the other. They are like two descriptions of a football match, one from a player on the pitch and one from a commentator high in the stands. Both are describing the same game."]},{"heading":"The Famous Explanation That Is Wrong","paragraphs":["If you look up 'how do wings work' in an old school textbook, you might find an explanation that goes like this. The top of the wing is curved, so it is longer than the flat bottom. Two little bits of air split apart at the front of the wing, one going over the top and one going underneath. They must meet up again at the back at exactly the same moment. Because the top path is longer, the air on top must go faster to keep up. Faster air means lower pressure, and so the wing is lifted.","It sounds neat and tidy. It has been printed in countless books and repeated in many classrooms. But it is wrong. Scientists call it the 'equal transit time' explanation, or sometimes the 'longer path' explanation, and they have been trying to get rid of it for years.","The first problem is simple: there is no rule of nature that says the two bits of air have to meet again. Air molecules are not friends who promised to hold hands at the back of the wing. When scientists actually film air flowing around a wing in a wind tunnel, using puffs of smoke released at the same instant above and below the wing, they see something striking. The air going over the top does not just keep up with the air underneath. It arrives at the back much earlier. It goes far faster than the 'equal time' idea predicts.","The second problem is that the numbers do not add up. On a typical wing, the top surface is only a few per cent longer than the bottom. If that small difference were the whole story, the air on top would only speed up a tiny bit, and the wing would make only a small fraction of the lift that real wings make. Real airplanes would never get off the ground.","The third problem is that the explanation cannot cope with all sorts of things that actually fly. Paper airplanes have flat wings with the same length on top and bottom, and they fly. Many stunt planes use symmetric wings that are curved the same amount on top and bottom, and they fly perfectly well. And aerobatic pilots regularly fly upside down. If lift came only from a longer curved top surface, an upside-down plane would be pushed toward the ground. Instead, the pilot simply tilts the nose so that the upside-down wing meets the air at a suitable angle of attack, and the wing turns the air downward again.","So remember the true version: what matters most is the shape of the wing together with its angle of attack, which make the wing bend the airflow downward. The air over the top does speed up and the pressure there does drop, just as Bernoulli said. But it speeds up because of the way the wing turns the flow, not because of any promise to meet its partner at the trailing edge."]},{"heading":"Stall: When a Wing Gives Up","paragraphs":["Since tilting a wing up makes more lift, you might wonder why pilots do not just point the nose sharply upward whenever they want lots of lift. The answer is a dangerous effect called a stall.","Air flowing over the top of a wing is a little like a crowd of people following a curving path. As long as the curve is gentle, everyone keeps to the path. But if the path suddenly turns too sharply, people near the edge cannot follow it and spill off to the side. The air over a wing behaves in a similar way. At small and medium angles of attack, it hugs the curved top surface smoothly all the way to the trailing edge. But as the angle gets steeper and steeper, the air can no longer follow the curve. It breaks away from the top of the wing, starting near the back and creeping forward. Behind the point where it breaks away, the air tumbles around in messy swirls. Scientists call this breaking away flow separation.","When the flow separates, the wing stops turning the air smoothly downward, and lift drops suddenly. Drag shoots up at the same time. That sudden loss of lift is a stall. For most wings, it happens at an angle of attack of roughly 15 degrees (usually somewhere between about 8 and 20 degrees), although the exact angle depends on the wing's shape and surface and on the speed and size of the wing.","There is one very important thing to understand about the word 'stall'. In a car, 'stalling' means the engine stops. In flying, it means something completely different. An airplane stall has nothing to do with the engine. A plane with all its engines roaring at full power can still stall if its wings meet the air at too steep an angle. Gliders, which have no engine at all, can stall too. A stall is all about the wing and the airflow.","Why does a stall happen at low speed? Remember that lift depends both on angle of attack and on speed. When a plane flies slowly, its wings meet less air each second, so the pilot must raise the nose to a bigger angle of attack to keep enough lift to match the weight. Slow down enough and that angle reaches the critical angle, and the wing stalls. The slowest speed at which a plane can fly level without stalling is called its stall speed. Stall speed goes up when the plane is heavier, and it also goes up in steep turns, because the wings have to work harder to pull the plane around the curve.","Stalls were a serious danger in the early days of flying. Many pioneers were hurt in stalls close to the ground; Otto Lilienthal's fatal crash, which you read about in Part 5, is usually blamed on a stall. Today, pilots train carefully to recognise the warning signs, such as the plane starting to shake or feel mushy, and they practise recovering high in the sky where there is lots of room. The cure is simple but goes against instinct: lower the nose to reduce the angle of attack, so the air can flow smoothly over the wing again, and add power. A properly handled stall in a small training plane usually costs only a few tens of metres of height.","Modern airplanes also have warning gadgets. Small planes often have a stall warning horn that beeps or wails as the wing nears the critical angle. Many airliners have a 'stick shaker' that rattles the pilot's controls, impossible to ignore, and computers that help keep the plane away from stalling angles. Some wings even have small fins on top called vortex generators, which stir the air near the surface so it clings to the wing a little longer."]},{"heading":"Speed Squared: Why Big Jets Need Long Runways","paragraphs":["Here is one of the most useful facts in all of aviation: lift grows with the square of speed. 'Square' means a number multiplied by itself. If you double the speed of a wing, the lift does not just double. It goes up by about two times two, which is four times. Triple the speed and you get about three times three, or nine times the lift, as long as the angle of attack stays the same.","Why the square? When a wing moves faster, two things happen at once. It meets more air every second, and it hits that air harder. Twice as much air, each bit turned down twice as hard, gives roughly four times the push. Drag follows the same kind of rule, which is why a strong wind feels so much fiercer than a breeze only half as fast.","This rule explains runways. A plane cannot take off until its wings are making enough lift to match its weight. A small two-seat training plane like a Cessna 150 is light and has big wings for its weight, so it can lift off at only about 100 kilometres per hour, after a run of a few hundred metres. A big airliner is far heavier for its wing size, so it must go much faster before it can fly. Jetliners typically take off at roughly 240 to 290 kilometres per hour. Reaching that speed takes a long run, which is why big international airports have runways 3 or even 4 kilometres long.","Other things matter too. A heavier plane needs a higher takeoff speed and a longer run, so a jet packed with fuel for a very long flight needs more runway than the same jet on a short hop. Hot air is thinner than cold air, and the air high in the mountains is thinner than air near the sea, so airports in hot, high places need extra-long runways. A headwind helps, because the wings are already meeting moving air before the plane even starts rolling. That is why planes almost always take off and land facing into the wind.","Landing has the same problem in reverse. Pilots want to touch down as slowly as possible, so the plane is easier to stop and safer if something goes wrong. But flying slowly means a big angle of attack and the risk of a stall. The clever solution is to change the shape of the wing.","If you have ever sat by the window in an airliner, you may have seen the wing seem to unfold before landing. Large panels called flaps slide out from the back of the wing and tilt downward. Flaps make the wing more curved and, on many planes, larger too, so it can make enough lift at a lower speed. One famous type, the Fowler flap, invented by the American engineer Harlan Fowler in 1924, slides backward before it tilts down, adding both area and curve. Along the front edge, many airliners also have slats, which move forward and open a narrow slot. The slot lets fast air flow from under the wing to the top, helping the airflow cling to the curved upper surface so the wing can reach a bigger angle of attack before it stalls.","With flaps and slats out, an airliner's wing can make up to roughly twice as much lift before stalling as the clean wing alone. The cost is extra drag, which is useful for slowing down to land but wasteful in cruise. So after takeoff, the pilots tuck the flaps and slats away again, and the wing goes back to its slim, fast shape. A modern airliner wing is really a shape-changing machine."]},{"heading":"Be a Wing Scientist: Experiments to Try","paragraphs":["Experiment 1: the paper airplane test. Fold a simple dart-shaped paper airplane and throw it gently, level, across a room or hallway. Then make small changes, one at a time, and test each change several times. Bend the back edges of both wings up a few millimetres, like tiny elevators, and see if the nose rises. Bend them down instead. Add a paper clip to the nose to move the weight forward. Try bigger wings and narrower wings. Keep a notebook of what happens. This is exactly the method the Wrights used: change one thing, test it, write it down. You may notice that if the nose points up too steeply, the plane suddenly drops or wobbles. That is a tiny stall.","Experiment 2: the hand out of the window. This one needs an adult driver, a quiet road and slow speeds, and you must never put your arm out near traffic, poles, hedges or other cars. If an adult says it is safe, sit in the back seat and hold your flat hand just outside the open window, fingers together, palm facing down, like a wing. Tilt the front of your hand up very slightly. You will feel your hand rise. You have just given your 'wing' a positive angle of attack. Tilt it down and your hand is pushed down. Now tilt it up very steeply, and you will feel the lift turn into a big backward push of drag: your hand is behaving like a stalled wing. If you notice the push is much stronger when the car goes a little faster, you are feeling the speed-squared rule.","Experiment 3: the paper strip. Cut a strip of paper about 3 centimetres wide and 20 centimetres long. Hold one end just under your bottom lip so the strip hangs down and curves away from you. Now blow steadily across the top. The strip rises! This is a classic science demonstration, and it is often explained by saying 'fast air has low pressure'. Scientists point out that the real explanation is a bit more interesting. Air coming out of your mouth starts at about normal pressure. What lifts the paper is that your stream of breath curves to follow the paper's curved top, and when air is forced to bend around a curve, the pressure on the inside of the curve drops. That is very close to what happens over the top of a real wing, where the air bends to follow the curved upper surface."]},{"heading":"Knowing It Before the Textbooks Did","paragraphs":["Here is an amazing thing to think about. In 1903, nobody in the world could fully explain how a wing makes lift. The mathematics that explains how a wing creates its flow pattern was being worked out in the early 1900s by scientists such as Wilhelm Kutta in Germany and Nikolai Zhukovsky in Russia, and Ludwig Prandtl in Germany was just beginning to study the thin layer of air close to a surface, the layer where flow separation and stalls begin. Their theories took years to develop.","The Wright brothers did not wait for the theory. They measured. With their wind tunnel, their balances made from bicycle spokes and scrap metal, and hundreds of glides on the sand at Kill Devil Hills, they learned exactly how much lift and drag their wings made at different angles of attack. They felt stalls through their own bodies. They learned to balance the four forces with their hands and hips. In 1903 they used that knowledge to fly.","By the end of 1905, as you will see in the next part, they could turn, circle and stay in the air for more than half an hour. They understood the practical science of the wing better than anyone alive. There was just one problem. Almost nobody believed them."]}],"deepDive":"Engineers write lift as L = 1/2 x rho x v^2 x S x CL, where rho is air density, v is true airspeed, S is wing area and CL is the lift coefficient, a single number that wraps up the effect of the wing's shape and angle of attack. The equation explains a lot at a glance. Because v is squared, a 10 per cent drop in speed costs about 19 per cent of the lift. Because rho appears, a plane taking off from a hot, high airport, where the air may be 20 per cent thinner than at a cool coastal one, needs noticeably more speed and runway. For an ordinary wing, CL rises almost in a straight line with angle of attack until it reaches a maximum, usually below about 1.5 for a plain airfoil; past that point the boundary layer, the thin sticky layer of air next to the surface, separates and the wing stalls. Multi-element flaps and slats can push the maximum above 3, which is why airliners can land at a fraction of their cruising speed. Why is the 'Newton versus Bernoulli' argument so persistent? Partly because each popular version leaves something out. 'The wing deflects air downward' is true, but it does not explain how a wing affects air far above it, or how the pressure field is set up. 'Faster air, lower pressure' is also true, but on its own it does not say why the air speeds up. A fuller account uses the idea of circulation: a wing with a sharp trailing edge forces the flow to leave the trailing edge smoothly (the Kutta condition), which sets up a net circulating motion of air around the wing, faster over the top and slower underneath. The Kutta-Zhukovsky theorem, from the early 1900s, links that circulation directly to lift. Pressure differences and the curving of the flow then sustain each other; neither is simply the cause of the other. In three dimensions, air also spills around the wingtips from the high-pressure underside to the low-pressure top, forming trailing wingtip vortices. These tilt the wing's total force slightly backward, creating 'induced drag', the price a wing pays for making lift. Long, slender wings reduce it, which is why gliders and fuel-saving airliners have such long wings and why many modern jets carry upturned winglets.","funFact":"Aerobatic planes can fly upside down for long periods, which shows that lift does not depend on the wing's top being curved: the pilot simply holds the upside-down wing at an angle of attack that turns the air downward.","check":{"q":"What causes a wing to stall?","a":"Its angle of attack gets too steep, so the airflow separates from the top of the wing and lift suddenly drops.","wrong":["The engine runs out of fuel and stops","The air on top fails to meet the air underneath at the trailing edge","The plane flies faster than the speed of sound"]},"sources":[{"title":"Four Forces on an Airplane","publisher":"NASA Glenn Research Center","url":"https://www1.grc.nasa.gov/beginners-guide-to-aeronautics/four-forces-on-an-airplane/"},{"title":"What is Lift?","publisher":"NASA Glenn Research Center","url":"https://www1.grc.nasa.gov/beginners-guide-to-aeronautics/what-is-lift/"},{"title":"Lift (force)","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Lift_(force)"},{"title":"Bernoulli's principle","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bernoulli%27s_principle"},{"title":"Stall (fluid dynamics)","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Stall_(fluid_dynamics)"},{"title":"Flap (aeronautics)","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Flap_(aeronautics)"},{"title":"Takeoff","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Takeoff"}],"photos":[{"src":"img/deep/airplane/09-1.jpg","alt":"Computer diagram of air flowing around a wing shape: the air rises in front and is turned downward behind.","file":"Streamlines relative to airfoil.png","url":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Streamlines_relative_to_airfoil.png","author":"Crowsnest (talk)","license":"CC BY-SA 3.0"},{"src":"img/deep/airplane/09-2.jpg","alt":"An Airbus A319 wing during landing: flaps fully extended and spoilers raised.","file":"EasyJet A319 wing spoilers.jpg","url":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:EasyJet_A319_wing_spoilers.jpg","author":"John Haslam from Dornoch, Scotland","license":"CC BY 2.0"},{"src":"img/deep/airplane/09-3.jpg","alt":"An early flow photo (about 1915): air tears away from the top of a steep wing in swirling eddies, causing a stall.","file":"1915ca abger fluegel (cropped and mirrored).jpg","url":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:1915ca_abger_fluegel_(cropped_and_mirrored).jpg","author":"Unknown author","license":"CC BY 3.0 de"}],"words":4381},{"n":10,"title":"Proving It to the World","when":"1904 – 1909","summary":"After their first flights, the Wright brothers practised in a cow field until they could fly in big circles for more than half an hour. But they kept their airplane hidden, and many people called them liars, until Wilbur flew in France in 1908 and amazed everyone who watched.","sections":[{"heading":"Flying in a Cow Pasture","paragraphs":["The 1903 Flyer had made history, but it had also been wrecked by a gust of wind, and it had only ever flown in straight lines for less than a minute. A machine like that was not much use to anyone. Wilbur and Orville Wright knew they still had a lot to learn. They needed a practice field closer to home, where they could fly week after week without travelling hundreds of kilometres to the sand dunes of North Carolina.","They found it about 13 kilometres (8 miles) northeast of Dayton, Ohio. It was a big, bumpy pasture called Huffman Prairie, owned by a Dayton banker named Torrence Huffman. He let the brothers use it without paying any rent. The only condition was that they had to move his cows and horses out of the way before they flew. The field was not perfect. It was dotted with tree stumps and anthills, a tall thorny tree stood near one edge, and the winds were weaker and more changeable than at Kitty Hawk. But it had one great advantage: an electric trolley line ran past it, so the brothers could ride out from their bicycle shop in Dayton whenever the weather looked good.","Their new machine, the Flyer II, looked a lot like the 1903 Flyer, but it had a new, slightly more powerful engine and sturdier parts. In May 1904 the brothers invited newspaper reporters out to see it fly. It was a flop. The engine was not running well and the wind died away, and the machine just slid off the end of its launching rail. A second try a few days later ended with a short hop and a heavy landing. The reporters went home unimpressed, and most newspapers decided the story of the flying brothers was not worth following.","Without strong winds to help, the Flyer II struggled to get up to flying speed on its short wooden launching rail. So in September 1904 the Wrights built a clever starting device. It was a tall wooden tower, called a derrick, with a heavy weight hanging from the top. A rope ran from the weight, down through pulleys, and along the rail to the airplane. When the weight was released and fell, it yanked the airplane forward along the rail like a giant catapult. At first they used a weight of about 360 kilograms (800 pounds), and later they increased it to around 730 kilograms (1,600 pounds). With the catapult, they could take off even on calm days.","Now they could practise in earnest. The hardest thing to learn was turning. Going straight is one thing; banking a flying machine into a smooth, safe turn without sliding sideways or stalling is quite another. On 20 September 1904, Wilbur did something no one had ever done before. He flew the Flyer II all the way around in a complete circle, covering about 1,240 metres (4,080 feet) in 1 minute and 16 seconds, and landed safely back near where he had started.","One person outside the family saw it. His name was Amos Root, a businessman from Medina, Ohio, who published a magazine about beekeeping called Gleanings in Bee Culture. Root was fascinated by new technology, and he had travelled from his home to watch the brothers practise. He wrote an excited account of the circling flight for his beekeeping readers, describing it as like a locomotive without wheels, flying through the air on white wings. It was one of the first eyewitness reports of a powered airplane turning in the air, and it appeared not in a famous newspaper but in a magazine about bees.","By the end of 1904, the brothers had made 105 flights at Huffman Prairie. That sounds impressive, but most were very short, and all of them added together came to only about 49 minutes in the air. Their longest flight lasted just over 5 minutes. They had also crashed several times, because the Flyer II kept bobbing up and down in pitch. They even bolted about 32 kilograms of iron bars near the front elevator to try to calm it. Clearly, something about the design was still not right."]},{"heading":"Flyer III: The First Truly Practical Airplane","paragraphs":["Over the winter of 1904 to 1905, the Wrights built a third machine, the Flyer III. They reused the engine and much of the hardware from the Flyer II, and at first the new airplane looked and behaved much like the old one. On 14 July 1905, Orville crashed it badly. The Flyer swooped up and down, then slammed into the ground. The machine was badly smashed, but luckily Orville was only shaken and bruised.","Instead of patching it up, the brothers thought carefully about the problem. They rebuilt the Flyer III with a much bigger front elevator and a bigger rudder, and they moved both of them farther away from the wings. This gave the controls more leverage, the way a longer spanner makes it easier to turn a stubborn bolt. They also gave the pilot a separate control for the rudder, instead of linking it automatically to the wing warping. Now the pilot could combine roll and yaw however he needed. These changes fixed the bobbing problem that had troubled all their earlier machines.","The difference was dramatic. Within weeks, flights were lasting longer than 20 minutes. The brothers could bank, turn, fly figure-eights and circle the field again and again until their fuel ran out. They had learned to handle a powered airplane as surely as they had once learned to handle a bicycle.","On 5 October 1905, Wilbur took off and circled Huffman Prairie again and again. He stayed up for 39 minutes and 23 seconds and flew about 39 kilometres (24 miles). He only came down because the fuel tank was empty. That single flight was longer than all of their flights in 1903 and 1904 added together. Across 1905, the brothers made 50 flights and spent about 262 minutes, more than four hours, in the air.","Many historians call the 1905 Flyer III the world's first practical airplane: a machine that could take off, stay up for a long time, go where its pilot wanted, and land safely, again and again. The brothers knew exactly what they had achieved. And then, at the very moment they had solved the problem of flight, they stopped flying.","The Flyer III still exists. Today it is displayed in the Wright Brothers Aviation Center at Carillon Historical Park in Dayton, after a restoration that Orville himself helped with before his death in 1948. It is the only fixed-wing airplane that has been named a National Historic Landmark. Huffman Prairie is now part of Dayton Aviation Heritage National Historical Park, and it still looks much as it did then: a wide, grassy field."]},{"heading":"Grounded on Purpose: Fliers or Liars?","paragraphs":["Why would two inventors stop flying just when they had finally cracked it? The answer has to do with money, patents and fear of being copied.","The Wrights had spent years of their lives and much of their savings on flying. They believed their control system, the combination of wing warping, elevator and rudder that let a pilot balance and steer, was their great invention, and they wanted to be paid for it. In March 1903, even before their first powered flight, they had applied for a patent on their 1902 glider's control system. A patent is a legal document that gives an inventor the right to stop others from copying an invention without permission for a number of years. Their patent was finally granted on 22 May 1906, as US Patent number 821,393.","The brothers worried that if they flew in public, or let anyone photograph their airplane closely, clever rivals would copy their ideas before the patent was secure or before they had signed any deals. So they dismantled the Flyer III in November 1905 and did not fly at all in 1906 or 1907. Instead, they tried to sell their airplane to governments. In October 1905 they wrote to the United States War Department, the British War Office and others. But there was a catch: the Wrights refused to show the machine until a buyer signed a contract, and the buyers refused to sign a contract until they had seen it fly. The US Army's answer was that it would do nothing until a machine had actually been shown to fly with a pilot aboard.","Meanwhile, in Europe, especially in France, many aviation enthusiasts simply did not believe the Wrights' story. Two bicycle makers from Ohio, flying in circles for over half an hour, with hardly any photographs and no public demonstrations? It seemed too good to be true. French newspapers called the brothers 'bluffeurs', which means bluffers. In February 1906 the Paris edition of the New York Herald wrote that the Wrights were 'either fliers or liars'. Ernest Archdeacon, a leading member of the Aéro-Club de France, was openly scornful.","To be fair to the doubters, the Wrights had not given them much to go on. There were letters, a few reports from local people and Amos Root's article in a bee magazine. But science runs on evidence that others can check, and the brothers were keeping most of their evidence locked away. Their secrecy protected their invention, but it also meant that, for a few years, other inventors in Europe believed they were racing to be first, and the Wrights' reputation suffered."]},{"heading":"Santos-Dumont and the 14-bis","paragraphs":["While the Wrights' airplane sat in pieces in Dayton, Paris was buzzing with excitement about a small, stylish man from Brazil. Alberto Santos-Dumont was the son of a rich coffee-plantation owner, and he had moved to Paris as a young man. He was already famous there for flying airships, which are balloons shaped like long cigars with engines and propellers. Parisians had seen him float over the rooftops in his little airships, and the story goes that he sometimes parked one outside a café while he stopped for a drink.","In 1906 he turned to airplanes. His machine looked very strange to modern eyes. It was a biplane built from box-kite-like cells, inspired by Lawrence Hargrave's box kites from Part 4. The wings were at the back, and a box-shaped steering cell stuck out at the front, so the 14-bis seemed to fly tail-first. This front-steering layout is called a canard, from the French word for duck, because the long neck at the front reminded people of a duck in flight. The pilot stood up in a small basket. A light French Antoinette engine of about 50 horsepower drove a propeller at the back.","The odd name came from how it was first tested. Santos-Dumont hung the new airplane underneath his airship Number 14 to see how it behaved in the air, so he called it '14-bis', meaning something like '14 again' or '14 part two'.","On 13 September 1906 he managed a tiny hop of a few metres. On 23 October 1906, at the Bagatelle park on the edge of Paris, he flew more than 50 metres in front of official observers from the Aéro-Club de France, winning a prize for the first flight of over 25 metres. A delighted crowd cheered. Then he added control surfaces to the wings, an early form of the hinged flaps you will learn about in Part 12, to help keep the wings level. On 12 November 1906, again at Bagatelle, he flew about 220 metres in roughly 21 to 22 seconds, at a height of a few metres.","These were the first powered airplane flights in Europe to be officially watched and measured. For many people in France, and especially in Brazil, Santos-Dumont became the true hero of flight. He had flown in public, on wheels, from a standing start, without a catapult, and he was generous with his ideas: he did not try to keep his designs secret.","But compare the numbers. Santos-Dumont's best flight in 1906 lasted about 21 seconds and travelled in a nearly straight line. A year earlier, in their cow pasture in Ohio, the Wrights had flown in circles for 39 minutes. Most historians give Santos-Dumont fair credit as a brilliant, brave pioneer and Europe's first airplane flier, while recognising that the Wrights were far ahead in the hardest part of the problem: control."]},{"heading":"Other Claims: Whitehead and Pearse","paragraphs":["Whenever something important is invented, other people come forward, sometimes many years later, with stories that someone else did it first. Flying is no different. Two of the most famous rival claims come from opposite sides of the world.","Gustave Whitehead was a German-born mechanic living in Bridgeport, Connecticut. On 18 August 1901, a local newspaper, the Bridgeport Herald, printed an exciting article saying that four days earlier, Whitehead had flown his machine, called Number 21, about half a mile (800 metres) near Fairfield, Connecticut. The article had no author's name and was illustrated with a drawing, not a photograph. A photograph of the flight was supposedly taken, but no such picture has ever been found, and many researchers believe it never existed.","Since then, people have argued about Whitehead for more than a hundred years. In 2013, an editorial in the respected aircraft reference book Jane's All the World's Aircraft supported his claim, which caused a big stir, though the publisher later said the piece was the editor's personal opinion. That same year, the state of Connecticut passed a law honouring Whitehead's flight. But most aviation historians, including the Royal Aeronautical Society in Britain, have looked carefully at the evidence and concluded that it does not show a controlled, powered flight. Whitehead built engines and machines and clearly loved flying, but he left no reliable record, no surviving photograph of a flight, and no successful aircraft that others could copy.","Richard Pearse was a farmer and inventor near Waitohi, close to Temuka in the South Canterbury region of New Zealand. Working alone on his farm, he built a monoplane, an airplane with a single wing, made of bamboo and fabric, with an engine of his own design. Some people who lived nearby later remembered seeing him take off and land, possibly as early as 31 March 1903. In one account, his machine rose into the air and ended up stuck in a tall gorse hedge.","The trouble is that most of these memories were collected 50 years or more after the events, and the exact dates are uncertain. Pearse himself never claimed to have beaten the Wrights. In letters and interviews he described his early efforts as short, uncontrolled hops, and he suggested his serious attempts came after 1903. He was a remarkable, imaginative inventor working far from anyone who could help him, and New Zealand rightly remembers him. But there is no solid evidence that he made a sustained, controlled, powered flight.","This is a good lesson in how history works. Exciting stories are not enough. Historians need evidence: photographs, measurements, letters written at the time, independent witnesses, and machines that could be studied or copied. The Wrights, despite their secrecy, eventually had all of these."]},{"heading":"Curtiss and the June Bug","paragraphs":["Back in the United States, another team was getting into the air. In 1907, Alexander Graham Bell, the inventor of the telephone, formed a group called the Aerial Experiment Association. Bell was then about 60 years old and had been experimenting with giant kites at his summer home in Nova Scotia, Canada. His wife, Mabel Bell, helped pay for the association. Its young members included two Canadian engineers, Frederick 'Casey' Baldwin and John McCurdy; a US Army lieutenant named Thomas Selfridge, who would soon play a tragic part in this story; and an engine builder and motorcycle racer from Hammondsport, New York, named Glenn Curtiss, who held the title of fastest man on Earth after riding a V8 motorcycle at about 219 kilometres per hour in 1907.","Each member designed an airplane in turn. Selfridge designed the Red Wing, and later became the first US military officer to fly an airplane on his own, in the association's White Wing. The third machine was Curtiss's design, a biplane called the June Bug.","The magazine Scientific American had offered a trophy for the first public flight of over 1 kilometre in the United States. On 4 July 1908, at Hammondsport, in front of a crowd and official judges from the Aero Club of America, Curtiss flew the June Bug about 1,550 metres (5,085 feet) in 1 minute and 42 seconds and won the trophy. It was the first pre-announced public airplane flight in America, and it made Curtiss famous.","The June Bug did not use wing warping. Instead, it had small triangular movable surfaces at its wingtips that could be tilted to roll the airplane. But to the Wrights, that did not matter. Their patent, they believed, covered any system that changed the angle of the wings on the two sides to keep the airplane balanced, however it was done. Soon after the July flight, Orville wrote to Curtiss warning that the brothers had not given permission to use their system for exhibitions or to make money. Curtiss refused to pay for a licence.","This was the beginning of a long and bitter legal fight between the Wrights and Curtiss. In 1913 a court ruled in the Wrights' favour, but appeals and arguments dragged on for years. Some historians believe the patent battle slowed down American aviation, while Europe raced ahead. Others point out that the Wrights had a real right to protect an invention that had cost them years of work. It is still a lively debate."]},{"heading":"August 1908: Wilbur Astonishes France","paragraphs":["By 1908, the Wrights' long wait was finally paying off. On 8 February 1908, the US Army Signal Corps agreed to buy a Wright airplane, provided it passed a series of tough tests. Soon afterwards, the brothers also struck a deal with a group of French businessmen. Both deals required public flights. The brothers split up: Wilbur would fly in France, and Orville would fly for the Army near Washington, D.C.","Wilbur went to Le Mans, a town in western France. He assembled his Flyer in a factory owned by Léon Bollée, an automobile maker, whose wife Carlotta helped translate the technical conversations. Then he moved the airplane to the Hunaudières horse-racing track just outside the town. Crowds of curious people, journalists and rival aviators gathered, many of them expecting to watch the 'bluffeur' fail.","On 8 August 1908, Wilbur took off. The flight lasted only 1 minute and 45 seconds, but it was enough. The spectators watched him bank smoothly into turns, fly in circles and land gently and precisely where he wanted. No European airplane could do anything like it. The French machines of the time could fly mostly in straight lines, turning in wide, flat, skidding curves. Wilbur's Flyer swooped and banked like a bird. Among those watching was Louis Blériot, who would make his own name very soon.","The reaction was astonishing. Georges Besançon, editor of the French aviation magazine L'Aérophile, wrote that the flights had 'completely dissipated all doubts'. Many of the people who had sneered at the brothers now apologised publicly. Ernest Archdeacon, who had been so scornful, admitted that he had done the Wrights an injustice.","Wilbur stayed in France for months, moving to a military camp nearby, Camp d'Auvours, where he had more room. He flew longer and longer, often with passengers. On 7 October 1908, Edith Berg, the wife of one of the Wrights' business agents, became the first American woman to ride in an airplane; she tied a rope around her skirt so it would not blow about, and fashionable French women later copied the look. On 31 December 1908, Wilbur won the Michelin Cup with a flight of about 2 hours and 20 minutes. Wilbur, a quiet man in a cloth cap who preferred work to parties, had become one of the most famous people in Europe.","In early 1909 he moved on to Pau in southern France, where he set up a flying school, and then to Rome, where he trained Italian military pilots. Kings came to watch: the rulers of Great Britain, Spain and Italy all saw him fly. Back home in October 1909, during a big celebration in New York, Wilbur flew up the Hudson River past the city, watched by perhaps a million people, with a canoe strapped beneath the Flyer in case he had to land on the water."]},{"heading":"Triumph and Tragedy at Fort Myer","paragraphs":["Meanwhile, Orville was flying for the US Army at Fort Myer, Virginia, just across the Potomac River from Washington, D.C. The Army's tests were demanding. The airplane had to carry two people, stay up for an hour, and average at least 40 miles per hour, about 64 kilometres per hour. The basic price was 25,000 dollars, with a bonus of 2,500 dollars for every mile per hour faster than 40.","Orville began flying there in early September 1908 and quickly broke one record after another. On 9 September he flew for 62 minutes and 15 seconds, the first airplane flight in history to last more than an hour. Thousands of people came out to watch, including government officials. Many were seeing an airplane for the first time in their lives.","Then, on 17 September 1908, something went terribly wrong. Orville was flying with a passenger: Lieutenant Thomas Selfridge, the young Army officer from Bell's Aerial Experiment Association, who was one of the Army's official observers. A few minutes into the flight, one of the propellers split. The broken propeller hit a bracing wire, which pulled loose part of the rudder system, and the Flyer dived out of control into the ground. Orville was badly hurt, with a broken leg, several broken ribs and injuries to his hip. Selfridge suffered a serious head injury and died that evening. He was the first person ever to die in the crash of a powered airplane.","It was a heartbreaking moment, both for the Wright family and for the small world of early flying, where everyone knew everyone. Orville's sister, Katharine, took time off from her job as a teacher to stay by his side in the hospital for weeks. Investigators and the Wrights themselves studied exactly what had broken, and the brothers changed their designs to make them stronger. That habit, of studying every accident carefully so the same thing never happens again, became one of the most important traditions in aviation, as you will see in Part 18. Today an air base in Michigan, Selfridge Air National Guard Base, is named in his memory.","Orville recovered, though he suffered pain from his injuries for the rest of his life. In June 1909 he returned to Fort Myer with a new Flyer, and on 27 July 1909 he completed the official tests with a passenger aboard. The airplane beat the required speed, earning the brothers a bonus, and the US Army bought it for a total of 30,000 dollars. It became the Army's first airplane, Signal Corps Aeroplane Number 1. The doubters had gone quiet. Later that year the brothers formed the Wright Company to build airplanes for sale.","In less than six years, the Wrights had gone from 12 seconds over the sand at Kitty Hawk to selling airplanes to armies and training pilots across Europe. But the rest of the world was catching up fast. In France, dozens of inventors were building and flying their own machines. On a summer morning in July 1909, one of them, Louis Blériot, stood on the coast of northern France near Calais, looking out across the grey waters of the English Channel toward the white cliffs of England, wondering if his little monoplane could make it to the other side."]}],"deepDive":"Who flew first depends on what you mean by 'flying', and historians are careful with their definitions. Most aviation historians credit the Wrights with the first sustained, powered, controlled flights of a heavier-than-air machine carrying a pilot: on 17 December 1903 the Flyer took off under its own power from a level rail, flew forward without losing speed, was steered by its pilot and landed on ground as high as its starting point. Supporters of Alberto Santos-Dumont, especially in Brazil, argue for different rules. They point out that the Wrights' 1903 flights relied on a strong headwind and that their 1904 and 1905 flights used a falling-weight catapult, while the 14-bis rolled away on wheels in calm air before official judges. Critics of that view reply that the 1903 Flyer used no catapult at all, that wheels and catapults are a matter of take-off method rather than the core problem of flight, and that the 14-bis had no proper roll control until ailerons were added and flew only short straight hops, while the Wrights had flown 39 kilometres in circles in 1905. Witnessing matters too. The Wrights' 1903 flights had five local witnesses and a photograph, and the 1904 and 1905 flights were seen by neighbours, passengers on the trolley line and Amos Root, but they were never measured by an official body such as the Aéro-Club de France. That is why some European writers continued to doubt them until 1908. The Whitehead and Pearse claims face a different problem: not the definition, but the lack of trustworthy evidence from the time. The debate has an odd twist involving the Smithsonian Institution. After Curtiss modified and flew Samuel Langley's old Aerodrome in 1914, the Smithsonian for years labelled it the first machine 'capable' of piloted flight, angering Orville, who sent the 1903 Flyer to London's Science Museum in protest. The Smithsonian published a correction in 1942, and in 1948 the Flyer came home under an agreement with Orville's estate that the Smithsonian would not describe any earlier aircraft as capable of carrying a person under its own power in controlled flight. Some Whitehead supporters argue that this agreement makes the museum biased; most historians say the evidence, not the contract, decides the matter.","funFact":"One of the first published eyewitness reports of an airplane flying in a circle appeared not in a newspaper but in Gleanings in Bee Culture, a magazine for beekeepers, written by its publisher Amos Root.","check":{"q":"Who was killed in the crash at Fort Myer, Virginia, in September 1908, the first death in a powered airplane crash?","a":"Lieutenant Thomas Selfridge","wrong":["Orville Wright","Glenn Curtiss","Alberto Santos-Dumont"]},"sources":[{"title":"Huffman Prairie Flying Field","publisher":"US National Park Service","url":"https://www.nps.gov/daav/learn/historyculture/huffman-prairie-flying-field.htm"},{"title":"Wright Flyer II","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Wright_Flyer_II"},{"title":"Wright brothers","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Wright_brothers"},{"title":"Santos-Dumont 14-bis","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Santos-Dumont_14-bis"},{"title":"Gustave Whitehead","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Gustave_Whitehead"},{"title":"Richard Pearse","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Richard_Pearse"},{"title":"Thomas Selfridge","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Thomas_Selfridge"},{"title":"AEA June Bug","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/AEA_June_Bug"}],"photos":[{"src":"img/deep/airplane/10-1.jpg","alt":"The Wright Flyer III banking in flight over Huffman Prairie near Dayton, Ohio, in 1905.","file":"1905 Wright Flyer III (flight 41).jpg","url":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:1905_Wright_Flyer_III_(flight_41).jpg","author":"Wilbur Wright","license":"Public domain"},{"src":"img/deep/airplane/10-2.jpg","alt":"Alberto Santos-Dumont flying his 14-bis canard biplane in Paris in 1906.","file":"Premier Envol de Santos-Dumont, 14 bis (brightened).png","url":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Premier_Envol_de_Santos-Dumont,_14_bis_(brightened).png","author":"Cliché ROL","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0"},{"src":"img/deep/airplane/10-3.jpg","alt":"Album page from 8 August 1908: Wilbur Wright, and his plane in full flight at Le Mans, France.","file":"Btv1b8433368f-p005.jpg","url":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Btv1b8433368f-p005.jpg","author":"Jules Beau","license":"Public domain"}],"words":4310},{"n":11,"title":"Daredevils and Record-Breakers","when":"1909 – 1914","summary":"In 1909 a French pilot with a hurt foot flew a tiny plane across the sea from France to England in about 37 minutes. Soon there were huge air shows, brave women pilots, planes that took off from water and ships, and even the very first airline ticket. In just five years, flying went from a crazy stunt to something people could buy a seat on.","sections":[{"heading":"Dawn on the Cliffs of Calais","paragraphs":["Imagine standing on a windy beach in northern France just before sunrise on Sunday, 25 July 1909. The sky is pale grey. A small crowd of mechanics, friends and reporters huddles around a strange little machine parked on a farm field at Les Baraques, between Calais and Sangatte. It looks more like a giant dragonfly than anything that should cross an ocean: one pair of cloth-covered wings, an open frame of wooden struts and wires behind them, and a tiny tail at the back. This is the Blériot XI, and its pilot, Louis Blériot, is 37 years old, limping on a foot burned in an earlier crash, and about to try something no one has ever done.","Blériot was not a beginner hoping for luck. He was a wealthy engineer who had made his fortune selling bright acetylene headlamps for early motor cars, and then spent much of that fortune building one flying machine after another. Many of them crashed. Some never left the ground. Type XI was his eleventh main design, and it was a monoplane, which means a plane with a single main wing rather than the two stacked wings of a biplane. Its engine came from the Italian-born engine maker Alessandro Anzani: a small three-cylinder motor of roughly 25 horsepower, about twice what the Wright brothers had in 1903, and still less than a modern ride-on lawnmower.","Why cross the English Channel at all? Because a London newspaper, the Daily Mail, had offered a prize of 1,000 pounds, a huge sum at the time, to the first person to fly a heavier-than-air machine across it. The newspaper's owner, Lord Northcliffe, loved bold publicity stunts, and he also wanted to wake Britain up to the coming age of flight. The narrowest part of the Channel, the Strait of Dover, is only about 34 kilometres wide, but in 1909 that was a frightening distance to fly over open water. Engines often stopped without warning, and there was nowhere to land except the waves.","Blériot was not even the favourite. That honour belonged to Hubert Latham, an elegant, daring Frenchman flying the beautiful Antoinette monoplane. On 19 July Latham had set off first, but his engine failed about 10 kilometres from Dover, and his plane glided down onto the sea. Rescuers in a French destroyer reportedly found him sitting calmly on the floating wreck, smoking a cigarette. While Latham waited for a replacement aircraft and better weather, Blériot moved in.","At 4:41 in the morning, after a short test hop, Blériot opened the throttle, bounced across the grass, and climbed out over the cliff edge and the sea. He had no compass. A French navy destroyer, the Escopette, steamed below as an escort, with his wife Alice on board, but his little plane quickly left it behind. For around ten minutes he later said he could see nothing at all: no ship, no France, no England, only grey water and grey sky. Imagine sitting in that open seat with the wind whipping your face, the engine rattling in front of you, trusting that the motor would keep going for just a few more minutes.","The wind pushed him east of his planned course. When the white cliffs finally appeared, he turned and followed the coast until he spotted a French journalist waving a large flag on the clifftop near Dover Castle, marking a dip in the cliffs called Northfall Meadow. Blériot cut the engine and dropped in rather hard. The landing gear was damaged and one propeller blade broke, but he climbed out unhurt. The flight had taken about 37 minutes (36 minutes and 30 seconds by most records)."]},{"heading":"Britain Is No Longer an Island","paragraphs":["Within hours the news raced around the world by telegraph. Blériot was greeted in London by huge crowds, and his little monoplane was put on display at the Selfridges department store, where thousands of people queued to see it. Orders for copies of the Type XI flooded into his workshop. Between 1909 and 1914 his company built roughly 900 aircraft, most of them versions of the Type XI. A plane that had nearly bankrupted him turned him into one of the world's leading aircraft makers.","But not everyone in Britain was simply cheering. For hundreds of years, the English Channel had protected Britain from invasion. The Spanish Armada had failed to cross it in 1588. Napoleon had gathered an army on these same French cliffs around 1804 and never managed to cross. The Royal Navy was the most powerful in the world, and as long as it controlled the sea, Britain felt safe. Now one man in a fragile machine of wood, wire and fabric had simply flown over the top in about half an hour.","Newspaper writers quickly drew the lesson. Several commentators, including the writer H. G. Wells, argued that from a military point of view Britain was no longer an untouchable island. They were thinking far ahead: in 1909 no plane could carry a useful load of soldiers or weapons, and Blériot could barely carry himself. Yet the direction was clear. If machines kept improving at this speed, the sea would no longer be a wall. Within just a few years, as Part 13 will show, airplanes would be flying over battlefields.","Blériot's crossing also changed how ordinary people thought about flight. The Wrights had flown longer and more skilfully in 1908, but their flights were circles over fields. Blériot had gone somewhere: from one country to another, over the sea, like a ship or a train but much faster. Flying was no longer just an amazing trick. It looked like a new way to travel. That idea would take more than a decade to come true for passengers, but it began on that clifftop at Dover. Today a granite outline of his plane, set into the grass, marks the spot where he landed."]},{"heading":"Champagne Country Hosts the World's First Great Air Show","paragraphs":["Only a month later, an even bigger spectacle took place. From 22 to 29 August 1909, the world's best pilots gathered on the plain of Bétheny, about 5 kilometres north of the French city of Reims. The event was called the Grande Semaine d'Aviation de la Champagne, which means the Great Aviation Week of the Champagne region. The famous champagne-making companies of the area paid for it, hoping to show off their region and their wine. Historians usually call it the first big international public air meet.","Picture the scene. Grandstands, restaurants, a post office and even a special railway station had been built on the open fields. Flags fluttered above wooden sheds where the planes were kept. Over the week, around half a million visitors came to watch, including the President of France, Armand Fallières, and the British politician David Lloyd George. For most of them, it was the first time they had ever seen an airplane fly. Thirty-eight aircraft were entered, and 23 actually got into the air, which was a remarkable number in an age when engines failed constantly.","Prizes were offered for speed, distance, altitude and carrying passengers. Henri Farman, an English-born Frenchman, won the distance prize by flying about 180 kilometres in roughly three hours, circling the course again and again until his fuel ran low. Hubert Latham, the Channel swimmer of July, won the altitude prize by climbing to about 155 metres, which seemed dizzyingly high at the time. Modern airliners cruise around 70 times higher.","The most exciting race was the Gordon Bennett Cup, a speed contest for two laps of the 10-kilometre course. The only American taking part was Glenn Curtiss, the motorcycle racer and engine builder you met in Part 10. He had brought one small biplane with a powerful engine he had built himself. Blériot flew a bigger new monoplane and was the favourite. Curtiss finished the two laps in 15 minutes and 50.4 seconds, an average of about 75 kilometres per hour. Blériot followed him, and when his time was announced he had lost by less than 6 seconds. Curtiss became world famous overnight.","Reims also showed the dangers and difficulties of early flying. The airfield was ordinary farmland with haystacks, and a few planes crashed into them. On the final day, Blériot's aircraft crashed and caught fire, though he escaped with burns. Many machines spent more time being repaired than flying. Engineers noticed that the biggest problem was not wings or controls any more, but unreliable engines. That made one newcomer especially important: the Gnome rotary engine, a light and surprisingly dependable motor that made its public debut at Reims. You will learn how this odd spinning engine worked in Part 12."]},{"heading":"Women Take the Controls","paragraphs":["In 1909 many people believed that flying was far too dangerous and technical for women. Two remarkable pilots proved them wrong almost immediately.","The first was a Frenchwoman born Élise Deroche in Paris in 1882. She had been an actress and loved fast new things like motor cars and ballooning. Performing under the stage name Raymonde de Laroche, she became friends with the aircraft builder Charles Voisin, who agreed to teach her to fly. On 22 October 1909, at Châlons in eastern France, she took a Voisin biplane into the air and flew about 270 metres. Her teachers had reportedly told her only to taxi along the ground, but she decided she was ready. Accounts of that day differ, which is common for early aviation stories, but her skill soon became undeniable.","On 8 March 1910 the Aéro-Club de France gave her pilot licence number 36. That made her the first woman in the world to hold an official pilot's licence. Newspapers began calling her Baroness de Laroche, a grand-sounding title she did not actually hold, and the name stuck. She flew at air meets in Egypt, Russia and Hungary, where Tsar Nicholas II of Russia personally congratulated her. Flying was risky, though. In July 1910 she was badly injured in a crash at the Reims air meet, and it took her a long time to recover. She kept going. In 1913 she won the Femina Cup, a prize for women pilots, with a nonstop flight of more than four hours, and in June 1919 she set a women's altitude record of about 4,800 metres. Sadly, a few weeks later, in July 1919, she died in a crash while flying as co-pilot in an experimental plane at Le Crotoy in France. She had hoped to become one of the first women test pilots.","Across the Atlantic, Harriet Quimby was a journalist and screenwriter in New York, writing theatre reviews and travel stories for a popular magazine called Leslie's Illustrated Weekly. In 1910 she watched an air meet on Long Island and decided she had to learn to fly. She took lessons at a flying school run by the Moisant family, sometimes practising at dawn when the air was calmest. On 1 August 1911 the Aero Club of America granted her a pilot's licence, making her the first American woman to earn one. Quimby designed her own flying suit of plum-coloured satin with a hood, which could be buttoned to turn into a skirt when she was on the ground. Photographers loved it, and she became one of the most famous women in America.","Quimby wanted to be the first woman to fly across the English Channel. She borrowed a Blériot monoplane and, on 16 April 1912, took off from Dover, flying the route in the opposite direction from Blériot. The weather was cold and misty. She had never flown this particular machine before and had only a compass to guide her through the clouds. About 59 minutes later she landed on a beach at Équihen, on the French coast near Boulogne, where astonished local fishermen ran out to greet her.","Her timing could not have been worse. Just the day before, the world had learned that the great ocean liner Titanic had sunk on its first voyage. Newspapers were full of that terrible disaster, and Quimby's achievement was printed in small stories on the inside pages. Less than three months later, on 1 July 1912, she was flying at an air meet near Boston with a passenger when her plane suddenly pitched forward. Neither of them was wearing a seat belt, which was normal at that time, and both were thrown out and killed. She was 37. Her death, like many others of the era, slowly helped convince pilots that safety belts and careful design mattered.","De Laroche and Quimby were not the only ones. Women such as Hélène Dutrieu of Belgium, Lydia Zvereva of Russia and Hilda Hewlett of Britain also earned licences in these years. Hilda Hewlett even co-founded a flying school and an aircraft factory. Their courage opened a door that later pilots, including Bessie Coleman and Amelia Earhart in Part 13, would walk through."]},{"heading":"Taking Off From Water and From Ships","paragraphs":["Most of the Earth is covered by water, so it was natural for inventors to wonder: could a plane take off from a lake or the sea? On 28 March 1910, a French engineer named Henri Fabre answered yes. On the Étang de Berre, a large saltwater lagoon near Marseille in southern France, he climbed into a strange machine he called the Hydravion, French for water-plane. Instead of wheels it had three flat floats, and its tail was at the front, a design sometimes called a canard, which is French for duck. It was powered by a 50-horsepower Gnome rotary engine. Fabre skimmed across the water and lifted off, making four flights that day, the longest about 600 metres. Remarkably, he is reported to have had little or no experience as a pilot beforehand.","Fabre's Hydravion is often called the first true seaplane, and the original machine still survives at the Musée de l'Air et de l'Espace near Paris. His patented floats were soon used by other builders, including Glenn Curtiss in the United States, who developed practical flying boats in 1911 and 1912. Fabre himself lived an extraordinarily long life: he died in 1984 at the age of 101, after seeing jet airliners and men walking on the Moon.","Navies around the world were watching closely. If a plane could fly from a warship, it could scout far over the horizon, spotting enemy ships long before they came into view. The United States Navy decided to try. On 14 November 1910, at Hampton Roads in Virginia, carpenters built a sloping wooden platform over the front of the cruiser USS Birmingham. Eugene Ely, a 24-year-old pilot who flew for Glenn Curtiss, sat at the top of the ramp in a Curtiss pusher biplane, so called because its propeller was behind the pilot and pushed the plane forward.","The weather was poor, and Ely decided to go before it got worse, even though the ship was still at anchor. He rolled down the short ramp and over the bow. The plane dipped so low that its wheels touched the water, and spray splashed over his goggles. Somehow the engine kept running and the plane climbed away. Unable to see clearly, Ely flew to the nearest shore and landed on a beach instead of the navy yard he had planned to reach. He had made the first takeoff of an airplane from a ship.","Two months later, on 18 January 1911, he tried something even harder: landing on a ship. The cruiser USS Pennsylvania, anchored in San Francisco Bay, was fitted with a wooden deck over its stern. Across the deck, workers stretched a row of ropes, each weighted at both ends with sandbags. Hooks under Ely's plane caught the ropes as he touched down, and the dragging sandbags slowed him to a stop. He then turned the plane around and took off again. This simple idea, the tailhook and arresting wires, is the ancestor of the system modern aircraft carriers still use to stop jets in just a couple of seconds.","Like many pilots of his generation, Ely did not live long. He died in October 1911 when his plane failed to pull out of a dive during an exhibition in Macon, Georgia. He was only 24, just two days short of his 25th birthday."]},{"heading":"Coast to Coast in the Vin Fiz","paragraphs":["In 1911 the newspaper owner William Randolph Hearst offered 50,000 dollars to the first person to fly across the United States, from coast to coast, in less than 30 days. At that time, the longest flights lasted a few hours. America is over 4,000 kilometres wide, with mountains, deserts and very few places to buy aviation fuel or spare parts.","Calbraith Perry Rodgers decided to try. He was a tall, cigar-smoking member of a famous naval family who had learned to fly at the Wright brothers' school in Dayton only a few months earlier. He bought a Wright Model EX biplane and found a sponsor, the meatpacking businessman J. Ogden Armour, who was launching a new grape-flavoured soft drink called Vin Fiz. In return for money, Rodgers named his plane the Vin Fiz and painted advertisements on it. A special train followed him across the country, with a sleeping car, a dining car and a workshop car crammed with spare parts. Riding along as chief mechanic was none other than Charlie Taylor, the man who had built the Wright brothers' first engine in 1903.","Rodgers took off from Sheepshead Bay in Brooklyn, New York, on 17 September 1911. Almost immediately, things began to go wrong. He crashed again and again: into trees, chicken coops, fences and fields. Each time, the train crew rebuilt the plane, sometimes nearly from scratch. Engines failed. Storms forced him down. The 30-day deadline passed while he was still in the middle of the country, so he lost any chance at Hearst's prize. He kept going anyway, saying he wanted to prove it could be done.","Following railway tracks to find his way, Rodgers made around 70 stops. He finally landed at Pasadena, California, on 5 November 1911, where a cheering crowd welcomed him. The journey had taken 49 days, though he had spent only a few days' worth of hours actually in the air, often reported as about 82 hours. Weeks later, after another bad crash that put him in hospital, he flew on to Long Beach with his crutches, it is often said, strapped to the plane, and, on 10 December 1911, rolled the wheels of the Vin Fiz into the Pacific Ocean. So many parts had been replaced along the way that, according to a story often told, only a few original pieces, such as the rudder, made it all the way across.","Rodgers had made the first flight across the United States, but it also showed how far airplanes still had to go. A train could make the same trip in about four days. Sadly, in April 1912, Rodgers was killed when his plane hit a flock of seagulls and crashed into the sea at Long Beach, close to where his great journey had ended. The Vin Fiz, rebuilt again, is now in the collection of the Smithsonian Institution."]},{"heading":"Look, No Hands: The First Autopilot","paragraphs":["Early airplanes were exhausting to fly. They wobbled with every gust of wind, and the pilot had to make constant small corrections with the controls, hour after hour. One young American had an idea: what if a machine could do some of that work?","Lawrence Sperry was the son of Elmer Sperry, an inventor who had built gyrocompasses for ships. A gyroscope is a fast-spinning wheel that strongly resists being tilted, so it can act as a steady reference that always knows which way is level, even when everything around it is bouncing. Lawrence, who learned to fly as a teenager, worked with his father to connect gyroscopes to a plane's controls. If the plane started to tip, the gyroscopes sensed the change and moved the control surfaces to bring it back to level, using power from the plane itself rather than the pilot's muscles.","In June 1914, Lawrence Sperry, then only 21, brought his invention to an aviation safety competition near Paris, on the River Seine. Flying a Curtiss flying boat low past the judges and the crowd, he lifted his hands off the controls and held them in the air. Then his French mechanic, named in accounts as Emil Cachin, climbed out of the cockpit and walked along the wing. Normally, a man's weight shifting onto one wing would make a plane roll over. Instead, the gyroscopic stabilizer quietly adjusted the controls, and the plane stayed level. Next, Sperry himself stood up in the cockpit while the plane flew on steadily. The crowd was astonished, and Sperry won the competition's top prize, reported as 50,000 francs.","This was the ancestor of the modern autopilot. Today, autopilots fly most of a typical airliner's journey while pilots monitor the systems, and as Part 19 will show, some can even land the plane in fog. Lawrence Sperry also helped develop instruments that let pilots fly in clouds, including an early artificial horizon. Sadly, he too died young: in December 1923 he set off to fly across the English Channel in a small plane in fog and never arrived."]},{"heading":"Tickets Please: The World's First Airline","paragraphs":["By 1913, airplanes had crossed seas, countries and a continent. But could anyone simply buy a ticket and fly somewhere, like taking a train? In Florida, a businessman named Percival Fansler thought they could. The cities of St. Petersburg and Tampa sat on opposite sides of Tampa Bay. By steamboat the trip took about two hours, and by train it could take anywhere from about four to twelve hours because the railway went all the way around the bay. Across the water, it was only about 37 kilometres.","Fansler teamed up with Thomas Benoist, an aircraft builder from St. Louis, who had designed a small flying boat called the Benoist XIV. It had a boat-shaped hull that floated on water, a biplane wing above, and room for the pilot and one passenger side by side in an open cockpit. The city of St. Petersburg agreed to help pay for the service. It was named the St. Petersburg–Tampa Airboat Line.","On the morning of 1 January 1914, about 3,000 people gathered at the waterfront in St. Petersburg. The first seat had been auctioned, and the winning bid of 400 dollars came from Abram C. Pheil, a former mayor of the city. That was an enormous amount, several months of wages for an ordinary worker in 1914. The pilot was Tony Jannus, a skilled 24-year-old flyer. At 10 o'clock the airboat skimmed across the water, lifted off, and flew low over the bay. About 23 minutes later it landed in Tampa. The world's first scheduled airline flight, using a winged airplane, had arrived.","After the first day, the normal fare was 5 dollars for a one-way trip, with extra charges reported for heavier passengers and baggage. The airboats made two round trips a day, and a second plane was added. Over about four months, the line carried 1,205 passengers on 172 flights without a single serious injury. When the winter tourist season ended and the city's money ran out, the service stopped in May 1914.","It was a tiny business, but it showed something huge: that ordinary people would pay to save time by flying. Earlier, German airships run by the company DELAG had carried paying passengers on pleasure trips, but the Airboat Line was the first regular, timetabled airline using airplanes. Today a memorial in St. Petersburg honours that first flight. About a century later, airlines were carrying more than four billion passengers a year.","In only five years, between 1909 and 1914, airplanes had gone from fragile machines that could barely circle a field to aircraft that crossed seas, took off from water and ships, flew across a continent and carried paying passengers. Pilots had learned a great deal. But how exactly did they steer these machines, climbing, diving and turning through the three-dimensional ocean of the sky? Up, down, left, right, tilting and twisting, what was actually happening at the controls? That is the subject of Part 12."]}],"deepDive":"Why did aviation explode between 1909 and 1914? Historians point to several overlapping forces. First, prize money. Newspapers such as the Daily Mail and the Hearst papers offered awards that could equal many years of a worker's pay, and air meets offered appearance fees and trophies. Prizes rewarded clear, measurable goals: cross this strait, beat this time, reach this height. That pushed designers toward reliability and speed rather than just getting off the ground. Second, engines. The Gnome Omega rotary of 1909 delivered about 50 horsepower for roughly 75 kilograms, a far better power-to-weight ratio than most earlier motors, and it was reliable enough to keep running for hours. Third, copying and competition. Once Blériot's Type XI was a proven design, hundreds were built and flown by others, and its layout (tractor propeller at the front, monoplane wing, tail at the back) became a template for future planes. At the same time, the Wright brothers' broad 1906 patent on lateral control led to lawsuits against Curtiss, Blériot and other builders. French and German courts largely rejected the Wrights' claims, but in the United States the legal fight is often said to have slowed American aviation until the government arranged a patent pool during World War I. By 1914, Europe, especially France, led the world in aircraft production. The human cost was high. Many of the pioneers in this part died in crashes within a few years: Ely in 1911, Rodgers and Quimby in 1912, de Laroche in 1919, Sperry in 1923. Early planes had no seat belts, no parachutes, weak structures and unreliable engines, and pilots learned largely by trial and error. Each accident taught something, and gradually safety belts, stronger structures, better training and pilot licensing became standard. A common historians' debate is about the 'first airline'. The St. Petersburg–Tampa Airboat Line is widely recognised as the first scheduled airline using fixed-wing aircraft, but the German airship company DELAG carried paying passengers from 1910, and the airlines that lasted, like KLM, did not appear until after World War I.","funFact":"When Harriet Quimby became the first woman to fly across the English Channel in April 1912, hardly anyone noticed, because the news of the Titanic sinking had broken the day before.","check":{"q":"Who flew the first scheduled airline flight in an airplane, across Tampa Bay on 1 January 1914?","a":"Tony Jannus","wrong":["Louis Blériot","Eugene Ely","Calbraith Perry Rodgers"]},"sources":[{"title":"Louis Blériot","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Louis_Blériot"},{"title":"Grande Semaine d'Aviation de la Champagne","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Grande_Semaine_d'Aviation_de_la_Champagne"},{"title":"Raymonde de Laroche","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Raymonde_de_Laroche"},{"title":"Harriet Quimby","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Harriet_Quimby"},{"title":"Henri Fabre","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Henri_Fabre"},{"title":"Eugene Burton Ely","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Eugene_Burton_Ely"},{"title":"Calbraith Perry Rodgers","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Calbraith_Perry_Rodgers"},{"title":"St. Petersburg–Tampa Airboat Line","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/St._Petersburg–Tampa_Airboat_Line"}],"photos":[{"src":"img/deep/airplane/11-1.jpg","alt":"Louis Blériot and his Type XI monoplane at Dover after the first flight across the English Channel, 25 July 1909.","file":"Louis Blériot Dover 1909.jpg","url":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Louis_Bl%C3%A9riot_Dover_1909.jpg","author":"Worlds Grafic Press","license":"Public domain"},{"src":"img/deep/airplane/11-2.jpg","alt":"Harriet Quimby, America's first licensed woman pilot, in her shiny satin flying suit and hood, 1911.","file":"Harriet Quimby in 1911.png","url":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Harriet_Quimby_in_1911.png","author":"Leslie Ronald Jones","license":"Public domain"},{"src":"img/deep/airplane/11-3.jpg","alt":"Eugene Ely's Curtiss pusher biplane flies off the platform on the bow of USS Birmingham, November 1910.","file":"First airplane takeoff from a warship NH 77545.jpg","url":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:First_airplane_takeoff_from_a_warship_NH_77545.jpg","author":"Ancient US-Navy sailor","license":"Public domain"}],"words":4335},{"n":12,"title":"Steering the Sky: Controls, Propellers and Engines","when":"Science explainer","summary":"A car can only go forward, back, left and right, but a plane can also tip its nose up and down and tilt its wings. Pilots steer with little hinged flaps on the wings and tail, while a spinning propeller, which is really a wing that goes round and round, pulls the plane along. In this part you will learn how a plane turns, what all those dials in the cockpit mean, and how engines grew from 12 horsepower to more than 2,000.","sections":[{"heading":"Three Ways to Spin: Pitch, Roll and Yaw","paragraphs":["Pick up a toy airplane, or just hold your hand out flat in front of you with your fingers pointing forward like the nose of a plane. Your hand can move in three different ways, and every pilot in history has had to master all three.","First, tip your fingertips up and down, like a nod. That is pitch. When a plane pitches up, its nose rises and it starts to climb. When it pitches down, the nose drops and it starts to descend. Second, keep your fingers pointing forward but tilt your hand so your thumb goes up and your little finger goes down, then the other way. That is roll. When a plane rolls, one wingtip rises and the other dips, and the plane is said to be banking. Third, keep your hand flat and swing your fingertips left and right, like shaking your head to say no. That is yaw. The nose points left or right while the wings stay level.","Engineers describe these movements as turns around three imaginary lines called axes. All three pass through a single point called the centre of gravity, the balance point of the whole plane. If you could hang the plane from that exact spot on a string, it would stay perfectly balanced. The roll axis runs from the nose to the tail. The pitch axis runs from one wingtip to the other. The yaw axis runs straight up and down through the middle of the plane. Every movement a plane makes in the air, from a gentle turn to a loop at an air show, is a mixture of rotations around these three axes.","A boat on a lake mainly yaws. It turns left or right, and the water holds it up. A car does the same on a road. But a plane floats in a three-dimensional ocean of air, with nothing holding it in place except the forces you met in Part 9. It can tip, tilt and twist in any direction at the same time. That is exactly why early inventors found flying so hard. As you saw in Part 7, the Wright brothers' great breakthrough was realising that a successful airplane must let the pilot control all three axes, and they did it with wing warping for roll, an elevator for pitch and a movable rudder for yaw. Every airplane since has followed the same basic idea, even if the parts have changed.","Here is a useful way to remember them. Pitch is like nodding yes. Yaw is like shaking your head no. Roll is like tilting your head toward one shoulder when you are puzzled. Try all three with your head now, and you have just flown your first set of aerobatic moves."]},{"heading":"Little Hinged Flaps With Big Jobs","paragraphs":["How does a pilot actually make a plane pitch, roll or yaw? With control surfaces: hinged panels on the wings and tail that can swing up, down or sideways into the flowing air. They work on the same principle as the wing itself. When a surface is angled into the airflow, it bends the air one way, and the air pushes back the other way. Because these surfaces sit far from the centre of gravity, even a small push from them can rotate the whole plane, just as a small push on the end of a long door swings it open easily.","Ailerons control roll. They are long, narrow flaps on the back edge of each wing, usually near the wingtips. They work as a pair: when one goes down, the other goes up. The aileron that goes down makes its wing curve more and meet the air at a steeper angle, so that wing produces more lift and rises. The aileron that goes up spoils some lift on the other wing, so that wing sinks. One wing up, one wing down: the plane rolls. The word aileron comes from French and means little wing.","Surprisingly, ailerons were patented long before anyone flew a powered airplane. In 1868 a British inventor named Matthew Piers Watt Boulton, the grandson of the famous engineer Matthew Boulton who worked with James Watt on steam engines, received a British patent describing hinged flaps for controlling the sideways balance of a flying machine. Nobody built them into a working aircraft, and his idea was largely forgotten. In 1904 the French pioneer Robert Esnault-Pelterie fitted aileron-like surfaces to a glider, and over the following years Alberto Santos-Dumont, Henri Farman and Alexander Graham Bell's Aerial Experiment Association all used them. Ailerons were easier to build than wing warping, and they worked better on stiff, strong wings. By about 1911 most new biplanes used them, and by about 1915 almost all monoplanes did too. Wing warping, the Wrights' clever solution, faded away, although the Wrights argued fiercely in court that their patent covered any method of rolling a plane.","The elevator controls pitch. It is a hinged surface on the back of the horizontal tail, the small wing at the rear of the plane called the horizontal stabilizer. When the pilot raises the elevator, the air pushes the tail down, and because the plane pivots around its centre of gravity, the nose swings up. Lower the elevator and the tail rises while the nose drops. It may sound backwards at first, but think of a seesaw: push one end down and the other end goes up.","The rudder controls yaw. It is a hinged panel on the back of the tall vertical fin at the tail. Swing the rudder to the left and the airflow pushes the tail to the right, so the nose swings left. It works much like the rudder of a boat, which is where the name comes from.","Most planes also have other moving surfaces. Flaps, which you met in Part 9, slide out from the back of the wings to give extra lift at low speed for takeoff and landing. Small adjustable tabs called trim tabs let the pilot set the controls so the plane flies straight and level without the pilot holding the controls all the time. Large airliners also have spoilers, panels that pop up from the top of the wing to reduce lift and add drag, helping to slow the plane down after landing."]},{"heading":"How a Plane Really Turns","paragraphs":["Here is a question that puzzles many people: if the rudder points the nose left or right, why don't pilots just use the rudder to turn, like steering a boat? Try it in a plane and something strange happens. The nose swings around, but the plane keeps sliding sideways through the air in its old direction, a bit like a car skidding on ice. It is clumsy, uncomfortable and inefficient. Real pilots turn mainly by banking.","To understand why, remember that a wing's lift always pushes at right angles to the wing. When the wings are level, lift points straight up and holds the plane against gravity. When the pilot uses the ailerons to roll the plane into a bank, say tipping the left wing down, the lift tilts to the left as well. Now part of the lift still pushes upward, but part of it pushes sideways, toward the inside of the turn. That sideways push is what pulls the plane around in a curve, just as a string pulls a ball whirled around your head into a circle. Scientists call this inward force a centripetal force.","There is a catch. Because some of the lift now points sideways, less of it is pushing up against gravity. If the pilot does nothing else, the plane will start to sink. So in a turn, the pilot also pulls back gently on the controls, raising the elevator. This tilts the wing to a slightly higher angle of attack, making more lift to make up the difference. In a steep bank of 60 degrees, the wings must produce twice as much lift as in level flight, and everyone on board feels pressed into their seat as if they weighed twice as much. Pilots call this pulling 2 g.","And the rudder? It has an important supporting role. When the ailerons roll a plane, the wing that rises makes more lift, and more lift also means more drag. That extra drag tugs the rising wing backward, so the nose tends to swing the wrong way, away from the turn. This annoying effect is called adverse yaw. The Wrights discovered it the hard way with their 1902 glider, as you saw in Part 7. Pilots fix it by pressing gently on the rudder pedal on the side of the turn, keeping the nose pointing neatly along the curved path. A well-flown turn like this is called a coordinated turn. In the cockpit, a little ball in a curved glass tube, like a carpenter's spirit level, shows the pilot whether the turn is coordinated. The rule pilots learn is simple: step on the ball, meaning press the rudder pedal on the side the ball has slid to.","If you ride a bicycle, you already know something about this. To go around a corner on a bike, you do not just turn the handlebars; you lean into the turn. Leaning tilts the forces acting on you so they help carry you around the curve. A plane banking into a turn is doing something similar, but in the air instead of on the road. A typical airliner turns gently, banking only about 25 to 30 degrees, so the passengers barely notice. Fighter planes and aerobatic planes can bank all the way to vertical and pull so hard that pilots feel many times their normal weight."]},{"heading":"Inside the Cockpit: Stick, Pedals, Throttle and Dials","paragraphs":["Climb into the pilot's seat of a small training plane. In front of you, or between your knees, is the main control. In many small planes and fighters it is a control stick, a lever that sticks up from the floor. In many airliners and light planes it is a yoke, which looks a bit like a car steering wheel cut in half and mounted on a column. Both do the same jobs. Push the stick or yoke forward and the elevator tilts down, so the nose drops. Pull it back and the nose rises. Move the stick left, or turn the yoke left, and the ailerons roll the plane to the left. Early aircraft connected these controls to the surfaces with steel cables, pulleys and rods, so the pilot could actually feel the air pushing back through the controls.","Under your feet are two rudder pedals. Press the left pedal and the rudder swings so the nose yaws left; press the right and it yaws right. On the ground, the same pedals often steer the nosewheel and work the brakes, so pilots taxi around the airport with their feet. It takes some practice: your hands control pitch and roll, while your feet control yaw.","Beside you is the throttle, a lever that controls engine power. Push it forward for more power, pull it back for less. In a simple propeller plane, more power means more thrust. Interestingly, pilots learn that the throttle and elevator work together. If you want to climb, you usually add power and raise the nose; if you want to go down, you often reduce power and let the nose lower. Speed and height are linked, and learning to balance them is one of the first skills every student pilot practises.","Then there are the instruments, the dials that tell the pilot what is happening, especially when clouds hide the ground. The airspeed indicator shows how fast the plane is moving through the air. It works by comparing the pressure of air rammed into a small forward-facing tube called a pitot tube, named after the French engineer Henri Pitot who invented a similar tube to measure the flow of water in the 1700s, with the still air pressure around the plane. The faster the plane flies, the harder air pushes into the tube. Airspeed is life for a pilot, because if a plane flies too slowly the wings can stall.","The altimeter shows height. Inside is a stack of thin, sealed metal capsules containing very little air. As the plane climbs, the air pressure outside drops, the capsules expand slightly, and gears turn the needles. Because air pressure also changes with the weather, pilots adjust their altimeters to the local air pressure, given to them by radio, before takeoff and landing.","The attitude indicator, also called the artificial horizon, shows whether the plane is climbing, diving or banking. Inside is a gyroscope, a fast-spinning wheel that stays pointing the same way in space, just like the gyroscopes Lawrence Sperry used in his autopilot in Part 11. Lawrence Sperry also helped develop this instrument. On the dial, a small symbol of the plane sits in front of a moving picture of the horizon, blue sky above and brown ground below. Inside a cloud, where the real horizon is invisible, this instrument is the pilot's best friend, because a pilot's own sense of balance can be fooled very easily without anything to look at outside.","Finally, the magnetic compass shows which direction the plane is heading, and a gyroscopic heading indicator gives a steadier reading that is easier to use in turns. In 1937 the British Royal Air Force chose six key instruments that every plane flown in cloud should have, and that idea spread worldwide. Pilots nicknamed the group the six pack. Today most airliners show the same information on computer screens called glass cockpits, but the basic arrangement still echoes those six round dials."]},{"heading":"Propellers: Wings That Go Round and Round","paragraphs":["Look closely at a propeller blade and you will see something surprising. It is not a flat paddle. Its cross-section is curved, with a rounded front edge and a thinner back edge, exactly like a wing. That is because a propeller really is a wing, or rather two or more wings, spinning around in a circle. As each blade slices through the air at an angle, it produces a force just as a wing does. But instead of lifting upward, this force points forward. A spinning propeller throws a great mass of air backward, and by Newton's third law the air pushes the propeller, and the plane, forward. That forward push is thrust.","In the 1800s, propellers were mostly thought of like ship propellers, screws twisting through a fluid. The Wright brothers, as you saw in Part 8, realised that no one had a proper theory for airplane propellers, so they treated each blade as a rotating wing and used the data from their own wind tunnel to design it. Their hand-carved wooden propellers of 1903 are estimated to have turned around 75 to 82 percent of the engine's power into useful thrust, a remarkable figure that is not far below many propellers in use today, which can reach about 85 to 90 percent.","Here is a clever detail. If you look along a propeller blade from the hub to the tip, it is twisted. Near the hub the blade is set at a steep angle, but near the tip it is much flatter. Why? Because when the propeller spins, the tip travels around a much bigger circle than the hub in the same time, so the tip moves through the air much faster. Twisting the blade keeps every part of it meeting the air at a sensible angle of attack. Without the twist, the tip might stall while the part near the hub hardly did any work at all.","A fixed propeller is a bit like a bicycle stuck in one gear. A blade angle that is good for a slow takeoff is not ideal for fast cruising. So in the 1920s and 1930s engineers developed variable-pitch propellers, whose blades can twist in the hub, with a fine angle for takeoff and climbing like a low gear, and a coarser angle for fast cruising like a high gear. Later constant-speed propellers adjusted the blade angle automatically to keep the engine turning at its best speed. These made planes of the 1930s and 1940s much faster and more efficient.","Propellers do have a speed limit. Because the tips move fastest of all, they reach close to the speed of sound well before the plane itself does. When that happens, shock waves form at the tips, noise soars, and the propeller wastes much of its power. That is one important reason propeller airplanes rarely fly faster than about 750 kilometres per hour, and why, as Part 15 will show, very fast flight needed a completely different kind of engine."]},{"heading":"Engines: From 12 Horsepower to 2,000","paragraphs":["A propeller is only as good as the engine turning it. The Wright Flyer of 1903 had a four-cylinder engine built by Charlie Taylor that produced about 12 horsepower and weighed around 80 kilograms. Horsepower is a measure of how much work an engine can do each second; one horsepower was originally meant to equal the pulling power of a strong horse. The secret for aircraft was not just power but power-to-weight ratio: how much power you get for each kilogram the plane has to carry. A heavy engine can use up most of its own power just lifting itself.","One of the most important early answers was the rotary engine, made famous by the French Gnome company, founded by the Seguin brothers. Their Gnome Omega of 1909 had seven cylinders arranged in a star shape like the spokes of a wheel, and it produced about 50 horsepower while weighing about 75 kilograms. The strange thing about a rotary engine is that the crankshaft, which in a car engine spins, was bolted firmly to the airplane and stayed still. Instead, the whole engine, cylinders and all, spun around it with the propeller attached to the front. Imagine a big metal star whirling round in front of the pilot, more than a thousand times a minute.","Spinning the whole engine sounds crazy, but it had real advantages. The cylinders cooled themselves as they whirled through the air, so no heavy water-cooling system was needed. The spinning mass also acted like a flywheel, helping the engine run smoothly. But there were problems too. The oil was mixed into the fuel and much of it was sprayed straight out of the engine unburned. The usual oil was castor oil, so pilots came home spattered with it, and many swallowed enough of the fumes to upset their stomachs. Most rotaries had no proper throttle, so pilots controlled power partly by switching the ignition on and off with a button nicknamed the blip switch. And the whirling engine acted like a giant gyroscope, making some planes, such as the Sopwith Camel of World War I, swing sharply in one direction during turns. By about 1918 rotaries had reached their limit of around 200 to 240 horsepower.","Two other kinds of engines took over. Inline and V-shaped engines arranged their cylinders in rows, like a car engine, usually cooled by water and antifreeze flowing through a radiator. These were long and narrow, which suited sleek, streamlined noses. The famous Rolls-Royce Merlin, a V-12 that powered the Spitfire and many other aircraft in World War II, eventually produced well over 1,000 horsepower. Radial engines kept the star-shaped arrangement of cylinders but held them still while the crankshaft turned the propeller, the way a normal engine does. Cooled by air flowing over metal fins, radials were tough and reliable and became the favourite engines of many bombers, fighters and airliners.","The growth in power was astonishing. The Pratt and Whitney R-2800 Double Wasp radial, introduced in 1939, had 18 cylinders arranged in two rows and produced about 2,000 horsepower, later reaching about 2,400. More than 125,000 were built, powering American fighters such as the P-47 Thunderbolt, the F6F Hellcat and the F4U Corsair, and later airliners such as the Douglas DC-6. That is about 170 times more power than the Wright brothers' first engine, from an engine that weighed well over 1 tonne, roughly fifteen times as much. Power had grown much faster than weight, and that is exactly what made bigger, faster, higher-flying planes possible."]},{"heading":"One Wing or Two? Why Monoplanes Won","paragraphs":["If you could look at a line of airplanes from 1914, most of them would have two wings stacked one above the other, held apart by wooden struts and criss-crossed with tight steel bracing wires. These were biplanes. A few, like Blériot's Type XI from Part 11, were monoplanes with a single wing. Why did designers argue about this for nearly thirty years?","The biplane's great advantage was strength without weight. Two wings joined by struts and wires form a deep, rigid box, a bit like a bridge truss, a structure made of triangles. Early wings were built of thin wood covered with fabric, and a single long wing of that kind could bend, twist or even snap. In a biplane each wing could be shorter, so the whole structure was lighter and stiffer, yet the two wings together still gave a large area for lift. That was perfect for the weak engines of the time, which needed planes to fly slowly and lift easily. Short wings also made biplanes nimble.","Early monoplanes had a worrying record. Their single wings were usually braced by wires running to posts above and below the body, and some broke in the air. In 1912, after several monoplane crashes, Britain's army briefly banned its pilots from flying monoplanes. For the next two decades, the biplane ruled.","But biplanes had a big disadvantage: drag. All those struts and wires stuck out into the wind, and every one of them slowed the plane down. Worse, air flowing over one wing disturbed the air around the other, so two wings did not give twice the lift of one. As engines grew more powerful and planes flew faster, this drag mattered more and more, because drag grows rapidly with speed.","The solution came from new materials and new ways of building. In 1915 the German engineer Hugo Junkers built an airplane entirely of metal, which you will meet in Part 13. Over the following years designers learned to build thick, strong wings with metal spars inside, a design called a cantilever wing, which needs no outside struts or wires at all. Even better, they covered wings and bodies with thin aluminium sheets that carried part of the load themselves, a method called stressed skin, like the shell of an egg sharing the weight. With strong metal wings, the main reason for biplanes disappeared.","By the mid-1930s sleek all-metal monoplanes, with retractable landing gear tucked inside the wings, were taking over. The Boeing 247 and Douglas DC-3 airliners you will meet in Part 14, and fighters like the Hawker Hurricane and Supermarine Spitfire, were all monoplanes. A few biplanes, such as the Gloster Gladiator and the Italian Fiat CR.42, still flew in the early part of World War II, and biplane trainers like the de Havilland Tiger Moth taught thousands of pilots to fly. Today biplanes are mostly found at air shows doing aerobatics, where their strength and agility still shine.","So by 1914 the basic ingredients of the airplane were in place: three-axis controls, ailerons, elevators and rudders, propellers designed as spinning wings, and engines getting lighter and more powerful every year. Yet the world was about to change in a dark way. In the summer of 1914, war broke out in Europe. Governments that had treated airplanes as curiosities suddenly wanted thousands of them, and within four years airplanes would be transformed faster than anyone had imagined. That story begins in Part 13."]}],"deepDive":"Why does banking turn a plane, and what does it cost? In a level, coordinated turn at bank angle θ, the lift vector tilts by θ. Its vertical component, L cos θ, must still equal the weight W, so the wing must make L = W / cos θ. The ratio L/W is called the load factor, n. At 30 degrees n is about 1.15, at 45 degrees about 1.41, and at 60 degrees exactly 2, so the structure and everyone aboard feel twice their normal weight. Because stall speed rises with the square root of the load factor, a plane that stalls at 100 km/h in level flight stalls at about 141 km/h in a 60-degree banked turn. Steep, slow turns close to the ground have caused many accidents for exactly this reason, which is why flight training stresses airspeed in turns. The horizontal component, L sin θ, supplies the centripetal force; the turn radius equals v²/(g tan θ), so faster planes need either steeper banks or much wider circles. A jet at about 900 km/h banked at 25 degrees turns in a circle with a radius of roughly 14 kilometres. Propellers have their own physics. Each blade section meets air coming from a combination of the plane's forward speed and the blade's rotational speed; the faster the plane flies, the more the blade must be twisted forward to keep a good angle of attack, which is why variable-pitch propellers mattered so much in the 1930s. Engine history is a story of specific power. Taylor's 1903 engine gave roughly 0.15 horsepower per kilogram. The Gnome Omega gave about 0.67. Large radials of the 1940s reached about 2 horsepower per kilogram. Historians also debate credit for ailerons. Matthew Piers Watt Boulton's 1868 patent described the principle clearly, but there is no evidence later builders knew of it, and practical development came from Esnault-Pelterie, Farman, the Aerial Experiment Association and others. The Wrights argued that their 1906 patent covered any form of lateral control. Courts in the United States largely agreed with them, while French and German courts did not, and the dispute is still discussed as an example of how patents can both reward and slow innovation.","funFact":"Pilots of rotary-engined planes in World War I were sprayed with so much unburned castor oil from the spinning engines that many suffered upset stomachs, because castor oil is also a strong laxative.","check":{"q":"Which control surface makes a plane roll by moving up on one wing and down on the other?","a":"The ailerons","wrong":["The rudder","The elevator","The throttle"]},"sources":[{"title":"Aircraft Rotations","publisher":"NASA Glenn Research Center","url":"https://www1.grc.nasa.gov/beginners-guide-to-aeronautics/aircraft-rotations/"},{"title":"Aileron","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Aileron"},{"title":"Flight instruments","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Flight_instruments"},{"title":"Propeller (aeronautics)","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Propeller_(aeronautics)"},{"title":"Rotary engine","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rotary_engine"},{"title":"Gnome et Rhône","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Gnome_et_Rhône"},{"title":"Pratt & Whitney R-2800 Double Wasp","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Pratt_%26_Whitney_R-2800_Double_Wasp"},{"title":"Biplane","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Biplane"}],"photos":[{"src":"img/deep/airplane/12-1.jpg","alt":"Diagram of the three ways an airplane turns: pitch (nose up/down), roll (tip wings) and yaw (nose left/right).","file":"Roll Pitch Yaw.JPG","url":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Roll_Pitch_Yaw.JPG","author":"NASA derivative work: labeling modified by Brews ohare (talk)","license":"Public domain"},{"src":"img/deep/airplane/12-2.jpg","alt":"A Gnome Omega rotary engine at the Newark Air Museum, its seven finned cylinders arranged like a star.","file":"Gnome Omega Newark Air Museum.jpg","url":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Gnome_Omega_Newark_Air_Museum.jpg","author":"Nimbus227","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0"},{"src":"img/deep/airplane/12-3.jpg","alt":"The 'six pack': airspeed, attitude, altimeter, turn coordinator, heading and climb-rate instruments in a small plane.","file":"Six Pack flight instruments.jpg","url":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Six_Pack_flight_instruments.jpg","author":"The original uploader was Mawilson at English Wikibooks.","license":"CC BY-SA 3.0"}],"words":4287},{"n":13,"title":"War, Mail and Crossing Oceans","when":"1914 – 1937","summary":"When war came in 1914, airplanes quickly changed from fragile scouts into fighting machines, and factories built them by the thousands. After the war, brave pilots carried the mail through the night and flew across the stormy Atlantic. Heroes like Alcock and Brown, Bessie Coleman, Charles Lindbergh and Amelia Earhart showed the world what airplanes could do.","sections":[{"heading":"From Eyes in the Sky to Guns in the Sky","paragraphs":["In August 1914, only about eleven years after the Wright brothers' twelve-second hop at Kill Devil Hills, the great powers of Europe went to war. The airplanes they took with them were still flimsy things of wood, wire and fabric. Germany had a paper strength of about 230 army airplanes, but only around 180 of them were fit to fly. Britain sent just a few squadrons to France, with about 30 machines that actually worked. Most generals thought of airplanes as interesting toys, useful perhaps for looking around, but not much more.","Looking around, it turned out, was hugely important. For centuries, armies had sent horse riders called cavalry ahead to spot the enemy. But in the summer of 1914 the armies were enormous and moved fast, and a pilot a thousand metres up could see more in an hour than a cavalry patrol could see in a day. On 22 August 1914, British airmen spotted German troops under General von Kluck moving in a way that threatened to surround the British army near the Belgian town of Mons. Their reports helped the British pull back in time. Suddenly, nobody laughed at the flying machines anymore.","Soon the war settled into long lines of muddy trenches stretching across France and Belgium. Airplanes became the army's eyes. Two-seater planes carried an observer with a camera that took pictures on glass plates, because the film of the time was not sharp enough. Back on the ground, photographers pieced hundreds of these pictures together like a jigsaw puzzle to make detailed maps of the enemy's trenches. Other planes helped artillery gunners hit targets they could not see. By March 1915 some observation planes carried a Morse-code radio transmitter, though not a receiver, so the gunners on the ground answered by laying out strips of white cloth in patterns the pilot could read from above.","If your enemy has eyes in the sky, the obvious next step is to try to poke them out. At first pilots and observers shot at each other with pistols and rifles, and some even threw bricks or dragged hooks on ropes. Mounting a machine gun was the obvious answer, but there was a tricky problem. On most planes the propeller was at the front, spinning more than a thousand times a minute. A pilot naturally wanted to aim his gun by pointing the whole airplane at the target, which meant firing straight forward. But firing straight forward meant shooting through your own propeller."]},{"heading":"The Gun That Fired Between the Blades","paragraphs":["The first bold solution came from the French pilot Roland Garros, who before the war had been famous for flying across the Mediterranean Sea. By March 1915 his Morane-Saulnier Type L monoplane had wedge-shaped steel plates bolted onto the back of each propeller blade. Most bullets passed between the blades, and the few that would have hit a blade bounced off the steel wedges instead. It was crude and it put extra strain on the engine, but it worked well enough for Garros to shoot down German planes in April 1915. Then, on 18 April, he was forced to land behind German lines and was captured. He tried to burn his plane, but the propeller survived well enough for the Germans to study his strange armoured design.","They asked the young Dutch aircraft maker Anthony Fokker, who was building planes for Germany, to look into it. Fokker's team, including the engineer Heinrich Lübbe, came up with something cleverer: a synchronization gear. The word synchronize means to make two things happen in time with each other. Here is how it worked. A cam, which is a small bump on a spinning shaft, was turned by the engine itself. Once every turn of the propeller, the cam pushed a rod or linkage that let the gun fire a single shot, timed so that the bullet passed through the gap while the blades were out of the way. The engine and the gun were, in a sense, dancing to the same beat. A popular story says Fokker invented it in just 48 hours, but historians now doubt that, and the idea itself was not entirely new: the Swiss engineer Franz Schneider and the Frenchman Raymond Saulnier had both designed similar gears before the war. Fokker's team was the first to make one that worked reliably in battle.","The gear was fitted to the Fokker E.I, a small single-seat monoplane called the Eindecker, meaning one-wing. The first victory by a plane with a synchronized gun is believed to have come on 1 July 1915. For several months German pilots held an advantage that British newspapers nicknamed the Fokker Scourge. But the basic idea could not stay secret for long, and by the middle of 1916 Britain and France had their own synchronizers in large numbers. This back-and-forth would repeat again and again for the rest of the war: one side built a better plane, then the other side caught up.","It is important to remember that behind the newspaper headlines about daring air duels, this was a terrible time. Pilots who shot down five or more enemy planes were called aces, and by the end of the war there were more than 1,800 of them. But many young airmen, often only 19 or 20 years old, did not survive. In April 1917, the worst month of the war for Britain's Royal Flying Corps, a British pilot at the front could expect, on average, to survive only about 93 hours of flying. Many others died in training accidents before they ever reached the front. Airships called Zeppelins, and later big Gotha bomber planes, dropped bombs on English towns and London, killing hundreds of ordinary people. The airplane, the dream of so many peaceful inventors, had become a weapon."]},{"heading":"Factories, Pilots and a Sheet-Metal Donkey","paragraphs":["War is cruel, but it also pours money and people into technology at an astonishing speed. In 1914, airplanes were built in small workshops, often one at a time. By 1918, France and Britain were each turning out tens of thousands of airplanes, and Germany, Italy and later the United States were building them by the thousands too. Engines became far more powerful and reliable. Factories learned to make identical parts, so a broken plane could be fixed with spares. Tens of thousands of people learned to fly, and many more learned to build, repair and design airplanes. In April 1918, Britain combined its army and navy flying services into a brand-new Royal Air Force, the first air force in the world that was independent of an army or a navy.","Most planes of the war were still biplanes made of wooden frames covered in fabric and braced with wires. But one German engineer was already thinking much further ahead. Hugo Junkers was a professor and businessman in his fifties who had made a fortune designing gas water heaters. He believed that the future of flying belonged to airplanes made entirely of metal, with thick wings strong enough to hold themselves up without any outside struts or wires. Engineers call this a cantilever wing, the same idea as a diving board that sticks out from one end with no support at the other.","His first all-metal plane, the Junkers J 1, was built in 1915. Aluminium was rare and expensive, so Junkers used thin sheets of steel, the kind normally used in electrical equipment. Steel is heavy, and the J 1 earned the affectionate nickname Blechesel, German for Tin Donkey or Sheet-Metal Donkey. On 12 December 1915, Leutnant Theodor Mallinckrodt took it on a first short hop that reached a height of almost 3 metres, and in January 1916 it made proper flights at Döberitz near Berlin. Powered by a 120-horsepower Mercedes engine, it could reach about 170 km/h, which was fast for its day, thanks to its smooth, clean shape with no drag-producing wires.","The Tin Donkey never went into battle; it was too heavy and climbed too slowly. But it proved a point that would shape every airliner you have ever seen: a metal airplane with an unbraced wing could fly, and fly well. Later Junkers designs switched to lighter aluminium alloys, and after the war his company built the F 13, one of the first all-metal airliners. The J 1 itself was put on display in the Deutsches Museum in Munich in 1926, but sadly it was destroyed in a bombing raid during the next world war, in December 1944.","When the fighting finally ended on 11 November 1918, the world was left with thousands of surplus airplanes, thousands of trained pilots, and a big question: what could flying machines do in peacetime?"]},{"heading":"The Mail Must Get Through","paragraphs":["One answer arrived in an envelope. On 15 May 1918, while the war in Europe was still going on, the United States started its first regular air mail route, linking Washington, D.C., Philadelphia and New York. The planes were Curtiss JN-4 biplanes, nicknamed Jennies, the same kind used to train army pilots. Sending a letter by air cost 24 cents, which was a lot of money at the time, and the Post Office printed a special red and blue 24-cent stamp showing a Jenny. Because the stamp went through the printing press twice, once for each colour, one sheet of 100 stamps was printed with the airplane upside down. A stamp collector bought that sheet at a post office counter for its normal price. Today a single Inverted Jenny is one of the most valuable stamps in the world; one sold in 2023 for about two million dollars.","At first the army flew the mail, then the Post Office took over with its own pilots. The bold dream was a route right across the country, from New York to San Francisco. But early planes could only fly safely in daylight, so at night the mail bags were loaded onto trains, which wiped out much of the time saved by flying. Members of Congress began to wonder whether air mail was worth the money at all.","The answer came in a dramatic winter dash on 22 and 23 February 1921. The Post Office set out to prove that mail could be flown coast to coast day and night. Several pilots took part in relays, but snowstorms grounded many of them. A pilot named Jack Knight was supposed to fly only one leg, but when his replacement could not get through, he kept going, flying through the dark and the snow from North Platte, Nebraska, toward Chicago, a distance of about 1,300 kilometres. He had a broken nose from an earlier crash, a simple compass and a torn road map. On the ground, postal workers, airfield staff and farmers lit bonfires to mark the way. Knight landed in Chicago at 8:40 in the morning, and the mail crossed the whole country in about 33 hours and 20 minutes. His flight made front-page news and helped convince Congress to keep paying for air mail.","Bonfires were not a long-term plan. Starting in 1923 the government built something new: a lighted airway, a road in the sky made of light. Steel towers about 16 metres tall were set up roughly every 16 kilometres, and each one carried a powerful rotating beacon that swept the sky six times a minute. On the ground beside many towers lay a huge concrete arrow, painted bright yellow, pointing toward the next beacon. Pilots flying at night simply hopped from one flashing light to the next, like following a trail of glowing breadcrumbs. Night air mail flights began on 1 July 1924, and by June 1925 the lit airway stretched about 4,300 kilometres from New York to San Francisco.","Flying the mail was dangerous work. In the early years many air mail pilots were killed in crashes caused by bad weather, fog and engine failure. But their flights showed that airplanes could keep a schedule, and in 1925 a new law let private companies bid to carry the mail. Some of those small mail companies would grow into the big airlines you will meet in Part 14."]},{"heading":"Fog, Ice and a Bog: Alcock and Brown","paragraphs":["Even before the war ended, a British newspaper, the Daily Mail, had offered a prize of 10,000 pounds, a fortune at the time, for the first airplane to fly across the Atlantic Ocean nonstop. In spring 1919, several teams gathered in Newfoundland, the closest point in North America to Europe, with their machines packed in crates.","One team was two Royal Air Force veterans. John Alcock, the pilot, was a 26-year-old from Manchester who had been fascinated by engines since boyhood. Arthur Whitten Brown, the navigator, was an engineer born in Glasgow to American parents. Both had been prisoners of war. Their airplane was a Vickers Vimy, a big biplane bomber designed for the war, with two 360-horsepower Rolls-Royce Eagle engines. Its bomb racks had been removed and replaced with extra fuel tanks holding about 865 gallons of petrol.","At about 1:45 in the afternoon on 14 June 1919, the heavily loaded Vimy lumbered off a field near St. John's, barely clearing the trees. Almost everything that could go wrong did. The little wind-driven generator failed, which meant no radio, no intercom and no heating in their open cockpit. An exhaust pipe split, so the engine roared deafeningly beside them all night. Thick fog and cloud hid the sea, the sky and the stars, and Brown needed to see the Sun or stars to work out their position with his sextant, an instrument for measuring angles. Twice, flying blind in cloud, Alcock lost control and the plane spiralled down until the fog cleared just above the waves. Around 3 a.m. they flew into a snowstorm, and ice built up on the plane and its engines. A famous story says Brown climbed out onto the wings in the freezing dark to chip ice off the engines, but Brown himself never described doing this, so it is probably a legend.","At about 8:40 the next morning, 15 June, they spotted the coast of Ireland and landed near the town of Clifden in County Galway on what looked like a lovely green field. It turned out to be Derrigimlagh Bog, and the Vimy's wheels sank into the soft ground and tipped the plane onto its nose. Neither man was hurt. They had flown about 3,040 kilometres in just under 16 hours, the first nonstop flight across the Atlantic.","Winston Churchill, then the minister in charge of the air force, presented their prize, and within about a week King George V knighted both men. Sadly, Sir John Alcock died in a crash in France just six months later, in December 1919. Their Atlantic crossing was a triumph, but it also showed how risky long flights still were. To be fair to everyone, they were not the only ocean-crossers that year. A few weeks earlier, in May 1919, a US Navy flying boat called the NC-4 had crossed the Atlantic in stages, stopping in the Azores islands along the way, and in July the British airship R34 crossed in both directions. Alcock and Brown's claim to fame is that they did it in one nonstop hop, so 1919 became the year the Atlantic was first conquered by air."]},{"heading":"Queen Bess: Bessie Coleman Learns in French","paragraphs":["Bessie Coleman was born on 26 January 1892 in Atlanta, Texas, the tenth of thirteen children. Her mother was African American and her father may have had Cherokee or Choctaw grandparents. The family were sharecroppers, poor farmers who worked someone else's land, and as a girl Bessie picked cotton and walked kilometres to a one-room school. She loved maths and books, saved her money, and later moved to Chicago, where she worked as a manicurist in a barbershop.","In the barbershop she heard soldiers home from the war telling stories about flying in France. She decided she would fly too. There was one enormous problem: in the United States at that time, no flight school would accept a student who was a woman, and none would accept a student who was Black. Bessie was both. Robert Abbott, the publisher of the Chicago Defender, an important Black newspaper, told her to try France instead, where attitudes were different. She took French lessons at night, and with help from Abbott and a Chicago banker named Jesse Binga, she sailed for Paris on 20 November 1920.","In France she learned to fly a Nieuport biplane. On 15 June 1921 the Fédération Aéronautique Internationale, the international organization that issues pilot licences and records, gave her a licence. She was the first African American woman, and the first woman of Native American descent, to earn a pilot's licence.","Back in America, Coleman became a barnstormer, a stunt pilot who toured the country performing at air shows. Crowds called her Queen Bess. She did loops and figure eights and walked on wings, and she used her fame for a purpose. She refused to perform at shows where Black spectators were not allowed in, or had to use separate gates, and she dreamed of opening a flying school so that other Black Americans would not face the barriers she had. She never got the chance. On 30 April 1926, while preparing for a show in Jacksonville, Florida, her plane went into a dive and she was thrown out and killed, along with her mechanic, William Wills. Investigators later found that a loose wrench had jammed the controls. She was only 34. Her dream did not die with her: within a few years, a flying club named after Bessie Coleman was helping Black Americans learn to fly in Los Angeles, and today every year pilots drop flowers over her grave in Chicago."]},{"heading":"Lucky Lindy and the Spirit of St. Louis","paragraphs":["In 1919 a New York hotel owner named Raymond Orteig, who had been born in France, offered a 25,000-dollar prize for the first nonstop flight between New York and Paris. That is about 5,800 kilometres, nearly twice as far as Alcock and Brown had flown. For years nobody could do it. Several famous pilots tried in big planes with multiple engines and crews, and some of them crashed or vanished.","Charles Lindbergh was a tall, quiet 25-year-old from Minnesota. He had been a barnstormer and an army-trained pilot, and since 1926 he had flown the air mail between St. Louis and Chicago, often at night and in bad weather. He believed the best way to win was the opposite of what everyone else was trying: one engine, one pilot, and as much fuel as possible. With money from St. Louis businessmen and his own air mail savings, he ordered a plane from the small Ryan Airline Company in San Diego. In about 60 days, Lindbergh and Ryan's engineer Donald Hall turned their design into the Spirit of St. Louis.","The Spirit was really a flying fuel tank. It had a single 223-horsepower Wright Whirlwind radial engine, and its wing was stretched to 14 metres to lift about 450 US gallons of fuel. The main fuel tank sat in front of the pilot for safety, so there was no front windshield at all; Lindbergh looked ahead through side windows or a small periscope. To save weight he left behind a radio, a parachute and even a proper seat cushion, using a wicker chair. He navigated by dead reckoning, which means working out your position from your speed, compass heading and the time that has passed.","At 7:52 a.m. on 20 May 1927, the Spirit bumped down the muddy runway at Roosevelt Field on Long Island and only just cleared the telephone wires at the end. Then came the long, lonely hours. Lindbergh flew over Nova Scotia and Newfoundland and out over the dark Atlantic, through fog and cloud where ice began to coat the wings. The hardest enemy was sleep. He had been awake for more than a day before he even took off, and he later wrote that the plane's twitchy handling, which forced him to keep correcting it, helped keep him awake. After a night over the ocean he sighted fishing boats, then the coast of Ireland, only a few kilometres from where he had aimed.","At 10:22 p.m. Paris time on 21 May, after about 33 and a half hours alone in the air, he landed at Le Bourget airfield. He had imagined a quiet arrival. Instead, roughly 150,000 people poured across the field, lifted him out of the cockpit and carried him around on their shoulders, while souvenir hunters began tearing pieces of fabric off his plane until soldiers and police rescued it.","Lindbergh became the most famous person in the world almost overnight. He won the Orteig Prize, and Time magazine named him its first Man of the Year. Remember, though, that he was not the first to fly the Atlantic; he was the first to fly solo and nonstop between New York and Paris. His real impact came next. He toured 82 American cities in all 48 states, and the excitement became known as the Lindbergh Boom: within months the amount of air mail jumped by about half, applications for pilot licences tripled, and the number of airplanes grew several times over. Ordinary people began to see flying not just as a stunt, but as the future. Later in life Lindbergh's reputation was damaged by his political views before the Second World War, and historians still discuss that side of his story, but nobody doubts the courage of his 1927 flight. The Spirit of St. Louis now hangs in the Smithsonian's National Air and Space Museum in Washington, D.C."]},{"heading":"Amelia Earhart: Across the Atlantic and Into Mystery","paragraphs":["A year after Lindbergh, in June 1928, a young social worker and pilot from Kansas named Amelia Earhart became the first woman to cross the Atlantic by air, aboard a three-engine plane called Friendship. The journey from Newfoundland to Burry Port in Wales took about 21 hours. But Earhart was a passenger, not a pilot, and she said she felt like a sack of potatoes. Newspapers called her Lady Lindy anyway. She was determined to earn the title properly.","On 20 May 1932, exactly five years after Lindbergh's departure, Earhart took off alone from Harbour Grace, Newfoundland, in a bright red Lockheed Vega 5B. That night her altimeter failed, ice formed on the wings, and flames flickered from a cracked exhaust pipe. Fighting strong winds, she decided not to try for Paris, and after about 15 hours she landed in a cow pasture at Culmore, just north of the city of Derry in Northern Ireland. A surprised farm worker asked if she had flown far, and she reportedly replied that she had come from America. She was the first woman to fly solo across the Atlantic, and only the second person ever to do it.","In 1937, Earhart set out on her biggest adventure: to be the first woman to fly around the world, roughly following the equator. Her plane was a twin-engine Lockheed Electra 10E, and her navigator was Fred Noonan, an experienced navigator of Pan Am's ocean flying boats. After a crash ended their first attempt in Hawaii, they started again, heading east from Miami on 1 June 1937. Hopping across South America, Africa, Asia and Australia, they reached Lae, in New Guinea, at the end of June with about three-quarters of the trip done. The hardest leg was next: about 4,100 kilometres across the Pacific Ocean to Howland Island, a tiny speck of land only about 2 kilometres long. A US Coast Guard ship, the Itasca, waited there to guide them by radio.","They took off on 2 July 1937. Many hours later the radio operators on the Itasca heard Earhart's voice saying she was running low on fuel and could not see the island. The ship could hear her, but for reasons that are still debated, the radio connection never worked well both ways, and the Electra never arrived. The United States Navy and Coast Guard searched a vast area of ocean, often given as about 650,000 square kilometres, for over two weeks, but found nothing.","What happened to Amelia Earhart and Fred Noonan is still unknown. The official conclusion was that they probably ran out of fuel and went down in the ocean near Howland. Others think they may have landed on a remote reef island to the south, called Nikumaroro, and expeditions have searched there, but nothing found so far has proved it. Other ideas are even less certain. What we do know is that Earhart inspired generations of pilots, especially women, and that her disappearance showed how much flying across oceans still depended on courage, luck and the tiny radios of the day.","By the late 1930s, people had watched pilots risk everything to fly mail, cross oceans and set records. The newspapers were full of heroes. But in towns and cities around the world, more and more ordinary people were looking up at the sky with a new question in their minds: not who will be the next hero, but could I fly somewhere too?"]}],"deepDive":"Historians often say that the First World War compressed decades of aviation progress into four years, and the numbers support them. In August 1914 the armies of Europe had only a few hundred usable airplanes between them, mostly unarmed scouts with engines of under 100 horsepower. By 1918 the leading powers were producing aircraft on an industrial scale, fighter engines of 200 to 300 horsepower were common, and air forces had developed specialised roles that still exist: reconnaissance, artillery spotting, fighters, ground attack and strategic bombing. The synchronization gear is a good example of how wartime pressure works. The idea had been patented before the war by Franz Schneider and tried by Raymond Saulnier, whose test failed largely because his Hotchkiss gun fired unevenly. Fokker's team succeeded in 1915 because they had a captured example to study, an urgent military demand and a gun that suited semi-automatic triggering. Historians now treat the old story of Fokker inventing it single-handedly in 48 hours as a myth. Interwar aviation, by contrast, was driven largely by prizes, mail contracts and publicity. The Daily Mail and Orteig prizes encouraged risky record attempts, and several crews died trying before Alcock and Brown and Lindbergh succeeded. Government air mail did something less glamorous but more important: it paid for routes, airfields, weather reports and night lighting, and the 1925 Contract Air Mail Act turned that infrastructure into a business for private airlines. Records also need careful wording. The NC-4 crossed the Atlantic in stages in May 1919, the R34 airship crossed in both directions that July, Alcock and Brown flew it nonstop, and Lindbergh's achievement was the first solo nonstop flight between New York and Paris. Earhart's 1932 flight was the first solo Atlantic crossing by a woman. As for her disappearance in 1937, the evidence is still incomplete: the official conclusion was that she probably ran out of fuel near Howland Island, while the Nikumaroro hypothesis remains unproven despite many expeditions. Good historians keep such questions open until real evidence settles them.","funFact":"Because one sheet of the 1918 air mail stamp was printed with its airplane upside down, a single 24-cent Inverted Jenny stamp sold in 2023 for about two million dollars.","check":{"q":"What was special about Charles Lindbergh's 1927 flight to Paris?","a":"It was the first solo nonstop flight from New York to Paris.","wrong":["It was the first flight of any kind across the Atlantic.","It was the first flight to carry passengers to Europe.","It was the first flight to use a jet engine."]},"sources":[{"title":"Transatlantic flight of Alcock and Brown","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Transatlantic_flight_of_Alcock_and_Brown"},{"title":"Charles Lindbergh","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Charles_Lindbergh"},{"title":"Spirit of St. Louis","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Spirit_of_St._Louis"},{"title":"Bessie Coleman","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bessie_Coleman"},{"title":"Amelia Earhart","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Amelia_Earhart"},{"title":"Synchronization gear","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Synchronization_gear"},{"title":"Junkers J 1","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Junkers_J_1"},{"title":"Transcontinental Airway System","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Transcontinental_Airway_System"}],"photos":[{"src":"img/deep/airplane/13-1.jpg","alt":"Alcock and Brown's Vickers Vimy tipped on its nose in the bog near Clifden, Ireland, after crossing the Atlantic, June 1919.","file":"Alcock-Brown-Clifden.jpg","url":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Alcock-Brown-Clifden.jpg","author":"Unknown author","license":"Public domain"},{"src":"img/deep/airplane/13-2.jpg","alt":"Charles Lindbergh standing in front of his plane, the Spirit of St. Louis, 1927.","file":"Charles Lindbergh and the Spirit of Saint Louis (Crisco restoration, with wings).jpg","url":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Charles_Lindbergh_and_the_Spirit_of_Saint_Louis_(Crisco_restoration,_with_wings).jpg","author":"Unknown author","license":"Public domain"},{"src":"img/deep/airplane/13-3.jpg","alt":"Bessie Coleman, the first African American woman pilot, in her leather flying helmet and goggles.","file":"Bessie Coleman, First African American Pilot - GPN-2004-00027.jpg","url":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Bessie_Coleman,_First_African_American_Pilot_-_GPN-2004-00027.jpg","author":"National Air and Space Museum","license":"Public domain"}],"words":4483},{"n":14,"title":"The Airliner Is Born","when":"1919 – 1950s","summary":"The first airline passengers sat in cold, noisy little cabins and wore coats to keep warm. Step by step, engineers built shiny metal airliners like the DC-3, nurses became the first stewardesses, and flying boats hopped across the Pacific. New instruments let pilots fly through clouds, and sealed cabins let planes fly high above the storms.","sections":[{"heading":"Buying a Ticket to the Sky","paragraphs":["Imagine it is a chilly morning in August 1919, and you are standing on a grassy field at Hounslow Heath, just west of London. A converted wartime biplane waits for you, its engine coughing into life. On 25 August 1919 a company called Aircraft Transport and Travel began a daily service from here to Le Bourget, near Paris. It is often called the world's first regular daily international airline service. On that first day, according to one account, the plane carried just a single passenger, plus some cargo.","A few weeks later, on 7 October 1919, a group of Dutch businessmen founded KLM, Royal Dutch Airlines, with a young former army pilot named Albert Plesman in charge. Its first flight, on 17 May 1920, flew from Croydon Airport near London to Amsterdam, piloted by Jerry Shaw. Many of the early airlines of 1919 disappeared within a year or two, merged into new companies or changed their names. KLM is the oldest airline still flying under its original name, more than a century later.","Flying in those first airliners was nothing like flying today. The cabins were small boxes, sometimes made of wood and fabric, with a few wicker chairs to save weight. There was no heating worth mentioning, so passengers wore heavy coats, gloves and sometimes blankets. The engines were so loud that people had to shout or pass notes, and some airlines handed out cotton wool for passengers' ears. Planes flew low, often only a few hundred metres up, where the air is bumpy, so many passengers were airsick. Pilots navigated by following roads, railway lines and rivers, and when fog rolled in they simply landed in a field and waited. A trip from London to Paris took about two and a half hours if all went well, which was still far faster than a train and a ferry.","It was also expensive. A ticket cost about as much as a working person earned in weeks, so early passengers were mostly wealthy business people, journalists and adventurers. And there was a deeper problem: most early airlines could not make money from passengers at all. They survived on government subsidies and on mail contracts, because a sack of letters paid far better than a nervous traveller. KLM, for example, became famous for its long route to the Dutch East Indies, now Indonesia, first flown experimentally in 1924, with regular service from 1929, carrying mail and a handful of passengers on a journey that took many days and dozens of stops.","To turn flying into a real business, airlines needed bigger, safer, more comfortable airplanes that could carry enough passengers to pay their own way. Over the next twenty years, engineers would build exactly that."]},{"heading":"Tin Geese and Modern Shapes","paragraphs":["In America, an unexpected company jumped into the airplane business: Ford, the maker of the Model T car. On 11 June 1926 the Ford Trimotor made its first flight. As its name suggests, it had three engines, one on the nose and one under each wing, so that it could keep flying if one failed. It was made of metal, with its body, wings and even its control surfaces covered in corrugated aluminium, which is aluminium pressed into ridges like a tin roof to make it stiffer. People nicknamed it the Tin Goose.","Later versions could carry 17 passengers. That might not sound like much, but it was a big step up, and the strong metal construction made passengers feel safer than in wood-and-fabric planes. Trimotors flew passengers and mail for several of the early American airlines, and in November 1929 one named Floyd Bennett carried the explorer Richard Byrd and his crew on the first flight over the South Pole. About 199 Trimotors were built before production ended in 1933. A few still fly today, giving rides at air shows.","Then, on 8 February 1933, a plane flew that looked like the future. The Boeing 247 had all the ideas that modern airliners would use. Its skin was smooth aluminium that carried part of the load itself, instead of just being a covering stretched over a frame; engineers call this semi-monocoque construction. Its single wing was a cantilever wing with no struts or wires outside, like Hugo Junkers had dreamed of. Its main wheels folded up into the engine housings after takeoff to reduce drag, though a little of each wheel still stuck out. It even had an autopilot and rubber de-icing boots on the wings that could crack off ice by inflating.","Boeing Air Transport, which later became United Air Lines, ordered the first 60, and one 247 crossed the United States from San Francisco to New York in about 19 and a half hours, a record at the time. But the 247 had two weaknesses. It carried only 10 passengers, and its main wing spar, the strong beam inside the wing, ran right through the cabin, so passengers had to step over it on their way down the aisle. Because Boeing was selling its first batch to its own sister airline, rival airlines could not get 247s quickly. One of those rivals, TWA, went to a different company in California with a challenge. That company was Douglas."]},{"heading":"The DC-3: The Plane That Paid Its Own Way","paragraphs":["Donald Douglas's team answered TWA with the DC-1, a single prototype flown in 1933, followed by the slightly bigger DC-2, which carried 14 passengers. Then came a famous long-distance telephone call. C. R. Smith, the head of American Airlines, wanted a plane with beds in it, so passengers could sleep on overnight flights across the country. Douglas agreed to build a wider, longer version of the DC-2 once American promised to buy 20 of them.","The result was the Douglas Sleeper Transport, which first flew on 17 December 1935, exactly 32 years after the Wright brothers' first flight at Kitty Hawk. The daytime version, with seats instead of berths, became the DC-3. American Airlines began passenger service on 26 June 1936. Its two radial engines, each giving about 1,000 to 1,200 horsepower, carried it along at about 330 km/h, and depending on the seating it could hold 21 to 32 passengers. It could fly from New York to Los Angeles with three stops, taking about 15 hours eastbound and about 17 and a half hours westbound, when the wind was against it.","Why was the DC-3 such a big deal? Because of simple arithmetic. Running an airplane costs money for fuel, pilots and repairs whether the seats are full or empty. The DC-3 carried enough passengers, flew fast enough and cost little enough to run that it was one of the first airliners able to make a profit from passengers alone, without needing mail money. Within a few years, the great majority of American airline passengers were flying on DC-3s, and airlines around the world, from KLM in Europe to companies in South America and Asia, bought them too.","When the Second World War came, the DC-3 put on a uniform. Its military versions, such as the C-47 Skytrain, known in Britain as the Dakota, carried troops, supplies, parachutists and wounded soldiers. The Soviet Union built its own version, the Lisunov Li-2, and Japan built one too. Altogether, counting civilian and military versions, more than 16,000 were built, and only about 600 of them were original civilian DC-3s.","Pilots loved the DC-3 for being tough and forgiving, and it lasted far longer than anyone expected. Some were still carrying passengers and cargo in remote places more than 85 years after the first one flew. A popular saying among pilots goes that the only replacement for a DC-3 is another DC-3."]},{"heading":"Ellen Church and the First Stewardesses","paragraphs":["Early airliners often had a co-pilot or a young cabin boy to help passengers, but in 1930 a young woman from Cresco, Iowa, had a new idea. Ellen Church was a registered nurse who had also learned to fly. She wanted to be an airline pilot, but airlines would not hire women to fly their planes. So she went to Steve Stimpson, a manager at Boeing Air Transport in San Francisco, with another suggestion. Many people were frightened of flying, she pointed out. Wouldn't they feel calmer if a trained nurse was on board to look after them?","Boeing Air Transport agreed to a three-month trial. Church became chief stewardess and recruited seven other nurses; together they are often remembered as the Original Eight. The rules were strict and show the attitudes of the time: stewardesses had to be single, under 25 years old, shorter than about 1.63 metres and lighter than about 52 kilograms, because space and weight on the planes were so precious. They earned 125 dollars a month.","On 15 May 1930, Ellen Church made her first flight as a stewardess, on a trip of about 20 hours from the San Francisco Bay Area to Chicago, with many stops along the way. Her job was much bigger than serving coffee and sandwiches. Stewardesses helped load luggage, sometimes helped with refuelling, wound the cabin clocks, swatted flies, comforted airsick passengers, and in an emergency used their nursing skills. Passengers liked the new service so much that other airlines quickly copied it.","Church's own flying career ended after about 18 months, when a car accident injured her. She went back to nursing and studied further at the University of Minnesota. During the Second World War she served as a flight nurse in the US Army Nurse Corps, caring for wounded soldiers on evacuation flights, and she was awarded the Air Medal. Today, the job she helped invent is called flight attendant and is done by women and men all over the world. Their most important role is still the one Church imagined: keeping passengers safe."]},{"heading":"Clippers Across the Pacific","paragraphs":["In the 1930s there was a big problem with crossing oceans: there were almost no long runways anywhere along the way. The solution was to land on water. Flying boats are airplanes whose bodies are shaped like the hull of a boat, so they can take off from and land on harbours, lagoons and calm bays. The American airline Pan American Airways, led by Juan Trippe, built a network of flying-boat routes and named its big planes Clippers, after the fast sailing ships of the 1800s.","The most daring route of all crossed the Pacific Ocean, the largest ocean on Earth. Pan Am built bases on a chain of islands like stepping stones: Hawaii, the tiny atolls of Midway and Wake, and Guam. On Wake Island there was nothing at all, so the company shipped in workers and equipment, and by some accounts even soil and plants, to build a hotel and a radio station.","On 22 November 1935, the Martin M-130 flying boat China Clipper took off from Alameda, on San Francisco Bay, carrying about 111,000 letters. Up to 100,000 people are estimated to have watched from the shores of the bay. The captain was Edwin Musick, and the navigator was Fred Noonan, the same man who would vanish with Amelia Earhart less than two years later. Hopping from Honolulu to Midway, Wake and Guam, the China Clipper reached Manila, in the Philippines, on 29 November, after a total flying time of about 59 hours and 48 minutes. A voyage that took weeks by ship had shrunk to less than a week.","Paying passengers followed in October 1936. Later, in 1939, Pan Am's even bigger Boeing 314 flying boats began crossing the Atlantic. The 314 was like a flying ocean liner: it could carry 68 passengers by day or 36 in sleeping berths at night, and its lounge turned into a dining room in the evening, with tablecloths and waiters serving full meals. In January 1943, President Franklin D. Roosevelt flew in one to a wartime conference in Casablanca, making the Boeing 314 the first airplane to carry a sitting American president.","Flying boats were glamorous, but their days were numbered. They were slow and expensive to run, and during the Second World War so many long runways were built around the world that land planes could go almost anywhere. By the late 1940s the great Clippers had all been retired."]},{"heading":"Flying Blind: Instruments and Radio Beams","paragraphs":["For the first 25 years of flying, a pilot's most important instrument was the window. Pilots kept the plane level by watching the horizon, and found their way by spotting landmarks. Fog and cloud were deadly. Inside a cloud, with no horizon to see, your inner ear, the balance organ inside your head, can easily be fooled. A pilot can be in a slowly tightening turn, or even diving, while feeling completely sure that the plane is flying straight and level. Many early crashes happened exactly this way.","To solve this, the Daniel Guggenheim Fund for the Promotion of Aeronautics, set up in 1926 by the businessman Daniel Guggenheim and his son Harry, paid for research into what it called fog flying. Its star pilot was Lieutenant Jimmy Doolittle of the US Army, who was not only a daring racing pilot but also held a doctorate in aeronautics from the Massachusetts Institute of Technology, one of the first such degrees in the United States.","Doolittle and his team gathered three new instruments. The first was an artificial horizon, made by the Sperry company, using a fast-spinning gyroscope that stays pointing the same way in space; a little picture of a plane in front of a horizon line showed the pilot whether his wings were level and his nose up or down. The second was a directional gyro, another gyroscope that showed which way the plane was heading far more steadily than a wobbly magnetic compass. The third was a new, very sensitive altimeter invented by Paul Kollsman, which measured air pressure so precisely that it could show the plane's height to within about 3 metres. On top of that, radio beacons on the ground sent out signals that a needle in the cockpit could follow toward the airfield.","On 24 September 1929, at Mitchel Field on Long Island, New York, Doolittle climbed into the rear cockpit of a Consolidated NY-2 biplane, and a canvas hood was pulled over him so that he could see nothing outside. In the front cockpit sat a safety pilot, Lieutenant Ben Kelsey, ready to take over if something went wrong, who held his hands up outside the cockpit so that observers could see he was not touching the controls. Doolittle took off, flew a course of about 15 minutes, turned, came back and landed, all without ever seeing the ground. It was the first time a pilot had taken off, flown and landed using instruments alone.","Through the 1930s, a system called the radio range spread across America. Ground stations sent out the Morse code letter A, dot-dash, in some directions and N, dash-dot, in others. Where the two signals overlapped, they merged into one steady hum in the pilot's headphones. Flying along that steady tone was like walking along an invisible fence; if you drifted off, you heard the dots and dashes of A or N and knew which way to turn. Together with gyroscopic instruments, radio navigation let airlines keep their schedules even when the weather was poor, which made flying far more reliable and safer."]},{"heading":"Above the Weather: The Pressurized Cabin","paragraphs":["Even with good instruments, most airliners of the 1930s had to fly through the weather, not over it. Storms, ice and bumpy clouds usually lie in the lower few kilometres of the sky. Higher up, the air is smoother and thinner, and thinner air also means less drag, so a plane can fly faster on the same fuel. But there is a catch: at around 6,000 metres the air has only about half as much oxygen in each breath as at sea level. Without help, passengers would become dizzy, confused and eventually unconscious.","The answer was to seal the cabin and pump extra air into it, like blowing up a balloon, so that the air inside stays thicker than the air outside. This is called a pressurized cabin. The first airliner to carry passengers this way was the Boeing 307 Stratoliner, which first flew on 31 December 1938. It used the wings and tail of Boeing's B-17 bomber attached to a new, round body, because a round tube is the best shape for holding air pressure, just as soda cans and submarines are round.","Pan American started flying the Stratoliner on 4 July 1940, followed by TWA a few days later. It carried 33 passengers in comfort and cruised at about 350 km/h at heights of up to about 6,000 metres. While it flew that high, the cabin was kept at a pressure similar to being on a mountain about 3,700 metres high; at slightly lower cruising heights the cabin felt more like 2,400 metres, which is about the cabin altitude of modern airliners today. Passengers could sit back while storms rumbled far below them.","Why not keep the cabin at full sea-level pressure? Because the difference between the air pressure inside and the thinner air outside pushes outward on every part of the cabin walls, windows and doors. The bigger that difference, the stronger and heavier the body must be, and every extra kilogram of metal means fewer passengers or less fuel. So engineers choose a compromise: air thick enough to breathe comfortably, but not so thick that the plane has to be built like a submarine. You can feel this compromise yourself on any flight today. As the plane climbs and the cabin pressure slowly drops, the air trapped inside your middle ear pushes outward, and your ears pop when it escapes through a small tube to the back of your throat. Swallowing or yawning helps that tube open, which is why people chew gum or give babies a drink during takeoff and landing.","The Stratoliner's story also shows the cost of progress. One of the prototypes crashed during a test flight in March 1939, killing all ten people on board. Only ten Stratoliners were built, because the Second World War soon took over Boeing's factories. Yet the idea was here to stay. One restored Stratoliner, once Pan Am's Clipper Flying Cloud, survives today in the Smithsonian's collection."]},{"heading":"World War II Reshapes the Sky","paragraphs":["The Second World War, from 1939 to 1945, was the most destructive war in history, and once again airplanes changed faster than ever before. As with the First World War, it is worth remembering the terrible human cost behind the technology, including cities bombed from the air. But several wartime developments later made peacetime flying safer and more practical for everyone.","The first was radar, a way of finding objects by sending out radio waves and catching their echoes. Britain had built a chain of radar stations along its coast before the war. Then, in 1940, John Randall and Harry Boot at the University of Birmingham built a device called the cavity magnetron, which produced powerful, very short radio waves. Short waves made radar sets small enough to fit inside airplanes and sharp enough to see much smaller objects. Later that year British scientists carried a magnetron to the United States on a secret mission, and American laboratories began producing radar on a huge scale. After the war, radar became the basis of air traffic control, letting controllers on the ground track airliners through cloud and darkness. The same magnetron, by the way, is the part that heats your food in a microwave oven.","The second was the big four-engine airplane. Wartime bombers and transports, such as the B-17, the B-29 and the Douglas C-54, which was the military version of the four-engine DC-4, flew long distances across oceans every day. Their engines grew more powerful and dependable, and the B-29 even had pressurized crew areas. Lockheed's sleek, pressurized Constellation first flew in January 1943 and served as a military transport; after the war, TWA began flying Constellations across the Atlantic in February 1946.","The third was runways. Armies and air forces built thousands of long, hard concrete runways all over the world, from Pacific islands to Africa and the North Atlantic. Many later became civilian airports. London's Heathrow, for example, began as a wartime airfield taken over by the government in 1944. When it opened to civilian flights in 1946, its first passenger terminal was a row of army tents with wooden boards laid across the mud.","The war also left behind thousands of trained pilots, navigators and mechanics, and thousands of surplus transport planes that new airlines could buy cheaply. By the end of the 1940s, ordinary people were starting to fly in pressurized four-engine airliners like the Constellation and the Douglas DC-6, high above the weather, guided by radio and radar.","Yet there was a limit hiding in the propellers themselves. As propeller tips spin faster and faster, they approach the speed of sound, and they start losing efficiency and making shock waves. Propeller airliners topped out at cruising speeds of roughly 500 to 600 km/h, and no amount of extra engine power could push them much faster. To go faster, airplanes would need a completely new kind of engine. In fact, two young engineers, one in Britain and one in Germany, had already had a radical idea about how to build it."]}],"deepDive":"Economic historians often argue that the airliner was born twice: first as a technical possibility around 1919, and then as a business in the mid-1930s. Early airlines in Europe lived on government subsidies, and in the United States they lived on air mail contracts; passengers were almost a side business. The turning point came from a cluster of technologies arriving together in the early 1930s: stressed-skin aluminium structures, cantilever wings, retractable landing gear, wing flaps, controllable-pitch propellers and powerful, reliable air-cooled radial engines. The Boeing 247 of 1933 combined many of these, but with only 10 seats its seat-mile costs, the cost of carrying one passenger one mile, were too high. The DC-3 of 1935 added roughly two to three times the seating for only modestly higher operating costs, and that is why it is usually described as the first airliner that could make money carrying passengers alone. Its huge production run of over 16,000, almost all military, then flooded the post-war market with cheap, rugged transports. Safety and reliability required a second cluster of inventions. Doolittle's 1929 hooded flight showed that a pilot trained to trust gyroscopic instruments and a precise altimeter could fly without seeing the ground, and the radio range gave airlines invisible highways to follow. Pressurization, first offered to passengers in the Boeing 307 in 1940, let airliners cruise above much of the weather, but it introduced a new engineering challenge: every flight inflates and deflates the fuselage like a balloon, and those repeated stresses, called fatigue, would become a deadly problem in the jet age. Finally, the Second World War acted like a giant accelerator. It produced microwave radar thanks to the cavity magnetron, a global network of long concrete runways, four-engine long-range transports such as the C-54 and Constellation, and a huge pool of trained aircrew. Historians still debate how much the war sped up civil aviation compared with what peacetime research would have achieved, but the post-war airline boom clearly drew on all of these wartime legacies.","funFact":"The DC-3's prototype first flew on 17 December 1935, exactly 32 years to the day after the Wright brothers' first flight.","check":{"q":"What did Jimmy Doolittle prove with his flight on 24 September 1929?","a":"That a pilot could take off, fly and land using instruments alone, without seeing outside.","wrong":["That an airplane could cross the Pacific Ocean without stopping.","That a jet engine could power an airliner.","That passengers could sleep in beds on an airplane."]},"sources":[{"title":"KLM","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/KLM"},{"title":"Aircraft Transport and Travel","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Aircraft_Transport_and_Travel"},{"title":"Douglas DC-3","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Douglas_DC-3"},{"title":"Boeing 247","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Boeing_247"},{"title":"Ellen Church","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ellen_Church"},{"title":"China Clipper","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/China_Clipper"},{"title":"Paul Kollsman","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Paul_Kollsman"},{"title":"Boeing 307 Stratoliner","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Boeing_307_Stratoliner"}],"photos":[{"src":"img/deep/airplane/14-1.jpg","alt":"An American Airlines Douglas DC-3 parked on the ramp with its passenger door and steps open.","file":"Douglas DC-3, American Airlines JP7076904.jpg","url":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Douglas_DC-3,_American_Airlines_JP7076904.jpg","author":"Jon Proctor","license":"GFDL 1.2"},{"src":"img/deep/airplane/14-2.jpg","alt":"Pan Am's Martin M-130 flying boat 'China Clipper' flying over the San Francisco waterfront in the 1930s.","file":"China Clipper over San Francisco.jpg","url":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:China_Clipper_over_San_Francisco.jpg","author":"Sunderland, Clyde H. , aerial photographer","license":"Public domain"},{"src":"img/deep/airplane/14-3.jpg","alt":"Museum display of the green cape and uniform worn by Boeing Air Transport's first air hostesses in 1930.","file":"Boeing Air Transport air hostess uniform (6202788516).jpg","url":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Boeing_Air_Transport_air_hostess_uniform_(6202788516).jpg","author":"InSapphoWeTrust from Los Angeles, California, USA","license":"CC BY-SA 2.0"}],"words":3855},{"n":15,"title":"The Jet Engine Roars","when":"1930 – 1960","summary":"Two young inventors, one in Britain and one in Germany, each dreamed up an engine with no propeller that sucks in air, burns fuel in it and blasts it out of the back. Their jet engines made planes fly almost twice as fast. The first jet airliner had a scary problem with cracking metal, but solving it made flying much safer for everyone.","sections":[{"heading":"The Propeller Hits a Speed Limit","paragraphs":["By the late 1930s, sleek metal fighters could dive at more than 700 km/h, and airliners like the DC-3 were carrying passengers across whole continents. But engineers had noticed a problem that extra horsepower could not fix. A propeller is a set of spinning wings, and its tips travel in a big circle as well as moving forward, so they go much faster than the plane. On a fast fighter the tips came close to the speed of sound, where little shock waves form and the blades waste energy shoving air about instead of pulling. Meanwhile the piston engines were turning into heavy, shaking monsters with dozens of cylinders. And high up, where thin air would let a plane fly faster on less fuel, both the engine and the propeller struggled for air.","The answer was a completely different kind of engine, with no pistons and, in its pure form, no propeller. It would suck in huge amounts of air, squeeze it, burn fuel in it and blast it out of the back. And it was invented twice, at almost the same time, by two young men in two countries who did not know each other: Frank Whittle in Britain and Hans von Ohain in Germany.","Frank Whittle was born on 1 June 1907 in Coventry, an industrial city in the middle of England, where his father ran a small engineering business. In January 1923 he tried to join the Royal Air Force as an apprentice but failed the medical, partly because he was very short and slight. Whittle refused to give up. He built himself up with exercise and a better diet, applied again, and in September 1923 became an apprentice at RAF Cranwell in Lincolnshire, learning to be an aircraft mechanic.","He was so clever that in 1926 he was picked for officer training at the RAF College, Cranwell, where he also learned to fly. Cadets had to write a thesis, a long scientific essay. Whittle argued that future planes would fly very fast at great heights, where the air is thin, and would need a new kind of power. Over the next two years he realised that a gas turbine could do the whole job by itself: instead of using it to turn a propeller, he could use the hot, fast gas rushing out of the back to push the plane directly. That is a turbojet. In January 1930, aged only 22, he filed a patent for it. (A patent is an official document saying you invented something and giving you the right to control who makes it.) The patent was granted in 1932."]},{"heading":"No Money, No Interest and a £5 Bill He Could Not Pay","paragraphs":["You might expect the Royal Air Force to have been thrilled. It was not. Experts at the Air Ministry, the government department in charge of military flying, decided the idea was impractical. To be fair, it was a huge challenge. A jet needs a compressor that squeezes air very efficiently, and a turbine that keeps spinning at enormous speed while being blasted by gas hot enough to make ordinary metal glow and weaken. In 1930 nobody had built parts that could do that. The patent was not kept secret, so Whittle's design was published for anyone in the world to read.","For years Whittle could do very little. He was a serving officer with ordinary duties and no money to build an engine. In January 1935 his patent came up for renewal. The fee was just £5, but Whittle could not afford it and the Air Ministry would not pay, so the patent lapsed. The man who invented the jet engine could not pay a five-pound bill.","Luck turned later that year. Two former RAF officers, Rolf Dudley-Williams and James Collingwood Tinling, helped him find investors, and in March 1936 a little company called Power Jets Ltd was formed to build his engine. The RAF let Whittle work on it part-time while he studied engineering at Cambridge University. The engine was built by the British Thomson-Houston company in Rugby, which made steam turbines for power stations, and on 12 April 1937 the first Whittle engine, called the WU (Whittle Unit), was started on a test stand there. According to Whittle's own later account, the early runs were frightening: at first the engine sometimes kept speeding up even after he turned the fuel down, because fuel had pooled where it should not have, and some onlookers ran for cover. By April 1938 a rebuilt version ran for about an hour and three-quarters at 13,000 revolutions per minute. In 1938 testing moved to an empty foundry at Ladywood near Lutterworth in Leicestershire.","Short of money, Whittle rebuilt the engine again and again, often with repaired parts. Only after the Second World War began in September 1939 did the government order an engine that could fly. That engine, the W.1, first ran in April 1941. But by then Britain had already been beaten into the air by a jet built in Germany."]},{"heading":"Hans von Ohain and the First Jet Flight","paragraphs":["In the university town of Göttingen in Germany, a young physics student named Hans Joachim Pabst von Ohain was thinking along very similar lines. He was born in 1911, four years after Whittle. A story often told about him says he became fed up with the noise and shaking of piston-engined planes after a ride in one, and wondered whether a smooth, continuous flow of burning gas could do the job better. Whatever sparked it, by the time he finished his doctorate in physics at the University of Göttingen in 1935, he was sketching a turbojet of his own.","Von Ohain did not have money either, so he paid a car mechanic named Max Hahn, who worked at a local garage, to build a first model of his engine. It did not work well, but it was enough to show the idea was serious. In 1936 he filed a patent, and his professor introduced him to Ernst Heinkel, the owner of a big aircraft company who loved fast, record-breaking machines. Heinkel hired both von Ohain and Hahn and gave them space, money and skilled workers. This was a huge advantage that Whittle never had.","While preparing his patent, von Ohain read a copy of Whittle's published one and adjusted his own application so the two would not overlap. Historians agree that the two men reached the turbojet independently: they had the same basic idea at about the same time, the way Isaac Newton and Gottfried Leibniz both invented calculus, and neither copied the other.","Von Ohain's team moved fast. In 1937 they ran a test engine called the HeS 1, which burned hydrogen gas because hydrogen is easy to light and burns cleanly, letting them test the compressor and turbine without fighting fuel problems. Then they built an engine that burned liquid fuel, the HeS 3, which produced roughly 4.4 kilonewtons of thrust, about the push you would need to hold up a 450-kilogram weight. It was fitted into a small, shoulder-winged test plane called the Heinkel He 178.","On 27 August 1939, at Heinkel's factory airfield at Marienehe, near the port city of Rostock on Germany's Baltic coast, test pilot Erich Warsitz opened the throttle. The He 178 rolled down the runway with a strange, rising howl and lifted into the sky. The flight lasted about six minutes. It was the first flight of an airplane powered by a turbojet engine, and it took place just five days before Germany invaded Poland and the Second World War began in Europe.","Oddly, Germany's leaders were not very impressed at first. When Heinkel showed the He 178 to senior air force officials on 1 November 1939, they reportedly thought it looked slow and unready, and the plane was never developed into a fighter. It ended up in an aviation museum in Berlin, where it was destroyed by bombing in 1943.","Decades later, Whittle and von Ohain met in person for the first time, in 1966, and again in 1978 at Wright-Patterson Air Force Base in Ohio, where von Ohain worked after moving to the United States in 1947. They became friendly, and in 1991 they shared the Charles Stark Draper Prize, one of the biggest awards in engineering, as co-inventors of the jet engine. That is the fairest way to remember them: two inventors, one idea, reached separately."]},{"heading":"Suck, Squeeze, Bang, Blow","paragraphs":["So how does a jet engine actually work? Mechanics and pilots often sum it up in four words: suck, squeeze, bang, blow. Picture a long metal tube, open at both ends, with a shaft running down the middle like the stick through a kebab.","Suck. At the front is the intake, where air rushes in. As the plane moves, air is rammed into the opening, and the spinning compressor blades just behind it grab even more.","Squeeze. The compressor is a set of fan-like blades on the spinning shaft. Whittle and von Ohain both used a centrifugal compressor, a spinning disc that flings air outward, a bit like a salad spinner throws off water. Later engines mostly use an axial compressor, rows and rows of small blades, each row squeezing the air a little more as it travels backwards. By the time the air leaves a modern compressor, it may be squashed to dozens of times its normal pressure, and it is already hot simply from being squeezed. (You can feel the same effect when you pump up a bicycle tyre and the pump gets warm.)","Bang. The squeezed air flows into the combustion chamber, where fuel, usually kerosene, is sprayed in as a fine mist and burned. It is not really a series of bangs like in a car engine. Once lit, the flame burns steadily, like a giant blowtorch. The burning makes the gas much hotter, and hot gas wants to expand, so it rushes towards the only way out: backwards.","Blow. On its way out, the hot gas rushes through the turbine, another set of blades fixed to the same shaft as the compressor. The gas spins the turbine like wind spinning a windmill, and the turbine turns the shaft, which drives the compressor at the front. This is the clever loop at the heart of the engine: the engine uses some of its own exhaust to keep sucking and squeezing fresh air. After the turbine, the gas still has plenty of energy left, and it shoots out of the nozzle at the back at very high speed.","Why does blowing gas backwards push the plane forwards? This is Newton's third law again, the same rule you met when learning how a wing turns air downwards: for every action there is an equal and opposite reaction. The engine throws a huge mass of air backwards every second, and the air pushes the engine forwards with the same force. Think of a skateboarder throwing a heavy ball backwards and rolling forward, or a balloon zooming around a room when you let go of the neck. A jet does not push against the air behind it. It pushes on the gas it is throwing out.","The hardest part for the early inventors was the turbine. It spins thousands of times a minute while sitting in a stream of gas at many hundreds of degrees Celsius. Much of jet history since the 1930s is really the story of better metals and cooling: today's turbine blades are grown as single crystals of special alloys, with tiny holes that let cooler air flow over their surface."]},{"heading":"Turbofans: The Quiet Giants on Today's Airliners","paragraphs":["The engines on a modern airliner look nothing like the skinny tubes of the 1940s. They are huge and round, with an enormous curved-bladed fan you can see spinning as the plane taxis. These are turbofans.","A turbofan is a jet engine with a big extra fan bolted to the front. The middle part, called the core, is still a turbojet: suck, squeeze, bang, blow. But the turbine also drives the giant fan, and most of the air that fan pushes never goes through the core at all. It flows around the outside of the core, inside the engine's smooth outer casing, and out of the back. This is called bypass air. Engineers describe engines by their bypass ratio. A bypass ratio of 6 means that for every kilogram of air going through the hot core, six kilograms go around it. Some of today's newest airliner engines have bypass ratios of about 11 or 12.","Why go to all this trouble? It comes back to Newton again. To make a certain amount of thrust, you can either throw a small amount of air backwards very fast, or throw a large amount of air backwards more slowly. Physics shows that the second way wastes much less energy, so it uses less fuel. A turbofan does exactly that. Depending on the design, the cool bypass air can produce somewhere between about 30 and 70 percent of the engine's total thrust, and in the biggest airliner engines the fan does most of the work.","There is a wonderful bonus: noise. Most of the roar of an old jet came from its exhaust, a narrow blast of extremely fast gas tearing into the still air behind the plane. A turbofan wraps that hot, fast jet in a thick blanket of slower, cooler bypass air, so the mixing is gentler and the noise drops a lot. Sound-absorbing linings inside the engine casing quieten it further. That is why people living near airports today hear much less noise from each plane than they would have from a 1960s jet.","Turbofans developed step by step. The British Rolls-Royce Conway, which went into service around 1960, is often called the first production turbofan, though only a small amount of its air bypassed the core. The big leap came in the late 1960s with high-bypass engines like the General Electric TF39, built for a giant military cargo plane, and the Pratt & Whitney JT9D, which powered the first Boeing 747 jumbo jets. Every large airliner built today uses turbofans. Smaller planes on short routes often use turboprops instead, where a jet core turns an ordinary propeller, because at lower speeds a propeller is the most efficient way of moving lots of air."]},{"heading":"The First Jets Go to War","paragraphs":["Britain's first jet plane was a small, tidy aircraft built by the Gloster company, with the dull official name E.28/39. On the evening of 15 May 1941, at RAF Cranwell, the same base where Whittle had trained as a cadet, Gloster's chief test pilot Gerry Sayer climbed in, opened up the W.1 engine and took off. He flew for about 17 minutes. Whittle watched from the ground as the plane he had dreamed of for eleven years climbed away with no propeller at all, just a whistling roar. In later tests, fitted with a more powerful engine, the little plane reached more than 800 km/h. That very aircraft hangs today in the Science Museum in London.","By then the world was at war, and both sides raced to turn jets into fighters. In Germany, the Messerschmitt company designed the Me 262, a twin-engined plane with slightly swept-back wings. It first flew on jet power alone on 18 July 1942, using two Junkers Jumo 004 engines. These used the axial compressor design that most later engines followed. The Me 262 entered service in 1944 and was the world's first operational jet fighter, able to fly at more than 850 km/h, around 150 km/h faster than any Allied fighter in Europe.","It sounds like a war-winning machine, but it was not. About 1,400 were built, yet fewer than 100 were usually ready to fly at any one time. Germany was short of the special metals, like nickel and cobalt, needed for heat-resistant turbines, so the Jumo engines wore out quickly and needed a major overhaul after only about 25 hours of running. Fuel was scarce, and the jets were very vulnerable when they were slowly taking off and landing. The Me 262 arrived too late and in too small numbers to change the outcome of the war.","Britain's answer was the Gloster Meteor, powered by two Rolls-Royce Welland engines developed from Whittle's designs. It first flew on 5 March 1943, and in July 1944 No. 616 Squadron of the Royal Air Force received the first Meteors, the only Allied jet fighter to see combat in the Second World War. Their first job was to chase down V-1 flying bombs, pilotless weapons launched towards London, and the first two V-1s were brought down by Meteors on 4 August 1944. In the United States, General Electric built Whittle-type engines from British drawings, and the first American jet, the Bell XP-59 Airacomet, flew in October 1942.","The money poured into jets came from the grim needs of war, but the knowledge stayed when the fighting stopped in 1945. In November 1945 a Meteor set a world speed record of about 975 km/h, and engineers everywhere began to ask a peaceful question: if a jet could carry one pilot that fast, could it carry passengers too?"]},{"heading":"The Comet: Britain's Beautiful, Doomed Pioneer","paragraphs":["The first country to build a jet airliner was Britain. The de Havilland company designed the Comet, a gleaming silver plane with four jet engines tucked neatly inside the roots of its wings. It first flew on 27 July 1949, piloted by John Cunningham. It looked like the future, and in many ways it was.","On 2 May 1952, the British airline BOAC began the world's first scheduled jet airliner service with paying passengers, from London to Johannesburg in South Africa, with several stops along the way to refuel. Passengers were amazed. The Comet cruised at about 750 to 800 km/h, nearly twice as fast as most propeller airliners. Because it flew high, often above 10,000 metres, it soared over much of the bad weather that tossed older planes about. With no pistons hammering away, the cabin was smooth and surprisingly quiet. It carried only around 36 to 44 passengers, but for a short while it was the most glamorous way to travel on Earth.","Then came tragedy. On 10 January 1954, a BOAC Comet broke apart in the air about 20 minutes after leaving Rome and fell into the Mediterranean Sea near the island of Elba. All 35 people on board died. The fleet was checked, small changes were made, and flights restarted. Then, on 8 April 1954, a Comet flown for South African Airways broke apart near Naples. All 21 people on board died. Every Comet was grounded, and the investigators were given an urgent mystery to solve.","To understand what went wrong, remember the pressurised cabin: sealed and pumped full of air so people can breathe high up. That makes the cabin like a balloon, blown up every time the plane climbs and let down every time it lands. The Comet flew higher than earlier airliners, so the push on its skin was bigger.","The Royal Navy helped drag wreckage from the seabed near Elba, and engineers at the Royal Aircraft Establishment in Farnborough came up with a brilliant test. They took a complete Comet fuselage and built a giant water tank around it. Then they pumped water into the cabin and let it out again, over and over, to copy the stress of real flights, while moving the wings up and down with hydraulic jacks to imitate the bumps of flying. Using water instead of air was safer: if the cabin split, water would just leak out, instead of the whole thing bursting like an exploding balloon.","On 24 June 1954, after the equivalent of 3,057 flights (1,221 real ones before the test plus 1,836 in the tank), the fuselage split open. The crack had started at the corner of an opening in the cabin's skin. In the wreckage of the Elba crash, investigators traced the break to a small cut-out in the roof, a window for a radio direction-finding aerial, where a crack had grown from a rivet hole near its corner.","This kind of failure is called metal fatigue. If you bend a paper clip back and forth, it does not break the first time, but after many bends it snaps. Each bend causes tiny damage that adds up. In the Comet, the metal around the corners of cut-outs was being stressed much more strongly than the designers had calculated, and with each pressurisation a microscopic crack crept a little further, until one day it suddenly ripped open. You may have heard that the Comet crashed because it had square windows. That is too simple. Its passenger windows actually had rounded corners. The real problem was that the skin was too thin and the stress around the corners of openings and rivet holes was far higher than expected.","The Comet's disaster taught the whole aviation world lessons that still protect you every time you fly. Later airliners were given thicker skins, rounder openings and stronger frames. They were designed to be 'fail-safe', so that if one part cracks, the load is carried by others and the crack stops before it becomes dangerous. Full-scale fatigue testing of fuselages became normal, and planes began to be inspected for tiny cracks throughout their lives. The investigation also invented new ways of rebuilding wreckage to find the cause of an accident. A redesigned Comet 4 returned to service and, on 4 October 1958, BOAC began the first regular jet passenger service across the Atlantic, between London and New York. But by then, an American rival was ready."]},{"heading":"The Boeing 707 and the True Jet Age","paragraphs":["In Seattle, Boeing, which built big jet bombers for the US Air Force, gambled much of its own money on a prototype jet transport, the Boeing 367-80 or 'Dash 80', which first flew on 15 July 1954. On 7 August 1955, test pilot Tex Johnston flew it over a boat race on Lake Washington in front of a huge crowd and rolled the big plane all the way over in a barrel roll. Boeing's president reportedly told him never to do it again, but it showed how strong the airplane was.","The Dash 80 became two famous aircraft: the KC-135 air-refuelling tanker for the Air Force and the Boeing 707 airliner. On 13 October 1955, Pan American World Airways, then America's leading international airline, ordered 20 Boeing 707s along with 25 of a rival jet, the Douglas DC-8. The 707 had wings swept back at a sharp angle and its four engines hung in pods under the wings, where they were easier to service and further from the cabin if something went wrong.","On 26 October 1958, a Pan Am 707 took off from New York's Idlewild Airport (now JFK) for Paris, stopping for fuel at Gander in Newfoundland. The 707 could carry well over 100 passengers, often around 140 to 180 depending on the seating, at roughly 900 km/h. That meant one jet could do the work of several propeller airliners, so tickets could slowly become cheaper. Including a close cousin called the 720, 1,010 were built, and the Douglas DC-8 soon joined it on routes all over the world.","Crossing the Atlantic now took hours instead of a long day, and people began to talk about the glamorous 'jet set'. Within a few years most big airlines were swapping their propeller fleets for jets. From Whittle's unpaid £5 bill to jets flying around the planet had taken less than 25 years.","Yet even as jets became normal, test pilots knew there was a frontier still out there. Back in the war years, pilots of the fastest propeller fighters had sometimes put their planes into steep dives and felt something terrifying. As the needle on the airspeed indicator crept higher, the whole plane began to shudder and buck. The controls went stiff or stopped working. Some planes would not come out of the dive at all. It was as if the sky itself was building an invisible wall in front of them. What was this wall, and could anyone fly through it?"]}],"deepDive":"Who invented the jet engine? Historians give a split answer, and it is a good example of how invention really works. Frank Whittle filed his British patent in January 1930 and ran the first turbojet on a test stand on 12 April 1937. Hans von Ohain filed his German patent in 1936 and, with Ernst Heinkel's money behind him, got the first jet-powered aircraft, the He 178, into the air on 27 August 1939, almost two years before Britain's Gloster E.28/39 flew on 15 May 1941. So Whittle was first on paper and first to run a working engine, while von Ohain was first to fly. Other names matter too: the French engineer Maxime Guillaume patented a gas-turbine propulsion idea in 1921, A. A. Griffith in Britain worked on axial compressors, and Anselm Franz at Junkers designed the Jumo 004, the first jet engine to be mass-produced. The physics of why turbofans won is neat. Thrust equals the mass of air moved each second multiplied by how much its speed is increased. But the energy wasted in the exhaust rises with the square of the exhaust speed. So pushing a large mass of air gently (a high-bypass turbofan) gives the same thrust for much less fuel than pushing a small mass violently (a pure turbojet), which is why modern engines have bypass ratios of around 10 or more and fans over 3 metres across. Pure turbojets survived only where speed mattered more than economy, such as on Concorde. The Comet investigation is a landmark in engineering safety. The Farnborough water-tank test failed at 3,057 pressure cycles, while the two crashed aircraft had made about 1,290 and 900 pressurised flights. The Cohen inquiry found that stresses around cut-outs were far higher than de Havilland had calculated. Engineers argue about how much blame to put on construction details such as countersunk rivet holes, but all agree on the main lesson: structures that are loaded again and again must be designed and tested for fatigue, not just for a single big load. 'Fail-safe' and later 'damage-tolerant' design, now required by aviation regulators around the world, grew directly out of the Comet disasters.","funFact":"In 1935 Frank Whittle let his jet engine patent lapse because he could not afford the £5 renewal fee.","check":{"q":"Which airplane made the first flight powered by a turbojet engine, on 27 August 1939?","a":"The Heinkel He 178","wrong":["The Gloster Meteor","The Boeing 707","The de Havilland Comet"]},"sources":[{"title":"Frank Whittle","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Frank_Whittle"},{"title":"Hans von Ohain","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hans_von_Ohain"},{"title":"Heinkel He 178","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Heinkel_He_178"},{"title":"Gloster E.28/39","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Gloster_E.28/39"},{"title":"Turbofan","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Turbofan"},{"title":"Messerschmitt Me 262","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Messerschmitt_Me_262"},{"title":"de Havilland Comet","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/De_Havilland_Comet"},{"title":"Boeing 707","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Boeing_707"}],"photos":[{"src":"img/deep/airplane/15-1.jpg","alt":"The Gloster E.28/39, Britain's first jet aircraft, hanging in the Science Museum in London.","file":"Gloster E.28-39, Science Museum, London.jpg","url":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Gloster_E.28-39,_Science_Museum,_London.jpg","author":"The wub","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0"},{"src":"img/deep/airplane/15-2.jpg","alt":"Diagram of a jet engine: air is squeezed by the compressor, burned with fuel, then spins the turbine and rushes out.","file":"Turbojet Engine (PSF).png","url":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Turbojet_Engine_(PSF).png","author":"Pearson Scott Foresman","license":"Public domain"},{"src":"img/deep/airplane/15-3.jpg","alt":"A BOAC de Havilland Comet jet airliner stops at Entebbe Airport, Uganda, on its way from London to Johannesburg, 1952.","file":"BOAC Comet 1952.jpg","url":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:BOAC_Comet_1952.jpg","author":"Ministry of Information official photographer","license":"Public domain"}],"words":4341},{"n":16,"title":"Breaking the Sound Barrier","when":"1940s – 2003","summary":"Sound travels fast, about 1,225 km/h near the ground, and early jets shook and shuddered when they flew close to it. In 1947 Chuck Yeager, with two broken ribs, flew a bullet-shaped orange rocket plane faster than sound for the first time. Later, Concorde carried passengers at twice the speed of sound, making a loud sonic boom wherever it went.","sections":[{"heading":"How Fast Is Sound?","paragraphs":["Clap your hands. The sound you hear is a ripple of squeezed air, a pressure wave, travelling outward from your hands in every direction, a bit like the rings that spread out when you drop a stone into a pond. Those ripples move fast, but not instantly. At sea level, on a mild day of about 15 degrees Celsius, sound travels at about 340 metres every second. That is about 1,225 km/h, or 761 miles per hour.","You can notice this yourself in a thunderstorm. Light arrives almost instantly, but the thunder takes about three seconds to travel each kilometre. Count the seconds between the flash and the rumble, divide by three, and you have a rough idea of how many kilometres away the lightning was.","The speed of sound is not fixed. It depends mainly on the temperature of the air: in cold air the molecules move more sluggishly and pass the ripple along more slowly. High up where airliners and jets fly, around 11 kilometres above the ground, the air can be about minus 56 degrees Celsius, and sound travels at only about 295 metres per second, roughly 1,060 km/h. So a plane can break the speed of sound at a lower actual speed high up than it could near the ground.","Because of this, pilots and engineers do not usually talk about the speed of sound in km/h. They use the Mach number, which is simply your speed compared to the speed of sound in the air around you. Mach 1 means exactly the speed of sound. Mach 0.8 is 80 percent of it, which is about where airliners cruise. Mach 2 is twice the speed of sound. Speeds below about Mach 0.8 are called subsonic, the tricky zone from about Mach 0.8 to 1.2 is called transonic, faster than that is supersonic, and above Mach 5 is hypersonic.","The number is named after Ernst Mach (1838–1916), an Austrian physicist who studied how things move through air faster than sound. In the 1880s he and his colleagues managed to take photographs of bullets in flight, using a very brief electric spark as a flash, and the pictures showed the shock waves around a speeding bullet for the first time. The Swiss engineer Jakob Ackeret suggested naming the speed ratio after him in 1929."]},{"heading":"Shock Waves and the 'Wall' in the Sky","paragraphs":["When a plane flies slowly, it is constantly sending out little pressure ripples ahead of itself, like the bow wave of a slow boat. These ripples travel faster than the plane, so they warn the air ahead that something is coming, and the air smoothly moves aside to let the wing through.","Now imagine the plane speeding up until it is almost as fast as its own ripples. The pressure waves can no longer run ahead. They pile up in front of the plane and on parts of the wing, squashing together into a thin, sudden jump in pressure called a shock wave. Air passing through a shock wave is suddenly slowed, squeezed and heated. Something important happens even before the plane itself reaches Mach 1: because air speeds up as it flows over the curved top of a wing, small patches of air there go supersonic first, perhaps when the plane is only at Mach 0.75 or 0.8, and shock waves start forming on the wing.","Those shock waves caused frightening problems for the fastest planes of the 1940s. Drag shot up sharply, as if the air had suddenly turned thick. The smooth flow behind the shock could break away from the wing, making the plane shake violently, an effect called buffeting. On some planes the shock waves on the tail made the elevator useless, so pulling back on the stick did nothing at all, and a fighter in a steep dive might keep going down until it reached thicker, warmer air lower down. A few designs had the opposite problem, called tuck-under, where the nose dropped even further on its own. Even the airspeed instruments gave wrong readings near Mach 1. Some pilots died in these early high-speed dives, among them the British test pilot Geoffrey de Havilland Jr., whose experimental tailless DH 108 broke up in 1946.","No wonder pilots and newspapers started calling it the 'sound barrier'. But it is important to understand that it was never a real wall. Bullets, and the tip of a cracking whip, had been going faster than sound for a long time. The 'barrier' was a set of engineering problems: too much drag, too little engine power, the wrong wing and tail shapes and controls that stopped working. Solve those, and the barrier would disappear. The question was who could solve them first."]},{"heading":"Glamorous Glennis and the Broom Handle","paragraphs":["In the United States, the Army Air Forces and the National Advisory Committee for Aeronautics (NACA, which later became NASA) decided to build a special research plane just to explore the zone around Mach 1. The Bell Aircraft company built it, and it was called the X-1.","Its designers borrowed a clever idea. They knew that a .50-calibre machine-gun bullet stayed stable when flying faster than sound, so they made the X-1's body almost the same shape as that bullet, just scaled up enormously, with thin, straight wings. It was bright orange, so it could be seen easily in the sky. It did not have a jet engine. It had a rocket engine, the XLR11, with four separate combustion chambers that the pilot could switch on one at a time. With all four burning it gave about 6,000 pounds of thrust. A rocket carries both its fuel and the oxygen to burn it, so it does not need to suck in air. The problem was that it burned through its fuel in just a few minutes, so it could not waste any taking off from the ground. Instead, the X-1 was carried up hanging under a big B-29 bomber and then dropped like a bomb, before the pilot lit the rocket.","The pilot chosen for the attempt was Captain Charles 'Chuck' Yeager, born on 13 February 1923 in the small town of Myra, West Virginia. He had flown P-51 Mustang fighters in the Second World War, had been shot down over France and escaped, and was known as a calm, gifted pilot with a superb feel for machines. He named the X-1 'Glamorous Glennis' after his wife, Glennis.","Over many flights in 1947, Yeager crept closer to Mach 1, a little faster each time, at Muroc Army Air Field in the Mojave Desert of California, a dry lake bed so flat and huge that it made a natural runway. At about Mach 0.94 he discovered a serious problem: the elevator stopped working, just as it had for the wartime fighter pilots. The team's solution was to use the adjustable horizontal stabilizer, the whole front part of the tail, which could be tilted by a small motor. Moving the whole surface still worked in the shock waves when the small hinged elevator did not.","Then, two nights before the big flight, Yeager went horse riding with his wife, fell and broke two ribs. Afraid he would be taken off the flight, he had a local doctor tape his ribs up and told almost no one. But there was a problem: to lock the X-1's door he needed to pull a lever hard with his right arm, which now hurt too much. His friend and fellow engineer-pilot Jack Ridley sawed off a piece of broom handle for him to use as a lever. It worked.","On 14 October 1947, the B-29 dropped the X-1 high over the desert. Yeager fired the rocket chambers and climbed. At about 13,700 metres (45,000 feet), the needle on his Machmeter wavered and then moved past 1. The X-1 reached about Mach 1.06, around 1,100 km/h at that height. And then something surprising happened: the ride became smooth. The shaking that had come before eased off once the plane was fully supersonic. On the ground, people heard the rumble of a sonic boom. Yeager had become the first person to fly faster than sound in level flight.","The achievement was kept secret at first, because it had military importance. News leaked to a magazine in December 1947, and in June 1948 the US Air Force officially announced it. Today 'Glamorous Glennis' hangs in the National Air and Space Museum in Washington, D.C., not far from the Wright brothers' 1903 Flyer. Only 44 years separated the two machines."]},{"heading":"Swept Wings, a Wasp Waist and Jackie Cochran","paragraphs":["The X-1 was a rocket plane built for research. To make fast jets that could fly every day, designers needed new shapes. The first big idea was the swept wing. Back in 1935, at a science conference in Rome, the German engineer Adolf Busemann pointed out something clever. When a wing is angled backwards, the air does not 'feel' the full speed of the plane. Only the part of the airflow that crosses the wing straight from front to back really matters, and on a swept wing that part is slower. So shock waves form later. A wing swept back at 45 degrees can fly roughly 30 percent faster before the shock-wave troubles begin.","German researchers tested swept wings in wind tunnels during the Second World War. When the war ended in 1945, American engineers found the reports, and the Boeing company redesigned its new B-47 jet bomber with wings swept back at 35 degrees. North American Aviation reshaped a straight-winged design into the swept-wing F-86 Sabre, and in the Soviet Union the MiG-15 fighter used swept wings too. Look at almost any airliner today and you will see the same thing: the wings slant backwards, so it can cruise close to Mach 0.85 without hitting the worst of the shock-wave drag.","The second big idea came from a quiet NACA engineer named Richard Whitcomb (1921–2009), working with wind tunnels at the Langley laboratory in Virginia. In the early 1950s, a new US fighter, the Convair F-102, had a frustrating problem: it was designed to fly faster than sound, but in tests it could not get past Mach 1 in level flight, because the drag near Mach 1 was far too high. Whitcomb realised that what mattered was how the plane's total cross-section, its slice-by-slice thickness from nose to tail including the wings, changed along its length. Where the wings joined, the plane suddenly got much 'fatter', and that sudden bulge caused extra shock-wave drag. The cure was to pinch the fuselage in where the wings joined, like a wasp's waist or an old-fashioned Coke bottle, so the total cross-section changed smoothly. This became known as the area rule. The reshaped F-102 went supersonic, and Whitcomb won the Collier Trophy, America's top aviation award, for 1954. (In fairness, a German engineer, Otto Frenzl, had noticed the same effect during the war, but Whitcomb discovered it independently and made it famous.)","Among the people who flew these new swept-wing jets was one of the greatest pilots of the century: Jacqueline 'Jackie' Cochran (1906–1980). Born Bessie Lee Pittman in Florida to a family without much money, she worked from childhood, later built her own cosmetics business, and learned to fly in the early 1930s, picking it up remarkably quickly. In 1938 she won the Bendix Trophy, a famous cross-country air race against the best male pilots. During the Second World War she led the Women Airforce Service Pilots (WASP), who flew military planes around the United States so that male pilots could be sent overseas.","On 18 May 1953, flying a Canadair Sabre, a Canadian-built version of the F-86, over Rogers Dry Lake in California, Cochran dived through Mach 1 and became the first woman to fly faster than sound. Her friend Chuck Yeager flew alongside her. By the time she died, she held more speed, distance and altitude records than any other pilot, man or woman, and had won the Harmon Trophy for the world's outstanding woman aviator five times."]},{"heading":"Hypersonic Rockets and the Blackbird","paragraphs":["Once Mach 1 was conquered, the race went on. In December 1953 Yeager flew an improved rocket plane, the X-1A, to Mach 2.44. But the most amazing research plane of all was the North American X-15.","The X-15 was a black, stubby-winged rocket plane about 15 metres long. Like the X-1, it was dropped from a bomber, this time from under the wing of a giant B-52. Its XLR99 rocket engine produced about 57,000 pounds of thrust, almost ten times as much as the X-1's. Flying that fast creates terrible heat, because the air in front of the plane is squeezed and rubbed against its skin, so the X-15 was covered in a nickel alloy called Inconel X that stays strong even when glowing hot. Between 1959 and 1968, twelve pilots made 199 flights. One of them was Neil Armstrong, who later became the first person to walk on the Moon.","On 3 October 1967, Air Force pilot William 'Pete' Knight flew a specially modified X-15A-2, coated with a heat-resistant layer that slowly burned away to protect it, and carrying extra fuel tanks. He reached Mach 6.7, about 7,270 km/h. That is fast enough to cross the United States in well under an hour. Even with its coating, the plane was badly scorched, and parts of a dummy engine attached under its tail melted and fell off. Knight's flight is still the record for the fastest crewed, powered airplane. In 1963 another X-15 pilot, Joe Walker, flew to about 108 kilometres high, above the line many scientists use as the edge of space. The program also had a sad day: on 15 November 1967, pilot Michael Adams died when his X-15 went into a spin and broke apart. The lessons learned about heat, high-speed control and pressure suits helped design the Space Shuttle.","The X-15 was a rocket that flew for only minutes. The Lockheed SR-71 Blackbird was a jet that could cruise at more than three times the speed of sound for over an hour. It was designed by Kelly Johnson's secret team at Lockheed, nicknamed the Skunk Works, and first flew on 22 December 1964. It was a spy plane, built to fly so high (around 24,000 metres) and so fast that nothing could catch it. About 85 percent of its structure was made of titanium, a metal that stays strong in heat, because at Mach 3.2 its skin got hotter than 260 degrees Celsius, hotter than a kitchen oven. The panels were made to fit loosely so they could expand in flight, which meant the Blackbird famously leaked fuel all over the runway before take-off and only sealed up once it heated in the air.","On 28 July 1976 an SR-71 set the world speed record for a jet-powered airplane: about 3,530 km/h. Fifty years later, no crewed jet has officially beaten it. In 1974, one flew from New York to London in under 1 hour and 55 minutes. Thirty-two were built, and although twelve were lost in accidents, none was ever shot down. The Air Force retired them in 1998, and NASA flew its last ones in 1999."]},{"heading":"Boom! The Noise That Grounded a Dream","paragraphs":["Every supersonic plane has a noisy side effect. Remember the pressure ripples that pile up into shock waves? Around a supersonic plane, the shock waves spread out behind it in the shape of a huge cone, like the V-shaped wake behind a speedboat, but in three dimensions. Where that cone sweeps over the ground, people hear a sonic boom: a sharp double bang, like a thunderclap or an explosion. It comes as two bangs because there is one shock wave at the nose and another at the tail. Scientists call the pressure pattern an N-wave, because on a graph it looks like the letter N: a sudden rise in pressure, a drop below normal, then a sudden jump back.","Many people think a sonic boom happens only once, at the moment a plane 'breaks' the barrier. That is not true. A plane makes a boom all the time it flies supersonically, dragging a 'boom carpet' along the ground beneath its path that can be tens of kilometres wide and as long as the whole flight. Everyone underneath hears it as the cone passes over them. The crack of a whip is a tiny sonic boom too: its tip moves faster than sound.","Sonic booms are startling. They can wake people, frighten animals and occasionally crack windows. In 1964, the US government tested what people would think by flying jets supersonically over Oklahoma City eight times a day for six months. About 15,000 people complained, and lawsuits followed. In the early 1970s the United States banned ordinary civil aircraft from flying faster than sound over land, and many other countries have similar rules. (In 2025 the US government told aviation officials to work on lifting that ban, so the rules may change as quieter designs are tested.) That decision would shape the fate of the world's first supersonic airliners: they could only fly fast over the sea."]},{"heading":"Concorde and the Tu-144: Airliners Faster Than a Bullet","paragraphs":["In the 1960s many people believed supersonic airliners were the obvious next step. In 1962 Britain and France signed a treaty to build one together: Concorde. In the Soviet Union, the Tupolev design bureau built a rival, the Tu-144, so similar-looking that Western newspapers nicknamed it 'Concordski'. The race to fly first was close. The Tu-144 won, taking off on 31 December 1968. It became the first passenger transport to go supersonic, on 5 June 1969, and the first to reach Mach 2, on 26 May 1970.","Concorde's first prototype took off from Toulouse in France on 2 March 1969, flown by test pilot André Turcat. It was a beautiful machine: a long, needle-thin body and a curving delta wing shaped like a paper dart. Its four Rolls-Royce/Snecma Olympus 593 turbojets used afterburners, extra fuel sprayed into the hot exhaust, to give a burst of power for take-off and for pushing through Mach 1. Because the nose stuck far out in front, pilots could not see the runway when landing at a steep angle, so the whole nose was hinged and drooped down for take-off and landing. At its cruising speed of about Mach 2, around 2,150 km/h at about 18,000 metres high, the air rubbing against it made the airframe so hot that the plane stretched slightly in flight, and passengers could see the dark blue sky and the curve of the Earth.","Concorde entered service with British Airways and Air France on 21 January 1976. It carried around 100 passengers and could cross from London or Paris to New York in about 3.5 hours, roughly half the time of an ordinary jet. Because of the time difference, you could leave London in the morning and land in New York at an earlier clock time than when you set off. But it was very expensive. It burned huge amounts of fuel, tickets cost far more than first class on other planes, and the sonic boom kept it off most land routes. Airlines that had ordered it cancelled, and only 20 were ever built.","The Tu-144 had an even shorter life. One crashed during a display at the Paris Air Show on 3 June 1973. It began carrying mail and cargo in December 1975 and passengers from 1 November 1977, between Moscow and Alma-Ata in Kazakhstan, but after only 55 passenger flights it was withdrawn in 1978. Much later, in the late 1990s, NASA borrowed a modified Tu-144 for supersonic research.","Concorde flew safely for almost 25 years. Then, on 25 July 2000, Air France Flight 4590 was taking off from Paris when it ran over a strip of metal that had fallen from another plane. A tyre burst, and pieces of rubber hit the underside of the wing and broke open a fuel tank. The leaking fuel caught fire, two engines lost power, and the plane crashed into a hotel near the town of Gonesse. All 109 people on board and 4 people on the ground were killed. Concorde was grounded while engineers added Kevlar linings to the fuel tanks and tougher tyres, and flights started again in November 2001.","But fewer people were flying after the attacks of September 2001, the planes were getting old and costly to maintain, and the airlines decided it was time to stop. British Airways flew Concorde's last commercial flights on 24 October 2003. Most of the surviving Concordes now rest in museums around the world. For now, ordinary passengers no longer fly faster than sound.","That might sound like a sad ending, but it teaches something important about inventions. The future of everyday flying did not turn out to be faster. While Concorde carried about a hundred wealthy passengers at twice the speed of sound, a different kind of plane was quietly changing the world by going the other way: slower than Concorde, but enormously BIGGER, and cheap enough for millions of ordinary families."]}],"deepDive":"Why is the region near Mach 1 so troublesome, and why does it get easier again above it? Below the speed of sound, pressure disturbances travel ahead of the aircraft and the air adjusts gradually. In the transonic range, roughly Mach 0.8 to 1.2, the flow is a messy mixture: supersonic pockets form over the wing and end in shock waves that move about, interact with the boundary layer (the thin slow layer of air next to the skin) and make it separate. This is what causes the sudden drag rise, buffeting and shifts in where the lift acts, which in turn produced the nose-down 'tuck' and useless elevators of the 1940s. Once the whole aircraft is supersonic, the shocks settle into fixed positions at the nose and tail, which is why Yeager reported a smoother ride after passing Mach 1. Engineers attacked the problem from several directions. Thin wings delay the supersonic pockets. Sweep reduces the airflow component that 'counts' by the cosine of the sweep angle. The area rule minimises wave drag by smoothing the cross-sectional area distribution, and all-moving tails, first proposed for Britain's cancelled Miles M.52, keep pitch control working through the shocks. Historians still debate some early claims of supersonic flight, such as German pilots in wartime dives or test pilot George Welch's dives in the XP-86 shortly before Yeager's flight, because the instruments of the time were unreliable near Mach 1 and no official measurements exist. Yeager's flight is recognised as first because it was measured and recorded in level flight. The economics of supersonic transport are just as interesting as the physics. Wave drag makes supersonic flight burn far more fuel per passenger than subsonic flight, and the sonic boom, an overpressure of roughly 90 to 100 pascals for Concorde, made flights over land socially unacceptable. NASA's current research aims to shape aircraft so the boom becomes a soft 'thump', which could one day change the rules.","funFact":"Chuck Yeager flew faster than sound for the first time with two broken ribs, using a sawed-off broom handle to close the X-1's door.","check":{"q":"What kind of engine powered the Bell X-1 when Chuck Yeager first flew faster than sound in 1947?","a":"A rocket engine","wrong":["A jet engine","A piston engine with a propeller","A steam engine"]},"sources":[{"title":"Mach number","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mach_number"},{"title":"Sound barrier","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sound_barrier"},{"title":"Bell X-1","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bell_X-1"},{"title":"Chuck Yeager","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Chuck_Yeager"},{"title":"Area rule","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Area_rule"},{"title":"Jacqueline Cochran","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Jacqueline_Cochran"},{"title":"North American X-15","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/North_American_X-15"},{"title":"Concorde","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Concorde"}],"photos":[{"src":"img/deep/airplane/16-1.jpg","alt":"The orange Bell X-1 'Glamorous Glennis', first plane to fly faster than sound (1947), hanging in the National Air and Space Museum.","file":"Bell X-1 Glamorous Glennis - www.joyofmuseums.com - National Air and Space Museum.jpg","url":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Bell_X-1_Glamorous_Glennis_-_www.joyofmuseums.com_-_National_Air_and_Space_Museum.jpg","author":"Joyofmuseums","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0"},{"src":"img/deep/airplane/16-2.jpg","alt":"Ernst Mach's 1888 photograph of a supersonic bullet, with V-shaped shock waves spreading out from its nose and tail.","file":"Photography of bow shock waves around a brass bullet, 1888.jpg","url":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Photography_of_bow_shock_waves_around_a_brass_bullet,_1888.jpg","author":"Ernst Mach (1838–1916)","license":"Public domain"},{"src":"img/deep/airplane/16-3.jpg","alt":"A British Airways Concorde comes in to land at Manchester Airport in 1998, wheels down and nose drooped.","file":"G-BOAB 1 B.Ae-Aerospatiale Concorde 102 British Airways MAN 13SEP98.jpg","url":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:G-BOAB_1_B.Ae-Aerospatiale_Concorde_102_British_Airways_MAN_13SEP98.jpg","author":"Ken Fielding","license":"CC BY-SA 3.0"}],"words":3802},{"n":17,"title":"Jumbo Jets and a Shrinking World","when":"1969 – 2019","summary":"In 1969 a giant plane called the jumbo jet took off, big enough to carry hundreds of people at once. Bigger planes and cheap airlines made tickets cheaper, so millions of families could fly on holiday or visit faraway relatives. Soon the whole world felt closer together.","sections":[{"heading":"A Plane Two and a Half Times Too Big","paragraphs":["In the mid-1960s, the jet age was already here. The Boeing 707 and the Douglas DC-8 were carrying travellers across oceans at more than 900 km/h, and airports were getting busier every year. But there was a problem: there were so many passengers that airports were filling up with planes, and each 707 could carry only about 140 to 190 people. Juan Trippe, the bold head of Pan American World Airways (Pan Am), had a very big idea. He wanted a jet roughly two and a half times the size of the 707.","Why so big? Trippe's reasoning was simple. Flying a plane costs a lot of money no matter how many people are inside: you need pilots, fuel, maintenance and a slot at the airport. If you can squeeze many more seats into one plane without needing many more pilots or much more fuel per person, every single seat becomes cheaper to fly. Cheaper seats mean cheaper tickets, and cheaper tickets mean more people can afford to travel.","In April 1966, Pan Am ordered 25 of the new giants for about 525 million US dollars, an enormous sum at the time. Because Pan Am was the first customer, it had more say in the design than almost any airline before or since. Boeing handed the job to an engineer named Joe Sutter, who had been working on the smaller 737. Sutter and his team at first imagined a double-decker plane, but they soon chose a different shape: one very wide main deck, wide enough for two aisles and ten seats across in economy class. This was the birth of the wide-body airliner, a plane whose cabin is so wide that it needs two aisles instead of one.","There was one more surprise in the design. Many people at the time believed that supersonic airliners like Concorde would soon take over passenger flying, and that big subsonic jets would end up carrying mainly cargo. So Sutter's team planned ahead. They lifted the cockpit up above the main deck, which gave the 747 its famous hump. With the pilots sitting up high, the whole nose of the plane could swing upward like a giant door, letting freight be loaded straight in from the front. The supersonic future never really arrived for most travellers, but the hump became the 747's trademark, and the area behind the cockpit became a lounge or a cabin with extra seats.","Building the plane was a challenge as huge as the plane itself. No Boeing factory was big enough, so the company built a brand-new one near Paine Field in Everett, about 50 kilometres north of Seattle in Washington State. It is still the largest building in the world measured by volume. The thousands of workers who designed and built the first 747s in a hurry, in under three years, were nicknamed “the Incredibles.”","On 9 February 1969, test pilots Jack Waddell and Brien Wygle took the first 747 into the air over Washington. The plane was about 70 metres long, its wings stretched about 60 metres from tip to tip, and its tail stood about as tall as a six-storey building. Reporters quickly gave it a nickname that stuck forever: the jumbo jet."]},{"heading":"Queen of the Skies","paragraphs":["Imagine walking into Pan Am's first 747 in New York in January 1970. After years of narrow, tube-like cabins, the space would have seemed astonishing: two aisles, rows of seats stretching far ahead, and, if you were lucky enough to travel first class, a spiral staircase leading up to a lounge in the hump. The first passenger service, from New York to London, took off on 22 January 1970, about six hours late because of an overheating engine on the plane originally planned for the trip. Even a giant can have first-day jitters.","The secret behind the 747 was not just its size but its engines. It was designed around the Pratt and Whitney JT9D, one of the first big high-bypass turbofans. You met turbofans in Part 15: a large fan at the front pushes most of the air around the outside of the hot engine core instead of through it. Moving a large amount of air fairly slowly is much more efficient than moving a small amount of air very fast. Engineers expected these engines to give roughly twice the push of older jet engines while burning about a third less fuel. Four of them hung under the 747's wings.","How many people could it carry? That depended on how an airline arranged the seats. A typical three-class layout held about 366 passengers, and later versions with a stretched upper deck and tightly packed economy seats could carry well over 400. Some Japanese airlines flew special short-distance 747s on busy domestic routes with more than 500 seats.","There was a catch, and it nearly sank Boeing. A jumbo jet was only cheap to fly when it was full. Figures from the time showed that a 747 flying 70 percent full still burned over 95 percent of the fuel of a completely full one. Soon after the 747 entered service, a recession hit, fewer people travelled and airlines had trouble filling their giant new planes. Boeing had spent so much developing the 747 that it had to lay off tens of thousands of workers around Seattle. Around that time, a famous billboard near the city joked: “Will the last person leaving Seattle turn out the lights.”","But travel bounced back, and the 747 became one of the most successful airliners ever. Airlines learned to fill seats by lowering fares, offering package holidays and selling cheap tickets to people who could book early. The jumbo carried rock bands, presidents, pilgrims, students and grandparents. Cargo versions with the swing-up nose carried racehorses, car parts and even, on one modified 747, a NASA Space Shuttle riding piggyback on its back.","Boeing kept improving the design for more than half a century, from the 747-100 to the 747-400 with its upturned wingtips and, finally, the 747-8. The last one, a cargo plane for Atlas Air, was delivered on 31 January 2023, ending a 54-year production run. In total 1,574 were built. Pilots and fans still call it the Queen of the Skies."]},{"heading":"Europe Joins the Race: Airbus","paragraphs":["In the 1960s, American companies such as Boeing, Douglas and Lockheed built most of the world's airliners. European countries had brilliant engineers, but each country's aircraft industry was small on its own. Britain's de Havilland had built the first jet airliner, the Comet, and Britain and France were working together on Concorde, but no single European company could match the giant American factories.","So European governments decided to team up. On 18 December 1970, France and West Germany formed Airbus Industrie, each holding half. Spain's CASA joined in 1971, and Britain joined in 1979. Instead of one company building everything, each partner built different pieces. Wings might come from Britain, parts of the body from Germany, the tail from Spain, and final assembly happened in Toulouse in the south of France.","That raised a funny problem: how do you move a whole section of an airliner's body from Hamburg to Toulouse? Airbus answered with one of the strangest-looking aircraft ever flown, the Super Guppy. It was built from an old Boeing propeller airliner with a gigantic bulging body on top, a bit like a pregnant whale with wings. Its nose swung open so that huge plane parts could slide inside. Because parts arrived just when they were needed for assembly, the A300 is described as the first airliner built with a “just-in-time” system.","The first Airbus plane, the A300, first flew on 28 October 1972 and began carrying passengers with Air France between Paris and London on 23 May 1974. It was a wide-body jet with two aisles, like the 747, but with an important difference: it had only two engines instead of four. In fact, it was the world's first twin-engine wide-body airliner. Two big modern turbofans used less fuel and needed less maintenance than three or four engines, which made the A300 cheap to run on busy medium-distance routes.","At first sales were slow, and many airlines were unsure about buying a European plane. Then, in 1977, the American airline Eastern Air Lines borrowed four A300s to try them out, liked them and ordered more. That was a turning point. In the following decades Airbus added more models, including the A320 family of narrow-body jets in the 1980s, which became one of the best-selling airliner families ever.","By the 2000s, the airliner business had become a contest between two giants, Boeing in the United States and Airbus in Europe. Each company tries to build planes that use less fuel, carry the right number of people and fly the right distances for what airlines want. That competition has pushed both companies to make quieter, more efficient planes and has helped keep the price of new aircraft from rising even faster. In total, 561 A300s were delivered before production ended in 2007."]},{"heading":"The Double-Decker Giant: the A380","paragraphs":["If the 747 was a jumbo, what would be bigger? In the 1990s Airbus decided to beat Boeing at its own game. It designed the A380, the only full-length double-deck jet airliner ever built. From nose to tail, both levels of the plane are full passenger cabins, connected by staircases.","The A380 first flew on 27 April 2005 from Toulouse. Its wingspan, about 80 metres, was carefully kept just under a limit of 80 metres agreed for the largest airport gates, so it could still fit into big airports. Even so, many airports had to rebuild gates with two-level boarding bridges, strengthen taxiways and widen runway edges to handle it.","Singapore Airlines received the first A380 in October 2007 and flew the first passenger service, flight SQ380 from Singapore to Sydney, on 25 October 2007. A typical layout held about 525 passengers in three classes, but the plane was certified to carry up to 853 people if every seat were packed in economy. Some airlines used the extra space for luxuries that seemed impossible in a plane: showers for first-class passengers, private suites with doors, and bars where travellers could stand and chat high above the ocean.","Yet the A380 never sold as well as Airbus had hoped. Only 254 were built, and the last one was delivered to Emirates, its biggest customer, on 16 December 2021. Why did such an amazing plane struggle?","Part of the answer is a big debate about how people will fly. Airbus bet that travellers would keep flying through a few giant hub airports, such as London Heathrow, Dubai or Singapore, where enormous planes would carry crowds between the hubs. Boeing bet that people would rather fly directly between more cities without changing planes, using medium-sized, very efficient twin-engine jets like the Boeing 777 and 787. Over time, Boeing's prediction turned out to be closer to what most airlines wanted.","Modern twin-engine jets also changed the rules. Engines became so reliable that safety regulators allowed two-engine planes to fly routes far from any airport, even across oceans, which once had been flown mainly by three- and four-engine jets. A twin-engine plane with 300 seats is easier to fill than a giant with 500, and it burns less fuel overall. When the A380's biggest customers stopped ordering more, Airbus announced in 2019 that it would end production.","The A380 did not fail at flying. Passengers love its quiet, roomy cabin, and many are still in service. But its story teaches an important lesson about inventions: a machine has to fit the way people actually want to live and travel, not just be the biggest or most impressive."]},{"heading":"Flying for Everyone: the Low-Cost Airlines","paragraphs":["For much of the 20th century, a plane ticket was something many families saved up for over many months. In many countries, governments controlled which airlines could fly which routes and even what prices they could charge. Airlines competed with fancy meals and friendly service rather than low fares.","A few rebels showed that things could be different. Pacific Southwest Airlines, which started flying inside California on 6 May 1949, is often called the world's first low-cost airline. Because it flew only within one state, it was not bound by the national fare rules. Its success inspired Herb Kelleher and Rollin King in Texas, who founded what became Southwest Airlines. Southwest started flying on 18 June 1971 between Dallas, Houston and San Antonio, again staying inside one state so it could set its own low prices.","In 1978 the United States passed the Airline Deregulation Act, which let airlines choose their own routes and fares. New airlines appeared, older ones had to compete, and prices for many trips fell. Across the Atlantic, the British businessman Freddie Laker launched his no-frills Skytrain between London and New York in 1977. It was eventually forced out of business, but it proved that cheap long-distance tickets could attract huge crowds. Europe opened up its own skies step by step in the late 1980s and 1990s, until any airline from one European Union country could fly between cities in other member countries.","That opened the door for Europe's budget airlines. EasyJet began in 1995, flying two Boeing 737s from Luton, near London, to Glasgow and Edinburgh, with the slogan that flying should be as affordable as a pair of jeans. Ryanair, an Irish airline founded in the 1980s, remade itself on the Southwest model and grew into Europe's largest budget airline by passenger numbers.","How can a low-cost airline sell such cheap tickets and still make money? It uses a whole box of tricks. Most budget airlines fly just one type of aircraft, so pilots, mechanics and spare parts can all be shared. They fly mostly short, direct routes and often use smaller, cheaper airports a little outside big cities. They turn each plane around very quickly at the gate, so it can fly more trips each day, because a plane sitting on the ground earns nothing. They sell tickets online instead of paying travel agents, and they charge extra for checked bags, choosing a seat or snacks. Seats are packed a bit closer together. Every small saving adds up.","The result was a revolution. Students could visit friends in other countries for a weekend. Workers could go home to see their families more often. Cities that had never had many visitors suddenly filled with tourists. In 2017, airlines around the world carried about 4.1 billion passengers on roughly 42 million scheduled flights, and the number kept climbing to well over 4 billion in 2019. Flying, once a luxury for the rich, had become something ordinary people did every day."]},{"heading":"A Shrinking World","paragraphs":["Of course the Earth did not actually get smaller. But when a trip that once took weeks by ship takes only hours by plane, the world starts to feel smaller. In 1900, crossing the Atlantic by steamship took about a week. By the 1970s, a jumbo jet could do it in about seven or eight hours. A journey from London to Australia that once took more than a month by sea could be done in about a day.","This changed ordinary life in many ways. Holidays abroad, once rare, became normal for millions of families. Whole towns, like beach resorts in Spain or islands in the Caribbean and Southeast Asia, grew up around tourists arriving by air. At the same time, families began to spread out across the globe. A grandmother might live in India while her grandchildren grow up in Canada, and a flight lets them meet in person instead of only by letter or video call. Students travel to universities on other continents, scientists fly to meetings, and sports teams play matches on the other side of the world the same week.","Planes also carry things, not just people. A large share of air cargo, often around half, travels in the bellies of passenger planes, under the passengers' feet, and the rest in dedicated freighters like the cargo 747. In 1971 a young American named Frederick W. Smith founded Federal Express (now FedEx) based on a simple idea: one company should pick up a package, fly it on its own planes overnight through a central hub, and deliver it the next morning. FedEx started flying on 17 April 1973 with 14 small jets serving 25 American cities. On its first night it carried just 186 packages. Today overnight delivery of letters, parcels and online shopping is something people take for granted.","Air freight is especially useful for things that are valuable, urgent or that spoil quickly. Fresh roses grown in Kenya or Colombia can be in a shop in Europe or North America within a couple of days of being cut. Fresh fish, strawberries out of season, newspapers, mobile phones, medicines, vaccines and even human organs for transplant operations travel by air. Factories around the world depend on parts delivered just in time, sometimes by plane.","There were deeper changes too. When people can travel easily, ideas, music, food and languages travel with them. Business meetings, scientific conferences and international friendships all became easier. Some historians say that cheap flights helped turn the world into one connected global economy. Of course, as later parts will show, flying also brings new problems. But there is no doubt that the jet age changed where people live, how they work and whom they meet."]},{"heading":"The Hidden Machinery of Flying","paragraphs":["A modern flight is the tip of an iceberg. Underneath every trip lies a huge system of other inventions, rules and workers that grew up because of airplanes.","Start with the airport. As more cities got flights, airlines realised they could not fly directly between every pair of cities. Delta was one of the first to build a hub-and-spoke system, using Atlanta as its hub. Picture a bicycle wheel: the hub is the middle, and the spokes reach out to the rim. Planes fly from many small spoke cities into the hub, passengers change planes there, and then fly out to their final destinations. This lets an airline connect hundreds of cities with far fewer routes and planes. It is why some airports, such as Atlanta, Dubai and Istanbul, have become enormous transfer stations where most passengers never even leave the terminal. In 2025, Atlanta alone handled about 106 million passengers, counting both arrivals and departures.","Next, how do you get onto the plane without walking across a windy, rainy apron? The answer is the jet bridge, a moving, enclosed corridor that stretches from the terminal right to the plane's door. An engineer named Frank Der Yuen is credited with inventing it. United Airlines tested a prototype in 1954, and the first working one opened at Chicago's O'Hare Airport in 1958. Its end can swing, rise, fall and stretch so it can fit planes of many different sizes, from small jets to the double-deck A380.","Then there is booking a seat. In the early days of airlines, reservations were tracked on paper cards in giant rotating files, and making a booking could take about 90 minutes. In 1953, an IBM salesman named Blair Smith happened to sit next to C. R. Smith, the president of American Airlines, on a flight from Los Angeles to New York. The two men (not related) talked about using computers to keep track of every passenger's reservation. The result was SABRE, which went online experimentally in 1960 and became fully working in 1964. It was the world's first fully working computer reservation system. Bookings that had taken an hour and a half now took seconds. Systems like it were among the first giant business computer networks, years before the internet.","Finally, airplanes cross borders, so countries had to agree on rules. Even before World War II ended, 52 countries signed the Convention on International Civil Aviation, often called the Chicago Convention, on 7 December 1944 in Chicago. It created the International Civil Aviation Organization (ICAO), which began work on 4 April 1947 and became part of the United Nations system. ICAO sets common standards that almost every country follows, such as pilot licensing, rules of the air, airport design, aircraft registration and accident investigation. Thanks to agreements like these, a pilot from Brazil and a controller in Japan can understand each other, usually in English, which became the common language of international aviation.","Almost nobody thinks about these hidden inventions while waiting for a flight. Yet without hubs, jet bridges, booking computers and shared rules, the jumbo jet could never have carried billions of people."]},{"heading":"When the Sky Went Quiet","paragraphs":["Being so well connected has a downside. Anything people carry with them can travel quickly too, including germs. In the Middle Ages, a disease might take months or years to cross a continent along trade routes. In the jet age, a traveller can catch an illness in one country and be on the other side of the world before even feeling sick.","This became very clear in early 2020. A new coronavirus, which causes the illness COVID-19, was first identified in the city of Wuhan, China, at the end of 2019. Within weeks, cases appeared in many countries, partly because infected travellers carried the virus on planes before anyone knew how widely it had spread. On 11 March 2020 the World Health Organization declared a pandemic, which means a disease spreading across the whole world.","To slow the virus, countries closed their borders and told people to stay home. Canada closed its border to most foreign travellers on 16 March 2020, and Australia closed its borders to non-residents on 20 March. Within weeks, flying almost stopped. In April 2020, the number of seats offered by airlines around the world was down by about 91 percent compared to normal. Airports that usually roared with activity fell eerily silent. Many families who were living apart suddenly could not visit each other at all.","With nowhere to fly, airlines had to park their planes. By mid-April 2020, roughly two out of every three mainline airliners in the world were sitting on the ground; at the peak, about 16,500 airliners were parked in storage. Rows of jumbo jets and A380s were lined up at airports and in desert storage yards in places like Arizona and Australia, where the dry air helps stop metal from rusting. Some older planes, including many 747s, never flew passengers again.","Over the whole year of 2020, the number of air passengers fell by more than half (about 60 percent by some counts). Airlines lost huge amounts of money, and many workers lost their jobs. Cargo flights, on the other hand, became even more important, carrying masks, medical supplies and later vaccines. Some airlines even took the seats out of passenger planes to fill the cabins with boxes.","Travel slowly came back as vaccines arrived and borders reopened. By 2023 and 2024, flying in many parts of the world had returned to roughly the levels of 2019. The pandemic showed two things at once: how deeply the world now depends on airplanes, and how they can carry dangers as well as people.","And that leads to some big questions. With tens of thousands of flights in the air every day, how do planes avoid bumping into each other? What happens when something goes wrong? And what are all those flights doing to the air we breathe and to the planet's climate? Part 18 takes a close look at safety, problems and the hard questions that come with the gift of flight."]}],"deepDive":"Why did bigger planes make flying cheaper? Airline economists measure cost per available seat-kilometre: the cost of moving one seat one kilometre. Many costs, such as the two or three pilots in the cockpit, landing fees and a large share of maintenance, are paid per flight, not per seat. Spread them across 400 seats instead of 150 and each seat gets cheaper. High-bypass turbofans added a second saving, because pushing a large mass of air slowly is more efficient than pushing a small mass fast, which is why the 747 was built around the JT9D. But the savings only appear when seats are filled. The load factor, the share of seats actually sold, matters enormously: a 747 that was 70 percent full still burned more than 95 percent of the fuel of a full one. That is why airlines invented discount fares, advance-purchase tickets and package tours, and why computer reservation systems that could juggle millions of bookings and prices became so valuable. Historians and economists still debate the best shape for an airline network. Hub-and-spoke systems connect many cities with few routes and help fill big planes, but they force passengers to change planes and concentrate delays at a few airports. Point-to-point networks are more convenient for travellers but need many medium-sized, efficient aircraft. Airbus's A380 was a bet on hubs; Boeing's 787 was a bet on point-to-point. The fact that 1,574 747s were built over 54 years but only 254 A380s suggests the market moved toward medium-sized twin-engine jets, helped by rules that let very reliable twins fly far from airports over oceans. Counting passengers is also trickier than it looks. Airline statistics count each person once per flight, giving roughly 4 to 4.5 billion passenger trips around 2019, while airport statistics count each traveller twice, once leaving and once arriving, which is why airport groups reported about 9.1 billion airport passengers in 2019. Neither number means 4 or 9 billion different people: many travellers fly dozens of times a year, while most people on Earth have still never flown.","funFact":"The factory Boeing built in Everett, Washington, to make the 747 is the largest building in the world measured by volume.","check":{"q":"Which was the world's first twin-engine wide-body airliner, and which company built it?","a":"The Airbus A300","wrong":["The Boeing 747","The Airbus A380","The Douglas DC-3"]},"sources":[{"title":"Boeing 747","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Boeing_747"},{"title":"Airbus A300","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Airbus_A300"},{"title":"Airbus A380","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Airbus_A380"},{"title":"Low-cost carrier","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Low-cost_carrier"},{"title":"Convention on International Civil Aviation","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Convention_on_International_Civil_Aviation"},{"title":"Sabre (travel reservation system)","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sabre_(travel_reservation_system)"},{"title":"FedEx Express","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/FedEx_Express"},{"title":"Impact of the COVID-19 pandemic on aviation","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Impact_of_the_COVID-19_pandemic_on_aviation"}],"photos":[{"src":"img/deep/airplane/17-1.jpg","alt":"The very first Boeing 747, the 'City of Everett' prototype, parked outdoors at the Museum of Flight in Seattle.","file":"Boeing 747-100 prototype N7470 \"City of Everett\" at Museum of Flight September 2011 1.jpg","url":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Boeing_747-100_prototype_N7470_%22City_of_Everett%22_at_Museum_of_Flight_September_2011_1.jpg","author":"4300streetcar","license":"CC BY 4.0"},{"src":"img/deep/airplane/17-2.jpg","alt":"The bulging Super Guppy that once flew Airbus parts between factories, now in the Aeroscopia museum in Toulouse, France.","file":"Boeing Super Guppy Turbine.jpg","url":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Boeing_Super_Guppy_Turbine.jpg","author":"Concorde642","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0"},{"src":"img/deep/airplane/17-3.jpg","alt":"A Singapore Airlines Airbus A380, the double-deck giant, climbs away from London Heathrow with its four engines.","file":"Singapore Airlines A380-841 (9V-SKA) taking off from London Heathrow Airport (1).jpg","url":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Singapore_Airlines_A380-841_(9V-SKA)_taking_off_from_London_Heathrow_Airport_(1).jpg","author":"Adrian Pingstone (Arpingstone)","license":"Public domain"}],"words":4240},{"n":18,"title":"Safety, Problems and Hard Questions","when":"1950s – today","summary":"Thousands of planes fly every day, so people invented radar, air traffic controllers and bright orange 'black boxes' to keep them safe. In 2009 a calm pilot landed a plane on a river and everyone survived. But planes also make noise and pollution, and people are still working to fix those problems.","sections":[{"heading":"A Sad Day over the Grand Canyon","paragraphs":["On the morning of 30 June 1956, two airliners took off from Los Angeles International Airport only minutes apart. One was TWA Flight 2, a graceful Lockheed Super Constellation with three tail fins, heading for Kansas City. The other was United Air Lines Flight 718, a Douglas DC-7, heading for Chicago. Both planes were flying east across the deserts of Arizona.","Back then, much of the sky over the United States was what is called uncontrolled airspace. Outside the busy areas near airports and along certain airways, nobody on the ground was watching where planes were or telling them how to keep apart. Pilots were expected to follow a rule called “see and be seen”: look out of the window and steer clear of other aircraft. That works on a clear day with few planes. It works much less well among towering clouds, at high speed, when two planes are approaching each other from awkward angles.","That day, big puffy cumulus clouds had built up over the Grand Canyon. Both crews steered around them, and somewhere above the canyon the two planes came together. The investigators later concluded simply that the pilots did not see each other in time. All 128 people on the two aircraft died. At the time it was the deadliest airline accident in American history.","The accident shocked the country, and people asked hard questions. Why, in an age of radar and jet engines, was nobody keeping track of airliners over open country? The answer was that air traffic control had not kept up with the huge growth in flying after World War II. The equipment was old, there were too few controllers, and responsibility was split between different government groups, with military planes following separate rules.","Congress responded. Money was found in 1957 to hire more controllers and buy radar, at first using surplus military equipment. Then the Federal Aviation Act of 1958 created a new body, the Federal Aviation Agency, which began on 23 August 1958. Its first leader was Elwood Quesada, a former Air Force general. For the first time, one agency had authority over all American airspace, including military flights. In 1967 it was renamed the Federal Aviation Administration (FAA), the name it still has today.","In 2014 the crash site deep in the canyon was named a National Historic Landmark, the first for an event that happened in the air. It is a quiet reminder that many of the safety rules we take for granted today were written after painful lessons."]},{"heading":"Eyes on the Sky: Air Traffic Control and Radar","paragraphs":["Air traffic control is a bit like a giant, invisible system of roads and traffic lights in the sky, run by people talking on radios. Its job is to keep planes safely apart, to keep traffic flowing, and to give pilots information such as weather and runway conditions. The pilot still has the final say over the aircraft, but controllers can see the bigger picture.","It began very simply. At Croydon Airport near London, a small wooden control tower was put into use in February 1920, and the first airport control tower in the United States opened at Cleveland in 1930. Early controllers used flags, light signals and blackboards. Radio made a huge difference, because a controller could finally talk to a pilot in the air.","Radar was the next giant step. Radar, short for radio detection and ranging, was developed in the 1930s and used heavily in World War II. A radar station sends out pulses of radio waves. When a pulse hits a plane, a tiny echo bounces back. By timing how long the echo takes to return, and noting which direction the antenna was pointing, the system can work out where the plane is. After the war, radar screens let controllers see aircraft as glowing dots, even in cloud or at night.","Later came secondary radar. Each airliner carries a box called a transponder. When a ground station's radar signal reaches it, the transponder answers with its own radio reply, giving a four-digit identification code and the plane's altitude. That way, the dot on the controller's screen comes with a label saying exactly which flight it is and how high it is flying. Today many planes also use a system called ADS-B, in which the aircraft works out its own position by satellite navigation and broadcasts it several times a second for controllers and other planes to receive.","A flight passes from one team of controllers to another, like a baton in a relay race. Ground controllers guide it along the taxiways. Tower controllers clear it to take off. Approach controllers guide it as it climbs away from the busy airport area. Then large en-route centres look after it high up, often for hundreds of kilometres, each controller watching one slice of sky called a sector. Near the destination, the process happens in reverse.","Planes also carry their own last line of defence. A system called TCAS (traffic collision avoidance system) listens to the transponders of nearby aircraft. If two planes get dangerously close, it tells one crew to climb and the other to descend, in a calm computer voice. Together, controllers, radar and these onboard systems make the “see and be seen” days of 1956 feel like a very long time ago."]},{"heading":"The Black Box That Is Really Orange","paragraphs":["When an airplane accident happens, one of the first things investigators look for is the so-called black box. Here is the first surprise: it is not black. Flight recorders are painted bright orange, often with reflective stripes, so they are easier to spot among wreckage. The name black box probably comes from World War II, when secret British electronic equipment was often kept in black-painted cases.","The man behind the modern flight recorder was an Australian scientist named David Warren, born in 1925 on Groote Eylandt, an island off northern Australia. His own life had been touched by an air accident: when he was a boy, his father died in the 1934 crash of an airliner called Miss Hobart over Bass Strait.","In the early 1950s Warren was working at the Aeronautical Research Laboratories in Melbourne when experts were trying to understand the mysterious crashes of the de Havilland Comet, the jet airliner you met in Part 15. Nobody knew exactly what had happened in the final moments of those flights. Around the same time, Warren saw a small new pocket voice recorder at a trade show. He realised that if a crash-proof recorder in every airliner kept track of both the instrument readings and the voices in the cockpit, investigators would no longer have to guess. He built a prototype flight data recorder in 1956 and a combined data and voice recorder in 1958. At first, some people were not keen; a few pilots worried about being spied on. But the idea's value became obvious.","After an inquiry into a 1960 crash in Queensland recommended recorders, Australia became the first country to make cockpit voice recorders compulsory. The United States introduced its first rules for them in 1964, and soon recorders were required around the world.","Today there are usually two recorders. The flight data recorder (FDR) stores many measurements, such as speed, altitude, heading, engine power and the positions of the controls, many times each second. The cockpit voice recorder (CVR) records what the pilots say and the sounds in the cockpit, from switches clicking to warning alarms. In some planes they are combined in a single unit.","They are built to survive almost anything. International standards require them to survive an impact of 3,400 times the force of gravity and fire hotter than 1,000 degrees Celsius. The memory is sealed inside layers of steel and insulation. Recorders are usually mounted near the tail, which is often the part of the aircraft most likely to survive a crash. If a plane goes down at sea, an underwater locator beacon attached to each recorder sends out a ping that search ships can hear, for up to about 30 days, from depths as great as 6,000 metres.","David Warren died in 2010, but his invention has helped solve countless accidents. Each time a recorder reveals what went wrong, the lesson can be used to protect every flight that follows."]},{"heading":"Checklists, Simulators and Teamwork","paragraphs":["Modern airliners are extraordinarily complicated, with thousands of switches, systems and warning lights. No human can remember every step perfectly every time, especially when tired or surprised. So pilots use one of the simplest safety inventions of all: the checklist.","A famous story is often told about how flying checklists became important. In 1935 a large new Boeing bomber prototype, the Model 299, crashed during a test flight because the crew forgot to release a lock on the controls. People said the plane was simply too complicated for one person to fly. Instead of giving up, test pilots wrote short lists of key steps to check before takeoff, landing and other stages of flight. Today every airliner crew works through checklists for normal flying, and special checklists for emergencies, such as an engine fire or a loss of cabin pressure.","Pilots also practise in flight simulators. One of the first, the Link Trainer, was built by an American organ and piano maker named Ed Link around 1929 and was used to train many thousands of pilots to fly using instruments. Modern full-flight simulators are astonishing machines: a real cockpit sits inside a big box mounted on powerful hydraulic legs that tilt and shake it, while wraparound screens show realistic views of airports around the world. Pilots can practise engine failures, storms and emergency landings that would be far too dangerous to try in a real plane. Airline pilots return to the simulator regularly throughout their careers.","The third key idea is teamwork. In the 1970s, investigators found that some accidents happened not because a plane broke, but because the crew did not talk to each other clearly. In 1978, United Airlines Flight 173 ran out of fuel near Portland, Oregon, while the captain was focused on a problem with the landing gear and other crew members did not speak up firmly enough about the falling fuel. After a NASA workshop in 1979, the psychologist John Lauber coined the term crew resource management, or CRM.","CRM teaches pilots and cabin crew to share information, to speak up when something seems wrong even to a senior captain, to divide up tasks and to double-check each other. United Airlines started the first full CRM training programme in 1981, and by the 1990s it had become standard around the world. The idea spread beyond aviation too. Hospitals now use surgical checklists and team training based on CRM, and so do firefighters, ship crews and railway workers.","Checklists, simulators and teamwork are not as dramatic as jet engines. But together they have saved many lives. And on one cold January afternoon in 2009, the world saw all three in action."]},{"heading":"The Miracle on the Hudson","paragraphs":["On 15 January 2009, a cold, clear afternoon in New York, US Airways Flight 1549 took off from LaGuardia Airport at about 3:25 pm. It was an Airbus A320 heading for Charlotte, North Carolina, with 150 passengers and 5 crew. In the cockpit were Captain Chesley Sullenberger, known as Sully, a former Air Force pilot with decades of experience, and First Officer Jeffrey Skiles.","About two minutes after takeoff, at roughly 2,800 feet (about 850 metres), the plane flew into a flock of Canada geese. Several of the big birds were sucked into the engines, and both engines lost almost all their power at once. Suddenly a heavy airliner full of people had become a glider, low over one of the most crowded cities in the world.","The crew had only moments to decide. Skiles began working through the emergency checklist for a dual engine failure, trying to restart the engines. That checklist had been written for failures at high altitude, where a crew might have several minutes, so he had to move through it very fast. The crew also switched on the auxiliary power unit, a small extra engine in the tail that kept electrical power flowing to the plane's systems.","Air traffic controller Patrick Harten quickly offered runways back at LaGuardia and then at Teterboro Airport in New Jersey. But Sullenberger judged that the plane was too low and too slow to reach either one safely. He told the controller calmly: “We're gonna be in the Hudson.” He chose the long, wide Hudson River beside Manhattan as the safest place to come down.","Gliding over the George Washington Bridge, the crew lined up with the river. Sullenberger kept the wings level and the nose slightly up, and at about 3:30 pm the A320 touched down on the water, skidding to a stop. The whole flight, from takeoff to landing on the river, lasted only about six minutes.","Inside, the three flight attendants, Donna Dent, Doreen Welsh and Sheila Dail, shouted commands and guided passengers to the exits. People climbed out onto the wings and into inflatable slides, standing in icy water up to their knees. Within minutes, commuter ferries and other boats arrived, and rescuers pulled everyone from the freezing river. Sullenberger walked the cabin twice to make sure nobody was left behind. All 155 people survived, although about 100 had injuries, five of them serious.","The newspapers called it the Miracle on the Hudson, but investigators from the National Transportation Safety Board (NTSB) saw it as the result of training, skill, teamwork and good design. They tested the situation in simulators and found that turning back to LaGuardia could only have worked in some attempts, and only if the pilots had turned instantly, with no time to understand what had happened. The NTSB concluded that ditching in the river was the right decision. It also recommended changes, such as better checklists for engine failures at low altitude and more research into bird strikes.","The aircraft itself was lifted out of the river and is now displayed at the Sullenberger Aviation Museum in Charlotte, the city it was flying to that day."]},{"heading":"Why Flying Became So Safe","paragraphs":["When you board a plane today, you are using one of the safest ways to travel ever invented. In 1970, there were about 6.35 fatal airliner accidents for every million flights. By 2019 that number had fallen to about 0.51, roughly twelve times lower, even though there were far more flights. One study of travel in the United Kingdom found that, per kilometre travelled, a car journey was about 60 times more likely to end in death than an airline flight.","How did this happen? There is no single invention. Instead, there are many layers of protection. Engines became more reliable. Radar and air traffic control kept planes apart. Weather radar helped pilots avoid storms. Ground proximity and terrain warning systems shout “Pull up!” if a plane is getting too close to a mountain or the ground. Collision avoidance systems, checklists, simulators and crew teamwork add more layers. Each layer has small holes, but it is very unlikely that the holes in all of them line up at the same time.","Perhaps the most important safety idea of all is learning from every mistake. When an accident or a serious incident happens, independent investigators, such as the NTSB in the United States, the Air Accidents Investigation Branch in Britain or the Australian Transport Safety Bureau, study everything: the wreckage, the flight recorders, weather, maintenance records, training and the design of the plane. International rules agreed through ICAO say these investigations are about finding causes and preventing future accidents, not about blaming people. Their reports are published, and the lessons are shared with airlines and manufacturers all over the world.","This means that every accident, however sad, makes future flights safer. The Comet crashes taught engineers about metal fatigue. The Grand Canyon collision led to modern air traffic control. Crashes caused by crew misunderstandings led to crew resource management. Pilots often say that the rules of aviation are written from hard experience.","Accidents still happen, and each one is taken very seriously. But in 2017, the safest year for airliners on record up to that time, there were only 10 fatal airliner accidents worldwide among tens of millions of flights. For a passenger today, the riskiest part of a plane trip is often the car ride to the airport."]},{"heading":"The Costs: Noise, Pollution and Climate","paragraphs":["Every invention has side effects, and the airplane has some big ones. The first one you notice is noise. If you live near a busy airport, planes may roar overhead every couple of minutes. Studies have found that aircraft noise can disturb sleep, make it harder for children in nearby schools to concentrate, and may even affect people's heart health over many years. Modern turbofans are much quieter than the early jets, and many airports now use quieter flight paths, limit night flights and charge noisier planes higher fees.","Then there is air pollution. Jet engines burn kerosene, a fuel made from oil. Besides carbon dioxide and water vapour, they release nitrogen oxides, tiny soot particles and other gases that affect air quality near airports and high in the atmosphere. Some small piston-engine planes still use fuel containing lead, which is being phased out because lead is harmful to people.","The biggest long-term worry is climate change. Burning jet fuel releases carbon dioxide (CO2), a gas that traps heat in the atmosphere. In 2018, aviation made roughly 2.4 to 2.7 percent of all the CO2 released by human activities around the world. That may sound small, but it comes from a fairly small share of the world's population, since most people on Earth rarely or never fly, and air travel keeps growing.","Planes also warm the climate in other ways. You have probably seen white streaks behind high-flying jets. These are contrails, short for condensation trails. Hot, wet exhaust meets the freezing air at cruising height, and the water vapour turns into tiny ice crystals, the same way your breath makes a cloud on a cold morning. Most contrails vanish quickly, but in moist air some spread into thin, wispy clouds that can last for hours. At night especially, these clouds can trap heat. When scientists add contrails and nitrogen oxides to CO2, their best estimate is that aviation causes about 3.5 percent of the human-caused warming effect on the climate, though the contrail part is still quite uncertain.","Engineers have made planes much more efficient. Jet airliners became about 70 percent more fuel efficient between 1967 and 2007, thanks to better engines, lighter materials and smarter wing shapes. But because the number of flights has grown so fast, total emissions have kept rising. In 2016, countries agreed through ICAO on a scheme called CORSIA, which asks airlines to balance growth in international emissions by paying for projects that reduce carbon elsewhere. Airlines are also starting to use sustainable aviation fuel made from waste oils, plants or other sources, but in 2023 it was still only about 0.2 percent of all jet fuel and cost two to four times as much. Part 20 looks at the cleaner planes engineers are working on now."]},{"heading":"Security, and Airplanes in War","paragraphs":["Another hard question is security. Because planes carry many people and can cross borders quickly, some criminals and violent groups have tried to take them over. This is called hijacking. Between 1968 and 1972 there were 326 hijacking attempts around the world, roughly one every five or six days, many of them in the United States. In response, the United States began screening every passenger and their carry-on bags, starting on 5 January 1973. Metal detectors and X-ray machines appeared in airports, and the number of hijackings dropped.","On 11 September 2001, a terrible attack in the United States used hijacked airliners, and nearly 3,000 people lost their lives. It changed airport security around the world. Cockpit doors were strengthened so that nobody can force their way in, and in the United States the Transportation Security Administration (TSA) was created in November 2001 to carry out passenger screening. Later rules limited liquids in hand luggage to small containers, and full-body scanners were introduced. Waiting in security lines is less fun than flying, but those checks are part of why hijackings have become very rare: about 15 worldwide between 2010 and 2019.","Finally, there is the hardest truth about the airplane. Almost as soon as it was invented, it was used in war. You saw in Part 13 how World War I turned scouting planes into fighters and bombers. In World War II, huge fleets of bombers attacked cities, and many civilians were killed on all sides. Since then, military aircraft have played a part in many conflicts. The same skills of flight that carry families on holiday can also carry weapons. Many of the Wright brothers' fellow pioneers hoped flying would bring nations together, and some lived long enough to be saddened to see how much destruction airplanes could cause.","Yet aircraft have also saved enormous numbers of lives in times of crisis. During the Berlin Airlift of 1948 and 1949, cargo planes flew food and coal into the blockaded city of West Berlin for more than a year. Today aircraft carry aid after earthquakes and floods, fight forest fires by dropping water, and act as air ambulances and flying hospitals. Whether an invention helps or harms depends on the choices people make with it.","The airplane, then, is safer than ever, but it also brings noise, pollution, security worries and the power to do harm. Many of the newest tools for safety and efficiency come from computers. In today's cockpits, computers help fly the plane, guide it with satellites and can even land it in fog. And some aircraft now fly with no pilot on board at all. Part 19 takes off into the world of smart planes and drones."]}],"deepDive":"Safety experts often explain aviation's record with the 'Swiss cheese model', proposed by the psychologist James Reason. Each defence, such as design, maintenance, training, air traffic control, checklists and warning systems, is pictured as a slice of cheese with holes, its weaknesses. An accident happens only when the holes in many slices briefly line up. The model shifts attention away from blaming a single person and toward strengthening the whole system, which is why investigations under ICAO rules aim to find causes, not culprits. Many airlines also run confidential reporting programmes, so pilots and mechanics can report mistakes and near misses without fear of punishment; this so-called just culture catches problems before they cause accidents. Statistics need care. Fatal accidents per million flights fell from about 6.35 in 1970 to about 0.51 in 2019. Comparing flying with driving per kilometre makes flying look extremely safe, because planes cover huge distances; comparing per trip or per hour narrows the gap, although flying still comes out well ahead. The climate debate is also full of uncertainty. Aviation's CO2 share, roughly 2.4 to 2.7 percent of human emissions in 2018, is measured fairly well. The non-CO2 effects are not. Estimates for contrail cirrus range widely, and contrails form only in certain cold, humid layers of air. Interestingly, research suggests that a small fraction of flights produce most of the warming contrails, so slightly changing the altitude of those flights might reduce warming at little cost; airlines and scientists are testing this idea. Meanwhile CO2 lasts for centuries while contrails last hours, so comparing them depends on the time scale chosen, which is one reason scientists use different measures and argue about them. Finally, the Hudson ditching is often studied for its human factors: the NTSB stressed that simulator runs showing a successful return to LaGuardia ignored the roughly 35 seconds a real crew needs to recognise an unexpected emergency and decide what to do.","funFact":"The Airbus A320 that landed on the Hudson River was flying to Charlotte, North Carolina, and today it is displayed in a museum in Charlotte, so in a way it finally reached its destination.","check":{"q":"What colour are an airliner's flight recorders, the so-called black boxes?","a":"Bright orange","wrong":["Black","Silver","Dark blue"]},"sources":[{"title":"1956 Grand Canyon mid-air collision","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/1956_Grand_Canyon_mid-air_collision"},{"title":"Air traffic control","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Air_traffic_control"},{"title":"Flight recorder","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Flight_recorder"},{"title":"David Warren (inventor)","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/David_Warren_(inventor)"},{"title":"Crew resource management","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Crew_resource_management"},{"title":"US Airways Flight 1549","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/US_Airways_Flight_1549"},{"title":"Aviation safety","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Aviation_safety"},{"title":"Environmental impact of aviation","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Environmental_impact_of_aviation"}],"photos":[{"src":"img/deep/airplane/18-1.jpg","alt":"Close-up of a bright orange flight data recorder, a so-called 'black box', on display in a museum in Brazil.","file":"FLIGHT DATA RECORDER (4869416057).jpg","url":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:FLIGHT_DATA_RECORDER_(4869416057).jpg","author":"André Gustavo Stumpf from Brasília, Brasil","license":"CC BY 2.0"},{"src":"img/deep/airplane/18-2.jpg","alt":"US Airways Flight 1549 floats in the Hudson River in 2009, passengers standing on its wings. Everyone on board survived.","file":"US Airways Flight 1549 (N106US) after crashing into the Hudson River (crop 1).jpg","url":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:US_Airways_Flight_1549_(N106US)_after_crashing_into_the_Hudson_River_(crop_1).jpg","author":"Greg L","license":"CC BY 2.0"},{"src":"img/deep/airplane/18-3.jpg","alt":"The tall air traffic control tower at Beijing Daxing International Airport, with airliners parked around it.","file":"Air traffic control at Beijing Daxing International Airport.jpg","url":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Air_traffic_control_at_Beijing_Daxing_International_Airport.jpg","author":"Benlisquare","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0"}],"words":3947},{"n":19,"title":"Smart Planes and Drones","when":"1918 – today","summary":"Today's planes have computer brains that help pilots fly, find their way by satellite and even land themselves in fog. Some aircraft have no pilot on board at all: they are drones, from buzzing camera quadcopters to delivery planes. And in 2021 a tiny helicopter called Ingenuity flew on Mars!","sections":[{"heading":"A Computer Between the Pilot and the Wings","paragraphs":["Imagine climbing into the cockpit of an Airbus A320, one of the most common airliners on Earth. There is no big steering wheel. Instead, beside each pilot's seat is a small joystick, called a sidestick, that looks a bit like a game controller. When the captain gently pushes it to one side, no steel cable runs from the stick to the wings. The stick sends an electrical signal to a set of computers, and the computers decide exactly how far to move the ailerons, the elevator and the rudder. This system is called fly-by-wire: the pilot's commands travel to the control surfaces as electronic messages instead of through mechanical links.","Why replace cables and rods with wires and computers? There are several good reasons. Electrical wiring is lighter than cables and rods, and lighter planes burn less fuel. Fewer moving parts means fewer parts to wear out. A computer can also make thousands of tiny corrections every second, far faster than any person, smoothing out bumps and gusts before the passengers even notice. Most importantly, the computer can act as a guardian. Every airplane has a 'flight envelope', a set of safe limits for speed, angle and how hard the structure can be bent. On many fly-by-wire airliners, the computers will not let the pilot push the plane outside that envelope. Pull back on the stick too hard, and the computer will stop the wing from reaching the steep angle where it would stall (remember from Part 9 that a stall is a sudden loss of lift on the wing, not an engine stopping).","The idea grew step by step. In 1958 the Avro Canada CF-105 Arrow, a Canadian fighter, became the first non-experimental aircraft designed and flown with a fly-by-wire system. In the 1960s, NASA built strange-looking Lunar Landing Research and Training Vehicles so that Apollo astronauts, including Neil Armstrong, could practise landing on the Moon; these machines were flown entirely through electronics. In 1972 NASA flew a modified F-8 Crusader fighter that used a digital computer, with no mechanical backup at all, to send the pilot's commands to the controls. Its first computer was borrowed from the Apollo Moon program. Concorde, the supersonic airliner you met in Part 16, used an older analog (non-digital) fly-by-wire system, and the American F-16 fighter became the first production aircraft with digital fly-by-wire. The F-16 was deliberately designed to be slightly unstable, which made it very nimble; its computers constantly kept it steady, rather like a person balancing a broom on one finger.","Then came the big leap for passenger planes. The Airbus A320 first flew on 22 February 1987 and began carrying passengers with Air France in April 1988. It was the world's first airliner with a digital fly-by-wire flight control system, and the first airliner to use sidesticks. Many pilots were nervous at first, but the A320 family went on to become the best-selling airliner ever built, with more than 12,000 delivered. Boeing adopted fly-by-wire on the Boeing 777, which first flew in 1994, though Boeing chose to keep a traditional control wheel and to give pilots more freedom to override the computer's protections. Pilots and engineers still enjoy debating which approach is better.","A sensible question is: what happens if a computer breaks? Designers plan for exactly that. A fly-by-wire airliner carries several flight control computers, each able to take over if another fails, with separate power supplies and separate wiring routes. In some designs, the computers even use different kinds of processors and software written by different teams, so that a single hidden bug cannot knock them all out at once. This idea of having backups for the backups is called redundancy, and it is one reason modern airliners are so safe."]},{"heading":"Finding the Way: Autopilots, GPS and Autoland","paragraphs":["In Part 11 you met Lawrence Sperry, who in 1914 amazed a crowd in Paris by standing up in the cockpit while a gyroscope kept his plane flying straight. That gyroscopic autopilot was the great-grandparent of the systems inside every airliner today. A modern autopilot is linked to a flight management computer. Before departure, the pilots type in the planned route, a chain of invisible waypoints across the sky, and the computer can fly the plane along it, climbing, turning and descending as planned. On a long flight the autopilot may do most of the hands-on flying while the pilots watch the instruments, talk to air traffic control, check the weather and keep thinking ahead. Pilots still usually fly the takeoff by hand, and they must always be ready to take over.","Early airmail pilots followed railway lines, and ocean navigators once measured the stars with a sextant. Today the answer comes from a new kind of landmark: satellites. The United States Department of Defense started the Global Positioning System, or GPS, in 1973. The first prototype satellite was launched in 1978, and a full set of 24 satellites was working by 1993. Today about 31 operational GPS satellites circle the Earth at roughly 20,200 kilometres up. Each one carries extremely precise atomic clocks and constantly broadcasts the time and its own position. A receiver on a plane, a ship or your phone listens for those signals and measures how long each one took to arrive. Radio signals travel at the speed of light, about 300,000 kilometres per second, so even a timing mistake of one millionth of a second would put you about 300 metres off. With signals from at least four satellites, the receiver can work out its position in three dimensions, including its height.","The most impressive trick of all is autoland, where the plane lands itself. It was developed largely in Britain, where thick winter fog, made worse by smoke from coal fires, often shut down airports such as London Heathrow. A government team called the Blind Landing Experimental Unit, set up just after World War II, spent years testing the idea. In March 1964, a British European Airways Hawker Siddeley Trident made a full automatic landing at the test airfield at Bedford, and on 10 June 1965 a Trident flying passengers from Paris to Heathrow landed itself, with pilots Eric Poole and Frank Ormonroyd watching closely. Autoland follows invisible radio beams from the runway's Instrument Landing System, which tell the plane whether it is left or right of the centreline and too high or too low. Today many large airliners can touch down automatically in fog so thick the pilots may barely see the runway until the wheels are on it, a long way from Jimmy Doolittle's daring 'blind' flight in 1929."]},{"heading":"Plastic Planes? The Carbon-Fibre Revolution","paragraphs":["The Wright Flyer was built of wood and cotton cloth, and from the 1930s shiny aluminium ruled the skies. Then a new material crept in. A composite is made by combining two different materials so that together they are better than either one alone. The most important one in aviation is carbon-fibre-reinforced plastic. Its secret ingredient is carbon fibre, threads of carbon each much thinner than a human hair. Thousands of these fibres are bundled, laid in careful layers and soaked in a sticky resin, a kind of plastic glue. The part is then baked under heat and pressure, often inside a giant oven-like pressure chamber called an autoclave. The fibres carry the pulling and bending loads, and the hardened resin holds them in shape. The result can be stronger and stiffer than aluminium for the same weight, and it does not rust or corrode.","At first, composites were used for small, less critical parts, such as fairings, floor panels and tail surfaces. The big step came with the Boeing 787 Dreamliner. It first flew on 15 December 2009 from Paine Field in Everett, Washington, and entered passenger service with All Nippon Airways on 26 October 2011, flying from Tokyo's Narita Airport to Hong Kong. About half of the 787's structure by weight is composite, and by volume the figure is about 80 percent. Its fuselage is built as large one-piece barrel sections, wound and baked as whole tubes, instead of being riveted together from thousands of separate aluminium sheets.","Lighter, stronger materials helped make the 787 much more efficient. Boeing aimed for about 20 percent better fuel efficiency than the older 767 it replaced, thanks to the composites, newer engines and better aerodynamics. Passengers noticed differences too. Because the carbon-fibre fuselage is very strong and does not corrode, the cabin can be kept at a pressure closer to what you would feel on the ground and the air can be kept a little less dry. The windows are larger than on most airliners, and instead of plastic shades they have an electronic dimming button that makes the glass slowly darken."]},{"heading":"Planes With No One Inside: The First Drones","paragraphs":["The first serious pilotless airplane came from Dayton, Ohio, the Wright brothers' hometown. In November 1917, during World War I, the US Army asked the inventor Charles Kettering for an unmanned aircraft that could hit a target about 40 miles (about 65 kilometres) away. Kettering was already famous for inventing the electric self-starter for cars. He worked with the Dayton-Wright Airplane Company; Orville Wright advised on the aeronautics, and Elmer Sperry, father of Lawrence Sperry, designed the gyroscope guidance system. Their machine was officially called the Kettering Aerial Torpedo, but everyone nicknamed it the 'Bug'.","The Bug was surprisingly simple. Its body was made of wood laminate and papier-mache, its wings of cardboard, and it was pushed along by a 40-horsepower engine. Loaded, it weighed about 240 kilograms (530 pounds). It took off from a little wheeled trolley that ran along a track. Before launch, technicians worked out how many turns of the engine would carry it to the target, taking the wind into account. A counter ticked off the engine's revolutions, and when it reached the right number, the engine was switched off and the bolts holding the wings were released. The wings fell away and the body dropped onto the target with its explosive load. The first test flight, on 2 October 1918, failed when the Bug climbed too steeply, stalled and crashed. Later tests went better, and about 45 were built, but the war ended in November 1918 before any were used. The Bug is remembered as an ancestor of today's cruise missiles, and a full-size reproduction has been on display at the National Museum of the United States Air Force in Dayton since 1964.","The word 'drone' itself seems to come from Britain in the 1930s. The Royal Air Force and Royal Navy needed realistic targets for anti-aircraft gunners to practise on. De Havilland built a radio-controlled target plane called the Queen Bee, using the wings of its famous Tiger Moth training biplane and a wooden body based on its earlier Moth design. It could be flown by radio from the ground, and more than 400 were built from 1935. In a beehive, male bees are called drones, and the story often told is that when Americans began building their own target planes, they named them 'drones' as a nod to the Queen Bee. Historians cannot be completely sure that is how the word began, but it is the most commonly given explanation."]},{"heading":"Drones in War, and a Hard Debate","paragraphs":["In the 1990s, drones became an important part of armed forces, especially in the United States. The best-known early example was the General Atomics Predator. It first flew on 3 July 1994 in the Mojave Desert of California and entered service in 1995, when it was sent to watch over the war-torn Balkans in Europe. At first the Predator carried only cameras and other sensors. It could circle over one area for many hours, sending pictures back to its crew.","The Predator's crew did not sit in the aircraft at all. They worked in a ground control station that looked like a small office filled with screens, with a pilot who flew the drone and a sensor operator who aimed its cameras. Thanks to satellite links, the crew could be thousands of kilometres away from the drone itself. In 2001 the Predator was fitted to carry missiles, turning a watching machine into an armed one. The US Air Force retired the Predator in 2018 and replaced it with a larger, more powerful drone called the MQ-9 Reaper. In more recent conflicts, such as the war that followed Russia's invasion of Ukraine in 2022, many small and cheap drones, including adapted quadcopters, have been used in huge numbers.","Military drones have started one of the most serious debates about any flying machine. Supporters argue that drones keep pilots out of danger, and that because a drone can watch an area for a long time, its operators may be able to check more carefully before they act. Critics worry about other things. They point out that people who are not fighters have sometimes been harmed in drone strikes. They ask whether making war less risky for one side could make it easier for leaders to start or continue fighting. They also worry about secrecy, and about who is responsible when something goes wrong."]},{"heading":"Four Rotors and a Clever Brain: The Quadcopter","paragraphs":["The drone most people picture today is the quadcopter: a small, light machine with four spinning propellers, one at each corner. Look closely and you will notice something clever. Two of the propellers spin clockwise and two spin anticlockwise. Every spinning propeller tries to twist the drone's body the opposite way, just as a helicopter's main rotor tries to spin the helicopter's body (which is why ordinary helicopters need a small tail rotor). On a quadcopter, the clockwise and anticlockwise twists cancel each other out, so the body stays still. Quadcopters became a hugely popular hobby in the 2010s, once cheap sensors, chips and batteries developed for smartphones made them affordable: the French company Parrot launched its phone-controlled AR.Drone in 2010, and the Chinese company DJI released its Phantom camera drone in 2013.","A quadcopter steers simply by changing how fast each motor spins. If all four spin a little faster, the drone rises; a little slower, and it sinks. To tilt forward, the flight computer speeds up the two back rotors and slows the two front ones, so the drone leans forward and some of its thrust now pushes it along. Tilting left or right works the same way. To turn on the spot, it speeds up the two clockwise propellers and slows the two anticlockwise ones. Now the twists no longer cancel out, and the whole drone slowly rotates. This is pitch, roll and yaw again, the same three axes the Wright brothers mastered, but achieved with no hinged surfaces at all.","There is a catch. A quadcopter is naturally unstable, and no human could adjust four motors fast enough to keep it level. The real pilot is a tiny flight computer. It reads an inertial measurement unit, a chip containing gyroscopes that sense spinning and accelerometers that sense tilting and speeding up, much like the chips that let a phone know which way up it is. Hundreds of times every second, the computer compares how the drone is actually moving with how it should be moving and makes tiny corrections to each motor. Many drones also carry GPS to hold their position, a barometer to sense height from air pressure, a compass, and downward-facing cameras or sensors. If the radio link is lost, many can fly themselves back to the spot where they took off.","Drones now do an astonishing range of jobs. Film-makers use them for sweeping shots that once needed a helicopter. Farmers fly them over fields with special cameras that can show which plants are thirsty or sick, and some drones spray crops. Surveyors use them to make detailed maps and 3D models of land and buildings. Engineers send them to inspect bridges, wind turbines and power lines without climbing. Search-and-rescue teams fly drones with heat-sensing cameras that can spot a lost hiker's body warmth in the dark.","Deliveries are one of the most exciting uses. In October 2016 a company called Zipline began delivering blood and medical supplies to rural clinics in Rwanda. Its drones are actually small fixed-wing airplanes, not quadcopters. An electric catapult flings each one into the air at about 108 kilometres per hour, and when it reaches the clinic it drops its package by paper parachute into a landing zone only about 5 metres across, then flies home and is caught by a hook that snags a line, a little like a plane landing on an aircraft carrier. Zipline went on to work in several countries and reported its millionth delivery in April 2024. For a patient who urgently needs blood, a drone that can skip muddy, winding roads can save precious time."]},{"heading":"Rules of the Sky for Drone Pilots","paragraphs":["With millions of drones now sold, the sky has become much busier, and that brings real risks. Even a small drone can badly damage an airliner's engine or windshield if they collide, and a falling drone can hurt someone on the ground. In December 2018, reports of drones near London's Gatwick Airport stopped flights there for about a day and a half, disrupting the journeys of many thousands of passengers just before Christmas. Events like that convinced governments around the world to make clear rules for drone flying.","The details are different in every country, but the main ideas are similar almost everywhere. First, register. In the United States, drones heavier than 250 grams must be registered with the Federal Aviation Administration, and recreational flyers must pass a short free online safety test. In the United Kingdom, drone users need IDs from the Civil Aviation Authority, and the Flyer ID involves an online theory test; children can take that test, but a parent or guardian must handle the registration of the drone itself. The European Union has similar rules, with online exams for pilots of heavier drones.","Second, keep it in sight. Recreational pilots are generally required to keep their drone within visual line of sight, meaning they can see it with their own eyes, not just on a screen. Third, keep it low. In the UK and EU the usual limit is 120 metres above the ground, and in the US it is 400 feet, almost the same height. That keeps drones well below most crewed aircraft, except near airports where planes are low.","Fourth, stay away from airports. The United Kingdom made it illegal in 2018 to fly near airport boundaries without permission and later widened the restricted zones, and in the EU drones heavier than 250 grams must stay at least 5 kilometres from airports and airfields in most cases. Fifth, keep away from people. Drone rules forbid flying over crowds and set safe distances from people who are not part of the flight. Pilots must also respect privacy and never fly near emergencies such as fires or accidents, where firefighting and rescue helicopters may be working. Since 2021, the US has also required most drones over 250 grams to broadcast a 'Remote ID', a kind of electronic licence plate that tells officials where the drone and its pilot are.","If you get a drone, the best plan is simple: fly with an adult, read the official rules for your own country before your first flight, use an approved map or app to check where flying is allowed, and practise in a big open space far from people, roads and buildings. Real pilots follow checklists and rules, and the best drone pilots do too."]},{"heading":"A Helicopter on Mars","paragraphs":["Now travel hundreds of millions of kilometres from Earth to a dusty, orange crater on Mars called Jezero. It is 19 April 2021. On the rusty ground stands a small machine with spindly legs and two rotors stacked one above the other. Its name is Ingenuity, and it is about to try something no aircraft had ever done: fly under its own power on another planet.","Flying on Mars is fiendishly difficult. The Martian air is only about 1 percent as thick as Earth's air at sea level, roughly like the air about 27 kilometres above Earth, far higher than any airliner flies. With so little air to push against, an ordinary helicopter would never get off the ground. Ingenuity's engineers at NASA's Jet Propulsion Laboratory in California made it extremely light, about 1.8 kilograms, and gave it two rotors about 1.2 metres across that spin in opposite directions, cancelling each other's twist just as a quadcopter's propellers do. To grip the thin air, the blades spin incredibly fast, about 2,400 revolutions per minute, many times faster than a passenger helicopter's rotor. Luckily, Mars's gravity is only about 38 percent of Earth's.","Ingenuity rode to Mars folded up under the belly of NASA's Perseverance rover, which landed in Jezero Crater on 18 February 2021. On 3 April the rover gently dropped it onto the surface and drove away. The first challenge was simply surviving the night, when temperatures can plunge to around minus 90 degrees Celsius. A solar panel above the rotors recharged six small lithium-ion batteries during the day, and some of that energy powered heaters to keep its electronics alive in the cold.","Ingenuity could not be steered with a joystick from Earth. Radio messages between the two planets take several minutes or more each way, so by the time a command arrived, the flight would be over. Instead, the helicopter flew itself. Its computer, using a processor similar to those found in smartphones, watched the ground through a downward-pointing camera, tracked how surface features moved below it, and adjusted the rotors many times a second. On 19 April 2021, Ingenuity rose about 3 metres into the Martian sky, hovered, turned, and landed after roughly 40 seconds. It was the first powered, controlled flight of an aircraft on another planet. The project team, led by project manager MiMi Aung, with chief engineer Bob Balaram and chief pilot Havard Grip, celebrated in the control room when the data came in.","NASA named the little patch of Martian ground where the flight happened Wright Brothers Field. There was a reason it felt so fitting. Tucked under Ingenuity's solar panel, wrapped around a cable, was a tiny piece of the cotton fabric from the wing of the 1903 Wright Flyer, about the size of a postage stamp. A scrap of the cloth that had lifted Orville Wright into the air at Kill Devil Hills had now flown on another world. It was not the first time the Flyer had travelled into space: in 1969, Neil Armstrong carried pieces of its wood and fabric to the Moon.","Ingenuity was a technology demonstration, planned to make no more than five test flights over about 30 days. It kept going. It became an aerial scout for Perseverance, photographing the land ahead and helping the rover's team choose routes and interesting rocks. In all, Ingenuity made 72 flights over almost three years, spent about 2 hours and 8 minutes in the air and travelled more than 17 kilometres. On its 72nd flight, on 18 January 2024, its navigation system struggled over a stretch of smooth, featureless sand ripples that gave its camera few landmarks to track. It came down hard, and its rotor blades were damaged. NASA announced the end of the mission on 25 January 2024. The little helicopter that was supposed to fly five times had flown more than fourteen times as often.","Ingenuity proved that flying machines can help explore other worlds. NASA is studying larger Mars helicopters that could carry scientific instruments, and a rotorcraft called Dragonfly is being prepared to explore Titan, Saturn's largest moon. Barely more than a century after a 12-second hop on a windy beach, the story of flight had leapt to another planet. So here is a question to carry into the final part: if we can fly on Mars, what will flying on Earth look like when you are grown up?"]}],"deepDive":"Fly-by-wire and quadcopters both rest on a branch of engineering called control theory: measure what a system is doing, compare it with what it should be doing, and correct the difference, over and over. A typical small drone runs a feedback loop hundreds of times a second, often using a 'PID' controller that reacts to the present error (proportional), the build-up of past error (integral) and how fast the error is changing (derivative). Because computers can react so quickly, designers can build aircraft that are deliberately less stable, like the F-16, which makes them more agile and can reduce drag. Airliners use computers mostly for smoothness and protection instead. This has created a long-running design debate. Airbus fly-by-wire 'hard' protections normally stop pilots from exceeding certain limits, while Boeing's approach generally lets pilots override protections with enough force. Accident investigators have studied cases on both sides, including situations where crews were confused about what the automation was doing, which is why training now stresses understanding the automation, not just using it. Composites raise their own engineering questions: they resist fatigue and corrosion well, but impact damage can hide below an undamaged-looking surface, so inspection methods such as ultrasound matter. Ingenuity is a showcase of extreme aerodynamics. With rotors about 1.2 metres across spinning at roughly 2,400 rpm, the blade tips move at about 150 metres per second. On Mars, where the cold, carbon-dioxide air carries sound at only about 240 metres per second, that is around two-thirds of the local speed of sound, so the team had to keep tip speeds below the range where shock waves would form. The thin air also means the blades work at very low 'Reynolds numbers', a regime in which air behaves more like a thick, sticky fluid relative to the blade, and wing shapes that work well on Earth perform poorly. Finally, the ethics of military drones remain contested: scholars debate whether remote warfare lowers the threshold for using force, how international law applies to targeted strikes, and whether future autonomous weapons should be restricted by treaty. Discussions on autonomous weapons have continued at the United Nations for years without a final agreement.","funFact":"In 1944 an army photographer snapped pictures of a young woman working at the Radioplane drone factory in California; her name was Norma Jeane Dougherty, and she later became the film star Marilyn Monroe.","check":{"q":"What did the Mars helicopter Ingenuity carry from the 1903 Wright Flyer?","a":"A tiny piece of the Flyer's wing fabric","wrong":["One of the Flyer's wooden propellers","A spark plug from the Flyer's engine","Orville Wright's pilot's goggles"]},"sources":[{"title":"Ingenuity (helicopter)","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ingenuity_(helicopter)"},{"title":"Ingenuity Mars Helicopter","publisher":"NASA Science","url":"https://science.nasa.gov/mission/mars-2020-perseverance/ingenuity-mars-helicopter/"},{"title":"Fly-by-wire","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Fly-by-wire"},{"title":"Airbus A320 family","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Airbus_A320_family"},{"title":"Boeing 787 Dreamliner","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Boeing_787_Dreamliner"},{"title":"Kettering Bug","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Kettering_Bug"},{"title":"Quadcopter","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Quadcopter"},{"title":"Regulation of unmanned aerial vehicles","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Regulation_of_unmanned_aerial_vehicles"}],"photos":[{"src":"img/deep/airplane/19-1.jpg","alt":"The Ingenuity helicopter standing on the dusty surface of Mars in 2021, photographed by the Perseverance rover.","file":"Mastcam-Z Gives Ingenuity a Close-up.jpg","url":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Mastcam-Z_Gives_Ingenuity_a_Close-up.jpg","author":"NASA/JPL-Caltech/ASU/MSSS","license":"Public domain"},{"src":"img/deep/airplane/19-2.jpg","alt":"The Kettering 'Bug', a pilotless flying torpedo from 1918, on display at the National Museum of the US Air Force.","file":"KetteringAerialTorpedo.jpg","url":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:KetteringAerialTorpedo.jpg","author":"Greg Hume","license":"CC BY-SA 3.0"},{"src":"img/deep/airplane/19-3.jpg","alt":"Cockpit of an Air France Airbus A320: the little sidestick controllers sit at the far left and far right instead of a control wheel.","file":"Cockpit of Airbus A320-211 Air France (F-GFKH).jpg","url":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Cockpit_of_Airbus_A320-211_Air_France_(F-GFKH).jpg","author":"Louis from Paris, France","license":"CC BY-SA 2.0"}],"words":4317},{"n":20,"title":"The Future of Flight and the Long Journey","when":"Today – the future, looking back","summary":"Inventors are now trying to make flying cleaner and quieter, with electric planes, hydrogen, new fuels and a plane that flew around the world on sunshine. Then we look back at the whole amazing journey, from kites and balloons to Mars. The next big idea in flight could be yours!","sections":[{"heading":"The Next Big Puzzle: Flying Without Harming the Planet","paragraphs":["Every great chapter of this story began with a puzzle. Cayley's puzzle was how to separate lift from thrust. The Wrights' puzzle was control. Whittle and von Ohain's puzzle was how to fly faster than propellers allowed. Today's engineers face a new puzzle that may be the hardest yet: how can people keep flying without warming the planet?","In Part 18 you learned that aviation produces roughly 2 to 3 percent of the carbon dioxide that humans release, plus extra warming from contrails, the long white trails of ice crystals that planes can leave high in the sky. That may sound small, but flying is growing, and it is one of the hardest activities to make clean. A car can run on a battery that weighs a few hundred kilograms. A long-distance airliner needs so much energy that, at takeoff, a large share of its weight can be fuel.","Engineers are attacking the puzzle from several directions at once. They are making planes lighter and more streamlined, with composite structures like the Boeing 787's and long, slender wings. They are building engines with ever-bigger fans that push more air more gently, saving fuel. Airlines are flying smarter routes and taxiing with fewer engines running. But the biggest changes would come from new kinds of energy: electricity stored in batteries, hydrogen, and fuels made from waste or even from air. None of these is easy, and each has a catch. Let's look at them one at a time, like scientists, and remember that careful testing beats guessing."]},{"heading":"Batteries Versus Fuel: The Electric Plane Challenge","paragraphs":["Electric planes sound wonderful. Electric motors are quiet, have few moving parts and turn about 90 percent of their energy into useful work, while most jet engines manage only around half. Electric motors also do not need air to burn fuel, so they do not lose power at high altitude. A short flight on electricity, using power from wind or solar farms, could produce almost no pollution at all.","So why are we not all flying electric already? The answer is one word: weight. Scientists compare fuels using energy density, the amount of energy packed into each kilogram. The best lithium-ion batteries in recent years store roughly 250 to 300 watt-hours per kilogram. Jet fuel stores about 12,000 watt-hours per kilogram, roughly 40 times as much. Even after you allow for electric motors being more efficient, a battery big enough to fly a large airliner across an ocean would be far too heavy to get off the ground. There is a second problem: a plane burning fuel gets lighter as it flies, but a battery weighs exactly the same when it is empty as when it is full.","Electric flight is not new. In 1973 a small motor glider called the Militky MB-E1 carried a person on battery power in Austria, using nickel-cadmium batteries. In 1981 the MacCready Solar Challenger, covered with solar cells, flew about 163 miles (262 kilometres) from France to England. More recently, on 10 December 2019, the Canadian seaplane airline Harbour Air flew a classic DHC-2 Beaver floatplane fitted with a 560-kilowatt electric motor from the company magniX, near Vancouver. On 10 June 2020, the two-seat Pipistrel Velis Electro, built in Slovenia, became the first electric aircraft to receive a type certificate, the official safety approval needed for normal use, from Europe's aviation safety agency, EASA. It is used mostly for pilot training, on flights of under an hour.","That shows where electric planes fit first: short hops, flight schools and small commuter planes carrying a handful of people between nearby islands or towns. Many companies are designing electric and hybrid-electric aircraft with nine to a few dozen seats. Hybrids, like many hybrid cars, combine a battery with a small fuel-burning engine or generator. Batteries keep improving, but for long flights across continents and oceans, most experts expect liquid fuels to remain necessary for many years. Whether that changes depends on discoveries that have not been made yet, perhaps by someone reading this."]},{"heading":"Hydrogen and Greener Fuels","paragraphs":["Hydrogen is the lightest element in the universe, and it packs about three times as much energy per kilogram as jet fuel. When it is used, the main product is water. That makes it very attractive for aviation. There are two ways to use it. It can be burned in a modified jet engine, or it can be fed into a fuel cell, a device that combines hydrogen with oxygen from the air to make electricity, which then drives electric motors.","The catch is volume. Hydrogen is a very light gas, so even when it is cooled into a liquid, it takes up about four times as much space as jet fuel for the same energy. Liquid hydrogen must also be kept colder than minus 253 degrees Celsius. That needs big, round, heavily insulated tanks, which do not fit inside thin wings the way jet fuel does, so designers would have to put them inside the fuselage, taking up space where passengers or cargo might go. Airports would also need completely new ways to make, store and pump hydrogen safely.","Engineers have been experimenting for decades. In 1988 a Soviet Tupolev Tu-155 airliner flew with one of its three engines running on hydrogen. In 2023 a company called ZeroAvia flew a Dornier 228 test plane in Britain with a hydrogen fuel-cell system driving one of its propellers. Airbus announced a program called ZEROe in 2020 to study hydrogen airliners, but it later pushed its plans back by several years because the technology and hydrogen supplies were not ready. Another start-up, Universal Hydrogen, closed in 2024 after it ran out of money. These setbacks are a normal part of invention; remember how many flying machines failed before 1903.","A more immediate idea is sustainable aviation fuel, often shortened to SAF. It is jet fuel made not from crude oil pumped out of the ground but from things like used cooking oil, waste animal fat, certain crops, farm and forest waste, or even from hydrogen combined with carbon dioxide captured from the air. Because the carbon in these fuels was recently taken out of the atmosphere by plants, or captured directly, the overall carbon dioxide released over the fuel's life can be much lower than for ordinary fuel, though how much lower depends greatly on how it is made.","The great advantage of SAF is that it is a 'drop-in' fuel: today's engines and airport fuel tanks can use it without changes. Current rules allow it to be blended up to 50 percent with regular jet fuel. In 2008 Virgin Atlantic flew a Boeing 747 with one engine partly running on biofuel, and in 2023 the same airline flew a Boeing 787 from London Heathrow to New York on 100 percent SAF as a special test. The problems are cost and quantity. In 2024 SAF made up only about 0.3 percent of the world's aviation fuel. The European Union now requires a growing share of SAF in fuel at its airports, 6 percent by 2030 and 70 percent by 2050, which will push companies to build many more SAF factories."]},{"heading":"Flying on Sunshine: Solar Impulse 2","paragraphs":["On 9 March 2015, a very strange airplane took off from Abu Dhabi in the United Arab Emirates. Its wings stretched about 72 metres from tip to tip, wider than a Boeing 747's, yet the whole aircraft weighed only about 2,300 kilograms, roughly as much as a large family car. It carried no fuel at all. Its wings, body and tail were covered in 17,248 solar cells, and inside were four lithium-ion battery packs. This was Solar Impulse 2, and its pilots planned to fly it all the way around the world on sunlight alone.","The two pilots took turns, because the cockpit had room for only one person. Bertrand Piccard, a Swiss psychiatrist and explorer, came from a famous family of adventurers: his grandfather Auguste Piccard rode a balloon into the stratosphere in 1931, and his father Jacques Piccard dived to the deepest point of the ocean in 1960. Bertrand himself had flown around the world nonstop in a balloon in 1999 with Brian Jones. His partner, André Borschberg, was a Swiss engineer and businessman who had trained as a jet pilot in the Swiss Air Force. Together they had spent more than a decade planning the project.","The trick was the day and night cycle. During the day, the solar cells powered the four propellers and charged the batteries, and the plane slowly climbed, often to around 8,500 metres and on some legs even higher. At night it glided gently down, saving energy, then flew on battery power until the Sun rose again. The plane was slow, averaging only about 76 kilometres per hour over the whole journey, about the speed of a car on a country road. The cockpit was not heated or pressurized, so the pilots wore oxygen masks at high altitude and dressed for extreme cold, and they slept only in short naps of about 20 minutes.","The hardest leg was across the Pacific Ocean. In June and July 2015, Borschberg flew from Nagoya in Japan to Hawaii, alone, for 117 hours and 52 minutes, nearly five days and nights without landing. It set records for the longest solar-powered flight and the longest solo flight ever made in an airplane. But the batteries had overheated during the climb, because they were wrapped in too much insulation, and the plane had to wait in Hawaii for repairs until April 2016.","After crossing the United States and the Atlantic Ocean, Solar Impulse 2 landed back in Abu Dhabi on 26 July 2016. It had made the trip in 17 stages over about 16 and a half months and flown roughly 40,000 kilometres (some counts give about 42,000), the first circumnavigation of the Earth by a piloted airplane powered only by the Sun. Solar Impulse was never meant to become an airliner. It was a flying demonstration, built to show what clean energy and careful engineering could do, just as Lilienthal's photographs once showed the world that a human could really glide."]},{"heading":"Faster but Quieter? The X-59 Experiment","paragraphs":["When Concorde made its last passenger flight in 2003, supersonic travel for ordinary passengers ended. One big reason, as you saw in Part 16, was the sonic boom. Many countries, including the United States, banned airliners from flying faster than sound over land, so Concorde could only go supersonic over the sea. Engineers began to wonder: what if a plane could be shaped so that its boom became a soft thump instead of a loud bang?","NASA's answer is the X-59, built by Lockheed Martin's famous Skunk Works for a mission called Quesst. It looks like a needle with wings. The X-59 is about 30 metres long, with a very long, pointed nose that makes up a large part of its length. Every curve of its shape is designed to spread out the shock waves so that they do not pile up into one sharp boom when they reach the ground. The nose is so long that the pilot cannot see straight ahead through a front window. Instead, the pilot uses an 'external vision system', a camera and screen that show the view in front of the plane.","The X-59 made its first flight on 28 October 2025, flying below the speed of sound from Palmdale to NASA's Armstrong Flight Research Center in California. In June 2026 it flew faster than sound for the first time, and soon after reached about Mach 1.4. By the autumn of 2026 it was flying regularly, sometimes several test flights in a single week. NASA plans to fly the X-59 over communities in the United States and ask people living below what they heard and how much it bothered them. Those answers will be shared with regulators, such as the US Federal Aviation Administration and the International Civil Aviation Organization, which could use them to decide whether quiet supersonic flight over land should one day be allowed.","Private companies are experimenting too. In January 2025, a small test jet called XB-1, built by the American company Boom Supersonic, flew faster than sound. The company hopes to build a supersonic airliner, but such plans are still on the drawing board and in testing. It is important to be honest about this: no new supersonic airliner is carrying passengers today, and history is full of exciting aircraft projects that never reached the airport. The X-59 is an experiment, and its job is to gather facts."]},{"heading":"Air Taxis: Electric Helicopters for Cities?","paragraphs":["Picture a small, quiet aircraft rising straight up from a rooftop pad, tilting its propellers forward and whisking four passengers across a crowded city in a few minutes instead of an hour in traffic. That is the dream of the eVTOL, short for electric vertical take-off and landing aircraft. Unlike helicopters, which usually have one big rotor powered by a jet-like engine, most eVTOLs use many small electric propellers, each driven by its own motor and controlled by a fly-by-wire computer, a bit like a giant, people-carrying cousin of a drone.","Designs come in several shapes. Some are multicopters, with lots of fixed rotors that only push upward, like a big quadcopter. Others are tilt-rotor designs: their propellers point up for takeoff and then swivel forward so that a wing can carry the weight in cruise, which is much more efficient. A third type uses one set of propellers for lifting and a separate set for cruising. Companies working on them include Joby Aviation and Archer Aviation in the United States, EHang in China, Wisk, which is backed by Boeing, and many others around the world.","In October 2023 the EHang EH216-S, a two-seat multicopter designed to fly without a pilot on board, received a type certificate from China's aviation authority, the first of its kind. Other companies have been carrying out long programs of test flights and working with regulators in the United States, Europe, Japan, the Middle East and elsewhere. Even so, eVTOL air taxis are still in testing and early trials. They are not yet a normal, everyday way for people to travel.","The challenges are real. Batteries limit how far and how long these aircraft can fly, and they must keep plenty of energy in reserve for safety. The aircraft must prove they are as safe as airliners, which takes years of testing. Cities would need landing pads, sometimes called vertiports, chargers and new air traffic systems to manage hundreds of small aircraft at low height. And people living below will want them to be quiet. Some well-funded companies have already run out of money, a reminder that in aviation, a good idea is only the beginning."]},{"heading":"The Long Journey: From Icarus to Mars","paragraphs":["Before we look ahead any further, let's look back at how far we have come. The journey began with stories: Icarus and his wax wings in Greek myth, flying chariots and winged gods. It continued with real objects that rode the wind, the kites of ancient China, made of wood, silk and bamboo more than 2,000 years ago. Brave tower jumpers like Abbas ibn Firnas and Eilmer of Malmesbury tried to copy birds, at least according to accounts written long afterwards, and Leonardo da Vinci filled notebooks with flying machines he never built.","In 1783 the Montgolfier brothers' hot-air balloon carried the first humans into the sky over Paris. Then Sir George Cayley stopped copying flapping birds and scratched a fixed wing, a body and a tail onto a silver disc in 1799, and he named the four forces of flight: lift, weight, thrust and drag. Steam-powered machines hopped but could not be controlled. Otto Lilienthal made more than 2,000 glides and showed the world, in photographs, that a person could truly fly a wing. Octave Chanute gathered knowledge and shared it freely.","Two bicycle makers from Dayton, Wilbur and Orville Wright, realised that control was the key. They built a homemade wind tunnel, tested about 200 small wing shapes, made hundreds of glides, and invented three-axis control. With Charlie Taylor's engine and their own propellers, on 17 December 1903, Orville flew for 12 seconds and about 37 metres, a distance shorter than the wingspan of a jumbo jet. It was the first sustained, powered and controlled airplane flight.","After that, the pace became astonishing. Blériot crossed the English Channel in 1909. Alcock and Brown crossed the Atlantic nonstop in 1919, and Lindbergh flew solo from New York to Paris in 1927. The DC-3 made airlines pay their way. Frank Whittle and Hans von Ohain, working separately, gave the world the jet engine. Chuck Yeager went faster than sound in a rocket plane in 1947. The Boeing 747 made flying across the world affordable for millions. Computers learned to fly alongside pilots, drones took to the air, and in 2021 Ingenuity flew on Mars.","Think about this: only 66 years passed between the Wright brothers' first flight in 1903 and astronauts landing on the Moon on 20 July 1969. Some people who were children when the Wrights first flew lived to watch the Moon landing on television. Neil Armstrong even carried a piece of the Wright Flyer's wooden propeller and a scrap of its wing fabric with him to the Moon.","The 1903 Wright Flyer itself survives. For 20 years, from 1928 to 1948, it was displayed at the Science Museum in London, because Orville Wright was upset that the Smithsonian Institution had given credit for the first flying machine to its own former leader, Samuel Langley. After the Smithsonian publicly accepted the Wrights' achievement in 1942, the Flyer came home in 1948. Today it hangs in the National Air and Space Museum in Washington, D.C., where millions of visitors can stand beneath the fragile wood and cloth machine that started it all."]},{"heading":"Lessons From the Sky, and Your Turn","paragraphs":["What can the story of the airplane teach us about inventing anything? The first lesson is that careful testing beats guessing. For centuries, people jumped off towers trusting their hopes. The Wrights succeeded partly because they refused to trust numbers they had not checked. When their 1901 glider produced only about a third of the expected lift, they did not give up; they built a wind tunnel and measured. Fifty years later, investigators found the cause of the Comet crashes by testing a whole fuselage in a water tank until it cracked. Good inventors treat every failure as data.","The second lesson is that sharing knowledge helps everyone. Cayley published his ideas. Lilienthal published his tables and his photographs. Chanute collected the world's flying research into one book and wrote encouraging letters to anyone who asked, including two brothers from Ohio. When Wilbur Wright wrote to the Smithsonian in 1899, the Smithsonian sent him papers to read. Today, whenever there is an accident, investigators share what they learn with airlines and aircraft makers around the world, which is a big reason flying became so safe.","The third lesson is that the airplane was built by many people, not by one hero. Behind every famous name stand others: Gustav Lilienthal helping his brother, Charlie Taylor building an engine in six weeks, John T. Daniels squeezing a camera bulb on a cold beach, engineers in wind tunnels, mechanics in hangars, air traffic controllers, flight attendants, factory workers and test pilots. People from France, Germany, Britain, Brazil, Russia, Australia, the United States, China and many other countries each added a piece. Even the disputed 'firsts' remind us that many minds were racing toward the same dream at the same time.","There is a fourth lesson too: inventions bring responsibilities. Airplanes brought families together, carried medicine and food, and let scientists explore the world from above. They have also been used in war, and they add to climate change. The people who design the next generation of aircraft will need to be not only clever but wise.","Which brings us to you. Every pioneer in this story started as a curious young person. The Wright brothers were inspired by a rubber-band toy helicopter. You can start right now: fold paper airplanes and change one thing at a time to see what happens, fly a kite and feel the lift in the string, build a balsa glider, or, with an adult, learn to fly a small drone safely. Learn maths and physics, because they are the language of flight. Write computer code, because software now helps fly almost every aircraft. Visit an air museum, and stand under a real wing. Keep a notebook, like Leonardo and Lilienthal did.","The next big idea in flight has not been invented yet. Maybe it will be a battery that changes everything, a silent supersonic airliner, a new kind of wing, or a helicopter exploring the clouds of another world. Maybe it will be yours. For thousands of years, people looked up at the birds and wondered why they could not fly. Now we can. So the next time you hear a plane passing overhead, look up at the sky, and ask yourself the question that started it all: what if?"]}],"deepDive":"The central obstacle to clean flight is physics, not willpower. Jet fuel stores roughly 12,000 watt-hours of energy per kilogram, while today's best lithium-ion cells store about 250 to 300, and a complete battery pack with cooling and casing stores less. Electric drivetrains are more efficient, turning perhaps 90 percent of stored energy into propeller power versus roughly 40 to 50 percent for a gas turbine, but that still leaves a gap of well over ten to one. Range is also hurt because batteries never get lighter, whereas a long-haul airliner may burn a large fraction of its takeoff weight in fuel. This is why most studies see battery aircraft serving short routes first, with hybrids, hydrogen and sustainable fuels handling longer ones. Hydrogen reverses the problem: per kilogram it holds about three times the energy of kerosene, but as a liquid at minus 253 degrees Celsius it needs roughly four times the volume, and its tanks are heavy and bulky. Burning hydrogen produces no carbon dioxide but does produce water vapour and some nitrogen oxides, and scientists are still studying how water vapour released at high altitude affects contrails and climate. Sustainable aviation fuel is chemically similar to kerosene, so its benefit depends on its full life cycle: estimates of how much it cuts emissions range widely, from modest to very large, depending on the raw material, how much land and energy are used, and whether it competes with food crops. Researchers also debate how much to prioritise contrail avoidance, since small changes in flight altitude could prevent many warming contrails. For supersonic flight, the key question is human response: the X-59 is designed to turn a boom into a quieter thump, and its community tests aim to gather the evidence regulators need. Looking back, historians also argue about how to describe 'firsts'. The Wrights are credited with the first sustained, powered, controlled airplane flights, while many Brazilians honour Santos-Dumont for the first publicly witnessed powered flights in Europe, and claims for Whitehead and Pearse lack solid evidence. Careful wording, rather than national pride, is what makes history trustworthy.","funFact":"When Neil Armstrong landed on the Moon in 1969, he carried with him a piece of wood from the 1903 Wright Flyer's propeller and a scrap of fabric from its wing.","check":{"q":"How did Solar Impulse 2 keep flying through the night?","a":"It used energy stored in batteries that its solar cells had charged during the day","wrong":["It burned a small tank of jet fuel at night","It landed every evening and took off again at sunrise","It was towed by another airplane until morning"]},"sources":[{"title":"Solar Impulse","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Solar_Impulse"},{"title":"Lockheed Martin X-59 Quesst","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Lockheed_Martin_X-59_Quesst"},{"title":"Quesst mission","publisher":"NASA","url":"https://www.nasa.gov/mission/quesst/"},{"title":"Electric aircraft","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Electric_aircraft"},{"title":"Hydrogen-powered aircraft","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hydrogen-powered_aircraft"},{"title":"Sustainable aviation fuel","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sustainable_aviation_fuel"},{"title":"eVTOL","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/EVTOL"},{"title":"Wright Flyer","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Wright_Flyer"}],"photos":[{"src":"img/deep/airplane/20-1.jpg","alt":"Solar Impulse 2, its long wing covered in solar cells, taking off in Switzerland in 2014 with Bertrand Piccard at the controls.","file":"Solar Impulse SI2 pilote Bertrand Piccard Payerne November 2014.jpg","url":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Solar_Impulse_SI2_pilote_Bertrand_Piccard_Payerne_November_2014.jpg","author":"Milko Vuille","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0"},{"src":"img/deep/airplane/20-2.jpg","alt":"NASA's X-59 quiet supersonic plane, with its very long pointed nose, on its first flight in October 2025 beside an F/A-18 chase jet.","file":"X-59 First Flight with F-A-18 Chase Aircraft (x-59-lbfd-first-flight).jpg","url":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:X-59_First_Flight_with_F-A-18_Chase_Aircraft_(x-59-lbfd-first-flight).jpg","author":"NASA/Jim Ross","license":"Public domain"},{"src":"img/deep/airplane/20-3.jpg","alt":"The original 1903 Wright Flyer, the first powered airplane, in a Smithsonian National Air and Space Museum photograph.","file":"1903 Wright Flyer - DPLA - 913052369bd2468611aff158239166f4 (page 1).jpg","url":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:1903_Wright_Flyer_-_DPLA_-_913052369bd2468611aff158239166f4_(page_1).jpg","author":"Unknown author","license":"CC0"}],"words":3840}],"words":82775};
